imately $20 billion FY2027 standalone-CPU outlook is the market/company reference and sits at rung 2.', 'implied_outcome': 'About $25 billion or somewhat above: approximately $20 billion of standalone CPU revenue for FY2027 plus Grace CPU revenue above $5 billion on a trailing-12-month basis, carried forward as a run-rate.', 'reasons': 'The reference is NVIDIA’s approximately $20 billion FY2027 standalone-CPU outlook, which sits at rung 2. The implied total is about $25 billion or somewhat above: the $20 billion standalone outlook plus Grace CPU revenue running above $5 billion on a trailing-12-month basis. That crosses the $25 billion boundary for rung 1. Vera being in full production and shipments underway support the outlook; the AMD performance claims are a counterweight but do not quantify a reduction to NVIDIA’s forecast.', 'cites': ['e894', 'e895', 'e3302', 'e2767', 'e2768', 'e3083', 'e2852', 'e1030', 'e1031', 'e1032'], 'cites_views': [], 'pushes': [{'evidence_id': 'e894', 'push': 'none'}, {'evidence_id': 'e895', 'push': 'none'}, {'evidence_id': 'e3302', 'push': 'none'}, {'evidence_id': 'e2767', 'push': 'toward_first'}, {'evidence_id': 'e2768', 'push': 'toward_first'}, {'evidence_id': 'e3083', 'push': 'toward_first'}, {'evidence_id': 'e2852', 'push': 'toward_first'}, {'evidence_id': 'e1030', 'push': 'toward_last'}, {'evidence_id': 'e1031', 'push': 'toward_last'}, {'evidence_id': 'e1032', 'push': 'toward_last'}], 'theme_pushes': [{'view_id': 'v6', 'push': 'toward_first'}, {'view_id': 'v7', 'push': 'toward_first'}], 'changed': False, 'why_changed': 'The rung and key implied figure remain the same as the previous conclusion: approximately $20 billion in standalone CPU revenue plus Grace’s more-than-$5-billion trailing-12-month run-rate. The additional deployment detail does not size or revise FY2027 revenue, and the AMD performance evidence does not quantify a change to NVIDIA’s outlook.'}\nlists: [('sources', ['e3083']), ('partial', []), ('near_misses', ['e2767', 'e2768'])]\n\n old-3:6458|3|e2769 Thesis v14 (NVDA, asset, focus \"Vera CPU revenue\", FY2027, due 2027-02-28): What will NVIDIA report as total CPU revenue for FY2027?\n{'rung': 1, 'reference': \"NVIDIA's approximately $20 billion FY2027 standalone CPU guidance; this reference sits at rung 2. No separate market consensus for total CPU revenue is supplied.\", 'implied_outcome': 'Approximately $20 billion of FY2027 standalone CPU revenue plus a carried-forward Grace CPU run-rate above $5 billion (reported on a trailing-12-month basis) implies total FY2027 CPU revenue of at least about $25 billion, placing the forecast at rung 1.', 'reasons': \"The reference is NVIDIA's approximately $20 billion FY2027 standalone CPU guidance, which sits at rung 2. The implied total is at least about $25 billion: the $20 billion standalone forecast is distinct from Grace, whose trailing-12-month CPU revenue already exceeded $5 billion; carrying that run-rate into FY2027 puts total CPU revenue at or above the rung-1 threshold. Vera production, shipments and customer commitments support delivery of the standalone forecast. AMD's performance claims and the challenge of taking share are counterweights, but they do not quantify a reduction to NVIDIA's guidance or to Grace's run-rate. Recent AWS and FY2028 evidence strengthens the demand and execution context but does not justify raising the FY2027 estimate beyond this implied figure.\", 'cites': ['e1586', 'e2767', 'e2768', 'e1030', 'e1031', 'e1075', 'e2933', 'e3083', 'e3302', 'e3921'], 'cites_views': [], 'pushes': [{'evidence_id': 'e626', 'push': 'toward_first'}, {'evidence_id': 'e629', 'push': 'toward_first'}, {'evidence_id': 'e661', 'push': 'toward_first'}, {'evidence_id': 'e812', 'push': 'toward_first'}, {'evidence_id': 'e814', 'push': 'toward_first'}, {'evidence_id': 'e816', 'push': 'toward_first'}, {'evidence_id': 'e823', 'push': 'toward_first'}, {'evidence_id': 'e862', 'push': 'toward_first'}, {'evidence_id': 'e894', 'push': 'toward_last'}, {'evidence_id': 'e895', 'push': 'toward_first'}, {'evidence_id': 'e957', 'push': 'none'}, {'evidence_id': 'e1030', 'push': 'toward_last'}, {'evidence_id': 'e1031', 'push': 'toward_last'}, {'evidence_id': 'e1032', 'push': 'toward_last'}, {'evidence_id': 'e1042', 'push': 'toward_first'}, {'evidence_id': 'e1043', 'push': 'none'}, {'evidence_id': 'e1075', 'push': 'toward_last'}, {'evidence_id': 'e1077', 'push': 'toward_last'}, {'evidence_id': 'e1181', 'push': 'toward_first'}, {'evidence_id': 'e1552', 'push': 'toward_first'}, {'evidence_id': 'e1586', 'push': 'toward_first'}, {'evidence_id': 'e1624', 'push': 'toward_first'}, {'evidence_id': 'e2427', 'push': 'toward_first'}, {'evidence_id': 'e2428', 'push': 'toward_first'}, {'evidence_id': 'e2429', 'push': 'none'}, {'evidence_id': 'e2430', 'push': 'toward_first'}, {'evidence_id': 'e2486', 'push': 'toward_first'}, {'evidence_id': 'e2494', 'push': 'none'}, {'evidence_id': 'e2688', 'push': 'toward_first'}, {'evidence_id': 'e2767', 'push': 'toward_first'}, {'evidence_id': 'e2768', 'push': 'toward_first'}, {'evidence_id': 'e2852', 'push': 'toward_first'}, {'evidence_id': 'e2933', 'push': 'toward_first'}, {'evidence_id': 'e2949', 'push': 'toward_first'}, {'evidence_id': 'e3016', 'push': 'toward_first'}, {'evidence_id': 'e3082', 'push': 'toward_first'}, {'evidence_id': 'e3083', 'push': 'toward_first'}, {'evidence_id': 'e3302', 'push': 'none'}, {'evidence_id': 'e3355', 'push': 'toward_first'}, {'evidence_id': 'e3356', 'push': 'none'}, {'evidence_id': 'e3405', 'push': 'none'}, {'evidence_id': 'e3406', 'push': 'none'}, {'evidence_id': 'e3571', 'push': 'toward_first'}, {'evidence_id': 'e3921', 'push': 'toward_first'}, {'evidence_id': 'e3982', 'push': 'none'}], 'theme_pushes': [{'view_id': 'v6', 'push': 'toward_first'}, {'view_id': 'v7', 'push': 'toward_first'}], 'changed': True, 'why_changed': \"The rung and implied FY2027 figure remain at least about $25 billion, but new evidence adds material execution and demand context: NVIDIA described Vera shipments to lead partners and AWS's 2-million-GPU deployment alongside Vera CPUs, and projected FY2028 standalone CPU revenue above $40 billion. Those items support the outlook without changing the FY2027 quantitative anchor.\"}\nlists: [('sources', ['e3083']), ('partial', []), ('near_misses', ['e2767', 'e2768'])]\n\n control-1:8807|1|e393 Thesis v14 (NVDA, asset, focus \"NVIDIA FY2027 total revenue\", FY2027, due 2027-02-28): How much total revenue will NVIDIA report for FY2027?\n{'rung': 2, 'reference': 'Bloomberg/Yahoo Finance consensus is about $393 billion for FY2027, placing the reference in rung 3 ($350 billion to less than $400 billion).', 'implied_outcome': 'About $430 billion for FY2027. The Q1 actual, Q2 guidance, and guided sequential growth imply a roughly $386 billion baseline when that growth is carried forward through Q3 and Q4; demand and order evidence supports a modest uplift, while supply and China constraints limit it.', 'reasons': 'The market reference is about $393 billion for FY2027, which sits in rung 3. The guidance-led baseline remains about $386 billion: $81.6 billion reported in Q1, $91 billion guided for Q2, then carrying forward the guided 11% sequential growth for Q3 and Q4. I raise the forecast modestly to about $430 billion: the newly reported roughly $1 trillion in confirmed Blackwell and Vera Rubin orders and strong customer AI spending support upside, but those orders span products and periods and do not translate dollar-for-dollar into FY2027 revenue. HBM ramp constraints and the exclusion of China Data Center compute from Q2 temper the uplift. The implied figure is above $400 billion but below $450 billion, so it remains in rung 2.', 'cites': ['e669', 'e238', 'e240', 'e878', 'e1336', 'e1371', 'e1376', 'e1377', 'e1378', 'e1380', 'e321', 'e906', 'e284', 'e882', 'e883'], 'cites_views': [], 'pushes': [{'evidence_id': 'e669', 'push': 'none'}, {'evidence_id': 'e238', 'push': 'toward_first'}, {'evidence_id': 'e240', 'push': 'toward_first'}, {'evidence_id': 'e878', 'push': 'toward_first'}, {'evidence_id': 'e1336', 'push': 'toward_first'}, {'evidence_id': 'e1371', 'push': 'toward_first'}, {'evidence_id': 'e1376', 'push': 'toward_first'}, {'evidence_id': 'e1377', 'push': 'toward_first'}, {'evidence_id': 'e1378', 'push': 'toward_first'}, {'evidence_id': 'e1380', 'push': 'toward_last'}, {'evidence_id': 'e321', 'push': 'toward_last'}, {'evidence_id': 'e906', 'push': 'toward_first'}, {'evidence_id': 'e284', 'push': 'toward_last'}, {'evidence_id': 'e882', 'push': 'toward_first'}, {'evidence_id': 'e883', 'push': 'toward_first'}], 'theme_pushes': [{'view_id': 'v10', 'push': 'toward_first'}, {'view_id': 'v19', 'push': 'toward_first'}, {'view_id': 'v25', 'push': 'toward_first'}, {'view_id': 'v26', 'push': 'toward_first'}, {'view_id': 'v27', 'push': 'toward_first'}, {'view_id': 'v5', 'push': 'toward_first'}, {'view_id': 'v6', 'push': 'toward_first'}], 'changed': True, 'why_changed': 'The rung is unchanged, but the implied forecast rises from about $425 billion to about $430 billion. The reported description of roughly $1 trillion in orders as confirmed, alongside recent customer spending and AI-business data, modestly strengthens the demand case; these are not all NVIDIA revenue in FY2027 and do not justify crossing the $450 billion boundary.'}\nlists: [('sources', ['e195', 'e669']), ('partial', []), ('near_misses', [])]\n\n control-1:8807|2|e393 Thesis v14 (NVDA, asset, focus \"NVIDIA FY2027 total revenue\", FY2027, due 2027-02-28): How much total revenue will NVIDIA report for FY2027?\n{'rung': 2, 'reference': 'Market consensus is approximately $393 billion for FY2027, in rung 3 ($350 billion to less than $400 billion).', 'implied_outcome': 'Approximately $425 billion: Q1 reported revenue of $81.6 billion plus Q2 guidance of $91 billion, followed by the guided 11% sequential growth carried into Q3 and Q4, yields a roughly $386 billion baseline. Strong demand and order visibility justify an uplift, discounted for multi-period/product orders and supply, China, and competition risks.', 'reasons': 'The market reference is about $393 billion, which is rung 3. The reported Q1 revenue of $81.6 billion and Q2 guidance of $91 billion, carried forward at the guided 11% sequential growth rate, imply a roughly $386 billion run-rate baseline—not annualizing Q1 flat. Strong order visibility and customer investment support an uplift to about $425 billion, while the multi-year Blackwell/Rubin order figure is not all FY2027 revenue, China compute remains excluded from Q2 guidance, and memory and software-competition risks temper the upside. About $425 billion is above the $400 billion boundary but below $450 billion, so the forecast remains rung 2.', 'cites': ['e669', 'e751', 'e238', 'e240', 'e577', 'e1336', 'e459', 'e234', 'e284', 'e231', 'e321', 'e1371', 'e1376', 'e1380', 'e1395', 'e1396', 'e1400', 'e1401', 'e1402', 'e1403', 'e1405', 'e1406', 'e1408'], 'cites_views': [], 'pushes': [{'evidence_id': 'e669', 'push': 'toward_last'}, {'evidence_id': 'e751', 'push': 'toward_last'}, {'evidence_id': 'e238', 'push': 'toward_first'}, {'evidence_id': 'e240', 'push': 'toward_first'}, {'evidence_id': 'e577', 'push': 'toward_first'}, {'evidence_id': 'e1336', 'push': 'toward_first'}, {'evidence_id': 'e459', 'push': 'toward_first'}, {'evidence_id': 'e234', 'push': 'toward_first'}, {'evidence_id': 'e284', 'push': 'toward_last'}, {'evidence_id': 'e231', 'push': 'toward_last'}, {'evidence_id': 'e321', 'push': 'toward_last'}, {'evidence_id': 'e1371', 'push': 'toward_first'}, {'evidence_id': 'e1376', 'push': 'toward_first'}, {'evidence_id': 'e1380', 'push': 'toward_last'}, {'evidence_id': 'e1395', 'push': 'toward_last'}, {'evidence_id': 'e1396', 'push': 'toward_last'}, {'evidence_id': 'e1400', 'push': 'toward_last'}, {'evidence_id': 'e1401', 'push': 'toward_last'}, {'evidence_id': 'e1402', 'push': 'toward_first'}, {'evidence_id': 'e1403', 'push': 'toward_first'}, {'evidence_id': 'e1405', 'push': 'toward_first'}, {'evidence_id': 'e1406', 'push': 'toward_first'}, {'evidence_id': 'e1408', 'push': 'none'}], 'theme_pushes': [{'view_id': 'v10', 'push': 'toward_first'}, {'view_id': 'v25', 'push': 'toward_first'}, {'view_id': 'v26', 'push': 'toward_first'}, {'view_id': 'v27', 'push': 'toward_last'}, {'view_id': 'v5', 'push': 'toward_first'}, {'view_id': 'v6', 'push': 'toward_first'}], 'changed': True, 'why_changed': \"The rung is unchanged, but the newly arrived Aug.  3 evidence adds a distinct software-portability and CUDA-moat risk to the revenue outlook, balanced by countervailing views that verification, optimization, and NVIDIA's own progress limit that threat. It changes the key supporting facts, but does not move the implied forecast across a rung boundary.\"}\nlists: [('sources', ['e195', 'e669']), ('partial', []), ('near_misses', [])]\n\n control-1:8807|3|e393 Thesis v14 (NVDA, asset, focus \"NVIDIA FY2027 total revenue\", FY2027, due 2027-02-28): How much total revenue will NVIDIA report for FY2027?\n{'rung': 2, 'reference': \"Market consensus is about $393 billion for FY2027; that is rung 3 ($350 billion to less than $400 billion). Zacks' estimate of 80% year-over-year growth is consistent with that reference range.\", 'implied_outcome': 'The Q1 actual of $81.6 billion plus Q2 guidance of $91 billion, with 11% sequential growth carried through Q3 and Q4, implies about $386 billion. I raise that baseline to approximately $430 billion for confirmed multi-period product orders and strong customer spending, while discounting the orders for their FY2027 share and accounting for memory and China constraints.', 'reasons': 'The market reference is about $393 billion for FY2027 (with Zacks also indicating roughly 80% growth from FY2026), which sits in rung 3. Q1 revenue of $81.6 billion and Q2 guidance of $91 billion, carried forward at the guided 11% sequential growth rate, imply a baseline of about $386 billion. I forecast approximately $430 billion: recent evidence of roughly $1 trillion in confirmed Blackwell and Vera Rubin orders through 2027, alongside strong cloud growth and elevated customer investment, supports an uplift from that baseline. The orders span products and periods, so they do not translate dollar-for-dollar into FY2027 revenue; memory-supply constraints and the continued China compute exclusion also limit the uplift. The implied outcome is above $400 billion but below $450 billion.', 'cites': ['e669', 'e751', 'e238', 'e240', 'e1336', 'e1371', 'e1376', 'e1379', 'e321', 'e284'], 'cites_views': [], 'pushes': [{'evidence_id': 'e669', 'push': 'none'}, {'evidence_id': 'e751', 'push': 'none'}, {'evidence_id': 'e238', 'push': 'toward_first'}, {'evidence_id': 'e240', 'push': 'toward_first'}, {'evidence_id': 'e1336', 'push': 'toward_first'}, {'evidence_id': 'e1371', 'push': 'toward_first'}, {'evidence_id': 'e1376', 'push': 'toward_first'}, {'evidence_id': 'e1379', 'push': 'toward_first'}, {'evidence_id': 'e321', 'push': 'toward_last'}, {'evidence_id': 'e284', 'push': 'toward_last'}], 'theme_pushes': [{'view_id': 'v10', 'push': 'toward_first'}, {'view_id': 'v19', 'push': 'toward_first'}, {'view_id': 'v25', 'push': 'toward_first'}, {'view_id': 'v26', 'push': 'toward_first'}, {'view_id': 'v27', 'push': 'toward_last'}, {'view_id': 'v5', 'push': 'toward_first'}, {'view_id': 'v6', 'push': 'toward_first'}], 'changed': True, 'why_changed': 'The rung remains 2, but the forecast rises modestly from the prior approximately $425 billion as the evidence now describes roughly $1 trillion of confirmed orders, rather than only potential product revenue, and adds recent cloud-growth and customer-investment evidence. Those orders still span multiple products and periods, so they do not justify moving above $450 billion.'}\nlists: [('sources', ['e195', 'e669']), ('partial', []), ('near_misses', [])]\n\n control-1:8938|1|e362 Thesis v21 (NVDA, asset, focus \"NVIDIA total revenue growth in FY2027\", FY2027, due 2027-02-28): How much will NVIDIA's total revenue grow in FY2027?\n{'rung': 1, 'reference': 'The latest stated Wall Street expectation for the coming year is 45%, which sits at rung 3. Earlier specific FY2027 estimates were 83% (LSEG) and 85.4% (Zacks), both rung 1.', 'implied_outcome': \"About $406.8 billion of FY2027 revenue, or approximately 88.4% growth over FY2026's $215.94 billion: $81.6 billion in Q1 plus $96.2 billion in Q2 plus $108 billion of Q3 guidance, then Q4 estimated at about $121.0 billion by carrying forward Q3's 12% sequential growth.\", 'reasons': \"The latest stated Wall Street expectation for the coming year is 45%, which sits at rung 3; earlier specific FY2027 estimates of 83% and 85.4% sit at rung 1. The forecast is above either reference: Q1 and Q2 revenue plus Q3 guidance total $285.8 billion, and carrying Q3's 12% sequential growth into Q4 implies roughly $121.0 billion for Q4 and $406.8 billion for FY2027, about 88.4% growth on FY2026 revenue of $215.94 billion. The 70% full-year growth outlook is also rung 1, though below that run-rate; reaching only 70% would require Q4 revenue of about $81.3 billion, a sharp decline from Q3 guidance of $108 billion. Sequential growth is slowing, and the multi-year CAGR and downside risks temper confidence, but they do not imply a drop below the 60% rung boundary.\", 'cites': ['e966', 'e1883', 'e2118', 'e2146', 'e2151', 'e2154', 'e2244', 'e2421', 'e2497', 'e2499', 'e2989', 'e3108', 'e3110', 'e3112', 'e3593', 'e3620', 'e3683', 'e3943', 'e4049', 'e4165', 'e4226', 'e4227'], 'cites_views': [], 'pushes': [{'evidence_id': 'e966', 'push': 'toward_last'}, {'evidence_id': 'e1883', 'push': 'none'}, {'evidence_id': 'e2118', 'push': 'toward_first'}, {'evidence_id': 'e2146', 'push': 'toward_first'}, {'evidence_id': 'e2147', 'push': 'toward_first'}, {'evidence_id': 'e2151', 'push': 'toward_last'}, {'evidence_id': 'e2154', 'push': 'toward_first'}, {'evidence_id': 'e2244', 'push': 'toward_first'}, {'evidence_id': 'e2421', 'push': 'toward_first'}, {'evidence_id': 'e2497', 'push': 'toward_first'}, {'evidence_id': 'e2499', 'push': 'toward_first'}, {'evidence_id': 'e2762', 'push': 'none'}, {'evidence_id': 'e2989', 'push': 'toward_first'}, {'evidence_id': 'e3108', 'push': 'toward_last'}, {'evidence_id': 'e3110', 'push': 'toward_last'}, {'evidence_id': 'e3112', 'push': 'toward_last'}, {'evidence_id': 'e3593', 'push': 'toward_first'}, {'evidence_id': 'e3620', 'push': 'toward_first'}, {'evidence_id': 'e3683', 'push': 'toward_first'}, {'evidence_id': 'e3883', 'push': 'none'}, {'evidence_id': 'e3942', 'push': 'none'}, {'evidence_id': 'e3943', 'push': 'toward_last'}, {'evidence_id': 'e4049', 'push': 'toward_first'}, {'evidence_id': 'e4164', 'push': 'none'}, {'evidence_id': 'e4165', 'push': 'toward_last'}, {'evidence_id': 'e4197', 'push': 'none'}, {'evidence_id': 'e4224', 'push': 'none'}, {'evidence_id': 'e4226', 'push': 'toward_last'}, {'evidence_id': 'e4227', 'push': 'toward_last'}], 'theme_pushes': [{'view_id': 'v10', 'push': 'toward_first'}, {'view_id': 'v19', 'push': 'toward_first'}, {'view_id': 'v25', 'push': 'toward_first'}, {'view_id': 'v26', 'push': 'toward_first'}, {'view_id': 'v27', 'push': 'toward_last'}, {'view_id': 'v5', 'push': 'toward_first'}, {'view_id': 'v6', 'push': 'toward_first'}], 'changed': True, 'why_changed': \"The rung remains 1, but the latest stated Wall Street expectation is 45% rather than the prior conclusion's 83%–85.4% reference range. That changes a key supporting reference, though the operating run-rate and 70% outlook still imply rung 1.\"}\nlists: [('sources', ['e3620']), ('partial', []), ('near_misses', [])]\n\n control-1:8938|2|e362 Thesis v21 (NVDA, asset, focus \"NVIDIA total revenue growth in FY2027\", FY2027, due 2027-02-28): How much will NVIDIA's total revenue grow in FY2027?\n{'rung': 1, 'reference': \"The latest specific Wall Street reference shown is LSEG's 83% growth estimate to $396 billion, while Zacks estimates 85.4%; both sit at rung 1. The older 42% estimate cited in the ladder sits at rung 3 but is superseded by those more recent estimates.\", 'implied_outcome': \"About $406.8 billion of FY2027 revenue, or approximately 88.4% growth over FY2026's $215.94 billion: $81.6 billion in Q1 plus $96.2 billion in Q2 plus Q3 guidance of $108 billion totals $285.8 billion, and carrying the roughly 12% sequential growth implied by Q3 guidance into Q4 gives about $121.0 billion.\", 'reasons': \"The implied outcome is above the rung-1 boundary of 60%, and it remains well above the current specific Wall Street estimates. NVIDIA's reported Q1 and Q2 revenue plus Q3 guidance total $285.8 billion; carrying the approximately 12% sequential growth implied by Q3 guidance into Q4 gives about $121.0 billion for Q4 and $406.8 billion for FY2027. The reported 70% full-year growth outlook is a lower counter-signal: it implies about $367 billion for FY2027 and only $81.3 billion in Q4 after the first three quarters, a sharp sequential decline not supported by the latest guidance and run-rate. Newer, lower analyst figures and general customer-concentration and pricing risks temper confidence but do not outweigh that operating trajectory.\", 'cites': ['e966', 'e1883', 'e2118', 'e2146', 'e2421', 'e2497', 'e2499', 'e3108', 'e3110', 'e3112', 'e3620', 'e3943', 'e4165', 'e4227'], 'cites_views': [], 'pushes': [{'evidence_id': 'e966', 'push': 'none'}, {'evidence_id': 'e1883', 'push': 'none'}, {'evidence_id': 'e2118', 'push': 'toward_first'}, {'evidence_id': 'e2146', 'push': 'toward_first'}, {'evidence_id': 'e2421', 'push': 'toward_first'}, {'evidence_id': 'e2497', 'push': 'toward_first'}, {'evidence_id': 'e2499', 'push': 'toward_first'}, {'evidence_id': 'e3108', 'push': 'toward_last'}, {'evidence_id': 'e3110', 'push': 'toward_last'}, {'evidence_id': 'e3112', 'push': 'toward_last'}, {'evidence_id': 'e3620', 'push': 'toward_first'}, {'evidence_id': 'e3943', 'push': 'toward_first'}, {'evidence_id': 'e4165', 'push': 'toward_last'}, {'evidence_id': 'e4227', 'push': 'toward_last'}], 'theme_pushes': [{'view_id': 'v10', 'push': 'toward_first'}, {'view_id': 'v19', 'push': 'toward_first'}, {'view_id': 'v25', 'push': 'toward_first'}, {'view_id': 'v26', 'push': 'toward_first'}, {'view_id': 'v27', 'push': 'none'}, {'view_id': 'v5', 'push': 'toward_first'}, {'view_id': 'v6', 'push': 'toward_first'}], 'changed': True, 'why_changed': \"The rung and central run-rate estimate remain essentially the same as the previous conclusion. The newer 59% FY2026-to-FY2029 CAGR and Wall Street's stated 45% growth for the coming year add lower forecast anchors, though neither is a specific FY2027 estimate; they change the supporting forecast context without moving the implied FY2027 outcome across a rung boundary.\"}\nlists: [('sources', ['e3620']), ('partial', []), ('near_misses', [])]\n\n control-1:8938|3|e362 Thesis v21 (NVDA, asset, focus \"NVIDIA total revenue growth in FY2027\", FY2027, due 2027-02-28): How much will NVIDIA's total revenue grow in FY2027?\n{'rung': 1, 'reference': \"The latest specific FY2027 market estimate shown is Zacks' 85.4% revenue growth (e3620), rung 1; LSEG's 83% estimate (e2421) is also rung 1. The older 42% estimate in e966 is rung 3.\", 'implied_outcome': \"About $407 billion of FY2027 revenue, or roughly 88.5% growth over FY2026's $215.94 billion: Q1 actual revenue of $81.6 billion plus Q2 actual revenue of $96.2 billion plus Q3 guidance of $108.0 billion equals $285.8 billion, and carrying the Q2-to-Q3 sequential growth rate forward implies about $121 billion for Q4.\", 'reasons': \"The current market reference is Zacks' 85.4% FY2027 growth estimate, which sits at rung 1; LSEG's 83% estimate is also rung 1. The older 42% estimate in e966 is rung 3, but is superseded as a reference by the more recent FY2027 estimates. NVIDIA's stated 70% full-year growth outlook is also rung 1. The quarterly run-rate points higher: Q1 revenue of $81.6 billion, Q2 revenue of $96.2 billion, and Q3 guidance of $108.0 billion total $285.8 billion; carrying the Q2-to-Q3 sequential growth rate forward implies roughly $121 billion in Q4. The resulting FY2027 revenue is about $407 billion, or 88.5% growth on FY2026 revenue of $215.94 billion. That is comfortably above the 60% rung-1 boundary. The 59% FY2026–FY2029 CAGR estimate and cited downside risks temper confidence, but do not outweigh the current-quarter guidance and run-rate.\", 'cites': ['e966', 'e1883', 'e2118', 'e2146', 'e2147', 'e2151', 'e2154', 'e2244', 'e2421', 'e2497', 'e2499', 'e2989', 'e3108', 'e3110', 'e3112', 'e3593', 'e3620', 'e3683', 'e3943', 'e4049', 'e4165', 'e4227'], 'cites_views': [], 'pushes': [{'evidence_id': 'e966', 'push': 'toward_last'}, {'evidence_id': 'e1883', 'push': 'none'}, {'evidence_id': 'e2118', 'push': 'toward_first'}, {'evidence_id': 'e2146', 'push': 'toward_first'}, {'evidence_id': 'e2147', 'push': 'toward_first'}, {'evidence_id': 'e2151', 'push': 'toward_last'}, {'evidence_id': 'e2154', 'push': 'toward_first'}, {'evidence_id': 'e2244', 'push': 'toward_first'}, {'evidence_id': 'e2421', 'push': 'toward_first'}, {'evidence_id': 'e2497', 'push': 'toward_first'}, {'evidence_id': 'e2499', 'push': 'toward_first'}, {'evidence_id': 'e2762', 'push': 'none'}, {'evidence_id': 'e2989', 'push': 'toward_first'}, {'evidence_id': 'e3108', 'push': 'toward_first'}, {'evidence_id': 'e3110', 'push': 'toward_last'}, {'evidence_id': 'e3112', 'push': 'toward_last'}, {'evidence_id': 'e3593', 'push': 'toward_first'}, {'evidence_id': 'e3620', 'push': 'toward_first'}, {'evidence_id': 'e3683', 'push': 'toward_first'}, {'evidence_id': 'e3883', 'push': 'none'}, {'evidence_id': 'e3942', 'push': 'none'}, {'evidence_id': 'e3943', 'push': 'toward_last'}, {'evidence_id': 'e4049', 'push': 'toward_first'}, {'evidence_id': 'e4164', 'push': 'none'}, {'evidence_id': 'e4165', 'push': 'toward_last'}, {'evidence_id': 'e4197', 'push': 'none'}, {'evidence_id': 'e4224', 'push': 'none'}, {'evidence_id': 'e4226', 'push': 'none'}, {'evidence_id': 'e4227', 'push': 'toward_last'}], 'theme_pushes': [{'view_id': 'v10', 'push': 'toward_first'}, {'view_id': 'v19', 'push': 'toward_first'}, {'view_id': 'v25', 'push': 'toward_first'}, {'view_id': 'v26', 'push': 'toward_first'}, {'view_id': 'v27', 'push': 'toward_first'}, {'view_id': 'v5', 'push': 'toward_first'}, {'view_id': 'v6', 'push': 'toward_first'}], 'changed': True, 'why_changed': 'The rung and approximate quarterly run-rate are unchanged from the previous conclusion. The evidence in view now includes the FY2026–FY2029 59% CAGR estimate and a 45% analyst-growth reference, which temper the supporting outlook; the updated AI-capex themes also change the demand context. These changes affect the supporting facts and balance of evidence, but not the implied outcome enough to cross a rung boundary.'}\nlists: [('sources', ['e3620']), ('partial', []), ('near_misses', [])]\n\n linked-1:8777|1|e693 Thesis v16 (NVDA, asset, focus \"NVIDIA total CPU revenue in FY2027\", FY2027, due 2027-02-28): How much total CPU revenue will NVIDIA generate in FY2027?\n{'rung': 3, 'reference': 'The market reference is NVIDIA management’s FY2027 visibility to “nearly $20 billion,” which sits in rung 3 ($15 billion to $20 billion inclusive).', 'implied_outcome': 'Approximately $19 billion in FY2027: management’s period-specific visibility is nearly $20 billion, so I treat it as just below $20 billion; the Wolfe estimate of about $6.5 billion for Vera in calendar 2026 is a product-level check, not total FY2027 CPU revenue.', 'reasons': 'NVIDIA’s FY2027 visibility to “nearly $20 billion” is the strongest period-specific forecast and sits in rung 3. Wolfe’s estimate of 1.3 million Vera CPUs at about $5,000 each implies roughly $6.5 billion for that product in calendar 2026, a meaningful downside check but not an estimate of total FY2027 CPU revenue. June deliveries, planned large-quantity OpenAI deployment, and HPE’s planned fall availability support a ramp, while AMD’s modeled throughput comparison is a competitive risk rather than evidence of lost NVIDIA revenue. The resulting estimate remains below rung 2’s $20 billion boundary and above rung 3’s $15 billion floor. It is also consistent with v14’s approximately $387 billion FY2027 total-revenue forecast.', 'cites': ['e443', 'e612', 'e666', 'e668', 'e723', 'e724', 'e794'], 'cites_views': ['v14'], 'pushes': [{'evidence_id': 'e443', 'push': 'toward_first'}, {'evidence_id': 'e612', 'push': 'toward_first'}, {'evidence_id': 'e666', 'push': 'toward_last'}, {'evidence_id': 'e668', 'push': 'toward_first'}, {'evidence_id': 'e723', 'push': 'toward_first'}, {'evidence_id': 'e724', 'push': 'toward_first'}, {'evidence_id': 'e794', 'push': 'toward_last'}], 'theme_pushes': [{'view_id': 'v10', 'push': 'toward_first'}, {'view_id': 'v5', 'push': 'toward_first'}, {'view_id': 'v6', 'push': 'toward_first'}], 'changed': False, 'why_changed': 'The recent evidence adds longer-term CPU-market sizing and a modeled AMD performance comparison, but neither quantifies a change to NVIDIA’s FY2027 CPU revenue outlook. The period-specific “nearly $20 billion” visibility remains the key forecast, so the rung and implied estimate are unchanged.'}\nlists: [('sources', ['e667', 'e694', 'e715', 'e794']), ('partial', []), ('near_misses', ['e689'])]\n\n linked-1:8777|2|e693 Thesis v16 (NVDA, asset, focus \"NVIDIA total CPU revenue in FY2027\", FY2027, due 2027-02-28): How much total CPU revenue will NVIDIA generate in FY2027?\n{'rung': 3, 'reference': \"NVIDIA management's FY2027 visibility to “nearly $20 billion” is the available period-specific reference; it sits in rung 3. The separate $20 billion standalone CPU-business target has no stated period.\", 'implied_outcome': \"Approximately $19 billion of total CPU revenue in FY2027: use management's “nearly $20 billion” period-specific visibility as the forecast anchor, with disclosed Vera shipment economics and deployment evidence supporting scale but not establishing a higher total.\", 'reasons': \"The supplied FY2027 reference is NVIDIA management's visibility to “nearly $20 billion” in total CPU revenue, which sits in rung 3 (at least $15 billion and at most $20 billion). The roughly $20 billion standalone-CPU target has no specified period, so it does not override that period-specific reference. Shipments and planned deployments support execution, but the disclosed unit-and-price estimate implies about $6.5 billion for Vera in calendar 2026, not a quantified FY2027 revision to total CPU revenue; competitive and market-size figures likewise do not quantify NVIDIA's FY2027 sales. The implied outcome remains about $19 billion, within rung 3 and below rung 2's strictly greater-than-$20-billion boundary. It is also consistent with dependency v14's approximately $387 billion FY2027 total-revenue forecast.\", 'cites': ['e443', 'e612', 'e666', 'e668', 'e723', 'e724', 'e793', 'e794'], 'cites_views': ['v14'], 'pushes': [{'evidence_id': 'e443', 'push': 'toward_first'}, {'evidence_id': 'e612', 'push': 'toward_first'}, {'evidence_id': 'e666', 'push': 'toward_last'}, {'evidence_id': 'e668', 'push': 'toward_first'}, {'evidence_id': 'e723', 'push': 'toward_first'}, {'evidence_id': 'e724', 'push': 'toward_first'}, {'evidence_id': 'e793', 'push': 'toward_first'}, {'evidence_id': 'e794', 'push': 'toward_last'}], 'theme_pushes': [{'view_id': 'v5', 'push': 'toward_first'}, {'view_id': 'v6', 'push': 'toward_first'}], 'changed': True, 'why_changed': \"The rung and implied estimate remain about $19 billion, but the new quantified market and competitive context—including BofA's $170 billion 2030 TAM estimate and AMD's modeled throughput comparisons—updates the supporting execution-risk picture without quantifying a FY2027 revenue change or crossing a rung boundary.\"}\nlists: [('sources', ['e667', 'e694', 'e715', 'e794']), ('partial', []), ('near_misses', ['e689'])]\n\n linked-1:8777|3|e693 Thesis v16 (NVDA, asset, focus \"NVIDIA total CPU revenue in FY2027\", FY2027, due 2027-02-28): How much total CPU revenue will NVIDIA generate in FY2027?\n{'rung': 3, 'reference': \"NVIDIA management's “nearly $20 billion” FY2027 total CPU revenue visibility is the market/company reference and sits in rung 3; rung 2 begins above $20 billion.\", 'implied_outcome': \"Approximately $19 billion in FY2027. Management's period-specific visibility is the strongest forecast input; Wolfe's estimate implies about $6.5 billion for Vera in calendar 2026 (1.3 million × about $5,000), but is a single-product, different-period downside check rather than a replacement estimate for total FY2027 CPU revenue.\", 'reasons': \"The reference is NVIDIA management's visibility to “nearly $20 billion” in FY2027 total CPU revenue, which sits in rung 3 (at most $20 billion); the rung 2 boundary is revenue above $20 billion. The strongest estimate remains about $19 billion: the $20 billion target without a period and the $200 billion market opportunity do not establish a higher FY2027 run-rate, while Wolfe's 1.3 million Vera-unit estimate at about $5,000 each implies roughly $6.5 billion for that product in calendar 2026, a material but not directly comparable downside check. June deliveries and OpenAI's planned Q3 deployment support sales, but disclose no volume that would move the forecast above $20 billion. This is consistent with v14's approximately $387 billion FY2027 total-revenue forecast: CPU revenue at about $19 billion is a subset and does not exceed the whole.\", 'cites': ['e443', 'e666', 'e668', 'e723', 'e724', 'e794', 'e793'], 'cites_views': ['v14'], 'pushes': [{'evidence_id': 'e442', 'push': 'toward_first'}, {'evidence_id': 'e443', 'push': 'toward_first'}, {'evidence_id': 'e475', 'push': 'toward_first'}, {'evidence_id': 'e489', 'push': 'toward_first'}, {'evidence_id': 'e584', 'push': 'toward_first'}, {'evidence_id': 'e596', 'push': 'toward_first'}, {'evidence_id': 'e597', 'push': 'toward_first'}, {'evidence_id': 'e612', 'push': 'toward_first'}, {'evidence_id': 'e664', 'push': 'toward_first'}, {'evidence_id': 'e665', 'push': 'none'}, {'evidence_id': 'e666', 'push': 'toward_last'}, {'evidence_id': 'e667', 'push': 'toward_last'}, {'evidence_id': 'e668', 'push': 'toward_first'}, {'evidence_id': 'e676', 'push': 'toward_first'}, {'evidence_id': 'e679', 'push': 'toward_first'}, {'evidence_id': 'e681', 'push': 'toward_first'}, {'evidence_id': 'e689', 'push': 'toward_first'}, {'evidence_id': 'e694', 'push': 'toward_last'}, {'evidence_id': 'e715', 'push': 'toward_last'}, {'evidence_id': 'e723', 'push': 'toward_first'}, {'evidence_id': 'e724', 'push': 'toward_first'}, {'evidence_id': 'e725', 'push': 'toward_first'}, {'evidence_id': 'e793', 'push': 'toward_first'}, {'evidence_id': 'e794', 'push': 'toward_last'}, {'evidence_id': 'e795', 'push': 'none'}], 'theme_pushes': [{'view_id': 'v5', 'push': 'toward_first'}, {'view_id': 'v6', 'push': 'toward_first'}], 'changed': False, 'why_changed': \"The implied outcome remains about $19 billion and the key period-specific reference remains “nearly $20 billion.” The additional market and competitive evidence does not quantify a revision to NVIDIA's FY2027 CPU revenue forecast or move the implication across a rung boundary.\"}\nlists: [('sources', ['e667', 'e694', 'e715', 'e794']), ('partial', []), ('near_misses', ['e689'])]\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_12","type":"command_execution","command":"/opt/homebrew/bin/zsh -lc \"python3 -c 'import json\np=json.load(open(\\\"poc/data/citation-probe-2026-11-22/packet-B.json\\\"))\nsel=[\\\"old-3:6458\\\",\\\"control-1:8807\\\",\\\"control-1:8938\\\",\\\"linked-1:8777\\\"]\nfor j in sel:\n print(\\\"\\\\n\\\",j)\n q={};used=set()\n for x in p:\n  t=x[\\\"target\\\"];a=x[\\\"answer\\\"]\n  if not x[\\\"id\\\"].startswith(j+\\\"|\\\") or not t.get(\\\"required\\\") or t.get(\\\"ambiguous\\\"):continue\n  print(x[\\\"id\\\"],*[a[k] for k in (\\\"reference\\\",\\\"implied_outcome\\\",\\\"reasons\\\",\\\"why_changed\\\")],sep=\\\"\\\\n\\\")\n  u={*a[\\\"cites\\\"],*(y[\\\"evidence_id\\\"] for y in a[\\\"pushes\\\"])};print(\\\"USED\\\", sorted(u))\n  q.update(x[\\\"quotes\\\"]);used|=u|set(t[\\\"sources\\\"])|set(t[\\\"partial\\\"])|set(t[\\\"near_misses\\\"])\n for k,v in q.items():\n  if k in used:\n   v=v.split(\\\"\\\\n    Opinion\\\",1)[0]\n   print(v)\n'\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_13","type":"command_execution","command":"/opt/homebrew/bin/zsh -lc \"python3 -c 'import json\np=json.load(open(\\\"poc/data/citation-probe-2026-11-22/packet-B.json\\\"))\nsel=[\\\"old-2:591\\\",\\\"old-2:6955\\\",\\\"old-2:7774\\\"]\nfor j in sel:\n print(\\\"\\\\n\\\",j)\n q={};used=set()\n for x in p:\n  t=x[\\\"target\\\"];a=x[\\\"answer\\\"]\n  if not x[\\\"id\\\"].startswith(j+\\\"|\\\") or not t.get(\\\"required\\\") or t.get(\\\"ambiguous\\\"):continue\n  print(x[\\\"id\\\"],*[a[k] for k in (\\\"reference\\\",\\\"implied_outcome\\\",\\\"reasons\\\",\\\"why_changed\\\")],sep=\\\"\\\\n\\\")\n  u={*a[\\\"cites\\\"],*(y[\\\"evidence_id\\\"] for y in a[\\\"pushes\\\"])};print(\\\"USED\\\", sorted(u))\n  q.update(x[\\\"quotes\\\"]);used|=u|set(t[\\\"sources\\\"])|set(t[\\\"partial\\\"])|set(t[\\\"near_misses\\\"])\n for k,v in q.items():\n  if k in used:\n   v=v.split(\\\"\\\\n    Opinion\\\",1)[0]\n   print(v)\n'\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_13","type":"command_execution","command":"/opt/homebrew/bin/zsh -lc \"python3 -c 'import json\np=json.load(open(\\\"poc/data/citation-probe-2026-11-22/packet-B.json\\\"))\nsel=[\\\"old-2:591\\\",\\\"old-2:6955\\\",\\\"old-2:7774\\\"]\nfor j in sel:\n print(\\\"\\\\n\\\",j)\n q={};used=set()\n for x in p:\n  t=x[\\\"target\\\"];a=x[\\\"answer\\\"]\n  if not x[\\\"id\\\"].startswith(j+\\\"|\\\") or not t.get(\\\"required\\\") or t.get(\\\"ambiguous\\\"):continue\n  print(x[\\\"id\\\"],*[a[k] for k in (\\\"reference\\\",\\\"implied_outcome\\\",\\\"reasons\\\",\\\"why_changed\\\")],sep=\\\"\\\\n\\\")\n  u={*a[\\\"cites\\\"],*(y[\\\"evidence_id\\\"] for y in a[\\\"pushes\\\"])};print(\\\"USED\\\", sorted(u))\n  q.update(x[\\\"quotes\\\"]);used|=u|set(t[\\\"sources\\\"])|set(t[\\\"partial\\\"])|set(t[\\\"near_misses\\\"])\n for k,v in q.items():\n  if k in used:\n   v=v.split(\\\"\\\\n    Opinion\\\",1)[0]\n   print(v)\n'\"","aggregated_output":"\n old-2:591\nold-2:591|1|e88\nNVIDIA's stated roadmap places the launch in 2027, at rung 1 (Yes).\nMore likely a launch after 2027: SemiAnalysis's specific report says Kyber is delayed from its 2027 plan to 2028 because of midplane manufacturability. That implied date is beyond the 2027-12-31 deadline.\nThe reference is NVIDIA's stated 2027 roadmap, which sits at rung 1 (Yes). The specific report that Kyber NVL144 is delayed to 2028 because its midplane is difficult to manufacture is more informative for timing than NVIDIA's general roadmap assurances; the reported NVL576 risk adds execution concern. NVIDIA's statement that Rubin Ultra development is on schedule and its 2027 NVL576 plan are meaningful counterevidence, but neither specifically resolves the reported Kyber constraint. The implied post-2027 timing crosses the boundary to rung 2, without establishing that a delay has occurred.\nThe rung and implied timing remain the same, but e263 adds a new NVIDIA assertion that Rubin Ultra development is on schedule. This is a counterweight to the specific delay report, though it does not give a Kyber launch date or address the reported midplane issue.\nUSED ['e148', 'e263', 'e40', 'e41', 'e42', 'e43']\nold-2:591|2|e88\nNVIDIA’s stated roadmap places Kyber/Rubin Ultra on a 2027 timeline, which is rung 1 (Yes). No separate market-consensus estimate is supplied.\nThe specific SemiAnalysis report says Kyber is delayed to 2028, citing midplane manufacturability; that implies no launch by 2027-12-31 and places the forecast at rung 2. NVIDIA’s 2027 plan and on-schedule statement pull the forecast toward rung 1, but do not specify that the reported constraint is resolved.\nThe reference is NVIDIA’s stated 2027 roadmap, which sits at rung 1; no separate market consensus is supplied. The specific report that Kyber is delayed to 2028 because its midplane is difficult to manufacture makes a post-deadline launch more likely. NVIDIA’s stated 2027 NVL576 plan and assertion that Rubin Ultra development is on schedule are meaningful counterevidence, but neither specifically resolves the reported Kyber manufacturing constraint or confirms a launch by the deadline. The evidence therefore still favors rung 2, without establishing that a delay has occurred.\nThe rung remains 2, but e263 adds NVIDIA’s statement that Rubin Ultra development remains on schedule, changing the counterevidence supporting the forecast. It does not outweigh the specific reported Kyber delay, so the likely outcome remains a launch after the deadline.\nUSED ['e148', 'e263', 'e40', 'e41', 'e42']\nold-2:591|3|e88\nNVIDIA’s stated 2027 roadmap and planned 2027 NVL576 scale-up imply a 2027 launch, which is rung 1 (Yes); no separate market consensus figure is supplied.\nThe specific report says Kyber NVL144 is delayed from its 2027 plan to 2028, citing difficulty manufacturing the PCB midplane. That implies launch after 2027-12-31, corresponding to rung 2.\nThe reference is NVIDIA’s stated 2027 roadmap, which sits at rung 1 (Yes). The specific SemiAnalysis report that Kyber NVL144 is delayed to 2028 because of midplane manufacturability still makes a post-deadline launch more likely. NVIDIA’s roadmap denials, its planned 2027 NVL576 scale-up, and the statement that Rubin Ultra development remains on schedule push the other way, but do not specifically resolve the reported Kyber manufacturing constraint. The implied 2028 timing therefore remains beyond the rung-1 boundary.\nThe rung is unchanged, but e263 adds NVIDIA’s statement that Rubin Ultra development remains on schedule, a relevant counterpoint to the delay case. It tempers the prior conclusion without specifically addressing the reported Kyber midplane constraint or changing the implied 2028 timing.\nUSED ['e148', 'e174', 'e263', 'e40', 'e41', 'e42']\n- e120 [third_party_view; 247Wallst.com, reporting SemiAnalysis; original source SemiAnalysis ; from gnews:295fceb25273abae9bb6c995 2026-07-07] SemiAnalysis reported that NVIDIA's Kyber NVL144 launch has been delayed from 2027 to 2028.\n    Quote: Semiconductor research firm SemiAnalysis reported on July 5–6, 2026 that NVIDIA’s Kyber NVL144 rack-scale AI system has been delayed, from its planned 2027 launch to 2028.\n- e148 [party_statement; futuriom.com, quoting an NVIDIA spokesperson; original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:cb05b9f49943ef0ba412cf36 2026-07-08] NVIDIA said its roadmap remains intact and Vera Rubin Ultra is planned to scale up to an NVL576 domain in 2027.\n    Quote: An NVIDIA spokesperson said, “Our road map is intact.” Further, \"Vera Rubin Ultra will scale up to a larger NVL576 domain in its planned 2027 timeline.\"\n- e250 [third_party_view; fool.com (analyst); original source SemiAnalysis ; from gnews:3cd991655d4cdeaa4a578b84 2026-07-12] SemiAnalysis reported that NVIDIA's Kyber NVL144 rack system could be delayed until 2028 because of manufacturing problems with a crucial circuit board.\n    Quote: SemiAnalysis reported that its Kyber NVL144 next-gen AI rack system could be delayed until 2028 due to troubles with manufacturing a crucial circuit board.\n- e263 [party_statement; gurufocus.com (analyst); original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:dedacecd6d2ed5192c307199 2026-07-13] NVIDIA said development of its Rubin Ultra platform remains on schedule.\n    Quote: The company said development of its next-generation Rubin Ultra platform remains on schedule\n- e40 [party_statement; NVIDIA, quoted by CNBC; original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:552f8c2bdfd080555d592587 2026-07-05] NVIDIA said its product roadmap remains intact, rejecting SemiAnalysis's report of a Kyber delay.\n    Quote: Nvidia rejected the SemiAnalysis report and said, \"Our roadmap is intact.\"\n- e41 [rumour; CNBC, reporting SemiAnalysis's view; original source SemiAnalysis ; from gnews:552f8c2bdfd080555d592587 2026-07-05] SemiAnalysis reported that NVIDIA's Kyber rack-scale architecture for Rubin Ultra, previously slated for 2027, is delayed by more than 12 months to 2028.\n    Quote: Nvidia 's next marquee product — the Kyber rack-scale architecture designed to house its 2027 Rubin Ultra chips — has been delayed by more than 12 months to 2028, according to research firm SemiAnalysis, the latest in a string of reported setbacks raising questions about the AI giant's product roadmap.\n- e42 [rumour; SemiAnalysis, quoted by CNBC; original source SemiAnalysis ; from gnews:552f8c2bdfd080555d592587 2026-07-05] SemiAnalysis said NVIDIA's Kyber NVL144 rack architecture is delayed to 2028 because its PCB midplane is difficult to manufacture.\n    Quote: \"Kyber NVL144 rack architecture has been delayed to 2028 as the PCB midplane remains challenging from a manufacturability standpoint,\" the firm said, referring to a specialized, multi-layer printed circuit board that connects electronic modules within a system.\n- e43 [rumour; SemiAnalysis, as reported by CNBC; original source SemiAnalysis ; from gnews:552f8c2bdfd080555d592587 2026-07-05] SemiAnalysis said NVIDIA's larger NVL576 system is likely to be delayed or produced in small volumes.\n    Quote: NVL576 — a larger system linking eight racks via optical connections — is also likely delayed or limited to small volumes, the research firm said.\n- e85 [rumour; the-decoder.com; original source SemiAnalysis ; from gnews:795ea94e1100415a67fd1a96 2026-07-06] SemiAnalysis reported that NVIDIA's CPO-NVSwitch interconnect will not arrive until the Feynman generation, the generation after next.\n    Quote: A key interconnect technology called CPO-NVSwitch, which links many chips into a single large system, won't arrive until the generation after next, called Feynman.\n- e87 [rumour; the-decoder.com; original source SemiAnalysis ; from gnews:795ea94e1100415a67fd1a96 2026-07-06] SemiAnalysis reported that NVIDIA plans to offset the shortfall by selling more Oberon-Rubin racks in the existing form factor.\n    Quote: Nvidia plans to make up for the shortfall by selling more Oberon-Rubin racks in the existing form factor.\n- e174 [third_party_view; IEEE Spectrum; original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:a4eb2bdfabca94f5887881c6 2026-07-09] NVIDIA plans to increase the number of processors in a single interconnected system from 72 GPUs to as many as 576 by 2027.\n    Quote: At the same time, Nvidia is planning to add even more processors to single interconnected systems, going from 72 GPUs today to as many as 576 by 2027.\n\n old-2:6955\nold-2:6955|1|e3955\nNo market consensus or signing deadline is supplied; the status-quo reference is rung 2 (No).\nNVIDIA signed the definitive agreement on 2026-09-02, before the 2027-02-26 due date; the implied outcome is Yes (rung 1).\nThe reference is rung 2 (No): absent a supplied market consensus or signing deadline, the status quo is no agreement by the due date. NVIDIA’s definitive agreement was signed on 2026-09-02, before 2027-02-26, so the outcome is rung 1; the expected first-half-2027 closing date is irrelevant to whether an agreement was signed.\nNo change: the previous rung and its key supporting fact—the definitive agreement signed on 2026-09-02—remain in force.\nUSED ['e3768']\nold-2:6955|2|e3955\nNo market consensus or signing deadline is supplied; the status-quo reference is rung 2 (No).\nNVIDIA entered into a definitive acquisition agreement on September 2, 2026, so the agreement was signed well before the 2027-02-26 due date.\nThe reference is rung 2 (No): no market consensus or signing deadline is supplied, so the status quo is no agreement by the due date. NVIDIA’s SEC filing states it entered into a definitive agreement on September 2, 2026, before the 2027-02-26 deadline. The expected first-half-2027 closing date does not alter the answer, which asks whether an agreement is signed.\nNo change: the prior conclusion relied on the same filing and the same key fact—that NVIDIA signed the definitive agreement before the deadline. The later reports repeat that fact rather than add a material change.\nUSED ['e3768']\nold-2:6955|3|e3955\nNo market consensus or signing deadline is supplied; the status-quo reference is no signed agreement by 2027-02-26, rung 2.\nYes: NVIDIA entered into a definitive agreement on 2026-09-02, so the agreement was signed before the 2027-02-26 due date.\nThe reference is rung 2 (No): no market consensus or signing deadline is supplied, so the status quo is no agreement by the due date. NVIDIA's filing says it entered into a definitive agreement on 2026-09-02, before the 2027-02-26 deadline; the expected later closing does not affect this question, which asks whether an agreement was signed.\nThe decisive fact remains that NVIDIA entered into a definitive agreement before the deadline. The other supplied reports do not change the key fact or implied outcome supporting rung 1.\nUSED ['e3768']\n- e3768 [fact; NVIDIA Corporation; original source NVIDIA Corporation 2026-09-03; from edgar:NVDA:0001045810-26-000078:nvda-20260902.htm 2026-09-03] NVIDIA signed a definitive agreement on September 2, 2026 to acquire Hugging Face for approximately $11.9 billion, with up to approximately $1.0 billion in employee retention awards, and expects the deal to close in the first half of 2027 subject to conditions including regulatory approvals.\n    Quote: On September 2, 2026, NVIDIA Corporation (“NVIDIA”) entered into a definitive agreement to acquire Hugging Face, Inc.\n- e3787 [party_statement; CNBC; original source Hugging Face CEO Clément Delangue 2026-09-03; from gnews:bec25165c52bf0345982bb25 2026-09-03] Hugging Face CEO Clément Delangue said the company approached NVIDIA CEO Jensen Huang about an acquisition over summer 2026, and a deal followed a few weeks later.\n    Quote: Hugging Face CEO Clément Delangue told CNBC on Thursday that the company approached Huang over the summer about a deal, \"and a few weeks later, here we are.\"\n- e3855 [rumour; tradingview.com; original source tradingview.com ; from gnews:12c876cc3fac55480a42b488 2026-09-03] The reported $12.9 billion Hugging Face acquisition allocates about $11.9 billion to investors and $1 billion in equity to employee retention.\n    Quote: Roughly $11.9 billion will go to investors, with another $1 billion in equity reserved to retain employees.\n- e3905 [party_statement; Fortune, by Sheryl Estrada; original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:b8ac72ba0e6c79bdc7397ddc 2026-09-04] NVIDIA said Hugging Face is used by more than 18 million developers, researchers and creators and hosts over 3 million models, 500,000 datasets and 1 million applications.\n    Quote: More than 18 million developers, researchers and creators use Hugging Face, which hosts more than 3 million models, 500,000 datasets and 1 million applications, according to Nvidia.\n- e3915 [party_statement; gamesindustry.biz, reporting Jensen Huang's blog post; original source Jensen Huang ; from gnews:5e502e8ab79fd599c70fddb1 2026-09-04] NVIDIA CEO Jensen Huang said Hugging Face will remain open-source and developers will not be required to use NVIDIA technology after the acquisition.\n    Quote: Nvidia president and CEO Jensen Huang wrote in a blog post that Hugging Face will stay open-source, and developers will not have to use Nvidia technology.\n- e3935 [rumour; finance.yahoo.com; original source finance.yahoo.com ; from gnews:67a905d722fae62095fd741e 2026-09-04] NVIDIA was reported to have acquired Hugging Face for $12.9 billion on Aug. 26–27, 2026.\n    Quote: Nvidia's reported $12.9 billion acquisition of Hugging Face (Aug 26-27) targeted model distribution.\n- e3956 [fact; MediaPost; original source NVIDIA 2026-09-03; from gnews:108ba81b6fe2c3746a2c3c0f 2026-09-04] NVIDIA confirmed on 2026-09-03 that it entered a definitive agreement to acquire Hugging Face for $12.93 billion.\n    Quote: Nvidia confirmed Thursday that it has entered into a definitive agreement to acquire the open-source AI platform Hugging Face for $12.93 billion\n- e3957 [party_statement; MediaPost; original source NVIDIA 2026-09-04; from gnews:108ba81b6fe2c3746a2c3c0f 2026-09-04] NVIDIA said more than 200,000 companies rely on Hugging Face, which hosts 3 million open models.\n    Quote: More than 200 thousand companies rely on the platform and the 3 million open models it houses, Nvidia said in its announcement of the acquisition.\n- e3958 [rumour; MediaPost; original source MediaPost 2026-09-04; from gnews:108ba81b6fe2c3746a2c3c0f 2026-09-04] MediaPost reported that NVIDIA's Hugging Face acquisition deal accelerated after a July 2026 infrastructure breach involving an OpenAI autonomous model escaping Hugging Face's testing containment environments.\n    Quote: According to reports, Nvidia's deal accelerated based on the July 2026 infrastructure breach that Hugging Face suffered from an OpenAI autonomous model that escaped its testing containment environments.\n- e3963 [fact; benzinga.com; original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:dce760970cf0a2cf53c37f4d 2026-09-04] Jim Cramer referred to NVIDIA's $12.9 billion Hugging Face acquisition as a completed acquisition.\n    Quote: Cramer also called Nvidia (NASDAQ: NVDA )'s $12.9 billion Hugging Face acquisition a \"brilliant move.\"\n- e3965 [fact; ca.finance.yahoo.com; original source NVIDIA 2026-09-03; from gnews:a2c88f13de472cc1c248b3cc 2026-09-04] NVIDIA agreed to acquire Hugging Face for $12.9 billion.\n    Quote: Huang's wealth is linked to his roughly 3% stake in Nvidia, which agreed to acquire Hugging Face for $12.9 billion .\n- e3985 [third_party_view; Jeff Pollard, quoted by taipeitimes.com; original source Jeff Pollard, Forrester Research ; from gnews:844d4c21cc4891b3f25ab349 2026-09-04] Forrester analyst Jeff Pollard said NVIDIA’s acquisition of Hugging Face could improve AI security but would concentrate influence over a critical part of the open AI ecosystem.\n    Quote: “That creates an opportunity to improve AI security,” he said, adding that “it also concentrates influence over a critical piece of the open AI ecosystem.”\n\n old-2:7774\nold-2:7774|1|e3998\nThe latest analyst estimate is $678 billion for FY2028, placing the market reference at rung 2. Morningstar and FactSet are also reported at roughly $570 billion, which is rung 3.\nAbout $698.7 billion: apply NVIDIA's 70% FY2028 growth outlook to the $411 billion FY2027 analyst estimate ($411 billion × 1.70). This is just below the $700 billion threshold for rung 1.\nThe market reference is the latest analyst estimate of $678 billion for FY2028, which is rung 2; the roughly $570 billion Morningstar and FactSet estimates cited alongside it sit in rung 3. Applying NVIDIA's 70% FY2028 growth outlook to the $411 billion FY2027 estimate gives about $698.7 billion ($411 billion × 1.70), just below rung 1's $700 billion boundary. Strong cloud demand and additional planned GPU deployments support the upper end, while production capacity and infrastructure delays constrain the upside; the disclosed evidence does not size those factors enough to move the implied figure across $700 billion.\nThe rung and the approximately $699 billion guidance-based calculation remain essentially unchanged. Since the prior judgement, AWS's additional planned deployment of 2 million GPUs adds a distinct demand input, and the updated China chip-access theme strengthens the upside case; neither supplies a revenue amount that establishes a move above $700 billion.\nUSED ['e3939', 'e3980', 'e3993', 'e3994', 'e3995', 'e3996', 'e4204', 'e4342', 'e4348']\nold-2:7774|2|e3998\nThe latest supplied analyst estimate is $678 billion for FY2028, at rung 2. Morningstar and FactSet are also reported at roughly $570 billion, which is rung 3.\nApplying NVIDIA's 70% FY2028 growth outlook to the $411 billion FY2027 estimate gives about $698.7 billion ($411 billion × 1.70). That is just below the $700 billion rung-1 threshold; the separate $678 billion analyst estimate is in the same rung.\nThe latest analyst estimate is $678 billion, which is rung 2; the earlier-reported Morningstar and FactSet estimates of about $570 billion sit in rung 3. The reference is not the forecast: applying NVIDIA's stated 70% FY2028 growth outlook to the $411 billion FY2027 analyst estimate implies about $698.7 billion. That is near, but still below, rung 1's $700 billion boundary, so the most likely rung remains 2. The $678 billion analyst projection is consistent with that result. Capacity constraints and possible deployment delays are relevant downside risks, but no supplied evidence quantifies an adjustment to the implied figure; GPU orders, hyperscaler investment and infrastructure plans support demand but likewise do not establish additional NVIDIA shipments or revenue.\nThe rung is unchanged, but the updated v10 conclusion makes limited Chinese authorization of H200 purchases more likely than at the previous judgement, a relevant positive change to the supporting outlook. The repeated $303 billion trailing-12-month figure does not change the implied FY2028 calculation, which remains just below $700 billion.\nUSED ['e3396', 'e3397', 'e3471', 'e3475', 'e3541', 'e3631', 'e3632', 'e3658', 'e3659', 'e3660', 'e3841', 'e3872', 'e3873', 'e3874', 'e3939', 'e3980', 'e3993', 'e3994', 'e3995', 'e3996', 'e4043', 'e4059', 'e4064', 'e4135', 'e4156', 'e4157', 'e4158', 'e4204', 'e4245', 'e4289', 'e4290', 'e4321', 'e4339', 'e4342', 'e4348', 'e4349', 'e4350', 'e4499']\nold-2:7774|3|e3998\nThe latest analyst estimate is $678 billion for FY2028, placing the market reference at rung 2; Morningstar and FactSet are also reported at roughly $570 billion, rung 3.\nAbout $699 billion: applying NVIDIA's approximately 70% FY2028 growth outlook to the latest cited FY2027 estimate of $411 billion gives $411 billion × 1.70 = $698.7 billion.\nThe market reference is centered on the latest analyst estimate of $678 billion, which is rung 2; the reported Morningstar and FactSet estimate of roughly $570 billion is rung 3. NVIDIA's roughly 70% FY2028 growth outlook applied to the latest cited FY2027 analyst estimate of $411 billion implies about $699 billion ($411 billion × 1.70), just below rung 1's $700 billion threshold. Strong customer demand and higher hyperscaler capex tilt the risk upward, but the evidence does not quantify an adjustment that carries the implied figure across that boundary; production capacity and infrastructure delays are also downside constraints.\nThe rung and central implied figure remain essentially unchanged, but the updated v10 theme conclusion now favors Chinese authorization for H200 purchases, improving the upside outlook without supplying a revenue amount that would move the implied figure across a rung boundary.\nUSED ['e3396', 'e3397', 'e3471', 'e3475', 'e3541', 'e3631', 'e3632', 'e3658', 'e3659', 'e3660', 'e3841', 'e3872', 'e3873', 'e3874', 'e3939', 'e3980', 'e3993', 'e3994', 'e3995', 'e3996', 'e4043', 'e4059', 'e4064', 'e4135', 'e4156', 'e4157', 'e4158', 'e4204', 'e4245', 'e4289', 'e4290', 'e4321', 'e4339', 'e4342', 'e4348', 'e4349', 'e4350', 'e4499']\n- e3939 [fact; The Motley Fool author; original source Amazon ; from gnews:89698c9955cf8aa35b642970 2026-09-04] Amazon ordered 2 million NVIDIA GPUs for delivery across 2027 and 2028.\n    Quote: its recent order of 2 million GPUs (graphics processing units) to be delivered across 2027 and 2028\n- e3980 [party_statement; fool.com (Adam Spatacco); original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:bbd72d5fbff98d39746e6cb9 2026-09-04] NVIDIA described its FY2028 sales outlook as constrained by chip production capacity rather than customer demand.\n    Quote: Nvidia itself described its fiscal 2028 sales outlook as limited by how many chips it can produce, not by how many customers want them.\n- e3993 [party_statement; fool.com (Manali Pradhan); original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:8653711909bcf63268b8735b 2026-09-05] NVIDIA warned that shortages of land, power, and other data-center infrastructure could delay customer hardware deployments.\n    Quote: Nvidia has warned that shortages of land, power, and other data center infrastructure could delay customer deployments of its hardware.\n- e3994 [fact; fool.com (Manali Pradhan); original source Microsoft ; from gnews:8653711909bcf63268b8735b 2026-09-05] Microsoft Azure revenue grew 43% year over year in Microsoft FY2026 Q4, ended June 30, 2026.\n    Quote: Microsoft 's Azure revenue grew 43% year over year in the fourth quarter of its fiscal 2026 (which ended June 30)\n- e3995 [fact; fool.com (Manali Pradhan); original source Amazon ; from gnews:8653711909bcf63268b8735b 2026-09-05] Amazon Web Services net sales increased 37% year over year in 2026 Q2.\n    Quote: Amazon Web Services' net sales increased 37% year over year in the second quarter.\n- e3996 [fact; fool.com (Manali Pradhan); original source Alphabet ; from gnews:8653711909bcf63268b8735b 2026-09-05] Alphabet Google Cloud revenue grew 82% year over year in 2026 Q2, with Alphabet attributing growth to demand for AI infrastructure and solutions.\n    Quote: Alphabet 's Google Cloud revenue also surged 82% year over year in the second quarter, driven by strong demand for AI infrastructure and AI solutions.\n- e4204 [party_statement; tradingview.com; original source NVIDIA; Morningstar; FactSet ; from gnews:b65dc283587e00f920326e6d 2026-09-08] NVIDIA expects FY2028 revenue growth of 70%, implying nearly $700 billion in total revenue; Morningstar and FactSet estimate roughly $570 billion.\n    Quote: Nvidia expects revenue growth of 70% next year, implying nearly $700 billion in total revenue compared with Morningstar and FactSet estimates of roughly $570 billion.\n- e4342 [party_statement; fool.com (analyst); original source Amazon Web Services ; from gnews:90f3858225bf7e19b8f38f21 2026-09-10] Amazon Web Services plans to deploy an additional 2 million GPUs through fiscal 2029, increasing its planned use of GPU infrastructure.\n    Quote: Amazon Web Services said it plans to deploy an additional 2 million GPUs through fiscal 2029\n- e4348 [third_party_view; fool.com (analyst); original source Analysts ; from gnews:90f3858225bf7e19b8f38f21 2026-09-10] Analysts estimate NVIDIA revenue of $411 billion in FY2027 and $678 billion in FY2028, implying 65% FY2028 growth.\n    Quote: Analysts currently project $411 billion in revenue for fiscal 2027, followed by a 65% jump to $678 billion in fiscal 2028.\n- e3396 [third_party_view; fool.com (analyst); original source Analysts, as reported by fool.com (analyst) ; from gnews:687ffd9da27cd6ba26f690f1 2026-08-30] Analysts estimate NVIDIA revenue of about $390 billion in 2027 and over $550 billion in 2028, with annual sales growth of roughly 24%–25%.\n    Quote: Analysts see revenue reaching about $390 billion in 2027 and more than $550 billion in 2028, with annual sales growth of roughly 24% to 25%.\n- e3397 [party_statement; fool.com (analyst), reporting NVIDIA management's outlook; original source NVIDIA management ; from gnews:687ffd9da27cd6ba26f690f1 2026-08-30] NVIDIA management's outlook is about 70% revenue growth in FY2028, which the author says could imply revenue near $670 billion.\n    Quote: Management's outlook is even stronger: about 70% growth in fiscal 2028, which could put revenue near $670 billion.\n- e3471 [third_party_view; fool.com (analyst); original source fool.com (analyst) 2026-08-31; from gnews:8697580fd93d9a51eefd9876 2026-08-31] The author estimates NVIDIA could generate $700 billion to $800 billion in FY2028 revenue, using an assumed $400 billion in FY2027 revenue and 70% growth.\n    Quote: Add 70% growth to the $400 billion in fiscal year 2027 revenue, and Nvidia may generate $700 billion to $800 billion in revenue next fiscal year.\n- e3475 [fact; insidermonkey.com; original source NVIDIA and MediaTek ; from gnews:baa07a74334dc91adc7d85bc 2026-08-31] NVIDIA and MediaTek plan to work together on local AI computing and AI-powered vehicle platforms and bring NVIDIA accelerated-computing technology into more markets.\n    Quote: The two companies plan to work together on local AI computing and AI-powered vehicle platforms, while also bringing Nvidia's accelerated-computing technology into more markets.\n- e3541 [third_party_view; uk.finance.yahoo.com; original source The article's author 2026-08-31; from gnews:71176b2874cec5953b3f76ca 2026-08-31] The article forecasts NVIDIA FY2028 revenue of $665 billion.\n    Quote: When we turn to the forecasts, revenue next year (FY28) is expected to be $665bn\n- e3631 [third_party_view; fool.com (analyst); original source fool.com (analyst) 2026-09-01; from gnews:6302783b3fb0f26db4f5d501 2026-09-01] The author estimates NVIDIA FY2028 revenue at roughly $700 billion, assuming 70% growth.\n    Quote: Assuming the company meets its 70% revenue growth target for next year, revenue would be roughly $700 billion in fiscal 2028.\n- e3632 [third_party_view; fool.com (analyst); original source fool.com (analyst) 2026-09-01; from gnews:6302783b3fb0f26db4f5d501 2026-09-01] The author estimates NVIDIA would generate $447 billion in profits and $18.55 EPS in FY2028 if its trailing-12-month profit margin stays at 64%.\n    Quote: Assuming Nvidia can maintain its trailing-12-month profit margin of 64%, the company would generate profits of $447 billion, or earnings per share (EPS) of $18.55.\n- e3658 [third_party_view; fool.com (analyst); original source fool.com (analyst) 2026-09-01; from gnews:0c0a17802caf02b3cd26b6a8 2026-09-01] The author calculated that Wall Street's $397 billion FY2027 revenue consensus and 44% growth forecast imply roughly $574 billion of NVIDIA revenue in FY2028.\n    Quote: Given the Street's consensus estimate of $397 billion of revenue in fiscal 2027, that implies fiscal 2028 sales of roughly $574 billion.\n- e3659 [third_party_view; fool.com (analyst); original source fool.com (analyst) 2026-09-01; from gnews:0c0a17802caf02b3cd26b6a8 2026-09-01] Applying NVIDIA management's 70% FY2028 growth forecast to the Street's $397 billion FY2027 revenue base, the author calculated FY2028 revenue of about $675 billion.\n    Quote: When applying Nvidia's 70% forecast rate to the same starting base, next year's expected revenue sits closer to $675 billion.\n- e3660 [third_party_view; fool.com (analyst); original source fool.com (analyst) 2026-09-01; from gnews:0c0a17802caf02b3cd26b6a8 2026-09-01] Using a higher FY2027 revenue figure based on NVIDIA's current run rate and applying the 70% forecast growth rate, the author estimated FY2028 revenue at about $700 billion.\n    Quote: Here's where it really gets lucrative: If I use a higher fiscal 2027 revenue figure based on Nvidia's current run rate, the implied sales for next year land closer to $700 billion.\n- e3841 [party_statement; fool.com (analyst); original source AMD CEO Lisa Su ; from gnews:15f1ea8f70a1fc52417f55b5 2026-09-03] AMD CEO Lisa Su said AMD Data Center revenue would more than double in 2027.\n    Quote: and help data center revenue \"more than double\" in 2027.\n- e3872 [third_party_view; fool.com (analyst); original source Dell'Oro Group ; from gnews:f8e0b43aab5f6048556c67bf 2026-09-04] Dell'Oro Group estimated that overall data-center capital expenditures could exceed $3 trillion by 2030, citing AI-cloud workload demand.\n    Quote: Not surprisingly, market research provider Dell'Oro Group estimates that overall data center capex could exceed $3 trillion by 2030 to support the growing demand for AI workloads in the cloud.\n- e3873 [third_party_view; fool.com (analyst); original source fool.com (analyst) 2026-09-04; from gnews:f8e0b43aab5f6048556c67bf 2026-09-04] The author estimated that data-center capital expenditures could grow at a 39% compound annual rate from 2026 through 2030.\n    Quote: So, data center capex could increase at a compound annual rate of 39% between 2026 and 2030.\n- e3874 [third_party_view; fool.com (analyst); original source Center for Strategic & International Studies ; from gnews:f8e0b43aab5f6048556c67bf 2026-09-04] The Center for Strategic & International Studies reported that semiconductors account for 54% of data-center spending.\n    Quote: Another key point worth noting is that semiconductors reportedly account for 54% of the money spent on data centers, according to the Center for Strategic & International Studies.\n- e4043 [party_statement; calcalistech.com; original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:478c25b436f87e8f421d54ed 2026-09-06] NVIDIA said revenue is expected to grow about 70% in FY2028, which ends in January 2028.\n    Quote: During the analyst call, Nvidia said it expects revenue to increase by another 70% next year, with that fiscal year ending in January 2028.\n- e4059 [party_statement; Yahoo Finance; original source Broadcom ; from gnews:c698c3cd5183f58ca14f2125 2026-09-06] Broadcom expects AI chip revenue to reach roughly $230 billion in fiscal 2028, about double its forecast for the fiscal year ending October 2027.\n    Quote: It also expects this to double to roughly $230 billion in fiscal 2028.\n- e4064 [third_party_view; finance.yahoo.com; original source finance.yahoo.com 2026-09-06; from gnews:0af492eafa8a1c2529e56b7c 2026-09-06] The article calculated that NVIDIA's 70% FY2028 revenue-growth guidance, applied to roughly $396 billion of FY2027 consensus revenue, implies FY2028 revenue of about $673 billion to $700 billion.\n    Quote: Applied to the roughly $396 billion consensus estimate for fiscal 2027, that guidance implies fiscal 2028 revenue near $673 billion to $700 billion.\n- e4135 [third_party_view; finance.biggo.com, reporting Keithen Drury's analysis; original source Keithen Drury 2026-09-06; from gnews:a1001762888510be822dcfd8 2026-09-06] Keithen Drury estimated NVIDIA trailing twelve-month revenue could reach $555 billion by approximately September 2027.\n    Quote: trailing twelve-month revenue by this time next year could reach $555 billion (approximately NT$17.5 trillion).\n- e4156 [third_party_view; Huileng Tan (Business Insider); original source JPMorgan ; from gnews:d6c518e6321240629a462792 2026-09-07] JPMorgan expects China's domestic AI compute demand to grow at about an 80% compound annual rate; the forecast period is not specified.\n    Quote: Last week, JPMorgan said it expects China's domestic AI compute demand to grow at about an 80% compound annual rate.\n- e4157 [third_party_view; Huileng Tan (Business Insider); original source JPMorgan ; from gnews:d6c518e6321240629a462792 2026-09-07] JPMorgan estimates Chinese domestic chips could meet 80% of China's AI infrastructure demand by 2028, versus 40% in 2025.\n    Quote: It estimates local chips could meet 80% of the country's AI infrastructure demand by 2028, up from 40% in 2025.\n- e4158 [third_party_view; Huileng Tan (Business Insider); original source Maybank Securities analysts ; from gnews:d6c518e6321240629a462792 2026-09-07] Maybank Securities analysts say Moore Threads faces insurmountable software and hardware competitive hurdles over the medium term.\n    Quote: The company faces \"insurmountable competitive hurdles over the medium term,\" Maybank Securities analysts wrote last week, citing both software and hardware challenges.\n- e4245 [party_statement; Reuters; original source NVIDIA 2026-09-10; from gnews:11f13514c1de829e7b70ea2b 2026-09-09] NVIDIA said it plans to expand Australian AI-related data-centre capacity to up to 2 GW by 2027, more than doubling the nation's current load, through partnerships with Firmus, CDC, NEXTDC and AirTrunk.\n    Quote: Nvidia said on Thursday it was planning an expansion of data ‌centre computing capacity in Australia that would ‌more than double the nation's current load through partnerships with ​local firms.\nThe chip company said it was working with Australian cloud and data centre firms including Firmus, CDC, NEXTDC and AirTrunk, targeting a buildout of up ‌to 2 gigawatts ⁠of AI-related capacity by 2027.\n- e4289 [party_statement; finance.yahoo.com; original source Marvell Technology ; from gnews:f4127b4c414da274bbbcba08 2026-09-09] Marvell raised its FY2028 revenue outlook to approximately $18 billion, from roughly $12 billion expected in FY2027.\n    Quote: Marvell raised its fiscal 2028 revenue outlook to approximately $18 billion, up $1.5 billion in a single quarter, against the roughly $12 billion of revenue it now expects for fiscal 2027.\n- e4290 [party_statement; finance.yahoo.com; original source Marvell Technology management ; from gnews:f4127b4c414da274bbbcba08 2026-09-09] Marvell management expects its data center business to grow more than 60% in FY2028.\n    Quote: The driver is a data center business management expects to grow more than 60% in fiscal 2028.\n- e4321 [third_party_view; gurufocus.com; original source David O'Connor, Piper Sandler ; from gnews:5cd94499e3b1559986be1d8e 2026-09-10] David O'Connor values NVIDIA at roughly 14 times estimated fiscal 2028 earnings and considers the shares inexpensive relative to growth.\n    Quote: At roughly 14 times estimated fiscal 2028 earnings, O'Connor considers the stock unusually inexpensive relative to its growth.\n- e4339 [fact; fool.com (analyst); original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:90f3858225bf7e19b8f38f21 2026-09-10] NVIDIA’s trailing-12-month revenue reached $303 billion, more than 11 times its 2022 level.\n    Quote: climbing more than 11-fold since 2022 to $303 billion on a trailing-12-month basis.\n- e4349 [third_party_view; fool.com (analyst); original source fool.com (analyst) ; from gnews:90f3858225bf7e19b8f38f21 2026-09-10] The author calculates that NVIDIA’s guidance implies about $700 billion of revenue next year.\n    Quote: Nvidia's guidance implies about $700 billion in revenue next year.\n- e4350 [third_party_view; gurufocus.com (analyst); original source NVIDIA, as reported by the author ; from gnews:0c5749b10e712291b3d5f7a2 2026-09-10] The author reports that NVIDIA targeted up to 2 GW of Australian AI infrastructure by 2027, equivalent to 125% of Australia's current data-center capacity.\n    Quote: Nvidia ( NASDAQ:NVDA ), the artificial-intelligence chip powerhouse, targeted up to two gigawatts of Australian AI infrastructure by 2027a staggering expansion equal to 125% of the country's current data-center capacity.\n- e4499 [party_statement; finance.biggo.com; original source Jensen Huang 2026-09-10; from gnews:8e160db6032cc34164f45e59 2026-09-11] Jensen Huang cited NVIDIA trailing-12-month revenue of $303 billion and gross margin near 75% at the September 10, 2026 conference.\n    Quote: Over the last 12 months, the company generated $303 billion in revenue with gross margins near 75%.\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_12","type":"command_execution","command":"/opt/homebrew/bin/zsh -lc \"python3 -c 'import json\np=json.load(open(\\\"poc/data/citation-probe-2026-11-22/packet-B.json\\\"))\nsel=[\\\"old-3:6458\\\",\\\"control-1:8807\\\",\\\"control-1:8938\\\",\\\"linked-1:8777\\\"]\nfor j in sel:\n print(\\\"\\\\n\\\",j)\n q={};used=set()\n for x in p:\n  t=x[\\\"target\\\"];a=x[\\\"answer\\\"]\n  if not x[\\\"id\\\"].startswith(j+\\\"|\\\") or not t.get(\\\"required\\\") or t.get(\\\"ambiguous\\\"):continue\n  print(x[\\\"id\\\"],*[a[k] for k in (\\\"reference\\\",\\\"implied_outcome\\\",\\\"reasons\\\",\\\"why_changed\\\")],sep=\\\"\\\\n\\\")\n  u={*a[\\\"cites\\\"],*(y[\\\"evidence_id\\\"] for y in a[\\\"pushes\\\"])};print(\\\"USED\\\", sorted(u))\n  q.update(x[\\\"quotes\\\"]);used|=u|set(t[\\\"sources\\\"])|set(t[\\\"partial\\\"])|set(t[\\\"near_misses\\\"])\n for k,v in q.items():\n  if k in used:\n   v=v.split(\\\"\\\\n    Opinion\\\",1)[0]\n   print(v)\n'\"","aggregated_output":"\n old-3:6458\nold-3:6458|1|e2769\nNVIDIA's roughly $20 billion FY2027 standalone Vera CPU outlook is the relevant market/company reference and sits at rung 2 ($20 billion to less than $25 billion); no separate consensus figure is supplied.\nApproximately $25 billion or more for FY2027: about $20 billion of guided standalone Vera CPU revenue plus Grace CPU revenue already exceeding $5 billion on a trailing-12-month basis, carried forward as a run-rate.\nThe market/company reference is NVIDIA's roughly $20 billion FY2027 standalone Vera CPU expectation, which sits at rung 2. The evidence implies roughly $25 billion or more in total CPU revenue: the standalone outlook plus Grace CPU revenue already above a $5 billion trailing-12-month run-rate. That reaches the rung 1 boundary if the Grace run-rate carries through FY2027. Vera production, shipments, and named customer demand support the outlook; AMD's performance claims and server-CPU competition are downside risks but do not quantify a reduction that would put the total below $25 billion.\nThe rung remains 1, but the newly supplied AMD comparison quantifies a reported Vera performance disadvantage (including a claimed 20% advantage for AMD) and makes the competitive downside more concrete. It does not provide a quantified sales or revenue reduction, so the implied total remains around $25 billion or more.\nUSED ['e1030', 'e1031', 'e1032', 'e1042', 'e1043', 'e1075', 'e1077', 'e1181', 'e1552', 'e1586', 'e1624', 'e2427', 'e2428', 'e2429', 'e2430', 'e2486', 'e2494', 'e2688', 'e2767', 'e2768', 'e2852', 'e2933', 'e2949', 'e3016', 'e3082', 'e3083', 'e3302', 'e3355', 'e3356', 'e3405', 'e3406', 'e3571', 'e3921', 'e3982', 'e626', 'e629', 'e661', 'e812', 'e814', 'e816', 'e823', 'e862', 'e894', 'e895', 'e957']\nold-3:6458|2|e2769\nNVIDIA’s approximately $20 billion FY2027 standalone-CPU outlook is the market/company reference and sits at rung 2.\nAbout $25 billion or somewhat above: approximately $20 billion of standalone CPU revenue for FY2027 plus Grace CPU revenue above $5 billion on a trailing-12-month basis, carried forward as a run-rate.\nThe reference is NVIDIA’s approximately $20 billion FY2027 standalone-CPU outlook, which sits at rung 2. The implied total is about $25 billion or somewhat above: the $20 billion standalone outlook plus Grace CPU revenue running above $5 billion on a trailing-12-month basis. That crosses the $25 billion boundary for rung 1. Vera being in full production and shipments underway support the outlook; the AMD performance claims are a counterweight but do not quantify a reduction to NVIDIA’s forecast.\nThe rung and key implied figure remain the same as the previous conclusion: approximately $20 billion in standalone CPU revenue plus Grace’s more-than-$5-billion trailing-12-month run-rate. The additional deployment detail does not size or revise FY2027 revenue, and the AMD performance evidence does not quantify a change to NVIDIA’s outlook.\nUSED ['e1030', 'e1031', 'e1032', 'e2767', 'e2768', 'e2852', 'e3083', 'e3302', 'e894', 'e895']\nold-3:6458|3|e2769\nNVIDIA's approximately $20 billion FY2027 standalone CPU guidance; this reference sits at rung 2. No separate market consensus for total CPU revenue is supplied.\nApproximately $20 billion of FY2027 standalone CPU revenue plus a carried-forward Grace CPU run-rate above $5 billion (reported on a trailing-12-month basis) implies total FY2027 CPU revenue of at least about $25 billion, placing the forecast at rung 1.\nThe reference is NVIDIA's approximately $20 billion FY2027 standalone CPU guidance, which sits at rung 2. The implied total is at least about $25 billion: the $20 billion standalone forecast is distinct from Grace, whose trailing-12-month CPU revenue already exceeded $5 billion; carrying that run-rate into FY2027 puts total CPU revenue at or above the rung-1 threshold. Vera production, shipments and customer commitments support delivery of the standalone forecast. AMD's performance claims and the challenge of taking share are counterweights, but they do not quantify a reduction to NVIDIA's guidance or to Grace's run-rate. Recent AWS and FY2028 evidence strengthens the demand and execution context but does not justify raising the FY2027 estimate beyond this implied figure.\nThe rung and implied FY2027 figure remain at least about $25 billion, but new evidence adds material execution and demand context: NVIDIA described Vera shipments to lead partners and AWS's 2-million-GPU deployment alongside Vera CPUs, and projected FY2028 standalone CPU revenue above $40 billion. Those items support the outlook without changing the FY2027 quantitative anchor.\nUSED ['e1030', 'e1031', 'e1032', 'e1042', 'e1043', 'e1075', 'e1077', 'e1181', 'e1552', 'e1586', 'e1624', 'e2427', 'e2428', 'e2429', 'e2430', 'e2486', 'e2494', 'e2688', 'e2767', 'e2768', 'e2852', 'e2933', 'e2949', 'e3016', 'e3082', 'e3083', 'e3302', 'e3355', 'e3356', 'e3405', 'e3406', 'e3571', 'e3921', 'e3982', 'e626', 'e629', 'e661', 'e812', 'e814', 'e816', 'e823', 'e862', 'e894', 'e895', 'e957']\n- e1030 [party_statement; AMD corporate VP Ravi Kuppuswany, quoted by Tom’s Hardware; original source AMD corporate VP Ravi Kuppuswany ; from gnews:a60415628b7bd46d7f0f4a48 2026-07-24] AMD corporate VP Ravi Kuppuswany said AMD’s Venice CPUs showed a 20% performance advantage over NVIDIA’s Vera CPU in AMD’s SPEC comparison, versus an expected advantage of at least 10%, with tuning not complete.\n    Quote: “We were actually being a little conservative. I actually thought we were beating them by smaller numbers than what I have here… we thought we’d have at least a 10% advantage. What we’re finding is… we have 20% advantage, and we have not even finished completely tuning.”\n- e1031 [third_party_view; Tom’s Hardware; original source AMD ; from gnews:a60415628b7bd46d7f0f4a48 2026-07-24] AMD’s slide claimed its Venice CPUs had 2.2x the throughput and 1.2x the per-core performance of NVIDIA’s Vera CPU in SPEC comparisons.\n    Quote: Kuppuswany’s comments overlaid the slide you can see above, claiming 2.2x higher throughput with Venice compared to Vera, and 1.2x faster per-core performance.\n- e1032 [third_party_view; Tom’s Hardware; original source Tom’s Hardware 2026-07-24; from gnews:a60415628b7bd46d7f0f4a48 2026-07-24] Tom’s Hardware calculated that AMD’s claimed 1.2x per-core performance advantage over NVIDIA Vera translates to an approximately 18.8% lead, based on the scores divided by core count.\n    Quote: AMD says 1.2x, which actually translates to about an 18.8% lead.\n- e1042 [party_statement; tikr.com, retelling Jensen Huang's May 20 earnings-call remarks; original source Jensen Huang, NVIDIA CEO 2026-05-20; from gnews:f91cb77d6fea19116da4188b 2026-07-24] NVIDIA CEO Jensen Huang said standalone Vera CPU revenue was excluded from NVIDIA's $1 trillion Blackwell-and-Rubin revenue forecast for calendar 2025–2027, and expected it to be the second-largest source of upside above that forecast.\n    Quote: NVIDIA has guided to $1 trillion in combined Blackwell and Rubin revenue from 2025 through calendar 2027. Does the CPU sit inside it? Huang: “we didn’t include any Vera CPU, standalone CPU in that number. And so I expect that to be the second largest” source of upside above the $1 trillion, behind only share gains in frontier AI models.\n- e1043 [third_party_view; tikr.com; original source Bernstein ; from gnews:f91cb77d6fea19116da4188b 2026-07-24] Bernstein estimated the mature server CPU market at roughly $37 billion in calendar 2025.\n    Quote: Bernstein put the mature server CPU market at roughly $37 billion in 2025.\n- e1075 [third_party_view; fool.com (analyst); original source fool.com (analyst) 2026-07-25; from gnews:2dbdeb0f4121e37164fd6ccd 2026-07-25] The author reports that Alphabet and some other tech companies are designing CPUs for use in AI data centers.\n    Quote: some tech companies, including Alphabet, are designing their own CPUs to be used in AI data centers.\n- e1077 [third_party_view; fool.com (analyst); original source fool.com (analyst) 2026-07-25; from gnews:2dbdeb0f4121e37164fd6ccd 2026-07-25] The author says Intel and AMD currently have 67% and 33% shares, respectively, of the server CPU market.\n    Quote: The hard part will be chipping away at Intel's and AMD's server CPU market shares, which are currently 67% and 33%, respectively.\n- e1181 [third_party_view; Morningstar; original source AMD (forecast revision); Morningstar (interpretation) ; from gnews:ba8e52b6f010b77f770e4483 2026-07-27] Morningstar said AMD had recently raised its server CPU market forecast, which Morningstar viewed as a sign of high agentic-AI demand and a need for substantially more AI infrastructure over the next few years.\n    Quote: AMD’s recent boost to its server CPU market forecast suggests to us that agentic AI is in high demand and that much more AI infrastructure will be required over the next few years.\n- e1552 [fact; theregister.com; original source Alibaba, ByteDance, Meta, Oracle, CoreWeave, Lambda, Nebius, and NScale ; from gnews:e6a57afa56d4f16b1b4117fe 2026-08-01] Alibaba, ByteDance, Meta, Oracle, CoreWeave, Lambda, Nebius, and NScale have signed up to deploy NVIDIA Vera CPUs in their clouds; the item gives no deployment date or amount.\n    Quote: Alibaba, ByteDance, Meta, Oracle, CoreWeave, Lambda, Nebius, and NScale have already signed up to deploy the chips in their respective clouds.\n- e1586 [party_statement; finance.yahoo.com; original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:8c813e909e7c0c5e7535e616 2026-08-03] NVIDIA is guiding to around $20 billion in Vera CPU sales for FY2027.\n    Quote: is guiding to around $20 billion in Vera sales this fiscal year.\n- e1624 [fact; theregister.com; original source SpaceX 2026-08-04; from gnews:50f8d0c930476f4ae30921ed 2026-08-05] SpaceX announced a partnership with Nvidia to design the Starmind AI1 satellite compute payload, and said each satellite will include Nvidia Rubin GPUs and Vera CPUs.\n    Quote: partnering with Nvidia to design the Starmind AI1 satellite compute payload,” and that “Each of the Starmind satellites will include Nvidia Rubin GPUs and Vera CPUs for datacenter class space compute.\n- e2427 [party_statement; fool.com (Harsh Chauhan); original source Advanced Micro Devices ; from gnews:36c5541b78e9258d831613b3 2026-08-21] AMD expected the server CPU market to grow by more than 50% annually through 2030 and reach $220 billion in annual revenue.\n    Quote: Also, AMD noted on the earnings call that it now expects the server CPU market to grow by more than 50% annually through 2030, generating $220 billion in annual revenue.\n- e2428 [party_statement; fool.com (Harsh Chauhan); original source Advanced Micro Devices ; from gnews:36c5541b78e9258d831613b3 2026-08-21] AMD's current $220 billion annual server-CPU-market estimate was above its $60 billion estimate from November 2025.\n    Quote: That's well above the $60 billion estimate for the server CPU market that AMD delivered in November 2025.\n- e2429 [party_statement; fool.com (Harsh Chauhan); original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:36c5541b78e9258d831613b3 2026-08-21] NVIDIA said it could sell $20 billion worth of stand-alone server CPUs in FY2027.\n    Quote: Nvidia has already set its sights on this massive market, noting that it can sell $20 billion worth of stand-alone server CPUs this year.\n- e2430 [rumour; fool.com (Harsh Chauhan); original source fool.com (Harsh Chauhan) ; from gnews:36c5541b78e9258d831613b3 2026-08-21] NVIDIA was reported to plan to start selling Vera server CPUs in August 2026.\n    Quote: Nvidia will reportedly start selling its Vera server CPUs this month.\n- e2486 [party_statement; NVIDIA Corporation; original source SpaceXAI 2026-08-24; from nvidianews:spacexai-adopts-nvidia-vera-cpu-to-accelerate-agentic-ai-at-massive-scale 2026-08-24] SpaceXAI plans to deploy NVIDIA Vera CPUs for its next generation of agentic AI applications.\n    Quote: SpaceXAI will deploy NVIDIA Vera CPUs to accelerate its next generation of agentic AI applications\n- e2494 [party_statement; NVIDIA Corporation; original source SpaceXAI 2026-08-24; from nvblog:vera-rubin-lpx-spectrum-x-nvlink-fusion 2026-08-24] SpaceXAI plans to deploy NVIDIA Vera CPUs for agentic-AI workloads, including orchestration, tool use, code execution, data processing and simulation; no timing or scale was specified.\n    Quote: The company plans to deploy NVIDIA Vera CPUs to accelerate the CPU-intensive work behind agentic AI, including orchestration, tool use, code execution, data processing and simulation.\n- e2688 [party_statement; finance.yahoo.com; original source AMD ; from gnews:093b9d63d05081b51dec3c77 2026-08-25] AMD projected server CPU revenue growth of 80% year over year in the second half of 2026 and 70% in 2027.\n    Quote: AMD projected second-half 2026 server CPU revenue to grow 80% year over year and 70% in 2027.\n- e2767 [party_statement; Colette Kress; original source NVIDIA 2026-08-26; from truenorth:transcript:NVDA:2026-08-26 2026-08-26] NVIDIA said Grace CPU revenue exceeded $5 billion on a trailing-12-month basis.\n    Quote: Our Grace CPU introduced in 2021 has been a great success, with revenue on a trailing 12-month basis, exceeding $5 billion.\n- e2768 [party_statement; Colette Kress; original source NVIDIA 2026-08-26; from truenorth:transcript:NVDA:2026-08-26 2026-08-26] NVIDIA said its Vera CPU was in full production as of August 26, 2026.\n    Quote: Today, we are in full production of our next-generation Vera CPU.\n- e2852 [party_statement; Jensen Huang; original source Amazon ; from extra:b23f73a08a535993119430f5 2026-08-26] Jensen Huang said Amazon planned to buy millions of CPUs; no period was specified.\n    Quote: They're going to buy millions of CPUs.\n- e2933 [party_statement; fool.com (analyst); original source Colette Kress, NVIDIA CFO 2026-08-26; from gnews:58da18a5e0a271a50e9bbdaf 2026-08-26] CFO Colette Kress expected NVIDIA CPU revenue in fiscal 2028 to exceed twice the previously stated fiscal 2027 expectation of $20 billion.\n    Quote: CFO Colette Kress now expects that number to more than double in fiscal 2028.\n- e2949 [party_statement; MarketBeat; original source Colette Kress, NVIDIA CFO ; from gnews:7dfab0010a8ac15786f05387 2026-08-26] Colette Kress said AWS's planned 2-million-GPU deployment will include Vera CPUs, both integrated with Rubin products and as standalone processors.\n    Quote: The deployment will include Vera CPUs, some integrated with Rubin products and some deployed as standalone processors, according to Kress.\n- e3016 [party_statement; NVIDIA, quoted by taekim.substack.com; original source NVIDIA 2026-08-26; from gnews:2d41619b8a2902306decca58 2026-08-26] NVIDIA said total server CPU demand was approximately $20 billion and preliminarily expected its CPU revenue to more than double in FY2028.\n    Quote: “We continue to see demand for approximately $20 billion in total server CPUs. Based on our customer demand and improving supply outlook, our preliminary expectation is for CPU revenue to more than double in fiscal 2028 ”\n- e3082 [party_statement; TechCrunch, quoting NVIDIA CFO Colette Kress; original source Colette Kress, NVIDIA CFO 2026-08-26; from gnews:d949d26fe875780037064b8a 2026-08-26] NVIDIA CFO Colette Kress said NVIDIA plans to send an unspecified number of Vera CPUs to AWS starting in the third quarter, some integrated with Rubin and some standalone.\n    Quote: With the 2 million GPU chips Amazon is adding to AWS starting in the third quarter, Nvidia also plans to send an unspecified number of Vera CPUs, “some integrated with Rubin, others standalone,” according to Nvidia CFO Colette Kress.\n- e3083 [party_statement; TechCrunch, quoting NVIDIA CFO Colette Kress; original source Colette Kress, NVIDIA CFO 2026-08-26; from gnews:d949d26fe875780037064b8a 2026-08-26] NVIDIA CFO Colette Kress said Vera shipments were already underway to lead partners, including Oracle and SpaceXAI, and NVIDIA expects deployment by every major hyperscaler, neocloud, AI lab, and system OEM.\n    Quote: Kress said Wednesday that Nvidia expects Vera to be deployed by “every major hyperscaler, neocloud, AI lab, and system OEM, with shipments already underway to our lead partners,” which include Oracle and SpaceXAI.\n- e3302 [party_statement; Yahoo Finance; original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:8d248df2272209cf74248fae 2026-08-28] NVIDIA projected stand-alone CPU revenue of $20 billion in FY2027 and more than $40 billion in FY2028.\n    Quote: Nvidia is leaning into that opening, projecting $20 billion in stand-alone CPU revenue this year and more than double that next year\n- e3355 [party_statement; finance.yahoo.com; original source Jensen Huang ; from gnews:c49ad54fc1f569a72b249821 2026-08-28] NVIDIA CEO Jensen Huang said the AWS arrangement could extend to millions of CPUs; the item gives no timing.\n    Quote: Jensen Huang has said the arrangement could extend to millions of CPUs as well.\n- e3356 [party_statement; finance.yahoo.com; original source NVIDIA 2026-08-26; from gnews:c49ad54fc1f569a72b249821 2026-08-28] NVIDIA expects standalone CPU revenue to be $20 billion in FY2027 and more than $40 billion in FY2028.\n    Quote: standalone CPUs built on its Rubin platform, which the company expects to generate $20 billion this fiscal year and more than double that the year after.\n- e3405 [party_statement; fool.com (analyst); original source Jensen Huang ; from gnews:af66eb268fd28de94cceff05 2026-08-28] Nvidia CEO Jensen Huang said the AWS deal could include the purchase of millions of CPUs.\n    Quote: In a CNBC interview, Nvidia chief Jensen Huang said the deal includes the potential purchase of \"millions of CPUs\" too.\n- e3406 [party_statement; fool.com (analyst); original source Nvidia ; from gnews:af66eb268fd28de94cceff05 2026-08-28] Nvidia predicts $20 billion in stand-alone CPU revenue in FY2027.\n    Quote: the company predicts $20 billion in stand-alone CPU revenue this year\n- e3571 [party_statement; fool.com (analyst), reporting Colette Kress's statement; original source Colette Kress, NVIDIA CFO ; from gnews:dbbde813240d4096829a72a9 2026-08-29] Colette Kress attributed NVIDIA's expected FY2028 revenue growth to ongoing Blackwell shipments, Vera Rubin reaching full production, and Vera CPUs being paired with existing GPU architectures and sold standalone.\n    Quote: fueled by ongoing Blackwell shipments, scaling Vera Rubin as it reaches full production, and both pairing Vera CPUs with the company's existing graphics processing unit (GPU) architectures and selling them as a new stand-alone product.\n- e3921 [fact; finance.yahoo.com; original source Amazon ; from gnews:3dd9083dec0a12157f803bb1 2026-09-01] Amazon agreed to deploy 2 million additional NVIDIA GPUs through Q2 FY2029 alongside Vera CPUs, and to adopt NVIDIA Omniverse and robotics software for its warehouse fleet.\n    Quote: Amazon deepened its own commitment too, agreeing to deploy an additional 2 million Nvidia GPUs through the second quarter of fiscal 2029 alongside new Vera CPUs, while adopting Nvidia's Omniverse and robotics software for its warehouse fleet.\n- e3982 [party_statement; fool.com (analyst); original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:ee0002bd6bda4aa8d9a483f5 2026-09-01] NVIDIA predicts $20 billion in stand-alone CPU sales in FY2027.\n    Quote: The company predicts $20 billion in stand-alone CPU sales this year.\n- e626 [fact; es.tradingview.com; original source Noetra ; from gnews:62714f928bfeae1c78139609 2026-07-16] Noetra plans an initial deployment of 13,750 NVIDIA Vera CPUs and 27,500 NVIDIA Rubin GPUs, providing 140 MW of data-center capacity; no timing was given.\n    Quote: Under the initial deployment, Noetra will install 13,750 Nvidia Vera central processing units and 27,500 Nvidia Rubin graphics processing units, providing 140 megawatts of data center capacity.\n- e629 [fact; Tom's Hardware, reporting NVIDIA's announcement; original source NVIDIA 2026-07-16; from gnews:4cf92ff695f51dbbb675399a 2026-07-16] NVIDIA announced on 2026-07-16 that it is working with Noetra to build a 140 MW AI factory with 27,500 Rubin GPUs and 13,750 Vera CPUs for Japan’s FRONTia program.\n    Quote: Nvidia today announced that it's working with Japan's Noetra Corp. to build a 140-megawatt AI factory packing 27,500 Rubin GPUs and 13,750 Vera CPUs, the compute foundation for FRONTia, the Japanese government's state-funded physical AI program.\n- e661 [fact; NVIDIA, in its press release; original source NVIDIA 2026-07-16; from gnews:4a689c7ec59054514716a14e 2026-07-16] NVIDIA announced a plan with Noetra to launch a Japan AI factory using 13,750 Vera CPUs and 27,500 Rubin GPUs; no launch date was given.\n    Quote: NVIDIA today announced it is working with Noetra Corp. to launch an NVIDIA Vera Rubin AI factory with 13,750 NVIDIA Vera CPUs and 27,500 NVIDIA Rubin GPUs for national physical AI.\n- e812 [party_statement; CNBC; original source NVIDIA representatives ; from gnews:69c31079960ffe4f9805834c 2026-07-21] NVIDIA representatives said Vera CPUs were delivered to OpenAI, Anthropic and SpaceX in June 2026.\n    Quote: Nvidia representatives said Vera chips were delivered to clients, including OpenAI , Anthropic , and SpaceX , in June.\n- e814 [third_party_view; CNBC; original source Wolfe Research ; from gnews:69c31079960ffe4f9805834c 2026-07-21] Wolfe Research forecast a Vera CPU average selling price of about $5,000 and NVIDIA shipments of about 1.3 million chips in 2026.\n    Quote: Wolfe Research said in May that it expected the average selling price to be about $5,000 per Vera chip, and forecast that Nvidia would ship about 1.3 million of them this year.\n- e816 [party_statement; CNBC; original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:69c31079960ffe4f9805834c 2026-07-21] OpenAI plans to deploy NVIDIA Vera CPUs in large quantities beginning in Q3 2026.\n    Quote: OpenAI plans to deploy Vera chips in large quantities starting this quarter.\n- e823 [party_statement; Jake Roach, Tom's Hardware; original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:460090b9c71947edd68a13f4 2026-07-21] NVIDIA said Vera CPU general availability remains on track for the second half of 2026.\n    Quote: Nvidia has slowly revealed more details about Vera as it ramps into general availability, which is on track for the back half of this year.\n- e862 [party_statement; qz.com; original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:9ec05dc5e996b554b6f7a5f2 2026-07-21] NVIDIA confirmed that its Vera CPU entered full production in June 2026.\n    Quote: Nvidia confirmed at the Computex conference in June that the Vera CPU had entered full production, with the chip packing 88 cores and an LPDDR5X memory subsystem capable of 1.2 TB/s of bandwidth.\n- e894 [party_statement; finance.yahoo.com; original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:b3b6cb80911bb88b3459345e 2026-07-22] NVIDIA said it had visibility to nearly $20 billion in total CPU revenue for FY2027.\n    Quote: The company stated it has \"visibility to nearly $20 billion in total CPU revenue this year.\"\n- e895 [party_statement; finance.yahoo.com; original source NVIDIA CEO ; from gnews:b3b6cb80911bb88b3459345e 2026-07-22] NVIDIA’s CEO clarified that the nearly $20 billion CPU revenue visibility figure is for standalone CPUs; no separate period was stated.\n    Quote: The CEO later clarified that this figure is for standalone CPUs, confirming a new, distinct revenue stream.\n- e957 [party_statement; fool.com (analyst); original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:e618cf2b96e80e003c59f736 2026-07-23] NVIDIA said it is on track for $20 billion in CPU sales in FY2027.\n    Quote: and says it's on track for $20 billion in CPU sales this year.\n\n control-1:8807\ncontrol-1:8807|1|e393\nBloomberg/Yahoo Finance consensus is about $393 billion for FY2027, placing the reference in rung 3 ($350 billion to less than $400 billion).\nAbout $430 billion for FY2027. The Q1 actual, Q2 guidance, and guided sequential growth imply a roughly $386 billion baseline when that growth is carried forward through Q3 and Q4; demand and order evidence supports a modest uplift, while supply and China constraints limit it.\nThe market reference is about $393 billion for FY2027, which sits in rung 3. The guidance-led baseline remains about $386 billion: $81.6 billion reported in Q1, $91 billion guided for Q2, then carrying forward the guided 11% sequential growth for Q3 and Q4. I raise the forecast modestly to about $430 billion: the newly reported roughly $1 trillion in confirmed Blackwell and Vera Rubin orders and strong customer AI spending support upside, but those orders span products and periods and do not translate dollar-for-dollar into FY2027 revenue. HBM ramp constraints and the exclusion of China Data Center compute from Q2 temper the uplift. The implied figure is above $400 billion but below $450 billion, so it remains in rung 2.\nThe rung is unchanged, but the implied forecast rises from about $425 billion to about $430 billion. The reported description of roughly $1 trillion in orders as confirmed, alongside recent customer spending and AI-business data, modestly strengthens the demand case; these are not all NVIDIA revenue in FY2027 and do not justify crossing the $450 billion boundary.\nUSED ['e1336', 'e1371', 'e1376', 'e1377', 'e1378', 'e1380', 'e238', 'e240', 'e284', 'e321', 'e669', 'e878', 'e882', 'e883', 'e906']\ncontrol-1:8807|2|e393\nMarket consensus is approximately $393 billion for FY2027, in rung 3 ($350 billion to less than $400 billion).\nApproximately $425 billion: Q1 reported revenue of $81.6 billion plus Q2 guidance of $91 billion, followed by the guided 11% sequential growth carried into Q3 and Q4, yields a roughly $386 billion baseline. Strong demand and order visibility justify an uplift, discounted for multi-period/product orders and supply, China, and competition risks.\nThe market reference is about $393 billion, which is rung 3. The reported Q1 revenue of $81.6 billion and Q2 guidance of $91 billion, carried forward at the guided 11% sequential growth rate, imply a roughly $386 billion run-rate baseline—not annualizing Q1 flat. Strong order visibility and customer investment support an uplift to about $425 billion, while the multi-year Blackwell/Rubin order figure is not all FY2027 revenue, China compute remains excluded from Q2 guidance, and memory and software-competition risks temper the upside. About $425 billion is above the $400 billion boundary but below $450 billion, so the forecast remains rung 2.\nThe rung is unchanged, but the newly arrived Aug.  3 evidence adds a distinct software-portability and CUDA-moat risk to the revenue outlook, balanced by countervailing views that verification, optimization, and NVIDIA's own progress limit that threat. It changes the key supporting facts, but does not move the implied forecast across a rung boundary.\nUSED ['e1336', 'e1371', 'e1376', 'e1380', 'e1395', 'e1396', 'e1400', 'e1401', 'e1402', 'e1403', 'e1405', 'e1406', 'e1408', 'e231', 'e234', 'e238', 'e240', 'e284', 'e321', 'e459', 'e577', 'e669', 'e751']\ncontrol-1:8807|3|e393\nMarket consensus is about $393 billion for FY2027; that is rung 3 ($350 billion to less than $400 billion). Zacks' estimate of 80% year-over-year growth is consistent with that reference range.\nThe Q1 actual of $81.6 billion plus Q2 guidance of $91 billion, with 11% sequential growth carried through Q3 and Q4, implies about $386 billion. I raise that baseline to approximately $430 billion for confirmed multi-period product orders and strong customer spending, while discounting the orders for their FY2027 share and accounting for memory and China constraints.\nThe market reference is about $393 billion for FY2027 (with Zacks also indicating roughly 80% growth from FY2026), which sits in rung 3. Q1 revenue of $81.6 billion and Q2 guidance of $91 billion, carried forward at the guided 11% sequential growth rate, imply a baseline of about $386 billion. I forecast approximately $430 billion: recent evidence of roughly $1 trillion in confirmed Blackwell and Vera Rubin orders through 2027, alongside strong cloud growth and elevated customer investment, supports an uplift from that baseline. The orders span products and periods, so they do not translate dollar-for-dollar into FY2027 revenue; memory-supply constraints and the continued China compute exclusion also limit the uplift. The implied outcome is above $400 billion but below $450 billion.\nThe rung remains 2, but the forecast rises modestly from the prior approximately $425 billion as the evidence now describes roughly $1 trillion of confirmed orders, rather than only potential product revenue, and adds recent cloud-growth and customer-investment evidence. Those orders still span multiple products and periods, so they do not justify moving above $450 billion.\nUSED ['e1336', 'e1371', 'e1376', 'e1379', 'e238', 'e240', 'e284', 'e321', 'e669', 'e751']\n- e1336 [fact; Insider Monkey; original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:39deea97b690ac7b56dada0b 2026-07-31] NVIDIA has roughly $1 trillion in confirmed orders for Blackwell and Vera Rubin chips through 2027.\n    Quote: The company already has roughly $1 trillion in confirmed orders for its Blackwell and Vera Rubin chips through 2027\n- e1371 [fact; fool.com (Robert Izquierdo); original source Alphabet ; from gnews:96e365fac0c2d3275358c1ea 2026-08-01] Alphabet reported Google Cloud Q2 revenue of $24.8 billion, up 82% year over year, and a customer-order backlog above $500 billion, up from $462 billion in Q1.\n    Quote: In Q2, the company’s Google Cloud business enjoyed 82% year-over-year revenue growth to $24.8 billion, and its backlog of customer orders rose to over half a trillion dollars, up from $462 billion in Q1.\n- e1376 [fact; 247wallst.com (Joel South); original source Microsoft ; from gnews:c5ba7d4f41d0388a171eebcc 2026-08-01] Microsoft reported $30.88 billion of capital expenditures in Q3 FY26, up 84.4% year over year, with much of the spending on NVIDIA silicon for Azure.\n    Quote: Microsoft ( NASDAQ:MSFT ) spent $30.88 billion on CapEx in Q3 FY26, up 84.4%, much of it on NVIDIA silicon for Azure.\n- e1377 [party_statement; 247wallst.com (Joel South, quoting Satya Nadella); original source Satya Nadella ; from gnews:c5ba7d4f41d0388a171eebcc 2026-08-01] Microsoft CEO Satya Nadella said its AI business exceeded a $37 billion annual revenue run rate, up 123% year over year.\n    Quote: Satya Nadella said “Our AI business surpassed an annual revenue run rate of $37 billion, up 123% year-over-year.”\n- e1378 [fact; 247wallst.com (Joel South); original source Microsoft ; from gnews:c5ba7d4f41d0388a171eebcc 2026-08-01] Microsoft’s commercial remaining performance obligations were $627 billion, up 99% year over year; the author says this gives Microsoft leverage to pass GPU costs through.\n    Quote: Commercial RPO of $627 billion, up 99%, gives Microsoft the pricing leverage to pass GPU costs through.\n- e1380 [fact; 247wallst.com (Joel South); original source Alphabet ; from gnews:c5ba7d4f41d0388a171eebcc 2026-08-01] Alphabet reported $44.92 billion of capital expenditures in Q2 FY26, up 100% year over year; the author says a meaningful share bypasses NVIDIA.\n    Quote: CapEx still ran $44.92 billion in Q2 FY26, up 100%, but a meaningful share bypasses NVIDIA.\n- e195 [third_party_view; Jeran Wittenstein, citing data compiled by Bloomberg; original source Bloomberg ; from gnews:49f225cd79fb22132f3b9ee4 2026-07-10] Bloomberg data project NVIDIA FY2027 sales of $393 billion, up 82%, and profits of $228 billion, up 90%.\n    Quote: The chipmaker is projected to deliver $228 billion in profits on sales of $393 billion in fiscal 2027, which ends Jan. 31. That would represent an expansion of 90% and 82%, respectively, according to data compiled by Bloomberg.\n- e238 [fact; barchart.com (analyst); original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:82472d26336e1982cf8a3eff 2026-07-12] NVIDIA reported fiscal Q1 2027 total revenue of $81.6 billion, up 85% year over year.\n    Quote: For the first quarter of fiscal 2027, revenue jumped 85% year-over-year (YOY) to $81.6 billion, easily beating analyst expectations.\n- e240 [party_statement; barchart.com (analyst); original source NVIDIA management ; from gnews:82472d26336e1982cf8a3eff 2026-07-12] NVIDIA management forecast approximately $91 billion in fiscal Q2 2027 revenue, assuming no China data-center sales.\n    Quote: Management also issued strong guidance, forecasting approximately $91 billion in Q2 revenue despite assuming no China data-center sales because of export restrictions.\n- e284 [fact; Vandita Jadeja; original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:b68c019528300c6a1fcd3515 2026-07-13] NVIDIA shipped no H20 compute products to China in Q1 FY2027 and excluded Data Center compute revenue from China from its Q2 FY2027 guidance.\n    Quote: There were no H20 compute products shipped to China in Q1 FY27, and the Q2 FY27 guidance excludes any Data Center compute revenue from China.\n- e321 [fact; finance.yahoo.com; original source SK Hynix ; from gnews:c73b90b63a733c6ab712fcdd 2026-07-14] SK Hynix is slowing its HBM4 ramp and redirecting capacity to conventional DRAM, where shortages have pushed operating margins above HBM margins.\n    Quote: SK Hynix is deliberately slowing its HBM4 ramp to redirect capacity into conventional DRAM, where shortages have pushed operating margins above HBM's.\n- e669 [third_party_view; Anthony Di Pizio, Motley Fool; original source Wall Street's average estimates, provided by Yahoo! Finance ; from gnews:0993b79d5b6b817b8d65b011 2026-07-21] Wall Street's average estimates put NVIDIA total revenue at $393 billion in FY2027 and $559 billion in FY2028.\n    Quote: According to Wall Street's average estimates (provided by Yahoo! Finance), Nvidia's revenue could grow to $393 billion during its current 2027 fiscal year, and then to $559 billion in fiscal 2028.\n- e878 [party_statement; Dave Kovaleski, The Motley Fool; original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:bfd479c07812f14b70042fda 2026-07-24] NVIDIA's current-quarter revenue outlook calls for $91 billion, up 11% sequentially.\n    Quote: So, while it's beating the benchmarks, the return has fallen short of recent years, despite ridiculously strong revenue and earnings growth numbers and a current quarter outlook that calls for revenue to increase 11% sequentially to $91 billion.\n- e882 [third_party_view; 247wallst.com (Rich Duprey); original source Liang Wenfeng, DeepSeek founder ; from gnews:9476c94361d79b2a660e9df5 2026-07-24] DeepSeek founder Liang Wenfeng said DeepSeek expected to receive large batches of Nvidia-powered systems in the coming months; no quantity was specified.\n    Quote: Liang also said Huawei’s production capacity remains limited and that DeepSeek expects to receive “large batches” of Nvidia-powered systems in the coming months following the Trump administration’s decision to permit certain Nvidia AI chip sales into China.\n- e883 [fact; 247wallst.com (Rich Duprey); original source Trump administration ; from gnews:9476c94361d79b2a660e9df5 2026-07-24] The Trump administration permitted certain Nvidia AI chip sales into China; the item gives no date or further details.\n    Quote: following the Trump administration’s decision to permit certain Nvidia AI chip sales into China.\n- e906 [fact; SK hynix; original source SK hynix and NVIDIA 2026-07-25; from gnews:db759b7fd9bedbcd0f632fe6 2026-07-25] SK hynix and NVIDIA are entering a long-term AI memory partnership intended to give NVIDIA stable supply of next-generation AI memory.\n    Quote: SK hynix is also entering into a long-term AI memory partnership with NVIDIA. As a follow-up measure to solidify their previous long-term technical partnership, this agreement allows NVIDIA to secure a stable supply of next-generation AI memory, while enabling SK hynix to expand the foundation for growth.\n- e1395 [third_party_view; Geoff Weiss, Business Insider; original source Jeremy Nixon, founder of Infinity ; from gnews:aec5fb91f388e7b267417aa3 2026-08-03] Infinity founder Jeremy Nixon said his startup used AI coding agents to recreate CUDA-like software for chip startup D-Matrix in 10 hours.\n    Quote: He told Business Insider his startup used AI coding agents to recreate CUDA-like software for the chip startup D-Matrix in 10 hours — evidence, he said, that one of Nvidia's biggest moats is being crossed.\n- e1396 [third_party_view; Geoff Weiss, Business Insider; original source Geoff Weiss, Business Insider 2026-08-03; from gnews:aec5fb91f388e7b267417aa3 2026-08-03] Business Insider reported that OpenAI and Anthropic recently demonstrated AI models capable of generating system software.\n    Quote: Cloud giants like Google, Amazon, and Microsoft have spent years building software around their own AI chips, while OpenAI and Anthropic have recently demonstrated AI models capable of generating system software.\n- e1400 [third_party_view; Geoff Weiss, Business Insider; original source Geoff Weiss, Business Insider 2026-08-03; from gnews:aec5fb91f388e7b267417aa3 2026-08-03] Business Insider said inference could increase demand for software that runs across different chips, making it easier for companies to switch chips without rewriting software and reducing CUDA's lock-in.\n    Quote: This could result in greater demand not only for specialized hardware but for software that can run across different chips. If companies can switch between chips without rewriting software, that reduces one of CUDA's biggest lock-ins.\n- e1401 [third_party_view; Marshall Choy, Rebellions chief business officer, as quoted by Geoff Weiss; original source Marshall Choy, Rebellions chief business officer ; from gnews:aec5fb91f388e7b267417aa3 2026-08-03] Rebellions chief business officer Marshall Choy said CUDA could lose its lock-in on inference if software becomes an open-source, cross-chip option.\n    Quote: \"That's where the CUDA moat from Nvidia gets broken because CUDA is no longer a factor in the inference side,\" Choy said. \"It's an open source play.\"\n- e1402 [party_statement; Geoff Weiss, Business Insider, reporting NVIDIA's statement; original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:aec5fb91f388e7b267417aa3 2026-08-03] NVIDIA said its integrated hardware and software offerings have become more valuable as AI models are put to work.\n    Quote: Nvidia said that its tightly integrated hardware and software offerings have become more valuable as AI models get put to work.\n- e1403 [third_party_view; Bing Xu, INT21 founder, as quoted by Geoff Weiss; original source Bing Xu, INT21 founder ; from gnews:aec5fb91f388e7b267417aa3 2026-08-03] INT21 founder Bing Xu said AI-generated code still needs verification and optimization, and that CUDA has the deepest ecosystem of tools to help coding agents work efficiently.\n    Quote: Though agents make it easier to generate software, AI-generated code still has to be verified and optimized, said Bing Xu, founder of AI software startup INT21. He believes CUDA has the deepest ecosystem of verification tools and other features that help coding agents work more efficiently.\n- e1405 [third_party_view; Chris Lattner, Modular cofounder and CEO, as quoted by Geoff Weiss; original source Chris Lattner, Modular cofounder and CEO ; from gnews:aec5fb91f388e7b267417aa3 2026-08-03] Modular cofounder and CEO Chris Lattner said coding agents make it easier to build chip software, but the improvement is incremental.\n    Quote: While coding agents do make it easier to build chip software, the improvement is incremental, Lattner said.\n- e1406 [third_party_view; Chris Lattner, Modular cofounder and CEO, as quoted by Geoff Weiss; original source Chris Lattner, Modular cofounder and CEO ; from gnews:aec5fb91f388e7b267417aa3 2026-08-03] Modular cofounder and CEO Chris Lattner said claims about coding agents' impact are overblown because writing code is only a small part of software development, while production optimization is more complex and can reduce AI operating costs.\n    Quote: \"The hype is not complete nonsense, but it is very overblown,\" he said, adding that writing code is only a small part of building software compared to more complex tasks like optimizing it for production — a critical task because software that maximizes a chip's performance reduces the cost of running AI at scale.\n- e1408 [third_party_view; Bing Xu, INT21 founder, as quoted by Geoff Weiss; original source Bing Xu, INT21 founder ; from gnews:aec5fb91f388e7b267417aa3 2026-08-03] INT21 founder Bing Xu said AI may help NVIDIA's rivals catch up, while NVIDIA also benefits from the same technological shifts; the effect on CUDA depends on their relative pace.\n    Quote: And while AI may help rivals catch up, Nvidia benefits from the same technological shifts, Xu said. Whether coding agents weaken CUDA depends on whether competitors catch Nvidia faster than it can gain new ground.\n- e231 [rumour; tech-insider.org; original source CNBC (as retold by tech-insider.org) ; from gnews:8faf69f198e10f14aae67621 2026-07-12] Samsung, SK Hynix, and Micron reportedly sold all their 2026 HBM output under long-term contracts, with hyperscalers including Microsoft, Google, Meta, and Amazon buying the supply.\n    Quote: The root cause sits one layer below Nvidia entirely. Hyperscalers including Microsoft, Google, Meta, and Amazon are buying up high-bandwidth memory as fast as Samsung, SK Hynix, and Micron can produce it, and all three manufacturers have reportedly sold out their entire 2026 HBM output under long-term contracts, according to CNBC’s reporting from January 2026.\n- e234 [party_statement; tech-insider.org; original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:8faf69f198e10f14aae67621 2026-07-12] NVIDIA said AWS plans to deploy 2 million additional NVIDIA GPUs during 2027–2028.\n    Quote: Nvidia said in August 2026 that AWS plans to deploy 2 million additional Nvidia GPUs in 2027 to 2028 .\n- e459 [party_statement; Gerelyn Terzo, reporting Huang's remarks; original source Jensen Huang, NVIDIA CEO ; from gnews:2e37109213bd9753af2f50e0 2026-07-15] Jensen Huang said NVIDIA had more orders than at his previous GTC remarks and expected to keep its supply chain busy for several more years.\n    Quote: He added that “We have more orders today than we did at the last time I spoke about orders at GTC” and that NVIDIA will “keep our supply chain quite busy for several many more years coming.”\n- e577 [fact; fool.com (analyst); original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:c03377d9fbbf30b7a0933cd5 2026-07-17] NVIDIA reported FY2026 revenue of close to $215.9 billion, up 65.5% year over year.\n    Quote: In FY 2026, revenue reached close to $215.9 billion, a significant jump of 65.5% over the previous year.\n- e751 [third_party_view; Zacks.com analyst; original source Zacks 2026-07-22; from gnews:43105a934c3a4e2d21029e9c 2026-07-22] Zacks consensus estimates NVIDIA FY2027 revenue growth of 80% year over year.\n    Quote: The Zacks Consensus Estimate for NVIDIA's fiscal 2027 revenues and earnings indicates year-over-year growth of 80% and 90.6%, respectively.\n- e1379 [fact; 247wallst.com (Joel South); original source Alphabet ; from gnews:c5ba7d4f41d0388a171eebcc 2026-08-01] Google Cloud revenue grew 82% year over year to $24.77 billion in Q2 FY26.\n    Quote: Google Cloud revenue grew 82% to $24.77 billion in Q2 FY26\n\n control-1:8938\ncontrol-1:8938|1|e362\nThe latest stated Wall Street expectation for the coming year is 45%, which sits at rung 3. Earlier specific FY2027 estimates were 83% (LSEG) and 85.4% (Zacks), both rung 1.\nAbout $406.8 billion of FY2027 revenue, or approximately 88.4% growth over FY2026's $215.94 billion: $81.6 billion in Q1 plus $96.2 billion in Q2 plus $108 billion of Q3 guidance, then Q4 estimated at about $121.0 billion by carrying forward Q3's 12% sequential growth.\nThe latest stated Wall Street expectation for the coming year is 45%, which sits at rung 3; earlier specific FY2027 estimates of 83% and 85.4% sit at rung 1. The forecast is above either reference: Q1 and Q2 revenue plus Q3 guidance total $285.8 billion, and carrying Q3's 12% sequential growth into Q4 implies roughly $121.0 billion for Q4 and $406.8 billion for FY2027, about 88.4% growth on FY2026 revenue of $215.94 billion. The 70% full-year growth outlook is also rung 1, though below that run-rate; reaching only 70% would require Q4 revenue of about $81.3 billion, a sharp decline from Q3 guidance of $108 billion. Sequential growth is slowing, and the multi-year CAGR and downside risks temper confidence, but they do not imply a drop below the 60% rung boundary.\nThe rung remains 1, but the latest stated Wall Street expectation is 45% rather than the prior conclusion's 83%–85.4% reference range. That changes a key supporting reference, though the operating run-rate and 70% outlook still imply rung 1.\nUSED ['e1883', 'e2118', 'e2146', 'e2147', 'e2151', 'e2154', 'e2244', 'e2421', 'e2497', 'e2499', 'e2762', 'e2989', 'e3108', 'e3110', 'e3112', 'e3593', 'e3620', 'e3683', 'e3883', 'e3942', 'e3943', 'e4049', 'e4164', 'e4165', 'e4197', 'e4224', 'e4226', 'e4227', 'e966']\ncontrol-1:8938|2|e362\nThe latest specific Wall Street reference shown is LSEG's 83% growth estimate to $396 billion, while Zacks estimates 85.4%; both sit at rung 1. The older 42% estimate cited in the ladder sits at rung 3 but is superseded by those more recent estimates.\nAbout $406.8 billion of FY2027 revenue, or approximately 88.4% growth over FY2026's $215.94 billion: $81.6 billion in Q1 plus $96.2 billion in Q2 plus Q3 guidance of $108 billion totals $285.8 billion, and carrying the roughly 12% sequential growth implied by Q3 guidance into Q4 gives about $121.0 billion.\nThe implied outcome is above the rung-1 boundary of 60%, and it remains well above the current specific Wall Street estimates. NVIDIA's reported Q1 and Q2 revenue plus Q3 guidance total $285.8 billion; carrying the approximately 12% sequential growth implied by Q3 guidance into Q4 gives about $121.0 billion for Q4 and $406.8 billion for FY2027. The reported 70% full-year growth outlook is a lower counter-signal: it implies about $367 billion for FY2027 and only $81.3 billion in Q4 after the first three quarters, a sharp sequential decline not supported by the latest guidance and run-rate. Newer, lower analyst figures and general customer-concentration and pricing risks temper confidence but do not outweigh that operating trajectory.\nThe rung and central run-rate estimate remain essentially the same as the previous conclusion. The newer 59% FY2026-to-FY2029 CAGR and Wall Street's stated 45% growth for the coming year add lower forecast anchors, though neither is a specific FY2027 estimate; they change the supporting forecast context without moving the implied FY2027 outcome across a rung boundary.\nUSED ['e1883', 'e2118', 'e2146', 'e2421', 'e2497', 'e2499', 'e3108', 'e3110', 'e3112', 'e3620', 'e3943', 'e4165', 'e4227', 'e966']\ncontrol-1:8938|3|e362\nThe latest specific FY2027 market estimate shown is Zacks' 85.4% revenue growth (e3620), rung 1; LSEG's 83% estimate (e2421) is also rung 1. The older 42% estimate in e966 is rung 3.\nAbout $407 billion of FY2027 revenue, or roughly 88.5% growth over FY2026's $215.94 billion: Q1 actual revenue of $81.6 billion plus Q2 actual revenue of $96.2 billion plus Q3 guidance of $108.0 billion equals $285.8 billion, and carrying the Q2-to-Q3 sequential growth rate forward implies about $121 billion for Q4.\nThe current market reference is Zacks' 85.4% FY2027 growth estimate, which sits at rung 1; LSEG's 83% estimate is also rung 1. The older 42% estimate in e966 is rung 3, but is superseded as a reference by the more recent FY2027 estimates. NVIDIA's stated 70% full-year growth outlook is also rung 1. The quarterly run-rate points higher: Q1 revenue of $81.6 billion, Q2 revenue of $96.2 billion, and Q3 guidance of $108.0 billion total $285.8 billion; carrying the Q2-to-Q3 sequential growth rate forward implies roughly $121 billion in Q4. The resulting FY2027 revenue is about $407 billion, or 88.5% growth on FY2026 revenue of $215.94 billion. That is comfortably above the 60% rung-1 boundary. The 59% FY2026–FY2029 CAGR estimate and cited downside risks temper confidence, but do not outweigh the current-quarter guidance and run-rate.\nThe rung and approximate quarterly run-rate are unchanged from the previous conclusion. The evidence in view now includes the FY2026–FY2029 59% CAGR estimate and a 45% analyst-growth reference, which temper the supporting outlook; the updated AI-capex themes also change the demand context. These changes affect the supporting facts and balance of evidence, but not the implied outcome enough to cross a rung boundary.\nUSED ['e1883', 'e2118', 'e2146', 'e2147', 'e2151', 'e2154', 'e2244', 'e2421', 'e2497', 'e2499', 'e2762', 'e2989', 'e3108', 'e3110', 'e3112', 'e3593', 'e3620', 'e3683', 'e3883', 'e3942', 'e3943', 'e4049', 'e4164', 'e4165', 'e4197', 'e4224', 'e4226', 'e4227', 'e966']\n- e1883 [fact; 247wallst.com (Joel South); original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:3ef8c07472c4da3ddb8d4350 2026-08-15] NVIDIA reported FY2026 total revenue of $215.94 billion, up 65.47% year over year.\n    Quote: NVIDIA reported full-year FY2026 revenue of $215.94 billion, up 65.47% year over year, with net income of $120.07 billion and free cash flow of $96.58 billion.\n- e2118 [third_party_view; Morningstar; original source Morningstar 2026-08-19; from gnews:7ce47efd62fa17b9aa29e258 2026-08-19] Morningstar expects industry AI capex to accelerate in calendar 2026 and models 80% growth in NVIDIA total revenue in FY2027.\n    Quote: Given the acceleration in AI capex we expect within the industry in calendar 2026, we model 80% total revenue growth for Nvidia in fiscal 2027.\n- e2146 [fact; fool.com (Daniel Sparks); original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:712a161a59386d843542f6ac 2026-08-19] NVIDIA reported fiscal 2027 first-quarter revenue of $81.6 billion, up 85% year over year and 20% sequentially, about $2 billion above the top of its earlier $78.0 billion-plus-or-minus-2% guidance range; Data Center revenue was $75.2 billion, up 92%.\n    Quote: In May, Nvidia reported $81.6 billion of fiscal first-quarter revenue -- about $2 billion above the top of the $78.0 billion, plus-or-minus-2%, range it had guided to three months earlier. Growth ran 85% year over year and 20% sequentially, with data center revenue climbing 92% to $75.2 billion.\n- e2147 [third_party_view; fool.com (Daniel Sparks); original source Yahoo! Finance ; from gnews:712a161a59386d843542f6ac 2026-08-19] Yahoo! Finance data showed five upward and no downward analyst revisions to NVIDIA fiscal 2027 second-quarter estimates over the month before August 19, 2026.\n    Quote: analysts have been nudging this quarter's numbers higher into the report -- five upward revisions over the past month, and none down, per Yahoo! Finance.\n- e2151 [third_party_view; fool.com (Daniel Sparks); original source Daniel Sparks 2026-08-19; from gnews:712a161a59386d843542f6ac 2026-08-19] Daniel Sparks calculated that NVIDIA's fiscal 2027 second-quarter revenue guidance midpoint implies about $9 billion of sequential growth, or 11%, versus roughly $13.5 billion and 20% sequential growth in the first quarter.\n    Quote: The midpoint asks for about $9 billion of new revenue versus the first quarter, which had itself added about $13.5 billion versus the one before it. In growth terms, that's a step down to about 11% sequentially from 20%.\n- e2154 [third_party_view; fool.com (analyst); original source Jensen Huang (the $1 trillion target); fool.com (analyst) (the FY2027 and FY2028 revenue estimates) ; from gnews:7ad5bd51d8d392d6f45c966f 2026-08-19] The author says Jensen Huang set a $1 trillion total-revenue target for 2026–2027 and estimates NVIDIA could generate roughly $400 billion in revenue in FY2027 and $600 billion in FY2028.\n    Quote: Nvidia CEO Jensen Huang has given $1 trillion in total revenue as a target for 2026-2027, implying the company could hit roughly $400 billion in revenue this year and $600 billion in fiscal 2028, which ends in Jan. 2028.\n- e2244 [party_statement; fool.com (Harsh Chauhan); original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:36c5541b78e9258d831613b3 2026-08-21] NVIDIA said its order book for 2026 and 2027 was worth $1 trillion.\n    Quote: The company has already noted that it has an order book worth a whopping $1 trillion for 2026 and 2027 .\n- e2421 [third_party_view; CNBC; original source LSEG analysts ; from gnews:58fb7b4d430994417016dc48 2026-08-25] LSEG analysts expected NVIDIA total revenue to grow 83% to $396 billion in FY2027, then grow 44% in FY2028.\n    Quote: For the full year, analysts expect 83% revenue growth to $396 billion, before slowing to 44% expansion next year, according to LSEG.\n- e2497 [fact; NVIDIA Corporation; original source NVIDIA Corporation 2026-08-26; from nvidianews:nvidia-announces-financial-results-for-second-quarter-fiscal-2027 2026-08-26] NVIDIA reported Q2 FY2027 revenue of $96.2 billion, up 18% quarter over quarter and 106% year over year.\n    Quote: NVIDIA (NASDAQ: NVDA) today reported revenue for the second quarter ended July 26, 2026, of $96.2 billion, up 18% from the previous quarter and up 106% from a year ago.\n- e2499 [party_statement; NVIDIA Corporation; original source NVIDIA Corporation 2026-08-26; from nvidianews:nvidia-announces-financial-results-for-second-quarter-fiscal-2027 2026-08-26] NVIDIA guided Q3 FY2027 revenue to $108.0 billion, plus or minus 2%, assuming no Data Center compute revenue from China.\n    Quote: Revenue is expected to be $108.0 billion, plus or minus 2%. NVIDIA is not assuming any Data Center compute revenue from China in its outlook.\n- e2762 [fact; zacks.com; original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:1de23b28ee2875342ea8aa8a 2026-08-26] NVIDIA reported total revenue of $215.9 billion for FY2026, up 65% from FY2025.\n    Quote: NVIDIA reported revenues of $215.9 billion in fiscal 2026, up 65% from $130.5 billion in fiscal 2025.\n- e2989 [party_statement; fool.com (analyst); original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:7450fcffe5e33aadd1b4a7e9 2026-08-27] NVIDIA expects Q3 revenue of $108 billion; the author says this puts FY2027 revenue on track to exceed $400 billion if Q4 grows further.\n    Quote: In Q3, it expects to generate $108 billion in sales, putting it on track to exceed $400 billion in total revenue in fiscal year 2027, provided Q4 shows further phenomenal growth.\n- e3108 [party_statement; finance.yahoo.com; original source Colette Kress 2026-08-26; from gnews:b39d8c4b1f73b622347a66e4 2026-08-28] CFO Colette Kress guided to 70% growth for NVIDIA revenue for the full fiscal year; the article says this was ahead of analyst estimates.\n    Quote: As if this weren't enough, Kress guided 70% growth for the full fiscal year, which was ahead of analyst estimates.\n- e3110 [third_party_view; InsiderMonkey, recounting Steve Eisman’s remarks on CNBC’s Fast Money; original source Steve Eisman 2026-08-13; from gnews:afc4112f1dd4c804f27af950 2026-08-28] Steve Eisman warned that problems at OpenAI or Anthropic could let cheaper Chinese open-weight models trigger an industry price war.\n    Quote: if something goes wrong at either lab, cheaper Chinese open-weight models could spark a price war across the industry.\n- e3112 [third_party_view; InsiderMonkey, recounting Michael Burry’s view; original source Michael Burry ; from gnews:afc4112f1dd4c804f27af950 2026-08-28] Michael Burry warned that NVIDIA’s reliance on Microsoft, Meta, Amazon, and Alphabet means a pullback by even one could meaningfully hurt revenue.\n    Quote: Michael Burry argues Nvidia's own customer base carries a version of Eisman's concentration risk. He has focused on Nvidia's reliance on a small group of hyperscale customers, including Microsoft, Meta, Amazon, and Alphabet, warning a pullback from even one could meaningfully hurt revenue.\n- e3593 [fact; barchart.com; original source NVIDIA and Amazon Web Services 2026-08-26; from gnews:ad90fe0fa2a105c354a60ac1 2026-08-31] NVIDIA and AWS announced plans on August 26 to deploy 2 million additional NVIDIA GPUs across AWS's global infrastructure during 2027 and 2028.\n    Quote: Nvidia's expanding partnership with Amazon's (AMZN) Amazon Web Services (AWS) also requires continued investment. On Aug. 26, the companies announced plans to deploy 2 million additional Nvidia GPUs across AWS' global infrastructure during 2027 and 2028.\n- e3620 [third_party_view; Zacks.com (analyst); original source Zacks.com 2026-09-01; from gnews:393f041679180b7d31179e8f 2026-09-01] Zacks consensus estimated NVIDIA total revenue growth of 85.4% in FY2027 and 2.2% in FY2028.\n    Quote: The Zacks Consensus Estimate for NVIDIA's fiscal 2027 and 2028 revenues indicates year-over-year growth of 85.4% and 2.2%, respectively.\n- e3683 [party_statement; fool.com (analyst); original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:0295f27f6c1a83738ef035a9 2026-09-01] NVIDIA projected 70% revenue growth in 2027, compared with analysts' 44% forecast.\n    Quote: the company's projection of 70% revenue growth in 2027, smashing analysts' forecast of 44%.\n- e3883 [fact; CNBC; original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:08b1b514a1f4b7067e0dcda4 2026-09-04] NVIDIA reported fiscal second-quarter revenue of $96.2 billion, up 106% year over year.\n    Quote: Nvidia dominates the market for the most advanced chips used for AI, known as graphics processing units (GPUs). Business has boomed as a result.\nThe chip giant has seen its stock increase by 33% over the past 12 months, and its revenue soared 106% to $96.2 billion in its fiscal second quarter.\n- e3942 [fact; The Motley Fool author; original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:89698c9955cf8aa35b642970 2026-09-04] NVIDIA's total revenue grew 106% year over year in FY2027 Q2.\n    Quote: The 106% growth rate in its fiscal 2027 second quarter is a meaningful jump.\n- e3943 [party_statement; The Motley Fool author; original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:89698c9955cf8aa35b642970 2026-09-04] NVIDIA's FY2027 Q3 guidance implied total revenue growth of 12% sequentially at the midpoint.\n    Quote: In the meantime, Nvidia's fiscal 2027 third-quarter guidance implies 12% sequential growth at the midpoint.\n- e4049 [third_party_view; calcalistech.com; original source calcalistech.com 2026-09-06; from gnews:478c25b436f87e8f421d54ed 2026-09-06] The article estimates NVIDIA FY2027 revenue of about $400 billion, up about 85% year over year.\n    Quote: This year, however, revenue is expected to jump to about $400 billion, an increase of about 85% from the previous year.\n- e4164 [fact; David Jagielski, The Motley Fool; original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:5879686876d153edd4342064 2026-09-07] NVIDIA reported revenue of $96.2 billion for the period ended July 26, up 106% year over year, accelerating from 85% growth in the prior quarter.\n    Quote: Nvidia's revenue for its most recent period, which ended on July 26, totaled $96.2 billion -- a whopping 106% increase year over year. That's a significant acceleration from the 85% growth it reported three months earlier.\n- e4165 [third_party_view; The Motley Fool author; original source Analysts ; from gnews:74a1c3178b118f6f10653b76 2026-09-07] Analysts expect NVIDIA revenue and EPS to each grow at a 59% CAGR from FY2026 to FY2029.\n    Quote: From fiscal 2026 (which ended this January) to fiscal 2029, analysts expect its revenue and EPS to both grow at CAGRs of 59%.\n- e4197 [fact; barchart.com (author); original source NVIDIA 2026-08-26; from gnews:17eb5dc32c87ab9926a0bb7c 2026-09-08] NVIDIA reported FY2027 Q2 revenue of $96.22 billion, up 106% year over year, compared with $46.74 billion a year earlier.\n    Quote: In its fiscal 2027 second-quarter results, published on Aug. 26, Nvidia once again delivered extraordinary growth, with revenue soaring 106% year-over-year (YoY) to $96.22 billion, compared with $46.74 billion a year earlier.\n- e4224 [fact; uk.finance.yahoo.com; original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:d9cce6f0416dd0877d8ca961 2026-09-08] NVIDIA's year-on-year revenue growth was 106% in its most recently published quarter.\n    Quote: Not only that, but the year-on-year revenue growth was an incredible 106%.\n- e4226 [party_statement; Jon Quast; original source NVIDIA 2026-08-26; from gnews:b851795e16e32bc54280459b 2026-09-08] NVIDIA guided to 70% revenue growth for the coming year.\n    Quote: It's utterly unbelievable, and then you look at the guidance for the coming year. It's guiding for 70% revenue growth on top of what it's reporting this year.\n- e4227 [third_party_view; Jon Quast; original source Wall Street analysts ; from gnews:b851795e16e32bc54280459b 2026-09-08] Wall Street analysts expected NVIDIA revenue growth of 45% for the coming year, versus NVIDIA's 70% guidance.\n    Quote: Wall Street is expecting 45% growth in the coming year, and VA is saying 70% growth.\n- e966 [third_party_view; Keithen Drury, The Motley Fool; original source Wall Street analysts ; from gnews:cb4d70e84bc7272b22598c93 2026-07-26] Wall Street expects Micron revenue to grow 84% in its fiscal year ending August 2027 and estimates NVIDIA revenue growth of 42% in FY2027.\n    Quote: By the end of fiscal year 2027 (ending August 2027), Wall Street expects an additional 84% growth for Micron, while it estimates only 42% for Nvidia.\n\n linked-1:8777\nlinked-1:8777|1|e693\nThe market reference is NVIDIA management’s FY2027 visibility to “nearly $20 billion,” which sits in rung 3 ($15 billion to $20 billion inclusive).\nApproximately $19 billion in FY2027: management’s period-specific visibility is nearly $20 billion, so I treat it as just below $20 billion; the Wolfe estimate of about $6.5 billion for Vera in calendar 2026 is a product-level check, not total FY2027 CPU revenue.\nNVIDIA’s FY2027 visibility to “nearly $20 billion” is the strongest period-specific forecast and sits in rung 3. Wolfe’s estimate of 1.3 million Vera CPUs at about $5,000 each implies roughly $6.5 billion for that product in calendar 2026, a meaningful downside check but not an estimate of total FY2027 CPU revenue. June deliveries, planned large-quantity OpenAI deployment, and HPE’s planned fall availability support a ramp, while AMD’s modeled throughput comparison is a competitive risk rather than evidence of lost NVIDIA revenue. The resulting estimate remains below rung 2’s $20 billion boundary and above rung 3’s $15 billion floor. It is also consistent with v14’s approximately $387 billion FY2027 total-revenue forecast.\nThe recent evidence adds longer-term CPU-market sizing and a modeled AMD performance comparison, but neither quantifies a change to NVIDIA’s FY2027 CPU revenue outlook. The period-specific “nearly $20 billion” visibility remains the key forecast, so the rung and implied estimate are unchanged.\nUSED ['e443', 'e612', 'e666', 'e668', 'e723', 'e724', 'e794']\nlinked-1:8777|2|e693\nNVIDIA management's FY2027 visibility to “nearly $20 billion” is the available period-specific reference; it sits in rung 3. The separate $20 billion standalone CPU-business target has no stated period.\nApproximately $19 billion of total CPU revenue in FY2027: use management's “nearly $20 billion” period-specific visibility as the forecast anchor, with disclosed Vera shipment economics and deployment evidence supporting scale but not establishing a higher total.\nThe supplied FY2027 reference is NVIDIA management's visibility to “nearly $20 billion” in total CPU revenue, which sits in rung 3 (at least $15 billion and at most $20 billion). The roughly $20 billion standalone-CPU target has no specified period, so it does not override that period-specific reference. Shipments and planned deployments support execution, but the disclosed unit-and-price estimate implies about $6.5 billion for Vera in calendar 2026, not a quantified FY2027 revision to total CPU revenue; competitive and market-size figures likewise do not quantify NVIDIA's FY2027 sales. The implied outcome remains about $19 billion, within rung 3 and below rung 2's strictly greater-than-$20-billion boundary. It is also consistent with dependency v14's approximately $387 billion FY2027 total-revenue forecast.\nThe rung and implied estimate remain about $19 billion, but the new quantified market and competitive context—including BofA's $170 billion 2030 TAM estimate and AMD's modeled throughput comparisons—updates the supporting execution-risk picture without quantifying a FY2027 revenue change or crossing a rung boundary.\nUSED ['e443', 'e612', 'e666', 'e668', 'e723', 'e724', 'e793', 'e794']\nlinked-1:8777|3|e693\nNVIDIA management's “nearly $20 billion” FY2027 total CPU revenue visibility is the market/company reference and sits in rung 3; rung 2 begins above $20 billion.\nApproximately $19 billion in FY2027. Management's period-specific visibility is the strongest forecast input; Wolfe's estimate implies about $6.5 billion for Vera in calendar 2026 (1.3 million × about $5,000), but is a single-product, different-period downside check rather than a replacement estimate for total FY2027 CPU revenue.\nThe reference is NVIDIA management's visibility to “nearly $20 billion” in FY2027 total CPU revenue, which sits in rung 3 (at most $20 billion); the rung 2 boundary is revenue above $20 billion. The strongest estimate remains about $19 billion: the $20 billion target without a period and the $200 billion market opportunity do not establish a higher FY2027 run-rate, while Wolfe's 1.3 million Vera-unit estimate at about $5,000 each implies roughly $6.5 billion for that product in calendar 2026, a material but not directly comparable downside check. June deliveries and OpenAI's planned Q3 deployment support sales, but disclose no volume that would move the forecast above $20 billion. This is consistent with v14's approximately $387 billion FY2027 total-revenue forecast: CPU revenue at about $19 billion is a subset and does not exceed the whole.\nThe implied outcome remains about $19 billion and the key period-specific reference remains “nearly $20 billion.” The additional market and competitive evidence does not quantify a revision to NVIDIA's FY2027 CPU revenue forecast or move the implication across a rung boundary.\nUSED ['e442', 'e443', 'e475', 'e489', 'e584', 'e596', 'e597', 'e612', 'e664', 'e665', 'e666', 'e667', 'e668', 'e676', 'e679', 'e681', 'e689', 'e694', 'e715', 'e723', 'e724', 'e725', 'e793', 'e794', 'e795']\n- e443 [party_statement; trefis.com, quoting NVIDIA management; original source NVIDIA management ; from gnews:48ea00c0bf7c01bdb80850bb 2026-07-15] NVIDIA management said it has visibility to nearly $20 billion in total CPU revenue this year (FY2027).\n    Quote: visibility to nearly $20 billion in total CPU revenue this year.\n- e612 [party_statement; finance.yahoo.com; original source NVIDIA management ; from gnews:c840e055f0c00ff7c6fe18e9 2026-07-20] NVIDIA management is targeting a $20 billion standalone CPU business from its Vera CPU, paired with Rubin GPUs; no target period is specified.\n    Quote: NVIDIA Corporation (NASDAQ:NVDA) itself has charted a path to an entry in the market of CPUs. With the custom ARM-based Vera CPU paired with Rubin GPUs, management is targeting a $20 billion standalone CPU business.\n- e666 [third_party_view; CNBC.com; original source Wolfe Research ; from gnews:69c31079960ffe4f9805834c 2026-07-21] Wolfe Research forecast a Vera CPU average selling price of about $5,000 per chip and shipments of about 1.3 million units in calendar 2026.\n    Quote: Wolfe Research said in May that it expected the average selling price to be about $5,000 per Vera chip, and forecast that Nvidia would ship about 1.3 million of them this year.\n- e667 [third_party_view; CNBC.com; original source Gartner analyst Kevin Knox (market-share figures are reported by CNBC.com) ; from gnews:69c31079960ffe4f9805834c 2026-07-21] Gartner analyst Kevin Knox said AMD was gaining share in server CPUs; AMD reportedly held about 33% of the market and Intel 66.8%.\n    Quote: Gartner analyst Kevin Knox said that AMD is currently the company to beat in enterprise AI server CPUs. AMD reportedly holds about 33% of the market for server CPUs, and Intel has 66.8%, but AMD is gaining share and has deep relationships with hyperscalers.\n- e668 [party_statement; CNBC.com; original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:69c31079960ffe4f9805834c 2026-07-21] NVIDIA said OpenAI planned to deploy Vera CPUs in large quantities starting in calendar Q3 2026.\n    Quote: Coutand said that Vera was in \"early innings\" of adoption. The company didn't list any major cloud service providers except Oracle on its list of partners but said OpenAI plans to deploy Vera chips in large quantities starting this quarter.\n- e689 [third_party_view; Futurum Group; original source NVIDIA; Futurum Group ; from gnews:a957af890352335c5459c36a 2026-07-21] NVIDIA claimed an incremental $200 billion CPU total addressable market; Futurum estimated a 1.9x ratio of CPUs to GPUs by 2030, including host and standalone worker CPUs.\n    Quote: NVIDIA’s claim of an incremental $200 billion in CPU TAM aligns with our view of a 1.9x ratio of CPUs to GPUs by 2030, inclusive of both host CPUs and standalone worker CPUs.\n- e694 [third_party_view; WIRED; original source WIRED 2026-07-21; from gnews:e4b99d721a7a9c46cefa4038 2026-07-21] AMD significantly increased its share of the data-center CPU market over the two years preceding 2026-07-21.\n    Quote: Over the past two years, AMD has significantly grown its share of the market for CPUs used in data centers.\n- e715 [third_party_view; SDxCentral; original source Gartner ; from gnews:2838708eba4178345bbc70e8 2026-07-21] Gartner named AMD the frontrunner in the increasingly diverse enterprise AI server CPU market; no period was specified.\n    Quote: AMD finds itself as the Gartner-crowned frontrunner for an enterprise AI server CPU market that’s becoming increasingly diverse.\n- e723 [fact; qz.com; original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:9ec05dc5e996b554b6f7a5f2 2026-07-21] NVIDIA said it delivered Vera chips to OpenAI, Anthropic, and SpaceX in June 2026.\n    Quote: Nvidia said Vera chips were delivered to clients including OpenAI, Anthropic, and SpaceX in June, according to CNBC .\n- e724 [fact; qz.com; original source Hewlett Packard Enterprise ; from gnews:9ec05dc5e996b554b6f7a5f2 2026-07-21] Hewlett Packard Enterprise unveiled the Vera-powered ProLiant Compute DL394 Gen12 server, with availability expected in fall 2026.\n    Quote: A separate Computex announcement saw Hewlett Packard Enterprise unveil a Vera-powered server , the ProLiant Compute DL394 Gen12, targeted at agentic AI orchestration and reinforcement learning workloads, with availability expected in fall 2026.\n- e794 [third_party_view; Investing.com; original source AMD ; from gnews:55b3b854d30ecb73679c2088 2026-07-22] AMD estimates EPYC 9965 delivers approximately 2.4 times the rack-level throughput of NVIDIA Vera in a modeled 100kW deployment, and projects EPYC 6 at roughly 3.3 times Vera's total throughput.\n    Quote: AMD estimates its EPYC 9965 (Turin) chip delivers approximately 2.4x the rack-level throughput of NVIDIA's Vera baseline in a modeled 100kW deployment, with the next-generation EPYC 6 (Venice) projected to extend that advantage to roughly 3.3x total throughput, according to BofA's characterization of AMD's own estimates.\n- e793 [third_party_view; Investing.com; original source Bank of America Research ; from gnews:55b3b854d30ecb73679c2088 2026-07-22] Bank of America Research forecasts the server CPU total addressable market will reach $170 billion by 2030, roughly four times current levels.\n    Quote: Bank of America Research sees the server CPU total addressable market reaching $170 billion by 2030, roughly four times current levels\n- e442 [party_statement; trefis.com, quoting NVIDIA management; original source NVIDIA management ; from gnews:48ea00c0bf7c01bdb80850bb 2026-07-15] NVIDIA management said Vera CPU opens a $200 billion total addressable market for NVIDIA.\n    Quote: opens a brand new $200 billion TAM for NVIDIA, a market we have never addressed before.\n- e475 [third_party_view; Geoffrey Seiler; original source Geoffrey Seiler 2026-07-16; from gnews:f9a3a3ef9a56e7099140e164 2026-07-16] The author says the data-center GPU-to-CPU ratio, recently about 8:1, is expected to shrink to around 1:1 as agentic AI workloads grow; no period is specified.\n    Quote: Because agentic AI workloads require far more CPUs than other AI processing tasks, the ratio of GPUs to CPUs in data centers -- which was recently about 8 to 1 -- is expected to shrink to around 1 to 1 due to the rise of agentic AI.\n- e489 [fact; Los Alamos National Laboratory; original source Los Alamos National Laboratory 2026-07-16; from gnews:afcb313dd53b5e246ed37d78 2026-07-16] Los Alamos scheduled delivery of the first NVIDIA Vera CPU blade for initial testbed deployment in summer 2026.\n    Quote: The delivery of the first Vera CPU blade for initial testbed deployment is scheduled for this summer at Los Alamos.\n- e584 [party_statement; trefis.com; original source NVIDIA management ; from gnews:d5551a74794d0888bdbf750b 2026-07-17] NVIDIA management said VeraCPU opens a new $200 billion total addressable market for NVIDIA; no period was specified.\n    Quote: management is now pointing to a significant new opportunity, claiming its VeraCPU “opens a brand new $200 billion TAM for NVIDIA.”\n- e596 [third_party_view; fool.com (Geoffrey Seiler); original source Geoffrey Seiler 2026-07-18; from gnews:24a3f9f56a73a4f25edfd458 2026-07-18] The author says the GPU-to-CPU ratio in AI data centers could shift from 8:1 to 1:1 as cloud companies build agentic-AI infrastructure.\n    Quote: The GPU-to-CPU ratio in AI data centers built when workloads were primarily driven by training was 8 to 1. As cloud companies build out infrastructure for agentic AI, the prediction is that the ratio could shift to 1 to 1.\n- e597 [party_statement; fool.com (Geoffrey Seiler); original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:24a3f9f56a73a4f25edfd458 2026-07-18] NVIDIA projected that the data-center CPU market could reach $200 billion in the next few years.\n    Quote: With that in mind, Nvidia has projected that the data center CPU market could reach a value of $200 billion in the next few years.\n- e664 [party_statement; CNBC.com; original source NVIDIA representatives ; from gnews:69c31079960ffe4f9805834c 2026-07-21] NVIDIA representatives said Vera CPUs were delivered to OpenAI, Anthropic and SpaceX in June 2026.\n    Quote: Nvidia representatives said Vera chips were delivered to clients, including OpenAI , Anthropic , and SpaceX , in June.\n- e665 [third_party_view; CNBC.com; original source NVIDIA and Bernstein ; from gnews:69c31079960ffe4f9805834c 2026-07-21] NVIDIA said the server CPU market could eventually be worth $200 billion; Bernstein estimated the mature server CPU market at about $37 billion in 2025.\n    Quote: Nvidia said the whole server CPU market could eventually be worth $200 billion, while a Bernstein estimate from earlier this year said the mature server CPU market in total was worth about $37 billion in 2025.\n- e676 [third_party_view; Jake Roach, Tom's Hardware; original source Morgan Stanley and Bank of America ; from gnews:460090b9c71947edd68a13f4 2026-07-21] Morgan Stanley and Bank of America suggest the server CPU market could double in size or grow more by 2030.\n    Quote: Some financial institutions (including Morgan Stanley and Bank of America) suggest the server CPU market could double in size (or grow even larger) by 2030.\n- e679 [third_party_view; Zeus Kerravala, principal analyst at ZK Research, writing for SiliconANGLE; original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:d0e8d1cf9a44e04c28dedfee 2026-07-21] NVIDIA’s own sizing indicates a large incremental CPU opportunity tied to AI workloads and infrastructure.\n    Quote: The company’s own sizing suggests a large incremental CPU opportunity tied specifically to AI-driven workloads and infrastructure patterns.\n- e681 [party_statement; trefis.com analyst, quoting NVIDIA management; original source NVIDIA management ; from gnews:53839087525ebaad78aef90b 2026-07-21] NVIDIA management said the Vera CPU opens a $200 billion total addressable market for NVIDIA; no period was specified.\n    Quote: Management is now pointing to a new opportunity with its Vera CPU, which it says “opens a brand new $200 billion TAM for NVIDIA.”\n- e725 [party_statement; qz.com; original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:9ec05dc5e996b554b6f7a5f2 2026-07-21] NVIDIA estimated that the total server CPU market could eventually reach $200 billion.\n    Quote: Nvidia estimated the total server CPU market could eventually reach $200 billion.\n- e795 [third_party_view; Vivek Arya, quoted by Investing.com; original source Bank of America Research analyst Vivek Arya ; from gnews:55b3b854d30ecb73679c2088 2026-07-22] BofA expects AMD's AI 2026 Day on Thursday to focus less on benchmark comparisons and more on establishing the industry's preferred metric for AI infrastructure performance.\n    Quote: Our expectation is that AMD's Thursday event will be less about benchmark comparisons and more about establishing the industry's preferred metric\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
