{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_15","type":"command_execution","command":"/opt/homebrew/bin/zsh -lc \"python3 -c 'import json\np=json.load(open(\\\"poc/data/citation-probe-2026-11-22/packet-C.json\\\"))\nimport sys,re\nfor n in map(int,sys.argv[1:]):\n x=p[n]\n print(\\\"\\\\n\\\"+x[\\\"key\\\"],x[\\\"question\\\"])\n used={}\n for arm in (\\\"v12\\\",\\\"v7\\\"):\n  for idx,a in enumerate(x[arm],1):\n   print(arm,idx,\\\"rung\\\",a[\\\"rung\\\"],\\\"changed\\\",a[\\\"changed\\\"])\n   for k in (\\\"reference\\\",\\\"implied_outcome\\\",\\\"reasons\\\",\\\"why_changed\\\"): print(a[k])\n   print(\\\"cite\\\",\\\",\\\".join(a[\\\"cites\\\"]),\\\"dependencies\\\",\\\",\\\".join(a[\\\"cites_views\\\"]),\\\"themes\\\",\\\",\\\".join(y[\\\"view_id\\\"]+\\\":\\\"+y[\\\"push\\\"].replace(\\\"toward_\\\",\\\"\\\") for y in a[\\\"theme_pushes\\\"]))\n   ps={k:[] for k in (\\\"toward_first\\\",\\\"toward_last\\\",\\\"none\\\")}\n   for y in a[\\\"pushes\\\"]:ps[y[\\\"push\\\"]].append(y[\\\"evidence_id\\\"])\n   print(\\\"push\\\",*(k.replace(\\\"toward_\\\",\\\"\\\")+\\\"=\\\"+\\\",\\\".join(v) for k,v in ps.items()))\n   for t in {*a[\\\"cites\\\"],*a[\\\"cites_views\\\"],*(y[\\\"evidence_id\\\"] for y in a[\\\"pushes\\\"]),*(y[\\\"view_id\\\"] for y in a[\\\"theme_pushes\\\"])}:used.setdefault(t,[]).append((\\\"N\\\" if arm==\\\"v12\\\" else \\\"O\\\")+str(idx))\n print(\\\"SOURCES\\\")\n for k in sorted(used,key=lambda k:(k[0],int(k[1:]) if k[1:].isdigit() else 99999)):\n  v=x[\\\"quotes\\\"].get(k,\\\"MISSING\\\")\n  print(k,\\\"\\\".join(used[k]),re.sub(r\\\"\"'^- '\"\\\\S+ \\\\[[\"'^'\"\\\\n]+?\\\\] \\\",\\\"\\\",v.split(\\\"\\\\n\\\",1)[0]))\n' 2 3\"","aggregated_output":"\nold-2:518 Thesis v7 (NVDA, asset, focus \"Whether NVIDIA launches the Kyber rack-scale architecture for Rubin Ultra by the end of 2027.\", 2027, due 2027-12-31): Will NVIDIA launch its Kyber rack-scale architecture by 2027-12-31?\nv12 1 rung 2 changed False\nNVIDIA’s stated roadmap places the 2027 launch plan at rung 1 (Yes); no separate market consensus is supplied.\nThe most specific schedule evidence implies a Kyber NVL144 launch in 2028, beyond the 2027-12-31 deadline. NVIDIA’s 2027 NVL576 plan is counterevidence, but it does not specify a launch date that displaces the reported NVL144 delay.\nThe reference is NVIDIA’s stated 2027 roadmap, which sits at rung 1 (Yes); no separate consensus is supplied. The specific report that Kyber NVL144 is delayed to 2028 because of midplane manufacturability problems points past the deadline and outweighs the company’s general roadmap denial and its 2027 NVL576 plan. Those statements are meaningful counterevidence, but the plan does not give a concrete launch schedule that resolves the reported constraint. The forecast is therefore rung 2, without treating the delay as established fact.\nThe rung and implied schedule remain as in the previous conclusion. The recent items mostly repeat the existing delay report or NVIDIA’s roadmap response; e148 reiterates the 2027 NVL576 plan already considered, and no new quantified schedule or resolution of the reported manufacturing constraint changes the forecast.\ncite e40,e41,e42,e43,e44,e45,e55,e71,e85,e87,e92,e99,e103,e110,e111,e120,e121,e147,e148,e167,e168,e174,e214,e215,e250,e251 dependencies  themes \npush first=e40,e55,e111,e121,e148,e167,e168,e174,e214,e251 last=e41,e42,e43,e44,e45,e71,e85,e87,e92,e99,e103,e110,e120,e147,e250 none=e215\nv12 2 rung 2 changed True\nNVIDIA’s stated 2027 roadmap places the reference at rung 1 (Yes).\nA launch in 2028 is more likely than one by 2027: SemiAnalysis reported Kyber NVL144 delayed to 2028, citing PCB-midplane manufacturability. NVIDIA’s 2027 NVL576 plan is significant counterevidence but does not specifically resolve that reported delay.\nThe reference is NVIDIA’s stated 2027 roadmap, which sits at rung 1 (Yes). The more likely outcome remains a launch after the deadline: SemiAnalysis specifically reported Kyber NVL144 delayed to 2028 because the PCB midplane is difficult to manufacture. NVIDIA’s more specific statement that Vera Rubin Ultra is planned to scale to an NVL576 domain in 2027, and the stated 2027 goal of up to 576 processors, strengthen the on-time case; however, they do not resolve the reported Kyber manufacturing delay. Reports that NVL576 could also be delayed or limited to small volumes add risk, though limited volume alone would not mean no launch. On balance, the evidence implies a 2028 launch, so it remains below the binary rung boundary for a 2027 launch.\nThe rung is unchanged, but NVIDIA’s specific statement that the NVL576 domain remains planned for 2027 adds a material on-time counterweight to the prior conclusion. The specific manufacturing-delay report still makes a post-deadline launch the more likely outcome.\ncite e40,e41,e42,e43,e44,e45,e71,e87,e92,e99,e103,e110,e148,e168,e174 dependencies  themes v5:first,v6:first\npush first=e40,e148,e168,e174 last=e41,e42,e43,e44,e45,e71,e85,e87,e92,e99,e110 none=e55,e103,e111,e120,e121,e147,e167,e214,e215,e250,e251\nv12 3 rung 2 changed False\nNVIDIA’s stated 2027 roadmap places the expected outcome at rung 1 (Yes).\nA launch after 2027, most likely in 2028: SemiAnalysis’s specific report says Kyber NVL144 is delayed by more than 12 months to 2028 due to midplane manufacturability, while the reported NVL576 constraints reinforce execution risk. NVIDIA’s 2027 NVL576 plan is counterevidence, but remains a roadmap target rather than demonstrated volume or a resolved schedule.\nThe reference is NVIDIA’s stated 2027 roadmap, which sits at rung 1 (Yes). The best specific contrary forecast remains that Kyber NVL144 is delayed to 2028 because of midplane manufacturability problems; reports of a delayed or constrained NVL576 system and the scrapped stopgap reinforce the execution risk. NVIDIA’s explicit statement that Rubin Ultra is planned to scale to an NVL576 domain in 2027 is meaningful counterevidence, but it is a future roadmap target, not evidence that Kyber has shipped or that the reported manufacturing constraint is resolved. On balance, the evidence still makes a post-deadline launch more likely.\nThe rung and the key forecast remain unchanged from the previous judgement: the specific 2028 delay report still outweighs the 2027 roadmap target. The later items add repetitions or broad, non-specific commentary, not a new fact that changes the implied launch timing.\ncite e41,e42,e43,e44,e45,e71,e85,e87,e99,e148,e174 dependencies  themes v5:first,v6:first\npush first=e40,e148,e174 last=e41,e42,e43,e44,e45,e71,e85,e87,e99 none=e55,e92,e103,e110,e111,e120,e121,e147,e167,e168,e214,e215,e250,e251\nv7 1 rung 2 changed False\nNVIDIA’s stated 2027 roadmap places the reference at rung 1 (Yes).\nThe most specific schedule in the evidence is a delay of more than 12 months, to 2028, due to midplane manufacturability problems; that implies no launch by 2027-12-31 and sits at rung 2. NVIDIA’s 2027 roadmap statements are counterevidence but do not quantify a revised schedule or resolve the reported constraint.\nThe reference is NVIDIA’s stated 2027 roadmap, which sits at rung 1 (Yes). The forecast remains rung 2: the specific report that Kyber NVL144 is delayed to 2028 because of midplane manufacturability issues implies a post-deadline launch, while NVIDIA’s roadmap denial and 2027 NVL576 statement are counterevidence but do not provide a more specific schedule or resolve that constraint. The recent items repeat claims already in force; they do not change the balance of evidence or establish that a launch has occurred.\nThe rung and its key supporting facts are unchanged. The recent delay reports and NVIDIA responses repeat claims already in force, and no new schedule or manufacturing resolution is supplied.\ncite e41,e42,e40,e148,e174 dependencies  themes v5:first,v6:first\npush first= last= none=e40,e41,e42,e43,e44,e45,e55,e71,e85,e87,e92,e99,e103,e110,e111,e120,e121,e147,e148,e167,e168,e174,e214,e215,e250,e251\nv7 2 rung 2 changed True\nNVIDIA’s stated 2027 roadmap places the reference at rung 1 (Yes).\nThe specific SemiAnalysis report says Kyber NVL144 is delayed to 2028; I therefore imply a 2028 launch, after the 2027-12-31 deadline. NVIDIA’s planned 2027 NVL576 timeline is counterevidence, but does not establish that the manufacturing issue is resolved or that the launch occurs by the deadline.\nThe reference is NVIDIA’s stated 2027 roadmap, which sits at rung 1 (Yes). The more specific report that Kyber NVL144 is delayed to 2028 because of midplane manufacturing difficulty, alongside the report that NVL576 may also be delayed or limited, still makes a post-deadline launch more likely. NVIDIA’s explicit statement that Vera Rubin Ultra is planned to scale to NVL576 in 2027 is meaningful counterevidence, but it remains a plan and does not resolve the reported manufacturing constraint; the implied date therefore remains 2028, crossing the boundary to rung 2.\nThe rung remains 2, but new evidence adds NVIDIA’s specific statement that Rubin Ultra is planned to scale to NVL576 in 2027 (e148), and the 2027 scale-up plan is also reported in e174. These strengthen the on-time case without outweighing the specific reported manufacturing delay.\ncite e40,e41,e42,e43,e44,e45,e55,e71,e85,e87,e92,e99,e103,e110,e111,e120,e121,e147,e148,e167,e168,e174,e214,e215,e250,e251 dependencies  themes v5:first,v6:first\npush first=e40,e55,e111,e121,e148,e167,e168,e174,e214,e251 last=e41,e42,e43,e44,e45,e71,e85,e87,e92,e99,e103,e110,e120,e147,e250 none=e215\nv7 3 rung 2 changed False\nNVIDIA’s stated roadmap places a Kyber launch in 2027, which is rung 1 (Yes).\nThe specific SemiAnalysis report implies a Kyber NVL144 launch in 2028, more than 12 months later than the previously slated 2027 timing, so the implied outcome is after 2027-12-31 (rung 2). NVIDIA’s stated 2027 NVL576 plan is a counterpoint, but does not quantify a resolution to the reported NVL144 midplane manufacturing issue.\nThe reference is NVIDIA’s stated 2027 roadmap, which sits at rung 1 (Yes). The forecast remains rung 2: the specific report that Kyber NVL144 is delayed to 2028 because its PCB midplane is difficult to manufacture points past the deadline. NVIDIA’s reaffirmation of its roadmap and its stated 2027 NVL576 plan are counterevidence, but do not resolve that reported manufacturing constraint; the additional scale-up and design risks reinforce the downside. The latest items largely repeat claims already reflected in the previous conclusion, so they do not change the most likely outcome.\nThe rung and key supporting facts are unchanged: the reported 2028 Kyber delay and manufacturing constraint remain opposed by NVIDIA’s 2027 roadmap statement. The new items are repetitions or restatements of claims already reflected in the previous conclusion.\ncite e41,e42,e43,e44,e71,e103,e148,e174 dependencies  themes \npush first=e40,e148,e168,e174 last=e41,e42,e43,e44,e45,e71,e85,e87,e92,e99,e103 none=e55,e110,e111,e120,e121,e147,e167,e214,e215,e250,e251\nSOURCES\ne40 N1N2N3O1O2O3 NVIDIA said its product roadmap remains intact, rejecting SemiAnalysis's report of a Kyber delay.\ne41 N1N2N3O1O2O3 SemiAnalysis reported that NVIDIA's Kyber rack-scale architecture for Rubin Ultra, previously slated for 2027, is delayed by more than 12 months to 2028.\ne42 N1N2N3O1O2O3 SemiAnalysis said NVIDIA's Kyber NVL144 rack architecture is delayed to 2028 because its PCB midplane is difficult to manufacture.\ne43 N1N2N3O1O2O3 SemiAnalysis said NVIDIA's larger NVL576 system is likely to be delayed or produced in small volumes.\ne44 N1N2N3O1O2O3 SemiAnalysis reported that NVIDIA scrapped a backup plan to bolt together two current-generation racks after cloud providers and hyperscalers objected to the design and operating burden.\ne45 N1N2N3O1O2O3 SemiAnalysis said NVIDIA had no proven way to expand Rubin Ultra's scale-up capacity and predicted this could create a technical opening for AMD and Google's chips in the high-end market.\ne55 N1N2N3O1O2O3 NVIDIA told media outlets that its product roadmap remained intact, rejecting the reported claims of delays and a scrapped rack design.\ne71 N1N2N3O1O2O3 SemiAnalysis had reported that NVIDIA canceled the four-die Rubin Ultra design in favor of a two-chiplet design because of manufacturing execution concerns.\ne85 N1N2N3O1O2O3 SemiAnalysis reported that NVIDIA's CPO-NVSwitch interconnect will not arrive until the Feynman generation, the generation after next.\ne87 N1N2N3O1O2O3 SemiAnalysis reported that NVIDIA plans to offset the shortfall by selling more Oberon-Rubin racks in the existing form factor.\ne92 N1N2N3O1O2O3 Analysts cited by Bloomberg said the reported technical setbacks could severely limit the scale-up capacity of NVIDIA's future hardware.\ne99 N1N2N3O1O2O3 SemiAnalysis reportedly said NVIDIA abandoned the NVL72x2 stopgap rack, leaving no proven alternative to expand Rubin Ultra scale-up in 2027.\ne103 N1N2N3O1O2O3 SemiAnalysis said a fully production-ready co-packaged optics NVSwitch would not arrive before the Feynman generation after Rubin, leaving copper as the near-term rack-scale linking solution for Rubin Ultra.\ne110 N1N2N3O1O2O3 SemiAnalysis reported that NVIDIA was at least a year behind on manufacturing its next-generation server racks.\ne111 N1N2N3O1O2O3 NVIDIA disputed SemiAnalysis's report that its next-generation Kyber server rack was facing delays.\ne120 N1N2N3O1O2O3 SemiAnalysis reported that NVIDIA's Kyber NVL144 launch has been delayed from 2027 to 2028.\ne121 N1N2N3O1O2O3 An NVIDIA spokesperson said the company's roadmap was intact, disputing the report of a Kyber delay.\ne147 N1N2N3O1O2O3 SemiAnalysis said NVIDIA’s Kyber NVL144 rack system had hit manufacturing setbacks; Futuriom said the setbacks could encourage NVIDIA to use its credit and revenue-sharing model to support demand.\ne148 N1N2N3O1O2O3 NVIDIA said its roadmap remains intact and Vera Rubin Ultra is planned to scale up to an NVL576 domain in 2027.\ne167 N1N2N3O1O2O3 NVIDIA denied reports that Kyber could be delayed until 2028, saying its roadmap remains on track.\ne168 N1N2N3O1O2O3 Kyber, designed for the Rubin Ultra platform, is expected to launch in 2027 as an integrated AI infrastructure system.\ne174 N1N2N3O1O2O3 NVIDIA plans to increase the number of processors in a single interconnected system from 72 GPUs to as many as 576 by 2027.\ne214 N1N2N3O1O2O3 NVIDIA denied reports that its Kyber platform is being delayed; no date or further detail was given.\ne215 N1N2N3O1O2O3 Semi Analysis said NVIDIA’s product schedule could be delayed broadly because one part of its business is late; the item gives no specific product or timing.\ne250 N1N2N3O1O2O3 SemiAnalysis reported that NVIDIA's Kyber NVL144 rack system could be delayed until 2028 because of manufacturing problems with a crucial circuit board.\ne251 N1N2N3O1O2O3 NVIDIA said its product roadmap remains intact despite questions about a possible Kyber NVL144 delay.\nv5 N2N3O1O2 - v5 (ai-capex, due 2027-03-31) How much will the four largest AI hyperscalers spend on data-center capital expenditures in calendar 2026?: now rung 3 $600 billion to below $700 billion; judged 2026-07-10; reasons: The market reference is around $650 billion for 2026, which sits in rung 3 ($600 billion to below $700 billion). The evidence still implies about $650 billion: the 2026 estimates and NVIDIA management's cited projection point to that level, while the power-constraint view gives no quantified reducti\nv6 N2N3O1O2 - v6 (ai-capex, due 2028-03-31) How much will the four largest AI hyperscalers spend on data-center capital expenditures in calendar 2027?: now rung 3 $900 billion to below $1.1 trillion; judged 2026-07-12; reasons: The reference is NVIDIA management’s forecast of more than $1 trillion for 2027, which sits in rung 3. The evidence still implies spending around, and modestly above, $1 trillion: it does not quantify a move to the $1.1 trillion boundary, while the stated power constraint is downside pressure withou\n\nlinked-1:8834 Thesis v16 (NVDA, asset, focus \"NVIDIA total CPU revenue in FY2027\", FY2027, due 2027-02-28): How much total CPU revenue will NVIDIA generate in FY2027?\nv12 1 rung 3 changed False\nNVIDIA management’s visibility to nearly $20 billion in total CPU revenue for FY2027 is the supplied reference; it maps to rung 3 ($15–20 billion inclusive). No separate market consensus is supplied.\nAbout $19 billion for FY2027: treat management’s nearly-$20-billion visibility as the leading forecast input, discounted slightly for execution and competition. Wolfe Research’s 1.3 million units at about $5,000 imply about $6.5 billion for calendar 2026, but that is a different period and is not carried forward flat.\nThe reference is NVIDIA management’s visibility to nearly $20 billion of total CPU revenue in FY2027, which sits in rung 3; no separate market consensus is supplied. I forecast about $19 billion: the nearly-$20-billion visibility is the strongest FY2027 input, with June deliveries and OpenAI’s planned large Q3 deployment supporting substantial sales. Wolfe Research’s roughly 1.3 million units at about $5,000 imply about $6.5 billion for calendar 2026, so the FY2027 forecast requires a meaningful ramp; AMD’s quantified performance comparisons and established server-CPU position temper that ramp but do not quantify a reduction sufficient to move the estimate below $15 billion. The implied figure remains below the greater-than-$20-billion boundary for rung 2. It is also comfortably below dependent thesis v14’s roughly $390–395 billion forecast for NVIDIA’s total FY2027 revenue, as a component must be below the whole. The repeated $20 billion and $200 billion claims add no new sizing.\nThe forecast remains about $19 billion and in rung 3. The additional outlet repetitions do not add new sizing, and the competitive evidence does not quantify a change to the FY2027 revenue forecast or move it across a rung boundary.\ncite e443,e489,e612,e666,e667,e668,e715,e723,e724,e793,e794,e813,e814,e884,e885,e886,e887,e896,e897,e1018,e1338,e1473 dependencies v14 themes v5:first,v6:first\npush first=e489,e612,e664,e668,e724,e793,e896,e897,e1018 last=e666,e667,e694,e715,e794,e814,e884,e885,e886,e887 none=e443,e723,e813,e1338,e1473\nv12 2 rung 3 changed True\nNVIDIA management's visibility to nearly $20 billion in total CPU revenue for FY2027 is the relevant market/company reference and sits in rung 3 ($15 billion to $20 billion, inclusive).\nApproximately $19 billion in FY2027: management's near-$20 billion visibility, clarified as standalone CPU revenue, is the strongest direct forecast, with deliveries and planned deployment supportive but competition and Wolfe's roughly $6.5 billion calendar-2026 shipment-value estimate tempering the estimate. That remains at or below $20 billion, not above the rung-2 boundary.\nThe reference is NVIDIA's visibility to nearly $20 billion of total CPU revenue in FY2027, which sits in rung 3 ($15 billion to $20 billion, inclusive); management clarified that this is standalone CPU revenue. The evidence still implies approximately $19 billion, rather than a figure above $20 billion: deliveries and OpenAI's planned large-scale deployment support sales, while Wolfe's 1.3 million units at about $5,000 each imply roughly $6.5 billion for calendar 2026 and AMD's newly reported performance advantages add execution risk without quantifying a revenue reduction. The AMD comparisons are not uniformly like-for-like, and none outweighs management's direct visibility enough to cross the rung boundary. This remains consistent with v14: CPU revenue is a component of, and far below, its roughly $390–395 billion FY2027 total-revenue forecast.\nThe rung and central implied estimate remain approximately $19 billion, but the newer, more specific AMD performance comparisons sharpen the competitive-risk evidence supporting that estimate; they do not quantify a reduction large enough to change the rung.\ncite e896,e897,e813,e666,e668,e723,e724,e1018,e884,e885,e886,e887 dependencies v14 themes v5:first,v6:first\npush first=e896,e897,e813,e668,e723,e724,e1018 last=e666,e884,e885,e886,e887 none=\nv12 3 rung 3 changed False\nNo separate market consensus is supplied. NVIDIA management's visibility to nearly $20 billion in FY2027 is the reference, and it sits in rung 3 ($15 billion to $20 billion inclusive).\nRoughly $19–20 billion in FY2027. Management's direct visibility anchors the forecast just below $20 billion; the Wolfe estimate of about 1.3 million Vera units at about $5,000 each implies roughly $6.5 billion for calendar 2026, while deliveries and planned Q3 volume support further ramp-up without establishing revenue above $20 billion.\nThe reference is NVIDIA management's visibility to nearly $20 billion of FY2027 total CPU revenue, which sits in rung 3. The implied outcome remains roughly $19–20 billion: the direct FY2027 visibility and disclosed standalone-CPU clarification outweigh the lower, narrower Wolfe estimate of about $6.5 billion for Vera shipments in calendar 2026, while announced deliveries and planned large Q3 deployment support meaningful ramp-up. AMD's competitive claims and the early deployment timeline are execution risks, but they do not quantify a reduction that moves the forecast below $15 billion; nor does the evidence support revenue above $20 billion, the boundary for rung 2. This remains consistent with v14's roughly $390–395 billion FY2027 total-revenue forecast, since CPU revenue is only a component of that total.\nThe key quantified FY2027 input remains management's nearly $20 billion visibility; the additional estimates and competitive evidence do not change the implied figure or cross a rung boundary.\ncite e443,e489,e612,e666,e667,e668,e694,e715,e723,e724,e794,e813,e814,e884,e885,e886,e887,e896,e897,e1018,e1338 dependencies v14 themes v5:first,v6:first\npush first=e443,e612,e668,e723,e813,e896,e897,e1018,e1338 last=e489,e666,e667,e694,e715,e724,e794,e814,e884,e885,e886,e887 none=\nv7 1 rung 3 changed True\nNo separate market consensus is supplied. NVIDIA management’s visibility to nearly $20 billion in total CPU revenue for FY2027 is the relevant reference, and it sits in rung 3 ($15 billion to $20 billion inclusive).\nApproximately $19–20 billion of FY2027 total CPU revenue, with the central implication just below $20 billion: management’s direct visibility anchors the estimate, while the undated $20 billion target is discounted and the Vera-only $6.5 billion shipment estimate is not treated as total CPU revenue.\nThe reference is NVIDIA management’s visibility to nearly $20 billion of total CPU revenue in FY2027; there is no separate market-consensus figure, and nearly $20 billion sits in rung 3. The July disclosures of an undated $20 billion standalone-CPU target and a $20 billion 2026 anticipation do not establish revenue above $20 billion; the target without a period is discounted, and management’s visibility remains the stronger FY2027 input. Wolfe’s 1.3 million Vera units at about $5,000 imply roughly $6.5 billion for calendar 2026, but that is a Vera-only estimate, not total CPU revenue, and OpenAI’s planned Q3 deployment supports ramping sales. AMD’s newly quantified performance comparisons add execution risk, but do not quantify a revenue reduction sufficient to move the forecast below the near-$20 billion reference. The implied result remains just under $20 billion, consistent with dependent thesis v14’s roughly $390–395 billion total-revenue forecast: CPU revenue is a component of, and cannot exceed, that total.\nThe rung and near-$20 billion forecast are unchanged, but the newly supplied AMD comparisons add specific performance figures to the competitive-risk case; they sharpen a supporting fact without establishing a quantified FY2027 revenue reduction.\ncite e896,e897,e612,e666,e668,e723,e724,e884,e885,e887,e1018 dependencies v14 themes v5:first,v6:first\npush first=e896,e897,e612,e668,e723,e724,e1018 last=e666,e884,e885,e887 none=\nv7 2 rung 3 changed True\nNo independent market consensus is supplied. NVIDIA management’s visibility to nearly $20 billion of total CPU revenue in FY2027 is the operative reference and sits in rung 3 ($15–20 billion, inclusive).\nRoughly $19–20 billion in FY2027, centered near $19 billion. This follows NVIDIA’s direct visibility statement; Wolfe’s 1.3 million-unit estimate at about $5,000 implies about $6.5 billion for calendar 2026, while the remaining launch and competition evidence does not quantify a different FY2027 total.\nThe reference is NVIDIA management’s visibility to nearly $20 billion of total CPU revenue in FY2027, which sits in rung 3; no separate market consensus is supplied. I infer roughly $19–20 billion, centered near $19 billion: the direct FY2027 visibility is the strongest period-specific forecast, while Wolfe’s 1.3 million-unit estimate at about $5,000 implies approximately $6.5 billion for calendar 2026 and the competitive evidence adds execution risk without quantifying a reduction to the FY2027 figure. The implied outcome remains at or below $20 billion, so it does not cross into rung 2. NVIDIA’s FY2027 total-revenue forecast in v14 is about $390–395 billion, comfortably above this CPU component and consistent with the dependency. The newer AMD comparisons sharpen the competitive risk, but do not change the implied figure enough to move the rung.\nThe rung and implied figure remain around $19–20 billion, but the new quantified AMD performance comparisons (e884–e887) sharpen the competitive-risk facts supporting that forecast. They do not translate into a quantified revenue reduction that outweighs NVIDIA’s FY2027 visibility.\ncite e443,e489,e612,e664,e666,e667,e668,e694,e715,e723,e724,e793,e794,e813,e814,e884,e885,e886,e887,e896,e897,e1018,e1338,e1473 dependencies v14 themes \npush first=e443,e489,e612,e664,e668,e723,e724,e793,e813,e896,e897,e1018 last=e666,e667,e694,e715,e794,e814,e884,e885,e886,e887 none=e1338,e1473\nv7 3 rung 3 changed True\nNo separate market consensus is supplied. NVIDIA management’s visibility to nearly $20 billion of total CPU revenue in FY2027 is the operative reference; nearly $20 billion sits in rung 3 ($15 billion to $20 billion inclusive).\nAbout $19 billion, near but not above $20 billion. This follows management’s period-specific FY2027 visibility, with customer deployment evidence supporting meaningful sales; the 1.3 million-unit estimate at about $5,000 implies about $6.5 billion for calendar 2026, but is a third-party estimate for a different period rather than a basis to replace the FY2027 visibility.\nThe reference is NVIDIA management’s visibility to nearly $20 billion of total CPU revenue in FY2027, which sits in rung 3; no separate market consensus is supplied. The evidence still implies revenue around $19 billion, near but not above $20 billion: management’s FY2027 visibility is the most direct period-specific input, and OpenAI’s planned large-quantity deployment and customer deliveries support it. Wolfe’s estimate of 1.3 million chips at about $5,000 each implies about $6.5 billion for calendar 2026, but it is a third-party estimate for a different period and does not overturn the FY2027 visibility. NVIDIA’s $20 billion targets and expanding-market evidence support the upside, while the AMD performance and share evidence adds execution risk without quantifying a reduction to the FY2027 forecast. The implied figure remains at or below rung 3’s $20 billion ceiling. This is consistent with dependency v14: CPU revenue is a component of NVIDIA’s roughly $390–395 billion FY2027 total-revenue forecast.\nThe rung remains 3, but the evidence in view now includes NVIDIA’s stated large-quantity OpenAI deployment plan, reported customer deliveries, a quantified third-party shipment/ASP estimate, and new AMD competitive-performance comparisons. These change the supporting evidence and risk picture, without moving the implied FY2027 figure across a rung boundary.\ncite e443,e489,e612,e664,e666,e667,e668,e694,e715,e723,e724,e793,e794,e813,e814,e884,e885,e886,e887,e896,e897,e1018,e1338,e1473 dependencies v14 themes v5:first,v6:first\npush first=e443,e489,e612,e664,e668,e724,e793,e813,e896,e897,e1018,e1338,e1473 last=e666,e667,e694,e715,e794,e814,e884,e885,e886,e887 none=e723\nSOURCES\ne443 N1N3O2O3 NVIDIA management said it has visibility to nearly $20 billion in total CPU revenue this year (FY2027).\ne489 N1N3O2O3 Los Alamos scheduled delivery of the first NVIDIA Vera CPU blade for initial testbed deployment in summer 2026.\ne612 N1N3O1O2O3 NVIDIA management is targeting a $20 billion standalone CPU business from its Vera CPU, paired with Rubin GPUs; no target period is specified.\ne664 N1O2O3 NVIDIA representatives said Vera CPUs were delivered to OpenAI, Anthropic and SpaceX in June 2026.\ne666 N1N2N3O1O2O3 Wolfe Research forecast a Vera CPU average selling price of about $5,000 per chip and shipments of about 1.3 million units in calendar 2026.\ne667 N1N3O2O3 Gartner analyst Kevin Knox said AMD was gaining share in server CPUs; AMD reportedly held about 33% of the market and Intel 66.8%.\ne668 N1N2N3O1O2O3 NVIDIA said OpenAI planned to deploy Vera CPUs in large quantities starting in calendar Q3 2026.\ne694 N1N3O2O3 AMD significantly increased its share of the data-center CPU market over the two years preceding 2026-07-21.\ne715 N1N3O2O3 Gartner named AMD the frontrunner in the increasingly diverse enterprise AI server CPU market; no period was specified.\ne723 N1N2N3O1O2O3 NVIDIA said it delivered Vera chips to OpenAI, Anthropic, and SpaceX in June 2026.\ne724 N1N2N3O1O2O3 Hewlett Packard Enterprise unveiled the Vera-powered ProLiant Compute DL394 Gen12 server, with availability expected in fall 2026.\ne793 N1O2O3 Bank of America Research forecasts the server CPU total addressable market will reach $170 billion by 2030, roughly four times current levels.\ne794 N1N3O2O3 AMD estimates EPYC 9965 delivers approximately 2.4 times the rack-level throughput of NVIDIA Vera in a modeled 100kW deployment, and projects EPYC 6 at roughly 3.3 times Vera's total throughput.\ne813 N1N2N3O2O3 NVIDIA is anticipating $20 billion of stand-alone Vera server CPU revenue in 2026.\ne814 N1N3O2O3 Mercury Research estimates Intel held just over two-thirds of the server CPU market in Q1 2026.\ne884 N1N2N3O1O2O3 AMD corporate VP Ravi Kuppuswany said AMD’s Venice CPUs showed a 20% per-core performance advantage over NVIDIA’s Vera CPU in AMD’s estimated SPEC comparison, with tuning unfinished.\ne885 N1N2N3O1O2O3 AMD’s slide claimed Venice had 2.2 times Vera’s throughput in a 2P comparison; the article says the throughput comparison used a 256-core Epyc 9996 against Vera’s 88-core configuration.\ne886 N1N2N3O2O3 NVIDIA’s published SPEC results gave Vera an overall score of 925, while AMD said its 96-core High Frequency Epyc achieved 1,210 in its estimated comparison.\ne887 N1N2N3O1O2O3 The article calculated that AMD’s per-core comparison implied about an 18.8% lead over Vera, based on approximately 6.3 versus 5.3 per-core scores in 2P configurations.\ne896 N1N2N3O1O2O3 NVIDIA CFO Colette Kress said Vera opens a $200 billion market for NVIDIA and that the company has visibility to nearly $20 billion in total CPU revenue this year, FY2027.\ne897 N1N2N3O1O2O3 NVIDIA CEO Jensen Huang said the nearly $20 billion CPU revenue visibility for FY2027 is for standalone CPU sales.\ne1018 N1N2N3O1O2O3 Morningstar said AMD’s recent upward revision to its server CPU market forecast suggested high demand for agentic AI and a need for substantially more AI infrastructure over the next few years.\ne1338 N1N3O2O3 The author estimates NVIDIA's Vera CPU revenue at $20 billion in the current fiscal year, against a target market of $200 billion.\ne1473 N1O2O3 NVIDIA described a $200 billion CPU market that it said it had never addressed before.\nv5 N1N2N3O1O3 - v5 (ai-capex, due 2027-03-31) How much will the four largest AI hyperscalers spend on data-center capital expenditures in calendar 2026?: now rung 3 $600 billion to below $700 billion; judged 2026-08-06; updated since this thesis was last judged; reasons: The reference is the four hyperscalers’ roughly $650 billion 2026 plan, which sits in rung 3. Updated company-level figures raise the implied total, but not enough to cross the $700 billion boundary into rung 4. Broader estimates for tech-giant or AI-infrastructure spending are not sized specificall\nv6 N1N2N3O1O3 - v6 (ai-capex, due 2028-03-31) How much will the four largest AI hyperscalers spend on data-center capital expenditures in calendar 2027?: now rung 3 $900 billion to below $1.1 trillion; judged 2026-08-01; reasons: The reference is NVIDIA management’s forecast of more than $1 trillion in 2027, following a projected $650 billion in 2026; that reference is in rung 3. The other evidence supports continued growth, including Alphabet’s statement that it will increase capex further in 2027 and Microsoft’s capex-grow\nv14 N1N2N3O1O2O3 - v14 (open; first_is_bullish) How much total revenue will NVIDIA report for FY2027?: now rung 3 Neutral, judged 2026-08-06\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_16","type":"command_execution","command":"/opt/homebrew/bin/zsh -lc \"python3 -c 'import json\np=json.load(open(\\\"poc/data/citation-probe-2026-11-22/packet-C.json\\\"))\nimport sys,re\nfor n in map(int,sys.argv[1:]):\n x=p[n]\n print(\\\"\\\\n\\\"+x[\\\"key\\\"],x[\\\"question\\\"])\n used={}\n for arm in (\\\"v12\\\",\\\"v7\\\"):\n  for idx,a in enumerate(x[arm],1):\n   print(arm,idx,\\\"rung\\\",a[\\\"rung\\\"],\\\"changed\\\",a[\\\"changed\\\"])\n   for k in (\\\"reference\\\",\\\"implied_outcome\\\",\\\"reasons\\\",\\\"why_changed\\\"): print(a[k])\n   print(\\\"cite\\\",\\\",\\\".join(a[\\\"cites\\\"]),\\\"dependencies\\\",\\\",\\\".join(a[\\\"cites_views\\\"]),\\\"themes\\\",\\\",\\\".join(y[\\\"view_id\\\"]+\\\":\\\"+y[\\\"push\\\"].replace(\\\"toward_\\\",\\\"\\\") for y in a[\\\"theme_pushes\\\"]))\n   ps={k:[] for k in (\\\"toward_first\\\",\\\"toward_last\\\",\\\"none\\\")}\n   for y in a[\\\"pushes\\\"]:ps[y[\\\"push\\\"]].append(y[\\\"evidence_id\\\"])\n   print(\\\"push\\\",*(k.replace(\\\"toward_\\\",\\\"\\\")+\\\"=\\\"+\\\",\\\".join(v) for k,v in ps.items()))\n   for t in {*a[\\\"cites\\\"],*a[\\\"cites_views\\\"],*(y[\\\"evidence_id\\\"] for y in a[\\\"pushes\\\"]),*(y[\\\"view_id\\\"] for y in a[\\\"theme_pushes\\\"])}:used.setdefault(t,[]).append((\\\"N\\\" if arm==\\\"v12\\\" else \\\"O\\\")+str(idx))\n print(\\\"SOURCES\\\")\n for k in sorted(used,key=lambda k:(k[0],int(k[1:]) if k[1:].isdigit() else 99999)):\n  v=x[\\\"quotes\\\"].get(k,\\\"MISSING\\\")\n  print(k,\\\"\\\".join(used[k]),re.sub(r\\\"\"'^- '\"\\\\S+ \\\\[[\"'^'\"\\\\n]+?\\\\] \\\",\\\"\\\",v.split(\\\"\\\\n\\\",1)[0]))\n' 0 1\"","aggregated_output":"\nold-3:6441 Thesis v23 (NVDA, asset, focus \"Whether NVIDIA takes on a financing obligation for OpenAI's purchases of NVIDIA chips.\", , due 2027-07-27): Will NVIDIA sign an agreement to finance OpenAI's chip purchases by July 27, 2027?\nv12 1 rung 1 changed True\nThe status-quo reference is rung 2 (No): no market consensus, NVIDIA guidance, or stated deadline for signing is supplied.\nAn agreement to finance at least some of OpenAI's NVIDIA chip purchases by July 27, 2027 is more likely than no agreement. The reported discussions are for up to $350 billion, but that ceiling is not established as the likely signed amount; NVIDIA's stated 4.25 GW initial deployment and approximately 12 GW of commitments through 2030 support substantial underlying demand.\nThe reference is rung 2 (No): no market consensus, NVIDIA guidance, or stated deadline for signing is supplied, so the status-quo reference is no agreement by July 27, 2027. The specific reports of NVIDIA discussing chip-purchase financing, together with NVIDIA's newly stated scale of planned OpenAI deployments, make an agreement of some amount by the deadline more likely than not. The reports still describe discussions, not a signed agreement, and those discussions are reported as early-stage and capable of collapsing; that limits confidence but does not outweigh the time remaining and the financing talks' specificity. Rung 1 requires any amount, not the full reported financing ceiling.\nThe rung remains Yes, but NVIDIA's party statements add new quantified support for the underlying OpenAI deployment demand: an initial 4.25 GW deployment and approximately 12 GW of existing and planned NVIDIA compute commitments through 2030. The reported financing discussions remain the same unconfirmed talks, so their repetition does not itself change the conclusion.\ncite e1291,e1363,e1381,e1385,e1300,e2781,e2782 dependencies  themes \npush first=e1291,e1300,e1363,e1385,e2781,e2782 last=e1381 none=e1197,e1295,e1297,e1298,e1299,e1337,e1371,e1379,e1380,e1404,e1422,e1426,e1509,e1724,e1740,e1878,e1984\nv12 2 rung 1 changed True\nThe status-quo reference is no signed agreement by July 27, 2027, which is rung 2. No market consensus, NVIDIA guidance, or stated signing deadline is supplied.\nThe evidence implies that NVIDIA is more likely than not to sign an agreement covering some amount of OpenAI's chip purchases by July 27, 2027. The reported financing talks are for up to $350 billion, but that is a ceiling under discussion, not an implied signed amount.\nThe specific reports of financing talks, including a reported amount of up to $350 billion, make an agreement covering some amount more likely than not by the deadline. NVIDIA's newly stated OpenAI deployment commitments support substantial underlying chip demand, but they are deployment commitments, not financing obligations; the talks are still described as early-stage and capable of collapsing. Because rung 1 requires only some financing agreement, the forecast remains Yes, without treating the reported $350 billion ceiling as an implied signed amount.\nThe rung is unchanged. NVIDIA's statements now quantify OpenAI's existing and planned deployments at approximately 12 GW through 2030, including an initial 4.25 GW deployment; that updates the supporting demand facts, but does not establish a financing agreement.\ncite e1291,e1379,e1381,e2781,e2782 dependencies  themes \npush first=e1291,e1379,e2781,e2782 last=e1381 none=\nv12 3 rung 1 changed True\nNo consensus, NVIDIA guidance, or stated signing deadline is supplied. The status-quo reference is no agreement by July 27, 2027, rung 2.\nThe evidence implies that NVIDIA is more likely than not to sign an agreement covering some amount of OpenAI’s chip purchases by July 27, 2027. The reported up-to-$350 billion figure describes discussions, not a committed amount; the binary question requires only some amount.\nThe reference is rung 2 (No): no market consensus, NVIDIA guidance, or stated deadline for signing is supplied, so the status-quo reference is no agreement by July 27, 2027. The specific report of talks to finance up to $350 billion of OpenAI chip purchases, alongside the report that chip financing is being discussed separately from lease and construction debt, makes signing some amount by the deadline more likely than not. NVIDIA’s stated 12 GW of existing and planned OpenAI compute commitments and the planned 4.25 GW initial deployment support substantial underlying chip demand, although they do not establish a financing obligation. The talks are not reported as an agreement and are described as early-stage and capable of collapsing; those caveats temper the forecast but do not outweigh the specific talks and the remaining time before the deadline. Since rung 1 requires an agreement for any amount, the forecast remains Yes.\nThe rung remains Yes, but NVIDIA’s own statement newly specifies approximately 12 GW of existing and planned OpenAI compute commitments through 2030, and a 4.25 GW initial deployment. This strengthens and quantifies the underlying demand supporting the forecast; it does not show that financing has been agreed.\ncite e1197,e1381,e1363,e2781,e2782 dependencies  themes \npush first=e1197,e1363,e2781,e2782 last=e1381 none=\nv7 1 rung 1 changed True\nNo consensus, NVIDIA guidance, or stated signing deadline is supplied. The status-quo reference is that NVIDIA has not signed such an agreement by July 27, 2027, which is rung 2 (No).\nThe evidence implies that NVIDIA is more likely than not to sign an agreement financing at least some OpenAI chip purchases by July 27, 2027. The approximately $350 billion figure is a reported discussion ceiling, not an agreed amount; NVIDIA's approximately 12 GW of existing and planned OpenAI deployments supports demand but does not by itself establish financing.\nThe reference is rung 2 (No): no consensus, NVIDIA guidance, or stated deadline for signing is supplied, so the status-quo reference is no agreement by July 27, 2027. The specific reports of discussions to finance up to $350 billion of OpenAI chip purchases make signing some amount by the deadline more likely than not, although they describe talks rather than an agreement and one report says the negotiations were early-stage and could collapse. NVIDIA's later statements about OpenAI's substantial planned deployments support the scale of underlying demand, but do not themselves establish financing. The forecast therefore remains Yes and crosses the rung boundary from the reference; the reported $350 billion ceiling is not treated as an amount already agreed.\nThe rung remains 1, but the supporting facts have changed: NVIDIA's statements now quantify OpenAI's planned deployments at approximately 12 GW through 2030 and describe an initial 4.25 GW deployment. These facts support underlying chip demand, though not a signed financing obligation.\ncite e1197,e1291,e1295,e1297,e1298,e1299,e1300,e1337,e1363,e1371,e1379,e1380,e1381,e1385,e1404,e1422,e1426,e1509,e1724,e1740,e1878,e1984,e2781,e2782 dependencies  themes \npush first=e1197,e1297,e1298,e1300,e1363,e2781,e2782 last=e1381 none=e1291,e1295,e1299,e1337,e1371,e1379,e1380,e1385,e1404,e1422,e1426,e1509,e1724,e1740,e1878,e1984\nv7 2 rung 1 changed True\nNo consensus, NVIDIA guidance, or stated signing deadline is supplied. The status-quo reference is no signed financing agreement by July 27, 2027, which is rung 2.\nThe evidence implies NVIDIA is more likely than not to sign an agreement covering some amount of OpenAI chip purchases by July 27, 2027. The reported figure is up to $350 billion, but it is a discussion ceiling rather than a forecast of the amount ultimately financed.\nThe reference is rung 2 (No): no market consensus, NVIDIA guidance, or stated deadline for signing is supplied, so the status-quo reference is no agreement by July 27, 2027. The specific reports of discussions to finance up to $350 billion of OpenAI chip purchases make an agreement for some amount more likely than not, despite the reports that talks are early-stage and could collapse. NVIDIA’s announced deployment scale supports underlying purchase demand, but does not itself establish a financing obligation. The $350 billion is a reported ceiling, not a forecast that this full amount will be financed; rung 1 requires only an agreement for any amount, and the evidence leaves that outcome more likely than rung 2.\nThe rung remains 1. NVIDIA’s party statements add quantified deployment commitments—4.25 GW for the initial PORTS-Pike deployment, approximately 1.5 million GPUs per system generation, and approximately 12 GW of existing and planned compute through 2030—which updates the supporting demand facts, but does not confirm a financing agreement.\ncite e1197,e1291,e1295,e1297,e1298,e1299,e1300,e1337,e1363,e1371,e1379,e1380,e1381,e1385,e1404,e1422,e1426,e1509,e1724,e1740,e1878,e1984,e2781,e2782 dependencies  themes \npush first=e1197,e1291,e1295,e1297,e1298,e1300,e1337,e1363,e1371,e1379,e1380,e1404,e1422,e1426,e1509,e1740,e1878,e1984,e2781,e2782 last=e1381 none=e1299,e1385,e1724\nv7 3 rung 1 changed False\nRung 2 (No): the status-quo expectation is that NVIDIA has not signed such an agreement by July 27, 2027; no consensus, company guidance, or stated signing plan is supplied.\nThe most likely outcome is that NVIDIA signs an agreement to finance some amount of OpenAI's chip purchases by July 27, 2027. The specific reports describe discussions of up to $350 billion, while the reported 12 GW of existing and planned compute commitments supports demand; neither figure establishes that the full amount will be financed.\nThe reference is rung 2 (No): absent a stated market expectation, company guidance, or signing deadline, the status-quo reference is no agreement by July 27, 2027. The specific reports of up to $350 billion in chip-purchase financing discussions make signing some amount by the deadline more likely than not; the talks are still unconfirmed and reportedly early-stage, so this is a forecast, not an established agreement. The reported $350 billion is a ceiling, not my forecast of the amount NVIDIA will finance. OpenAI's stated 12 GW of existing and planned NVIDIA compute commitments supports underlying demand, but does not itself establish a financing obligation. These facts do not move the forecast back across the binary rung boundary.\nThe conclusion remains rung 1. The financing reports and the deployment-scale statements are repetitions or corroborations of claims already reflected in the previous conclusion; no new signed agreement or materially different financing status is shown.\ncite e1197,e1295,e1363,e1381,e2781,e2782 dependencies  themes \npush first=e1197,e1295,e1363,e2781,e2782 last=e1381 none=e1291,e1297,e1298,e1299,e1300,e1337,e1371,e1379,e1380,e1385,e1404,e1422,e1426,e1509,e1724,e1740,e1878,e1984\nSOURCES\ne1197 N1N3O1O2O3 Nvidia is reportedly discussing financing up to an additional $350 billion of OpenAI chip purchases; the discussions are not reported as an agreement.\ne1291 N1N2O1O2O3 The Wall Street Journal reported on July 26, 2026, that NVIDIA was discussing up to $350 billion in financing to help OpenAI purchase NVIDIA AI chips.\ne1295 N1O1O2O3 NVIDIA is reportedly discussing a separate $350 billion financing arrangement to help OpenAI purchase NVIDIA chips; no agreement is reported as signed.\ne1297 N1O1O2O3 OpenAI recorded an operating loss of $20.9 billion in 2025.\ne1298 N1O1O2O3 OpenAI was estimated to have an operating loss of $7 billion in the first quarter of 2026.\ne1299 N1O1O2O3 OpenAI raised $122 billion in a March funding round, with Amazon, NVIDIA and SoftBank providing $110 billion combined.\ne1300 N1O1O2O3 OpenAI has committed to $1.4 trillion in spending in the coming years, according to Barron’s.\ne1337 N1O1O2O3 The Wall Street Journal reported that NVIDIA was in discussions to finance a $350 billion OpenAI chip purchase; the report was described as published Monday, July 27, 2026.\ne1363 N1N3O1O2O3 The reported guarantee would cover the OpenAI project’s lease and construction debt, but not its NVIDIA chips, which the companies are discussing separately.\ne1371 N1O1O2O3 NVIDIA is reportedly weighing a separate deal to finance up to $350 billion of OpenAI GPU purchases, in addition to the proposed Ohio-campus guarantees.\ne1379 N1N2O1O2O3 NVIDIA is reportedly discussing financing $350 billion of OpenAI's chip purchases for the U.S. project.\ne1380 N1O1O2O3 People familiar with the matter said a prospective deal would help OpenAI lease SB Energy's planned $500 billion, 10-gigawatt Ohio data-center hub.\ne1381 N1N2N3O1O2O3 People familiar with the negotiations said the NVIDIA-OpenAI financing talks are at an early stage, could collapse, and could have changing terms.\ne1385 N1O1O2O3 The article reports that SoftBank has committed nearly $65 billion to OpenAI by October and signed a $40 billion bridge loan to finance that investment.\ne1404 N1O1O2O3 NVIDIA is reportedly discussing financing an additional $350 billion in chip purchases for OpenAI; the discussions are not reported as an agreement.\ne1422 N1O1O2O3 NVIDIA is reportedly evaluating financing approximately US$350 billion worth of AI chips for the Ohio facility.\ne1426 N1O1O2O3 Unnamed media reports said NVIDIA was discussing a separate arrangement that could help finance up to $350 billion of OpenAI chip purchases; negotiations were in early stages.\ne1509 N1O1O2O3 NVIDIA is reportedly in talks to guarantee up to $250 billion of data-center lease payments for OpenAI’s use of a SoftBank facility and provide up to $350 billion in financing for OpenAI GPU purchases.\ne1724 N1O1O2O3 SiliconANGLE, citing Bloomberg, reported unconfirmed ongoing talks for NVIDIA to provide $250 billion to help finance OpenAI’s Ohio data-center lease and debt; it said no agreement had been confirmed.\ne1740 N1O1O2O3 People familiar with the situation said NVIDIA was in discussions to finance US$350 billion of OpenAI chip purchases for the Ohio project.\ne1878 N1O1O2O3 NVIDIA was reportedly discussing financing US$350 billion of OpenAI’s chip purchases for the Ohio data-center project.\ne1984 N1O1O2O3 The author reported talks about NVIDIA backstopping up to $250 billion of OpenAI compute leases at SB Energy’s planned 10-gigawatt Ohio campus and financing about $350 billion of OpenAI chip purchases.\ne2781 N1N2N3O1O2O3 NVIDIA said the planned initial PORTS-Pike deployment for OpenAI would support 4.25 GW of AI factory capacity and could represent approximately 1.5 million NVIDIA GPUs per system generation.\ne2782 N1N2N3O1O2O3 NVIDIA said OpenAI had committed to substantial NVIDIA infrastructure deployments through 2030, with existing and planned commitments representing approximately 12 GW of compute.\n\nlinked-1:8886 Thesis v21 (NVDA, asset, focus \"NVIDIA total revenue growth in FY2027\", FY2027, due 2027-02-28): How much will NVIDIA's total revenue grow in FY2027?\nv12 1 rung 1 changed True\nWall Street's 42% FY2027 revenue-growth estimate is the market reference and sits in rung 3 (Neutral).\nApproximately 82% FY2027 revenue growth, implying about $393 billion of revenue on FY2026 revenue of $215.94 billion. The specific annual analyst estimate is consistent with reported Q1 growth, Q2 management guidance, and Q2/Q3 analyst growth estimates.\nThe reference is Wall Street's 42% FY2027 growth estimate, which sits in rung 3. The evidence still implies about 82% growth, or approximately $393 billion of FY2027 revenue on FY2026 revenue of $215.94 billion: the specific annual estimate is consistent with Q1 growth of 85%, the $91 billion Q2 guide, and analyst estimates of 96% Q2 and 81% Q3 growth. Recent AI-customer demand and CoreWeave backlog evidence add support, but do not quantify NVIDIA FY2027 revenue enough to raise the implied figure above 82%; the multi-period backlog and customer run rates are not NVIDIA revenue. The implied growth remains above rung 1's 60% boundary. The dependency's approximately $19–20 billion FY2027 CPU-revenue conclusion is a component of total revenue, not an amount to add, and is consistent with the implied total.\nThe rung and implied annual growth figure remain the same, but the evidence base has changed: CoreWeave reported more commitments beyond its backlog, Anthropic and OpenAI reported large revenue run rates, and the AI-capex theme v5 has been updated since the prior judgement. These strengthen demand support without providing a sufficiently direct, quantified basis to revise the approximately 82% FY2027 estimate.\ncite e966,e1333,e1348,e1350,e1388,e1390,e1883,e1884,e1705,e1764,e2040,e2041 dependencies v16 themes v10:first,v19:first,v25:first,v26:first,v27:none,v5:first,v6:first\npush first=e1333,e1348,e1350,e1388,e1390,e1884,e1705,e1764,e2040,e2041 last=e966 none=e1883\nv12 2 rung 1 changed True\nWall Street's 42% estimate for NVIDIA FY2027 revenue growth is the market reference and sits in rung 3 (Neutral).\nAbout 82% FY2027 revenue growth, or roughly $393 billion of total revenue, derived from the specific analyst estimate and supported by Q1 reported growth, the Q2 revenue guide, and Q2/Q3 growth estimates. The approximately $19–20 billion CPU-revenue dependency is included within this total.\nThe reference is Wall Street's 42% FY2027 growth estimate, which is rung 3. The evidence still implies about 82% growth—roughly $393 billion of revenue on FY2026 revenue of $215.94 billion—supported by the specific FY2027 analyst estimate, 85% Q1 growth, the $91 billion Q2 guide, and 96% and 81% analyst growth estimates for Q2 and Q3. Those inputs remain well above rung 1's 60% boundary. Recent customer and AI-demand evidence reinforces the outlook but does not provide a quantified FY2027 revision. The dependency's approximately $19–20 billion FY2027 CPU-revenue conclusion is a component of total revenue, not an amount to add, and is consistent with this total.\nThe rung and central implied figure remain unchanged, but the newly supplied OpenAI and Anthropic revenue-run-rate information adds relevant demand context, alongside the updated AI-capex theme. These strengthen the supporting picture without giving a quantified basis to revise the approximately 82% FY2027 estimate.\ncite e966,e1148,e1285,e1333,e1348,e1350,e1388,e1390,e1883,e1884,e1764,e1705,e2040,e2041,e1278 dependencies v16 themes v10:first,v19:first,v25:first,v26:first,v27:none,v5:first,v6:first\npush first=e1333,e1348,e1350,e1388,e1390,e1883,e1884,e1764,e1705,e2040,e2041,e1278 last=e966,e1148,e1285 none=\nv12 3 rung 1 changed True\nWall Street’s 42% FY2027 revenue-growth estimate, cited in e966, is in rung 3 (Neutral).\nAbout 82% FY2027 total revenue growth, or roughly $393 billion of revenue, applying the specific FY2027 estimate in e1333 to reported FY2026 revenue of $215.94 billion in e1883. The strong reported Q1 growth, Q2 guidance, and Q2/Q3 analyst estimates support that forecast rather than merely annualizing Q1.\nThe market reference is Wall Street’s 42% FY2027 growth estimate, which sits in rung 3. The evidence implies about 82% growth (roughly $393 billion of FY2027 revenue on FY2026 revenue of $215.94 billion): the specific FY2027 estimate is consistent with reported 85% Q1 growth, the $91 billion Q2 guide, and analyst estimates of 96% Q2 and 81% Q3 growth. The implied growth is above rung 1’s 60% boundary. Recent customer revenue-run-rate and CoreWeave backlog evidence strengthen the demand backdrop but do not quantify additional NVIDIA revenue, so they do not justify raising the 82% estimate. The dependency’s approximately $19–20 billion FY2027 CPU-revenue conclusion is part of total revenue, not an addition to it, and is consistent with the implied whole.\nThe rung and implied 82% outcome are unchanged, but the newly supplied OpenAI and Anthropic revenue-run-rate figures add demand-side support; they do not disclose NVIDIA revenue or customer spending amounts that would change the implied figure.\ncite e966,e1333,e1249,e1350,e1388,e1390,e1883,e1884,e1764,e1705,e1706,e2040,e2041 dependencies v16 themes v10:first,v19:first,v25:first,v26:first,v27:none,v5:first,v6:first\npush first=e1333,e1249,e1350,e1388,e1390,e1884,e1764,e1705,e1706,e2040,e2041 last= none=e966,e1883\nv7 1 rung 1 changed True\nWall Street's 42% FY2027 growth estimate, cited in e966, is in rung 3 (Neutral).\nAbout 82% FY2027 revenue growth, or roughly $393 billion of revenue, derived from the specific analyst estimate and FY2026 revenue of $215.94 billion. The Q1 result, Q2 guide and Q2/Q3 analyst growth estimates support that forecast.\nThe reference is Wall Street's 42% FY2027 growth estimate, which sits in rung 3. The forecast remains materially higher: the specific 82% FY2027 analyst estimate, supported by 85% Q1 growth, management's $91 billion Q2 guide, and analyst estimates of 96% Q2 and 81% Q3 growth, implies about $393 billion of FY2027 revenue on FY2026 revenue of $215.94 billion. That is above rung 1's 60% boundary. Strong Q4 growth and recent customer/backlog evidence reinforce the high-growth case without providing a quantified basis to revise the 82% estimate. NVIDIA's roughly $19–20 billion FY2027 CPU revenue forecast is part of total revenue, not an amount to add; it is consistent with this whole-company outcome.\nThe rung and the 82% implied growth estimate are unchanged. Recent evidence adds material demand-side support, including customer revenue run rates and CoreWeave's reported backlog and bookings; it reinforces the forecast but does not quantify a reason to revise the implied FY2027 figure.\ncite e966,e1148,e1285,e1333,e1249,e1280,e1348,e1350,e1388,e1390,e1278,e1705,e1706,e1764,e1883,e1884,e2040,e2041 dependencies v16 themes v10:last,v19:last,v25:last,v26:last,v27:none,v5:last,v6:last\npush first=e966,e1148,e1285 last=e1097,e1249,e1278,e1280,e1333,e1348,e1350,e1384,e1388,e1389,e1390,e1701,e1702,e1705,e1706,e1764,e1884,e2036,e2037,e2040,e2041 none=e1369,e1391,e1707,e1883,e2001,e2002,e2061\nv7 2 rung 1 changed True\nWall Street's 42% FY2027 revenue-growth estimate is the market reference and sits in rung 3 (Neutral).\nAbout 82% FY2027 total revenue growth, the specific analyst estimate in e1333; applied to reported FY2026 revenue of $215.94 billion, that implies roughly $393 billion of FY2027 revenue.\nThe market reference is Wall Street's 42% FY2027 growth estimate, which sits in rung 3. The evidence-supported forecast remains about 82% growth: the specific analyst estimate implies roughly $393 billion of FY2027 revenue on reported FY2026 revenue of $215.94 billion. Q1 growth of 85%, the $91 billion Q2 guide, and analyst estimates of 96% growth in Q2 and 81% in Q3 support that trajectory; this clears rung 1's 60% threshold. The $1 trillion cumulative chip-sales target is not FY2027 revenue alone and is discounted, while NVIDIA's roughly $19–20 billion FY2027 CPU revenue is part of total revenue, not additive to it, and is consistent with this whole-company forecast.\nThe rung and implied 82% growth forecast are unchanged. Recent Anthropic and OpenAI revenue-run-rate disclosures add new, quantified AI-demand context, but do not provide a quantified NVIDIA revenue revision, so they strengthen the supporting picture without changing the forecast.\ncite e966,e1249,e1278,e1333,e1350,e1388,e1390,e1883,e1884,e2040,e2041,e1764 dependencies v16 themes v10:first,v19:first,v25:first,v26:first,v27:none,v5:first,v6:first\npush first=e1097,e1249,e1278,e1333,e1350,e1384,e1388,e1389,e1390,e1701,e1702,e1705,e1706,e1764,e1884,e2036,e2037,e2040,e2041,e2061 last= none=e966,e1148,e1280,e1285,e1348,e1369,e1391,e1707,e1883,e2001,e2002\nv7 3 rung 1 changed False\nWall Street's 42% estimate for NVIDIA FY2027 revenue growth is the market reference and sits in rung 3 (Neutral).\nAbout 82% FY2027 revenue growth, or approximately $393 billion of revenue, derived by carrying the specific 82% analyst forecast forward from NVIDIA's $215.94 billion FY2026 revenue base. Reported Q1 growth of 85%, the $91 billion Q2 guide, and analyst estimates of 96% Q2 and 81% Q3 growth support the elevated outlook.\nThe market reference is Wall Street's 42% FY2027 growth estimate, which sits in rung 3. The evidence still implies about 82% growth—well above rung 1's 60% threshold—supported by the specific FY2027 analyst estimate and elevated reported and forecast quarterly growth. The recent customer and AI-infrastructure demand evidence is supportive but does not provide a quantified NVIDIA FY2027 revision, so it does not change the implied figure. NVIDIA's FY2027 CPU-revenue dependency remains about $19–20 billion; that is part of total revenue, not additive to it, and is consistent with the whole-revenue forecast.\nThe prior conclusion already implied about 82% FY2027 growth from the same specific analyst estimate and FY2026 revenue base. The additional demand evidence does not quantify a changed NVIDIA revenue forecast or move the implied outcome across a rung boundary.\ncite e966,e1333,e1883,e1348,e1350,e1388,e1390,e1764,e2040,e2041 dependencies v16 themes v10:first,v19:first,v25:first,v26:first,v27:none,v5:first,v6:first\npush first=e1333,e1348,e1350,e1388,e1390,e1764,e2040,e2041 last= none=e966,e1883\nSOURCES\ne966 N1N2N3O1O2O3 Wall Street expects Micron revenue to grow 84% in its fiscal year ending August 2027 and estimates NVIDIA revenue growth of 42% in FY2027.\ne1097 O1O2 The author judged that Alphabet was likely to buy more NVIDIA AI chip systems as it raised spending, creating a solid chance of faster NVIDIA revenue growth.\ne1148 N2O1O2 Keithen Drury estimated that NVIDIA revenue would grow 42% in the following year (FY2027).\ne1249 N3O1O2 NVIDIA reported that total revenue rose 85% year over year in fiscal Q1, which ended in late April.\ne1278 N2O1O2 At Nvidia's GTC conference in March, CEO Jensen Huang forecast $1 trillion of cumulative chip sales through 2027, up from his previous $500 billion forecast through 2026.\ne1280 O1O2 NVIDIA reported that Q1 FY2027 revenue rose 85% year over year and net income rose 211% year over year; the quarter ended April 26.\ne1285 N2O1O2 Wall Street analysts expect NVIDIA revenue to grow 42% in 2027.\ne1333 N1N2N3O1O2O3 Analysts expect NVIDIA revenue to grow 82% in FY2027.\ne1348 N1N2O1O2O3 NVIDIA reported fiscal Q1 2027 revenue of $81.6 billion, up 85% year over year and 20% sequentially.\ne1350 N1N2N3O1O2O3 NVIDIA management guided fiscal Q2 2027 revenue to $91 billion, plus or minus 2%.\ne1369 O1O2 Simply Wall St’s model increased its NVIDIA revenue-growth estimate from 38.69% to 41.23%; the forecast period is not specified.\ne1384 O1O2 The author says NVIDIA trades at 22x forward earnings after 85.23% revenue growth and 92% year-over-year Data Center segment growth.\ne1388 N1N2N3O1O2O3 Wall Street analysts expect NVIDIA revenue growth of 96% in fiscal Q2 2027.\ne1389 O1O2 The author expects NVIDIA's revenue growth rate could return to at least 100% in fiscal Q2 2027.\ne1390 N1N2N3O1O2O3 Analysts estimate NVIDIA revenue growth of 81% in fiscal Q3 2027.\ne1391 O1O2 The author expects NVIDIA's stock to rise quickly if the company projects revenue growth around 90% for fiscal Q3 2027.\ne1701 O1O2 One-year H100 rental rates rose from roughly $1.70 per GPU-hour in October 2025 to $2.35 per GPU-hour by March 2026.\ne1702 O1O2 B200 Blackwell cloud pricing was about $5.30–$7.05 per GPU-hour, with the article describing these as current rates.\ne1705 N1N2N3O1O2 CoreWeave reported Q1 revenue growth of 111.6% year over year and a backlog of nearly $100 billion.\ne1706 N3O1O2 CoreWeave CEO Michael Intrator said the company’s bookings in the last quarter were the strongest in its history.\ne1707 O1O2 NVIDIA reported Q1 FY2027 revenue of $81.61 billion.\ne1764 N1N2N3O1O2O3 CoreWeave reported a $104 billion revenue backlog, excluding more than $25 billion in new commitments booked since July.\ne1883 N1N2N3O1O2O3 NVIDIA reported FY2026 total revenue of $215.94 billion, up 65.47% year over year.\ne1884 N1N2N3O1O2 NVIDIA reported Q4 FY2026 total revenue of $68.13 billion, up 73.2% year over year.\ne2001 O1O2 NVIDIA reported FY2026 revenue of $215.9 billion, up 65%.\ne2002 O1O2 NVIDIA reported Q1 FY2027 revenue of $81.6 billion, up 85% year on year.\ne2036 O1O2 Cantor analysts said NVIDIA's latest agreement signaled that the AI investment cycle would be prolonged and durable.\ne2037 O1O2 Cantor analysts said NVIDIA's agreement would facilitate the AI buildout and strengthen its competitive position.\ne2040 N1N2N3O1O2O3 Anthropic told investors that its annualized revenue run rate reached $65 billion in July 2026, seven times the year-earlier level.\ne2041 N1N2N3O1O2O3 OpenAI's revenue run rate recently reached $40 billion.\ne2061 O1O2 The author says Wall Street analysts expect NVIDIA to grow faster than AMD in 2026.\nv5 N1N2N3O1O2O3 - v5 (ai-capex, due 2027-03-31) How much will the four largest AI hyperscalers spend on data-center capital expenditures in calendar 2026?: now rung 4 $700 billion to below $800 billion; judged 2026-08-18; updated since this thesis was last judged; reasons: The market-tracking reference for the four largest is about $745 billion, which is rung 4. The outcome implied by the current evidence is around $700–$745 billion: the company-level spending figures and the earlier estimate of roughly $600 billion for Amazon, Microsoft, and Alphabet, combined with M\nv6 N1N2N3O1O2O3 - v6 (ai-capex, due 2028-03-31) How much will the four largest AI hyperscalers spend on data-center capital expenditures in calendar 2027?: now rung 3 $900 billion to below $1.1 trillion; judged 2026-08-17; reasons: The market reference is the $1.08 trillion calendar-2027 consensus, which is in rung 3. The evidence supports substantial spending growth: Alphabet said capex would increase further in 2027, Microsoft said fiscal-2027 capex would grow year over year, and the four hyperscalers were reported to expect\nv10 N1N2N3O1O2O3 - v10 (chip-export-controls, due 2027-01-31) Will China's authorities authorize Chinese AI companies to purchase Nvidia H200 chips by 2027-01-31?: now rung 1 Yes; judged 2026-07-31; reasons: No explicit market consensus is supplied. The observable status reference—Chinese authorization is not established, with a report that Beijing refused purchases—sits at rung 2. The forecast is rung 1 because reports specifically describe plans to permit selected Chinese AI firms to buy limited H200 \nv16 N1N2N3O1O2O3 - v16 (open; first_is_bullish) How much total CPU revenue will NVIDIA generate in FY2027?: now rung 3 Neutral, judged 2026-08-18\nv19 N1N2N3O1O2O3 - v19 (chip-export-controls, due 2026-12-31) Will the U.S. Senate pass the Remote Access Security Act by 2026-12-31?: now rung 2 No; judged 2026-07-29; reasons: The observable reference is rung 2: the bill had passed the House but was still awaiting Senate action on July 23; no separate market consensus is supplied. That status is not itself proof of the year-end outcome, but the evidence shows no Senate action or schedule in the remaining period. The White\nv25 N1N2N3O1O2O3 - v25 (chip-export-controls, due 2027-01-31) Will Taiwan's Ministry of Economic Affairs introduce performance-threshold export controls for advanced chips by 2027-01-31?: now rung 2 No; judged 2026-07-28; reasons: The required outcome is formal adoption or entry into force by 2027-01-31, not consultation or consideration. Consultations are confirmed, but the Ministry gave no timeline and the controls were only reported as being weighed. That makes adoption by the deadline less likely than no adoption; the evi\nv26 N1N2N3O1O2O3 - v26 (chip-export-controls, due 2027-01-31) Will Taiwan introduce a criminal ban on AI chip exports to China by 2027-01-31?: now rung 2 No; judged 2026-07-29; reasons: No market consensus is supplied; the status-quo reference is no criminal ban by the deadline (rung 2). Taiwan's statement that it is considering a ban is a meaningful signal toward Yes, but it supplies neither a formal proposal nor a timetable. On the evidence in force, introduction by 2027-01-31 is\nv27 N1N2N3O1O2O3 - v27 (chip-export-controls, due 2027-01-31) Will the U.S. Department of Commerce conclude its investigation into Chinese firms' access to advanced U.S. chips by 2027-01-31?: now rung 2 No; judged 2026-08-07; reasons: The investigation was formally underway in late July, and the subsequent reports describe alleged access to Blackwell chips and tighter screening, not a public Commerce conclusion or a completion timetable. On balance, the evidence makes the investigation remaining publicly unconcluded by 2027-01-31\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_17","type":"command_execution","command":"/opt/homebrew/bin/zsh -lc \"python3 -c 'import json\np=json.load(open(\\\"poc/data/citation-probe-2026-11-22/packet-C.json\\\"))\nimport sys,re\nfor n in map(int,sys.argv[1:]):\n x=p[n]\n print(\\\"\\\\n\\\"+x[\\\"key\\\"],x[\\\"question\\\"])\n used={}\n for arm in (\\\"v12\\\",\\\"v7\\\"):\n  for idx,a in enumerate(x[arm],1):\n   print(arm,idx,\\\"rung\\\",a[\\\"rung\\\"],\\\"changed\\\",a[\\\"changed\\\"])\n   for k in (\\\"reference\\\",\\\"implied_outcome\\\",\\\"reasons\\\",\\\"why_changed\\\"): print(a[k])\n   print(\\\"cite\\\",\\\",\\\".join(a[\\\"cites\\\"]),\\\"dependencies\\\",\\\",\\\".join(a[\\\"cites_views\\\"]),\\\"themes\\\",\\\",\\\".join(y[\\\"view_id\\\"]+\\\":\\\"+y[\\\"push\\\"].replace(\\\"toward_\\\",\\\"\\\") for y in a[\\\"theme_pushes\\\"]))\n   ps={k:[] for k in (\\\"toward_first\\\",\\\"toward_last\\\",\\\"none\\\")}\n   for y in a[\\\"pushes\\\"]:ps[y[\\\"push\\\"]].append(y[\\\"evidence_id\\\"])\n   print(\\\"push\\\",*(k.replace(\\\"toward_\\\",\\\"\\\")+\\\"=\\\"+\\\",\\\".join(v) for k,v in ps.items()))\n   for t in {*a[\\\"cites\\\"],*a[\\\"cites_views\\\"],*(y[\\\"evidence_id\\\"] for y in a[\\\"pushes\\\"]),*(y[\\\"view_id\\\"] for y in a[\\\"theme_pushes\\\"])}:used.setdefault(t,[]).append((\\\"N\\\" if arm==\\\"v12\\\" else \\\"O\\\")+str(idx))\n print(\\\"SOURCES\\\")\n for k in sorted(used,key=lambda k:(k[0],int(k[1:]) if k[1:].isdigit() else 99999)):\n  v=x[\\\"quotes\\\"].get(k,\\\"MISSING\\\")\n  print(k,\\\"\\\".join(used[k]),re.sub(r\\\"\"'^- '\"\\\\S+ \\\\[[\"'^'\"\\\\n]+?\\\\] \\\",\\\"\\\",v.split(\\\"\\\\n\\\",1)[0]))\n' 10 11\"","aggregated_output":"\nlinked-1:8768 Thesis v9 (NVDA, asset, focus \"NVIDIA FY2027 Data Center segment revenue\", FY2027, due 2027-02-28): How much Data Center segment revenue will NVIDIA report for FY2027?\nv12 1 rung 3 changed True\nBloomberg’s $393 billion FY2027 total-revenue projection, translated using the latest 92% Data Center mix to approximately $362 billion; this reference is in rung 3.\nApproximately $375 billion: the Q2 guide and about 11.5% sequential growth carried through Q3 and Q4 imply roughly $387 billion of FY2027 total revenue; applying the 92% mix gives about $356 billion, then adding the approximately $19 billion stand-alone CPU estimate gives about $375 billion. This is above $350 billion and below $400 billion.\nThe market reference is Bloomberg’s $393 billion FY2027 total-revenue projection, translated at the latest 92% Data Center mix to about $362 billion; that sits in rung 3. The forecast is about $375 billion: using the approximately $91 billion Q2 total-revenue guide and carrying the implied roughly 11.5% sequential growth through Q3 and Q4 gives about $387 billion of FY2027 total revenue; applying the 92% mix gives about $356 billion, and adding the approximately $19 billion stand-alone CPU estimate gives about $375 billion of Data Center revenue. That is above rung 3’s $350 billion floor but below the $400 billion rung 2 boundary, and is consistent with v14’s higher approximately $406 billion total-revenue forecast. The Q1 result, Q2 growth guidance, and newer demand and supply signals support the run-rate, but do not quantify an increase large enough to cross $400 billion. The newly announced Rubin factory is a product target without a delivery date, so it is discounted rather than counted in full for FY2027.\nThe rung and approximately $375 billion implied outcome are unchanged, but the supporting demand picture now includes NVIDIA’s statement that AI clouds are roughly half of Data Center demand and likely its fastest-growing customer group, alongside the report of more orders. These change the key qualitative support for the forecast; they do not supply a quantified FY2027 revision that crosses a rung boundary.\ncite e74,e170,e171,e255,e459,e604,e556,e557,e558,e559,e501,e538,e294,e109,e227,e289,e446 dependencies v14 themes v10:first,v5:first,v6:first\npush first=e74,e170,e171,e255,e459,e604,e556,e557,e558,e559,e501,e538,e294 last=e109,e227,e289,e446 none=\nv12 2 rung 3 changed False\nBloomberg's $393 billion FY2027 total-revenue projection, translated using the latest 92% Data Center mix to approximately $362 billion of Data Center revenue; that is rung 3 ($350 billion to below $400 billion).\nApproximately $375 billion for FY2027: carrying Q2's $91 billion total-revenue guide forward at about 11.5% sequential growth for Q3 and Q4 implies roughly $387 billion of baseline FY2027 revenue; applying the 92% Data Center mix gives about $356 billion, then adding the dependency's approximately $19 billion stand-alone CPU estimate gives about $375 billion.\nThe market reference is Bloomberg's $393 billion FY2027 total-revenue projection; applying the latest 92% Data Center mix puts it at about $362 billion, in rung 3. The forecast is about $375 billion: carry Q2's $91 billion guide forward at the roughly 11.5% Q1-to-Q2 sequential growth rate for Q3 and Q4, giving about $387 billion of baseline FY2027 revenue; apply the 92% mix (about $356 billion) and add the dependency's approximately $19 billion stand-alone CPU estimate. That is above rung 3's $350 billion floor but below rung 2's $400 billion boundary, and remains below v14's approximately $406 billion total-revenue forecast. Q1 Data Center growth, Q2 acceleration guidance and platform visibility support the run-rate, while memory supply pressure and competition temper it. The recent customer-mix detail does not provide a new revenue figure sufficient to change the forecast.\nThe rung and implied Data Center outcome remain about $375 billion, as in the previous conclusion; the newer customer-mix detail does not quantify a forecast change.\ncite e74,e170,e171,e204,e255,e604,e116,e117,e109,e153,e156,e289,e227 dependencies v14 themes v10:first,v5:first,v6:first\npush first=e74,e170,e171,e204,e255,e604 last=e116,e117,e109,e153,e156,e289,e227 none=\nv12 3 rung 3 changed True\nBloomberg's $393 billion FY2027 total-revenue projection, translated using NVIDIA's reported 92% Data Center mix to approximately $362 billion; that reference is in rung 3.\nApproximately $375 billion: carrying the Q1-to-Q2 sequential growth rate from $81.615 billion to $91 billion through Q3 and Q4 implies about $387 billion of FY2027 total revenue; 92% of that is about $356 billion, and adding the approximately $19 billion stand-alone CPU estimate gives about $375 billion.\nThe market reference is Bloomberg's $393 billion FY2027 total-revenue projection; applying the reported 92% Data Center mix gives roughly $362 billion, which sits in rung 3. The evidence-based estimate is about $375 billion: carrying the Q1-to-Q2 sequential total-revenue growth rate through Q3 and Q4 implies roughly $387 billion of FY2027 revenue, applying the 92% mix gives about $356 billion, and adding the approximately $19 billion stand-alone CPU estimate reflected in dependency v14 gives about $375 billion. This is above rung 3's $350 billion floor but below rung 2's $400 billion boundary, and is consistent with v14's higher total-revenue forecast. Strong demand indicators and the updated AI-cloud customer disclosure support the outlook, while competition, China substitution/export restrictions, and memory constraints temper it; none of the supplied evidence implies a Data Center figure crossing $400 billion.\nThe rung and implied estimate remain approximately $375 billion, but the newly supplied CFO disclosure that AI clouds represent roughly half of Data Center demand and are likely the fastest-growing customer group changes the supporting demand facts. It strengthens the case for the estimate without quantifying enough additional FY2027 revenue to move the forecast across a rung boundary.\ncite e74,e170,e255,e171,e459,e604,e109,e153,e156,e289,e227,e116,e117 dependencies v14 themes v10:first,v5:first,v6:first\npush first=e74,e170,e255,e171,e459,e604 last=e109,e153,e156,e289,e227,e116,e117 none=\nv7 1 rung 3 changed False\nBloomberg's $393 billion FY2027 NVIDIA total-revenue projection, translated using the latest 92% Data Center mix to about $362 billion of Data Center revenue; that sits in rung 3.\nAbout $375 billion for FY2027 Data Center revenue: the Q1-to-Q2 total-revenue sequential growth rate carried through Q3 and Q4 implies roughly $387 billion before the stand-alone CPU estimate; applying the 92% Data Center mix gives about $356 billion, and adding the dependency's approximately $19 billion CPU estimate gives about $375 billion.\nThe market reference is Bloomberg's $393 billion FY2027 total-revenue projection; applying the latest 92% Data Center mix puts it at about $362 billion, in rung 3. The implied Data Center outcome is about $375 billion: carry the Q1-to-Q2 total-revenue sequential growth rate through Q3 and Q4 to get roughly $387 billion before the stand-alone CPU estimate, apply the 92% mix to get about $356 billion, then add the dependency's approximately $19 billion CPU estimate. This remains below rung 2's $400 billion boundary and is consistent with v14's approximately $406 billion total-revenue forecast: Data Center does not exceed the whole. Q2 growth-acceleration guidance and the multi-year platform visibility support the run-rate, but the $1 trillion visibility spans calendar 2026 and 2027 and is not a FY2027 Data Center revenue figure. China restrictions and memory tightness are offsets, but do not change the implied figure enough to cross a rung boundary.\nThe quantified run-rate remains about $375 billion and stays in rung 3. The additional evidence on customer mix is qualitative and does not change the key estimate enough to cross a rung boundary.\ncite e74,e255,e170,e171,e289,e116,e109,e162,e604 dependencies v14 themes v10:first,v5:first,v6:first\npush first=e74,e255,e170,e171,e162,e604 last=e289,e116,e109 none=\nv7 2 rung 3 changed True\nBloomberg's $393 billion FY2027 total-revenue projection, translated at the roughly 92% Data Center mix, implies about $362 billion of Data Center revenue; that reference is in rung 3.\nAbout $375 billion: carrying the Q1-to-Q2 total-revenue sequential growth rate through Q3 and Q4 implies about $387 billion for FY2027; applying the roughly 92% Data Center mix gives about $356 billion, then adding v14's approximately $19 billion stand-alone CPU estimate gives about $375 billion.\nThe carried market reference is Bloomberg's $393 billion FY2027 total-revenue projection. Applying the latest roughly 92% Data Center mix puts that reference near $362 billion, in rung 3. The forecast also remains in rung 3: the current-quarter total-revenue guide and the existing run-rate imply about $387 billion of FY2027 revenue before the stand-alone CPU estimate; applying the Data Center mix gives about $356 billion, and adding v14's approximately $19 billion stand-alone CPU estimate gives about $375 billion. That is within rung 3 and below its $400 billion boundary. Q1 growth, the Q2 guide, order visibility and expanding demand support the run-rate; zero China shipments, rising domestic substitution, competition and memory constraints temper it. The new figures do not move the implied estimate across a rung boundary, and approximately $375 billion remains below v14's approximately $406 billion total-revenue forecast.\nThe rung and central implied estimate remain unchanged at rung 3 and about $375 billion. The current evidence adds material figures affecting the supporting balance—notably the surveyed China domestic-budget shift from 30% to 46% and TSMC's first-half growth and 2026 outlook—so the supporting facts have changed even though they do not warrant changing the run-rate estimate or rung.\ncite e74,e255,e170,e171,e459,e604,e162,e289,e153,e109,e227,e116,e556,e559 dependencies v14 themes v10:first,v5:first,v6:first\npush first=e74,e255,e170,e171,e459,e604,e162,e556,e559 last=e289,e153,e109,e227,e116 none=\nv7 3 rung 3 changed True\nBloomberg's $393 billion FY2027 total-revenue projection, translated using the reported 92% Data Center mix, implies about $362 billion of Data Center revenue and sits in rung 3.\nAbout $375 billion for FY2027 Data Center: Q1 revenue of $81.615 billion and Q2 total-revenue guidance of $91 billion imply about $387 billion of FY2027 total revenue when the Q1-to-Q2 sequential growth rate is carried through Q3 and Q4; 92% of that is about $356 billion, plus the dependency's approximately $19 billion stand-alone CPU estimate.\nThe market reference is Bloomberg's $393 billion FY2027 total-revenue projection; applying the reported 92% Data Center mix puts the reference at about $362 billion, in rung 3. The forecast remains about $375 billion: Q1 total revenue of $81.615 billion and Q2 guidance of $91 billion imply about $387 billion for FY2027 if the Q1-to-Q2 sequential growth rate carries through Q3 and Q4; applying the 92% mix gives about $356 billion, and the approximately $19 billion stand-alone CPU contribution in the dependency's forecast framing brings Data Center to about $375 billion. That is consistent with NVIDIA's approximately $406 billion total-revenue forecast and remains below the $400 billion rung boundary. Q1 growth, Q2 acceleration guidance, platform visibility, and the CFO's description of AI clouds as the fastest-growing demand group support the outlook, but the multi-year platform visibility is not allocated to FY2027 Data Center and the fresh demand detail is not quantified. Memory tightness, competition, and zero H20 shipments to China are counterweights; together the evidence does not imply the additional $25 billion needed to cross $400 billion.\nThe rung and implied figure remain at rung 3 and about $375 billion. The CFO's new characterization of AI clouds as roughly half of Data Center demand and likely the fastest-growing customer group adds a relevant demand-quality fact; it is supportive but not quantified enough to change the forecast. Memory-supply concerns and China-related evidence are countervailing inputs, not a new quantified revision.\ncite e74,e170,e171,e255,e458,e604,e116,e117,e109,e289 dependencies v14 themes v10:first,v5:first,v6:first\npush first=e74,e170,e171,e255,e458,e604 last=e116,e117,e109,e289 none=\nSOURCES\ne74 N1N2N3O1O2O3 NVIDIA’s Data Center segment generated $75.25 billion in revenue in FY2027 Q1, equal to 92% of total sales, up 92% year over year and 21% sequentially.\ne109 N1N2N3O1O2O3 Amazon and Alphabet are extending access to their in-house AI processors, adding competition for NVIDIA’s AI computing products.\ne116 N2N3O1O2O3 Micron said tight memory-market conditions could last until 2028.\ne117 N2N3O3 The author said new capacity for many companies in the memory-chip industry would not be online until 2027 or later.\ne153 N2N3O2 Chinese company executives surveyed by Bloomberg Intelligence said they plan to allocate 46% of their AI-accelerator budgets to domestic products over the next 12 months, up from 30% currently.\ne156 N2N3 The Bloomberg Intelligence survey expects Nvidia’s market share in China to shrink as H20 chips become harder to find and local suppliers gain share.\ne162 O1O2 Bank of America reported that NVIDIA’s sales to hyperscalers rose 115% year over year, nearly twice the growth in cloud capital expenditures.\ne170 N1N2N3O1O2O3 NVIDIA management guided to accelerating Data Center segment revenue growth in the second quarter; no amount is stated.\ne171 N1N2N3O1O2O3 NVIDIA management cited roughly $1 trillion of revenue visibility for Blackwell and Vera Rubin processors across calendar 2026 and 2027, anchored by multiyear commitments from hyperscalers and large enterprise customers.\ne204 N2 NVIDIA reported Q1 FY2027 revenue of $81.6 billion, up 85% year over year and 20% sequentially, including Data Center revenue of $75.2 billion.\ne227 N1N2N3O2 Broadcom plans to ramp production of custom AI chips for Meta Platforms, Anthropic, and OpenAI starting late 2026 and continuing into 2027.\ne255 N1N2N3O1O2O3 NVIDIA management guided FY2027 Q2 revenue to approximately $91 billion, assuming no China Data Center revenue.\ne289 N1N2N3O1O2O3 NVIDIA shipped zero H20 chips to China in the quarter discussed, compared with $4.6 billion of H20 shipments a year earlier.\ne294 N1 Morgan Stanley forecasts that Google, Amazon, Microsoft, and Meta will spend over $1 trillion on AI infrastructure in 2027 and add 19.5 GW of incremental compute capacity.\ne446 N1 NVIDIA's revised authorization restrictions excluded more than half of its previous customers; companies that failed the initial check can reapply.\ne458 O3 NVIDIA reported Q1 FY2027 total revenue of $81.615 billion, up 85.2% year over year, and Data Center revenue of $75.246 billion; Networking revenue rose 199% year over year.\ne459 N1N3O2 Jensen Huang said NVIDIA had more orders than at his previous GTC remarks and expected to keep its supply chain busy for several more years.\ne501 N1 NVIDIA announced a plan with Japan's Noetra Corp. to build a 140-megawatt AI factory with 27,500 Rubin GPUs and 13,750 Vera CPUs for Japan's FRONTia program; no deployment date was given.\ne538 N1 NVIDIA said the planned Noetra AI factory will deliver 140 megawatts of data-center capacity using its Vera Rubin DSX architecture and related networking and DPU products.\ne556 N1O2 TSMC's first-half 2026 revenue was up 35.6% year over year.\ne557 N1 TSMC's June 2026 revenue grew 67.9% year over year.\ne558 N1 TSMC plans capital expenditures of $52 billion to $56 billion in calendar 2026.\ne559 N1O2 TSMC's full-year 2026 outlook was for near 30% revenue growth in U.S. dollars, dependent on 3nm and 2nm ramps holding through the second half.\ne604 N1N2N3O1O2O3 NVIDIA CFO Colette Kress said AI clouds account for roughly half of Data Center demand alongside traditional hyperscalers, and are likely the fastest-growing customer group.\nv5 N1N2N3O1O2O3 - v5 (ai-capex, due 2027-03-31) How much will the four largest AI hyperscalers spend on data-center capital expenditures in calendar 2026?: now rung 3 $600 billion to below $700 billion; judged 2026-07-17; reasons: The market reference is around $650 billion for calendar-2026 data-center capex, which sits in rung 3. The evidence still implies roughly $650 billion for the specific data-center measure: the roughly $800 billion estimate is for broader AI infrastructure, and the $725 billion pledge has no stated s\nv6 N1N2N3O1O2O3 - v6 (ai-capex, due 2028-03-31) How much will the four largest AI hyperscalers spend on data-center capital expenditures in calendar 2027?: now rung 3 $900 billion to below $1.1 trillion; judged 2026-07-16; reasons: The reference is NVIDIA management’s forecast of more than $1 trillion in 2027, after projected 2026 spending of $650 billion; that reference sits in rung 3. The evidence supports spending above $1 trillion, but does not imply the $1.1 trillion needed to enter rung 4. The qualitative power constrain\nv10 N1N2N3O1O2O3 - v10 (chip-export-controls, due 2027-01-31) Will China's authorities authorize Chinese AI companies to purchase Nvidia H200 chips by 2027-01-31?: now rung 1 Yes; judged 2026-07-17; reasons: No deadline-specific consensus or company guidance is supplied. The observable status quo—no established Chinese authorization—sits at rung 2, but the forecast is for the outcome by 2027-01-31, not the status today. Reports citing the same underlying claim say Chinese officials told named firms they\nv14 N1N2N3O1O2O3 - v14 (open; first_is_bullish) How much total revenue will NVIDIA report for FY2027?: now rung 2 Bullish, judged 2026-07-18\n\ncontrol-1:8790 Thesis v14 (NVDA, asset, focus \"NVIDIA FY2027 total revenue\", FY2027, due 2027-02-28): How much total revenue will NVIDIA report for FY2027?\nv12 1 rung 3 changed True\nBloomberg's $393 billion FY2027 revenue estimate is the market reference and sits in rung 3; Zacks' estimate of 80% year-over-year growth also maps to rung 3.\nAbout $386 billion from Q1 revenue of $81.6 billion plus Q2 guidance of $91 billion and two further quarters carrying forward the guided 11% sequential growth rate (about $101 billion and $112 billion). The upside evidence modestly raises the forecast around that run-rate, but does not support a quantified move above $400 billion.\nThe reference is Bloomberg's $393 billion FY2027 estimate, in rung 3; Zacks' 80% growth estimate also implies about $389 billion against FY2026 revenue of $215.9 billion. The disclosed Q1 revenue of $81.6 billion and Q2 guidance of $91 billion, carried forward at the guided 11% sequential growth rate, imply about $386 billion for the year ($81.6B + $91B + roughly $101B + roughly $112B). New support from expected AI investment, NVIDIA's processor-sales target, and improved HBM access raises the upside risk, while memory constraints and export-related limits temper it. The $1 trillion target spans Blackwell and Vera Rubin sales across multiple periods and is not evidence of FY2027 volume in hand; the $500 billion SK initiative is not NVIDIA revenue. On balance, the forecast remains around the high-$380 billions, below the $400 billion rung boundary.\nThe rung is unchanged, but the updated China export-control theme and new disclosures on 2027 GPU deployments and the SK partnership alter the supporting forecast balance. They add upside without providing enough quantified FY2027 revenue to move the implied outcome across $400 billion.\ncite e195,e669,e751,e577,e238,e240,e851,e817,e924,e234,e949,e906,e321,e231,e284,e599,e252,e1263 dependencies  themes v10:first,v19:last,v25:last,v26:last,v27:last,v5:first,v6:first\npush first=e238,e240,e851,e817,e924,e234,e949,e906,e599 last=e321,e231,e284,e252,e1263 none=e195,e669,e751,e577\nv12 2 rung 3 changed True\nWall Street's average FY2027 revenue estimate is $393 billion, in rung 3 ($350 billion to less than $400 billion).\nAbout $395 billion: Q1 revenue of $81.6 billion plus Q2 guidance of $91 billion, carrying the 11% sequential growth forward to about $101 billion in Q3 and $112 billion in Q4, gives a roughly $386 billion run-rate. Strong demand and easing export-policy signals justify a modest uplift, partly offset by supply and China-related constraints.\nThe market reference is Wall Street's FY2027 estimate of $393 billion, which sits in rung 3. The reported Q1 revenue of $81.6 billion and $91 billion Q2 guidance, with the guided 11% sequential growth carried through Q3 and Q4, imply about $386 billion ($81.6B + $91B + $101B + $112B). Strong demand signals and export-policy developments lift the forecast modestly above that run-rate, but the $1 trillion Blackwell/Vera Rubin sales target spans products and periods and is not a stated FY2027 revenue figure; memory and China-related constraints offset some upside. The resulting implied outcome is around $395 billion, still below the $400 billion boundary.\nThe rung remains 3, but the implied outcome and supporting facts have changed: the recent evidence and updated themes support a modest uplift from the prior roughly $386 billion run-rate estimate. The uplift does not take the forecast to $400 billion.\ncite e669,e458,e851,e878,e975,e924,e284,e231,e321,e599,e1260 dependencies  themes v10:first,v19:first,v25:first,v26:first,v27:first,v5:first,v6:first\npush first=e458,e851,e878,e975,e924,e599 last=e284,e231,e321,e1260 none=e669\nv12 3 rung 3 changed True\nBloomberg's $393 billion FY2027 revenue estimate is the market reference and sits in rung 3 ($350 billion to below $400 billion).\nAbout $386 billion: Q1 revenue of $81.6 billion plus Q2 guidance of $91 billion, then carrying the guided 11% sequential growth through Q3 and Q4 (about $101 billion and $112 billion).\nThe market reference is Bloomberg's $393 billion FY2027 estimate, which sits in rung 3; the separate 80% growth estimate applied to FY2026 revenue of about $215.9 billion also implies about $389 billion, in rung 3. The reported Q1 revenue of $81.6 billion and Q2 guidance of $91 billion, up 11% sequentially, imply about $386 billion for FY2027 if that sequential growth carries through Q3 and Q4. Positive demand signals—including the $1 trillion Blackwell/Vera Rubin sales target, AWS's planned GPU deployments and strong capex expectations—raise the upside case, but the product target spans products and calendar years and is not quantified as FY2027 NVIDIA revenue; the SK initiative is likewise not sized as NVIDIA revenue. Export and memory constraints temper the outlook. The evidence therefore does not support crossing the $400 billion boundary.\nThe rung is unchanged, but the supporting evidence and policy backdrop have changed: the current view includes newer demand and supply signals and updated export-control and capex theme conclusions. These alter the balance of support without moving the implied outcome across a rung boundary.\ncite e195,e669,e751,e577,e238,e851,e878,e975,e817,e234,e459,e460,e949,e284,e1263,e231,e321,e599 dependencies  themes v10:first,v19:first,v25:first,v26:first,v27:none,v5:first,v6:first\npush first=e238,e851,e878,e975,e817,e234,e459,e460,e599 last=e284,e1263,e231,e321 none=e195,e669,e751,e577,e949\nv7 1 rung 3 changed True\nBloomberg's FY2027 sales estimate of $393 billion (also reflected in Wall Street estimates) is the market reference and sits in rung 3.\nAbout $386 billion: Q1 revenue of $81.6 billion plus Q2 guidance of $91 billion, with the guided roughly 11% sequential growth carried forward to about $101 billion in Q3 and $112 billion in Q4.\nThe reference is approximately $393 billion for FY2027, based on Bloomberg and Wall Street estimates, and sits in rung 3. The latest reported quarter and Q2 guidance imply about $386 billion if the guided 11% sequential growth carries forward through Q3 and Q4. New supply-partnership and demand signals are supportive, but the $500 billion SK initiative is not NVIDIA revenue guidance, and the $1 trillion Blackwell/Vera Rubin sales figure spans products and periods; neither supplies enough FY2027 revenue to move the forecast across $400 billion. China-related constraints and reported memory tightness also temper the upside. Updated export-policy themes are, on balance, supportive, but do not change the implied figure enough to cross a rung boundary.\nThe rung remains 3 and the core Q1/Q2 run-rate calculation remains below $400 billion. Since the previous judgement, the in-force picture includes additional NVIDIA–SK supply and investment disclosures and updated export-policy conclusions; these change the supporting facts and policy balance, but do not establish enough FY2027 revenue to cross the rung boundary.\ncite e195,e458,e851,e878,e577,e459,e460,e975,e949,e906,e234,e599,e284,e252,e231,e321,e751 dependencies  themes v10:first,v19:first,v25:first,v26:first,v27:last,v5:first,v6:first\npush first=e458,e851,e878,e577,e459,e460,e975,e906,e234,e599 last=e284,e252,e231,e321 none=e195,e949,e751\nv7 2 rung 3 changed True\nBloomberg's FY2027 revenue estimate of $393 billion is the reference and sits in rung 3 ($350 billion to below $400 billion). Zacks' estimate of 80% growth is also consistent with that rung, using FY2026 revenue of about $215.9 billion.\nAbout $387 billion: starting with Q1 revenue of $81.615 billion and Q2 guidance of $91 billion, carry the approximately 11.5% sequential increase implied by those figures forward to estimate Q3 at about $101.5 billion and Q4 at about $113.2 billion. The total is below $400 billion and within rung 3.\nThe forecast remains in rung 3. The quarterly run-rate is just below $400 billion, while the $1 trillion Blackwell/Vera Rubin sales target and broad partnership figures do not establish how much NVIDIA will recognize in FY2027. Strong demand and supply-commitment signals support the upper part of the rung, but memory constraints and China-related limits offset them; none supplies a sufficiently quantified FY2027 revenue revision to cross $400 billion.\nThe rung is unchanged, but the current supporting facts include the reported $119 billion in supply-related commitments and the long-term SK hynix memory partnership, alongside additional quantified demand and constraint signals. These update the evidence supporting the forecast without moving its implied outcome across a rung boundary.\ncite e195,e669,e751,e458,e851,e878,e577,e975,e460,e949,e906,e231,e321,e284,e252,e599 dependencies  themes v10:first,v19:first,v25:first,v26:first,v27:first,v5:first,v6:first\npush first=e458,e851,e878,e975,e460,e949,e906,e599 last=e231,e321,e284,e252 none=e195,e669,e751,e577\nv7 3 rung 3 changed True\nBloomberg's $393 billion FY2027 estimate is the market reference and sits in rung 3. Zacks' estimate of 80% growth on FY2026 revenue of $215.9 billion implies about $389 billion, also rung 3.\nAbout $386 billion: take reported Q1 revenue of $81.615 billion and Q2 guidance of $91 billion, then carry forward roughly 11% sequential growth for Q3 and Q4 (about $101 billion and $112 billion).\nThe market reference is Bloomberg's FY2027 estimate of $393 billion, which sits in rung 3; the Zacks estimate of 80% growth on FY2026 revenue of $215.9 billion is about $389 billion and also sits in rung 3. The reported Q1 revenue of $81.615 billion and Q2 guidance of $91 billion imply about $386 billion for the year if Q2's roughly 11% sequential growth carries forward to Q3 and Q4: $81.6 billion + $91 billion + about $101 billion + about $112 billion. That is below the $400 billion rung-2 boundary. The $1 trillion Blackwell/Vera Rubin sales forecast and the $500 billion SK initiative are upside signals, but they span products or periods and do not state NVIDIA FY2027 revenue; they do not justify lifting the estimate across that boundary. Continued memory and China-related constraints are offsets. The evidence therefore supports rung 3, not merely because the consensus is there.\nThe rung and the roughly $386 billion run-rate estimate remain unchanged. However, the newly disclosed SK initiative valued at more than $500 billion and the report that Wistron has begun mass-producing GB300 add material upside context; neither specifies NVIDIA revenue recognized in FY2027, so neither changes the implied figure or crosses a rung boundary.\ncite e195,e669,e751,e577,e458,e460,e851,e975,e949,e735,e234,e231,e232,e321,e284,e376,e599,e883,e882,e1262,e1263 dependencies  themes v10:first,v19:first,v25:first,v26:first,v27:none,v5:first,v6:first\npush first=e577,e458,e460,e851,e975,e949,e735,e234,e599,e883,e882 last=e231,e232,e321,e284,e376,e1262,e1263 none=e195,e669,e751\nSOURCES\ne195 N1N3O1O2O3 Bloomberg data project NVIDIA FY2027 sales of $393 billion, up 82%, and profits of $228 billion, up 90%.\ne231 N1N2N3O1O2O3 Samsung, SK Hynix, and Micron reportedly sold all their 2026 HBM output under long-term contracts, with hyperscalers including Microsoft, Google, Meta, and Amazon buying the supply.\ne232 O3 Micron suggested memory supply may not meaningfully normalize until 2027 and may return gradually to balance by 2028; Kearney’s PERLab reportedly projected the shortage could last into 2030.\ne234 N1N3O1O3 NVIDIA said AWS plans to deploy 2 million additional NVIDIA GPUs during 2027–2028.\ne238 N1N3 NVIDIA reported fiscal Q1 2027 total revenue of $81.6 billion, up 85% year over year.\ne240 N1 NVIDIA management forecast approximately $91 billion in fiscal Q2 2027 revenue, assuming no China data-center sales.\ne252 N1O1O2 A Bloomberg survey reported that Chinese technology companies expect to allocate about 46% of their AI chip spending to domestic suppliers over the next year, up from about 30% currently.\ne284 N1N2N3O1O2O3 NVIDIA shipped no H20 compute products to China in Q1 FY2027 and excluded Data Center compute revenue from China from its Q2 FY2027 guidance.\ne321 N1N2N3O1O2O3 SK Hynix is slowing its HBM4 ramp and redirecting capacity to conventional DRAM, where shortages have pushed operating margins above HBM margins.\ne376 O3 The Financial Times reported that NVIDIA more than halved its list of Asian customers authorised to buy AI chips after creating a new compliance-checked whitelist to prevent shipments reaching China.\ne458 N2O1O2O3 NVIDIA reported Q1 FY2027 total revenue of $81.615 billion, up 85.2% year over year, and Data Center revenue of $75.246 billion; Networking revenue rose 199% year over year.\ne459 N3O1 Jensen Huang said NVIDIA had more orders than at his previous GTC remarks and expected to keep its supply chain busy for several more years.\ne460 N3O1O2O3 NVIDIA reported $119.0 billion in supply-related commitments and guided to Q2 revenue of $91.0 billion, excluding China Data Center compute sales.\ne577 N1N3O1O2O3 NVIDIA reported FY2026 revenue of close to $215.9 billion, up 65.5% year over year.\ne599 N1N2N3O1O2O3 On July 10, 2026, the US Commerce Department moved the UAE into its highest-trust export tier, easing export authorization for NVIDIA Blackwell processors to UAE entities.\ne669 N1N2N3O2O3 Wall Street's average estimates put NVIDIA total revenue at $393 billion in FY2027 and $559 billion in FY2028.\ne735 O3 Wistron’s first U.S. manufacturing facility, D1 in Fort Worth, has begun mass-producing NVIDIA’s GB300 Grace Blackwell Ultra Superchip.\ne751 N1N3O1O2O3 Zacks consensus estimates NVIDIA FY2027 revenue growth of 80% year over year.\ne817 N1N3 NVIDIA has lined up potential revenue of $1 trillion from Vera Rubin and Blackwell AI processor sales across 2026 and 2027.\ne851 N1N2N3O1O2O3 NVIDIA guided to $91 billion in revenue, plus or minus 2%, and 75% non-GAAP gross margin for fiscal Q2 2027, excluding Data Center compute revenue from China.\ne878 N2N3O1O2 NVIDIA's current-quarter revenue outlook calls for $91 billion, up 11% sequentially.\ne882 O3 DeepSeek founder Liang Wenfeng said DeepSeek expected to receive large batches of Nvidia-powered systems in the coming months; no quantity was specified.\ne883 O3 The Trump administration permitted certain Nvidia AI chip sales into China; the item gives no date or further details.\ne906 N1O1O2 SK hynix and NVIDIA are entering a long-term AI memory partnership intended to give NVIDIA stable supply of next-generation AI memory.\ne924 N1N2 The author says NVIDIA's Vera Rubin platform has ramped into full production and has demand visibility in the trillions of dollars.\ne949 N1N3O1O2O3 Nvidia said its AI initiative with SK Group will involve more than US$500 billion in business in total.\ne975 N2N3O1O2O3 Jensen Huang forecast combined Blackwell and Vera Rubin sales of $1 trillion through 2027.\ne1260 N2 NVIDIA created a customer whitelist and investigated firms it plans to keep selling to, sending staff to customer data centers for verification at the White House's urging.\ne1262 O3 The U.S. government takes a 25% revenue share from sales of licensed older NVIDIA GPUs to China, with licenses reviewed case by case.\ne1263 N1N3O3 Reported licensed sales of older NVIDIA GPUs to China total about 75,000 units across 10 Chinese companies.\nv5 N1N2N3O1O2O3 - v5 (ai-capex, due 2027-03-31) How much will the four largest AI hyperscalers spend on data-center capital expenditures in calendar 2026?: now rung 4 $700 billion to below $800 billion; judged 2026-07-29; updated since this thesis was last judged; reasons: The reference is the roughly $650 billion plan for the four hyperscalers, which sits in rung 3. The revised Alphabet guidance and other spending disclosures support the prior estimate of about $705 billion; the new $750 billion estimate for tech giants’ data-center spending adds upside, but its grou\nv6 N1N2N3O1O2O3 - v6 (ai-capex, due 2028-03-31) How much will the four largest AI hyperscalers spend on data-center capital expenditures in calendar 2027?: now rung 3 $900 billion to below $1.1 trillion; judged 2026-07-28; reasons: The reference is NVIDIA management's forecast of more than $1 trillion in 2027, following projected 2026 spending of $650 billion; that reference sits in rung 3. The forecast and qualitative indications of further spending support an outcome around or somewhat above $1 trillion, but none quantifies \nv10 N1N2N3O1O2O3 - v10 (chip-export-controls, due 2027-01-31) Will China's authorities authorize Chinese AI companies to purchase Nvidia H200 chips by 2027-01-31?: now rung 1 Yes; judged 2026-07-29; updated since this thesis was last judged; reasons: No explicit market consensus is supplied. The observable reference is that Chinese authorization has not been confirmed, which sits at rung 2. The forecast is rung 1: specific reports say Chinese officials told Alibaba, ByteDance and DeepSeek they would be permitted to buy some H200s, with limited, \nv19 N1N2N3O1O2O3 - v19 (chip-export-controls, due 2026-12-31) Will the U.S. Senate pass the Remote Access Security Act by 2026-12-31?: now rung 2 No; judged 2026-07-29; reasons: The observable reference is rung 2: the bill had passed the House but was still awaiting Senate action on July 23; no separate market consensus is supplied. That status is not itself proof of the year-end outcome, but the evidence shows no Senate action or schedule in the remaining period. The White\nv25 N1N2N3O1O2O3 - v25 (chip-export-controls, due 2027-01-31) Will Taiwan's Ministry of Economic Affairs introduce performance-threshold export controls for advanced chips by 2027-01-31?: now rung 2 No; judged 2026-07-28; reasons: The required outcome is formal adoption or entry into force by 2027-01-31, not consultation or consideration. Consultations are confirmed, but the Ministry gave no timeline and the controls were only reported as being weighed. That makes adoption by the deadline less likely than no adoption; the evi\nv26 N1N2N3O1O2O3 - v26 (chip-export-controls, due 2027-01-31) Will Taiwan introduce a criminal ban on AI chip exports to China by 2027-01-31?: now rung 2 No; judged 2026-07-29; updated since this thesis was last judged; reasons: No market consensus is supplied; the status-quo reference is no criminal ban by the deadline (rung 2). Taiwan's statement that it is considering a ban is a meaningful signal toward Yes, but it supplies neither a formal proposal nor a timetable. On the evidence in force, introduction by 2027-01-31 is\nv27 N1N2N3O1O2O3 - v27 (chip-export-controls, due 2027-01-31) Will the U.S. Department of Commerce conclude its investigation into Chinese firms' access to advanced U.S. chips by 2027-01-31?: now rung 2 No; judged 2026-07-29; updated since this thesis was last judged; reasons: No explicit market consensus, guidance, or stated completion deadline is supplied. The available reference is the investigation's status on 2026-07-29: it is formally underway, which maps to rung 2 absent evidence of a scheduled conclusion. The evidence describes it as likely to remain an ongoing po\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_18","type":"command_execution","command":"/opt/homebrew/bin/zsh -lc \"python3 -c 'import json\np=json.load(open(\\\"poc/data/citation-probe-2026-11-22/packet-C.json\\\"))\nimport sys,re\nfor n in map(int,sys.argv[1:]):\n x=p[n]\n print(\\\"\\\\n\\\"+x[\\\"key\\\"],x[\\\"question\\\"])\n used={}\n for arm in (\\\"v12\\\",\\\"v7\\\"):\n  for idx,a in enumerate(x[arm],1):\n   print(arm,idx,\\\"rung\\\",a[\\\"rung\\\"],\\\"changed\\\",a[\\\"changed\\\"])\n   for k in (\\\"reference\\\",\\\"implied_outcome\\\",\\\"reasons\\\",\\\"why_changed\\\"): print(a[k])\n   print(\\\"cite\\\",\\\",\\\".join(a[\\\"cites\\\"]),\\\"dependencies\\\",\\\",\\\".join(a[\\\"cites_views\\\"]),\\\"themes\\\",\\\",\\\".join(y[\\\"view_id\\\"]+\\\":\\\"+y[\\\"push\\\"].replace(\\\"toward_\\\",\\\"\\\") for y in a[\\\"theme_pushes\\\"]))\n   ps={k:[] for k in (\\\"toward_first\\\",\\\"toward_last\\\",\\\"none\\\")}\n   for y in a[\\\"pushes\\\"]:ps[y[\\\"push\\\"]].append(y[\\\"evidence_id\\\"])\n   print(\\\"push\\\",*(k.replace(\\\"toward_\\\",\\\"\\\")+\\\"=\\\"+\\\",\\\".join(v) for k,v in ps.items()))\n   for t in {*a[\\\"cites\\\"],*a[\\\"cites_views\\\"],*(y[\\\"evidence_id\\\"] for y in a[\\\"pushes\\\"]),*(y[\\\"view_id\\\"] for y in a[\\\"theme_pushes\\\"])}:used.setdefault(t,[]).append((\\\"N\\\" if arm==\\\"v12\\\" else \\\"O\\\")+str(idx))\n print(\\\"SOURCES\\\")\n for k in sorted(used,key=lambda k:(k[0],int(k[1:]) if k[1:].isdigit() else 99999)):\n  v=x[\\\"quotes\\\"].get(k,\\\"MISSING\\\")\n  print(k,\\\"\\\".join(used[k]),re.sub(r\\\"\"'^- '\"\\\\S+ \\\\[[\"'^'\"\\\\n]+?\\\\] \\\",\\\"\\\",v.split(\\\"\\\\n\\\",1)[0]))\n' 8 9\"","aggregated_output":"\nlinked-1:8776 Thesis v14 (NVDA, asset, focus \"NVIDIA FY2027 total revenue\", FY2027, due 2027-02-28): How much total revenue will NVIDIA report for FY2027?\nv12 1 rung 3 changed True\nBloomberg's $393 billion FY2027 revenue estimate (also reported as Wall Street's average) is the market reference and sits in rung 3. Zacks' 80% growth estimate on FY2026 revenue of about $215.9 billion implies roughly $388.6 billion, also rung 3.\nAbout $387 billion: Q1 revenue of $81.615 billion and Q2 guidance of $91 billion imply approximately 11.5% sequential growth; carrying it through Q3 and Q4 gives about $81.615B + $91B + $101.5B + $113.2B. This is within rung 3's $350 billion to less than $400 billion range.\nThe market reference is Bloomberg's $393 billion FY2027 estimate, which sits in rung 3; Wall Street's reported average is also $393 billion. Q1 revenue of $81.615 billion followed by Q2 guidance of $91 billion implies about 11.5% sequential growth; carrying that growth through Q3 and Q4 gives roughly $387 billion for FY2027. That is below the reference, but it remains between rung 3's $350 billion and rung 2's $400 billion boundaries. The current v8 forecast of roughly $17 billion in stand-alone CPU sales is part of total revenue, not additive to this run-rate, and is consistent with the total. New evidence of GB300 mass production and deployment plans supports execution, while the HBM4 capacity redirection and continued exclusion of China compute sales weigh against the upside; neither quantifies a shift sufficient to cross a boundary.\nThe rung remains 3, but the current v8 dependency forecast is roughly $17 billion in stand-alone CPU sales, replacing the previous comparison's $8–9 billion figure. Recent evidence also adds Wistron's GB300 mass-production start and SK Hynix's HBM4 capacity redirection; the run-rate estimate remains around $387 billion and does not cross a rung boundary.\ncite e195,e458,e460,e669,e751,e234,e321,e284,e735,e459 dependencies v8 themes v10:first,v5:first,v6:first\npush first=e458,e460,e234,e735,e459 last=e321,e284 none=e195,e669,e751\nv12 2 rung 3 changed True\nBloomberg/Wall Street's $393 billion FY2027 estimate is the main market reference and sits in rung 3. Zacks' 80% growth estimate, applied to FY2026 revenue of $215.9 billion, implies about $389 billion, also rung 3; these references locate market expectations but do not determine the forecast.\nQ1 revenue of $81.615 billion followed by Q2 guidance of $91 billion implies sequential growth of about 11.5%; carrying that growth into Q3 and Q4 gives approximately $101.5 billion and $113.1 billion, or about $387 billion for FY2027.\nThe market reference is Bloomberg/Wall Street's $393 billion FY2027 estimate, which is in rung 3; the separate 80% growth estimate also implies about $389 billion from FY2026 revenue of $215.9 billion, likewise rung 3. The evidence-based run-rate is about $387 billion, below rung 2's $400 billion threshold, so it does not cross a boundary. New GB300 mass production and longer-dated demand plans support the upside, but do not quantify additional FY2027 revenue; China sales exclusions and HBM supply pressure weigh against it. The current v8 forecast of roughly $17 billion in stand-alone CPU sales is a component of total revenue, not an amount to add to the total-revenue run-rate, and is consistent with the forecast.\nThe rung remains 3 and the total-revenue run-rate remains below $400 billion, but the supporting facts have changed: v8 now forecasts roughly $17 billion of stand-alone CPU sales rather than the previous $8–9 billion, and Wistron has begun mass-producing GB300. Neither fact provides a quantified increment to add to the total-revenue run-rate.\ncite e577,e458,e460,e669,e751,e735,e698,e234,e459,e284,e321,e599 dependencies v8 themes v10:first,v5:first,v6:first\npush first=e458,e460,e735,e698,e234,e459,e599 last=e284,e321 none=e577,e669,e751\nv12 3 rung 3 changed True\nBloomberg's FY2027 estimate of $393 billion is rung 3. Zacks' 80% revenue-growth consensus applied to FY2026 revenue of $215.9 billion implies about $388.6 billion, also rung 3.\nAbout $387.2 billion: Q1 revenue of $81.615 billion and Q2 guidance of $91 billion imply approximately 11.5% sequential growth; carrying that rate through Q3 and Q4 gives about $387.2 billion for FY2027.\nThe market reference is Bloomberg's FY2027 revenue estimate of $393 billion, which is rung 3; Zacks' 80% growth consensus, applied to FY2026 revenue of $215.9 billion, implies about $388.6 billion and is also rung 3. The run-rate implied by the evidence is about $387.2 billion: Q1 revenue of $81.615 billion followed by Q2 guidance of $91 billion is sequential growth of about 11.5%, carried into Q3 and Q4. That falls within rung 3's $350 billion–$400 billion range, so it does not cross a boundary. The current v8 conclusion forecasts about $17 billion of stand-alone CPU sales, a component of total revenue rather than an amount to add; the total-revenue forecast remains comfortably above it. AWS's 2027–2028 deployment plan and the Fort Worth start of GB300 mass production support execution, but do not quantify enough FY2027 revenue to move the estimate above $400 billion. China compute exclusions and the reported HBM4 ramp slowdown weigh the other way.\nThe rung remains 3, but the supporting facts have changed: the current v8 dependency forecasts about $17 billion in stand-alone CPU sales, versus the prior conclusion's $8–9 billion figure, and the newly supplied Zacks 80% growth estimate implies about $388.6 billion using FY2026 revenue. These changes do not take the total-revenue forecast across a rung boundary.\ncite e458,e240,e577,e669,e751,e234,e735,e284,e321 dependencies v8 themes v10:first,v5:first,v6:first\npush first=e458,e240,e751,e234,e735 last=e284,e321 none=e577,e669\nv7 1 rung 3 changed True\nBloomberg's FY2027 revenue estimate of $393 billion is the market reference and sits in rung 3 ($350 billion to less than $400 billion). Zacks' 80% growth estimate, applied to FY2026 revenue of $215.9 billion, implies about $388.6 billion, also rung 3.\nAbout $387 billion: Q1 FY2027 revenue of $81.615 billion and Q2 guidance of $91 billion imply roughly 11.5% sequential growth; carrying that guided growth through Q3 and Q4 gives about $101.5 billion and $113.2 billion, respectively, for approximately $387.3 billion across FY2027.\nThe reference is Bloomberg's $393 billion FY2027 estimate, which sits in rung 3; Zacks' 80% growth estimate on FY2026 revenue of $215.9 billion also implies about $388.6 billion, in rung 3. The guided-growth run-rate implies about $387 billion, below rung 2's $400 billion boundary. Strong orders, AWS's planned GPU deployment, production ramping, and the upward-pushing capex and export-control themes support demand, but none supplies a quantified FY2027 revenue increment to take the forecast across $400 billion. China Data Center compute is excluded from Q2 guidance, and memory constraints temper the upside. The current v8 estimate of roughly $17 billion in stand-alone CPU revenue is part of total revenue, not an amount to add to the run-rate.\nThe rung and central implied figure remain around $387 billion, but the in-force v5 capex theme has been updated since the prior judgement, and v8's current stand-alone CPU forecast is roughly $17 billion rather than the prior $8–9 billion context. These change the supporting context without providing a quantified increment that moves the forecast across the $400 billion boundary.\ncite e195,e751,e577,e458,e460,e459,e234,e735,e284,e321,e232,e231 dependencies v8 themes v10:first,v5:first,v6:first\npush first=e458,e460,e459,e234,e735 last=e284,e321,e232,e231 none=e195,e751,e577\nv7 2 rung 3 changed True\nBloomberg's $393 billion FY2027 revenue estimate is the market reference and sits in rung 3 ($350 billion to less than $400 billion).\nAbout $387–389 billion: carrying the 11.5% sequential increase from Q1 revenue of $81.615 billion to Q2 guidance of $91 billion through Q3 and Q4 implies about $387 billion; independently, FY2026 revenue of $215.9 billion grown by Zacks' 80% FY2027 estimate implies about $389 billion.\nThe reference is Bloomberg's $393 billion FY2027 estimate, which sits in rung 3; Zacks' 80% growth estimate also implies about $389 billion using FY2026 revenue of $215.9 billion. Q1 revenue of $81.615 billion and Q2 guidance of $91 billion imply about $387 billion for FY2027 when the 11.5% Q1-to-Q2 sequential growth rate is carried through Q3 and Q4. These estimates point to roughly $387–389 billion, below rung 2's $400 billion threshold and above rung 3's $350 billion floor. New evidence supports execution and demand, including mass production of GB300, but the $500 billion infrastructure plan spans several years and AWS's additional 2 million GPUs span 2027–2028, so neither is a quantified FY2027 NVIDIA revenue revision. China sales exclusions and HBM constraints weigh against an upward move. The v8 forecast of roughly $17 billion in stand-alone CPU sales is a component of total revenue, not an amount to add to the total run-rate.\nThe rung remains 3, but the newly supplied Zacks 80% FY2027 growth estimate and FY2026 revenue base provide a quantified cross-check of about $389 billion, modestly updating the prior implied figure of about $387 billion. New execution and demand evidence is balanced by China and memory-supply constraints and does not support crossing $400 billion.\ncite e195,e751,e577,e458,e460,e735,e698,e234,e599,e284,e252,e231,e321 dependencies v8 themes v10:first,v5:first,v6:first\npush first=e458,e460,e735,e698,e234,e599 last=e284,e252,e231,e321 none=e195,e751,e577\nv7 3 rung 3 changed True\nBloomberg's FY2027 revenue estimate is $393 billion, in rung 3. Zacks' 80% growth estimate implies about $389 billion using FY2026 revenue of $215.9 billion, also in rung 3.\nAbout $387 billion: Q1 revenue of $81.615 billion followed by Q2 guidance of $91 billion implies roughly 11.5% sequential growth; carrying that growth through Q3 and Q4 gives approximately $101.5 billion and $113.2 billion, respectively, for about $387 billion total.\nThe reference is Bloomberg's $393 billion FY2027 estimate, which sits in rung 3; Zacks' 80% growth estimate also implies about $389 billion using FY2026 revenue of $215.9 billion. The Q1 result and Q2 guidance imply about $387 billion if the 11.5% sequential increase from Q1 to Q2 carries through Q3 and Q4. That is below the reference and rung 2's $400 billion boundary, but above rung 3's $350 billion floor. Production ramp evidence and planned deployments support the outlook, while memory constraints and export-related limits weigh against upside; neither supplies a quantified FY2027 change sufficient to cross a boundary. The v8 forecast of roughly $17 billion in stand-alone CPU revenue is a component of total revenue, not an amount to add to it, and is consistent with this total.\nThe rung remains 3 and the run-rate calculation remains near $387 billion. The supporting picture has changed: new production and deployment evidence leans upward, while the reported memory-supply constraints and tighter customer compliance controls lean downward; the current v8 dependency forecast is also roughly $17 billion rather than the prior $8–9 billion component estimate. These changes do not quantify a total-revenue shift across a rung boundary.\ncite e195,e238,e240,e232,e321,e234,e735,e252,e599,e376 dependencies v8 themes v10:first,v5:first,v6:first\npush first=e238,e240,e234,e735,e599 last=e195,e232,e321,e252,e376 none=\nSOURCES\ne195 N1O1O2O3 Bloomberg data project NVIDIA FY2027 sales of $393 billion, up 82%, and profits of $228 billion, up 90%.\ne231 O1O2 Samsung, SK Hynix, and Micron reportedly sold all their 2026 HBM output under long-term contracts, with hyperscalers including Microsoft, Google, Meta, and Amazon buying the supply.\ne232 O1O3 Micron suggested memory supply may not meaningfully normalize until 2027 and may return gradually to balance by 2028; Kearney’s PERLab reportedly projected the shortage could last into 2030.\ne234 N1N2N3O1O2O3 NVIDIA said AWS plans to deploy 2 million additional NVIDIA GPUs during 2027–2028.\ne238 O3 NVIDIA reported fiscal Q1 2027 total revenue of $81.6 billion, up 85% year over year.\ne240 N3O3 NVIDIA management forecast approximately $91 billion in fiscal Q2 2027 revenue, assuming no China data-center sales.\ne252 O2O3 A Bloomberg survey reported that Chinese technology companies expect to allocate about 46% of their AI chip spending to domestic suppliers over the next year, up from about 30% currently.\ne284 N1N2N3O1O2 NVIDIA shipped no H20 compute products to China in Q1 FY2027 and excluded Data Center compute revenue from China from its Q2 FY2027 guidance.\ne321 N1N2N3O1O2O3 SK Hynix is slowing its HBM4 ramp and redirecting capacity to conventional DRAM, where shortages have pushed operating margins above HBM margins.\ne376 O3 The Financial Times reported that NVIDIA more than halved its list of Asian customers authorised to buy AI chips after creating a new compliance-checked whitelist to prevent shipments reaching China.\ne458 N1N2N3O1O2 NVIDIA reported Q1 FY2027 total revenue of $81.615 billion, up 85.2% year over year, and Data Center revenue of $75.246 billion; Networking revenue rose 199% year over year.\ne459 N1N2O1 Jensen Huang said NVIDIA had more orders than at his previous GTC remarks and expected to keep its supply chain busy for several more years.\ne460 N1N2O1O2 NVIDIA reported $119.0 billion in supply-related commitments and guided to Q2 revenue of $91.0 billion, excluding China Data Center compute sales.\ne577 N2N3O1O2 NVIDIA reported FY2026 revenue of close to $215.9 billion, up 65.5% year over year.\ne599 N2O2O3 On July 10, 2026, the US Commerce Department moved the UAE into its highest-trust export tier, easing export authorization for NVIDIA Blackwell processors to UAE entities.\ne669 N1N2N3 Wall Street's average estimates put NVIDIA total revenue at $393 billion in FY2027 and $559 billion in FY2028.\ne698 N2O2 NVIDIA said it plans to produce up to $500 billion of AI infrastructure in the United States over the next several years with named partners including Wistron, TSMC, Foxconn, Corning, Lumentum, Coherent and Amkor.\ne735 N1N2N3O1O2O3 Wistron’s first U.S. manufacturing facility, D1 in Fort Worth, has begun mass-producing NVIDIA’s GB300 Grace Blackwell Ultra Superchip.\ne751 N1N2N3O1O2 Zacks consensus estimates NVIDIA FY2027 revenue growth of 80% year over year.\nv5 N1N2N3O1O2O3 - v5 (ai-capex, due 2027-03-31) How much will the four largest AI hyperscalers spend on data-center capital expenditures in calendar 2026?: now rung 3 $600 billion to below $700 billion; judged 2026-07-22; updated since this thesis was last judged; reasons: The reference is around $650 billion for the four hyperscalers’ 2026 data-center capex, which is rung 3. The newer company figures and higher-spending reports lean upward, but Google’s, Meta’s, and Amazon’s figures are total-capex guidance, not data-center amounts; Microsoft’s latest quarterly capex\nv6 N1N2N3O1O2O3 - v6 (ai-capex, due 2028-03-31) How much will the four largest AI hyperscalers spend on data-center capital expenditures in calendar 2027?: now rung 3 $900 billion to below $1.1 trillion; judged 2026-07-22; reasons: The reference is NVIDIA management's forecast of more than $1 trillion in 2027, alongside projected 2026 spending of $650 billion; that reference sits in rung 3. The evidence continues to imply spending around $1 trillion, but supplies no quantified amount that would take the forecast to the $1.1 tr\nv8 N1N2N3O1O2O3 - v8 (open; first_is_bullish) How much revenue will NVIDIA generate from stand-alone CPU sales in FY2027?: now rung 3 Neutral, judged 2026-07-22\nv10 N1N2N3O1O2O3 - v10 (chip-export-controls, due 2027-01-31) Will China's authorities authorize Chinese AI companies to purchase Nvidia H200 chips by 2027-01-31?: now rung 1 Yes; judged 2026-07-20; reasons: The reference is rung 2 for the observable status quo: no Chinese authorization is established in the evidence. However, the specific forward-looking reports that Chinese officials told named firms they would be permitted to buy limited H200 quantities, together with reported progress in import revi\n\nold-1:7758 Thesis v5 (NVDA, asset, focus \"NVIDIA total revenue in FY2028\", FY2028, due 2028-02-25): What will NVIDIA's total revenue be in FY2028?\nv12 1 rung 1 changed True\nWall Street's approximately $678 billion FY2028 estimate (e4594) is in rung 1.\nApproximately $699 billion: apply NVIDIA's approximately 70% FY2028 growth outlook to the $411 billion FY2027 analyst estimate ($411 billion × 1.70).\nThe market reference is approximately $678 billion for FY2028, which is rung 1. NVIDIA's supply-constrained FY2028 growth outlook of approximately 70%, applied to the $411 billion FY2027 analyst estimate, implies about $699 billion. The forecast remains above the $609.4 billion rung-1 boundary: supply constraints temper the demand signal, but the 70% outlook already reflects the stated supply limit. The 65% analyst growth estimate also implies about $678 billion, still rung 1. Updated AI-capex themes and recent supply and deployment evidence reinforce the forecast; infrastructure limits and customers' ability to delay purchases are counterweights, not enough to move it below the boundary.\nThe rung and implied revenue estimate remain essentially unchanged, but the newly reported TSMC August revenue growth and the updated AI-capex theme conclusions add current support to the same forecast.\ncite e2682,e2691,e3586,e4594,e4596,e4256,e4248,e4713,e2664,e3162,e4220,e4260,e2300,e2306 dependencies  themes v16:first,v22:first,v26:first,v27:first,v6:first,v7:first,v9:first\npush first=e2682,e2691,e4596,e4256,e4248,e4713,e2664,e3162 last=e3586,e4220,e4260,e2300,e2306 none=e4594\nv12 2 rung 1 changed True\nAnalyst consensus of approximately $678 billion for FY2028 (e4594), which sits in rung 1.\nApproximately $699 billion: apply NVIDIA's approximately 70% FY2028 growth outlook (e2682) to the $411 billion FY2027 analyst estimate (e4594).\nThe market reference is approximately $678 billion for FY2028, which is rung 1. NVIDIA's approximately 70% FY2028 growth outlook applied to the $411 billion FY2027 analyst estimate implies about $699 billion, around $89 billion above the rung-1 threshold. Supply constraints and possible customer deployment delays temper that forecast, but the stated growth outlook and demand signals still imply revenue above $609.4 billion; the AWS GPU plan spans two years and is not separately sized in revenue.\nThe rung and implied revenue remain consistent with the previous conclusion. The newly reported TSMC August revenue record, up 53.3% year over year, adds a distinct supplier-side capacity signal; it supports the forecast but does not quantify NVIDIA revenue or change the roughly $699 billion implication.\ncite e2682,e2691,e2722,e2846,e3586,e4220,e4256,e4260,e4574,e4594,e4713 dependencies  themes v16:last,v22:last,v26:last,v27:first,v9:last,v6:last,v7:last\npush first=e3586,e4220,e4260 last=e2682,e2691,e2722,e2846,e4256,e4574,e4713 none=e4594\nv12 3 rung 1 changed False\nThe latest cited analyst projection is $678 billion for FY2028 (e4594), placing the market reference in rung 1.\nApplying NVIDIA's approximately 70% FY2028 growth outlook to the $411 billion FY2027 revenue projection implies approximately $699 billion ($411 billion × 1.70).\nThe reference is the latest cited analyst projection of $678 billion, which sits in rung 1. NVIDIA's approximately 70% FY2028 growth outlook, applied to the $411 billion FY2027 revenue base, implies about $699 billion—roughly $89 billion above the $609.4 billion rung-1 boundary. Supply constraints temper that forecast, while demand indications, higher prices, customer deployment plans, and elevated hyperscaler capex support it; none of the supplied evidence quantifies a constraint sufficient to pull the implied outcome below the boundary.\nThe latest analyst projection still gives the same $411 billion FY2027 base and $678 billion FY2028 consensus cited in the previous conclusion, while NVIDIA's 70% outlook was reiterated rather than materially changed. The implied outcome and rung therefore remain the same.\ncite e4594,e4565,e3586,e4250,e3162,e2664,e4256 dependencies  themes v16:first,v22:first,v26:first,v27:last,v6:first,v7:first,v9:first\npush first=e4565,e4250,e3162,e2664,e4256 last=e3586 none=e4594\nv7 1 rung 1 changed False\nThe latest supplied analyst projection is $678 billion for FY2028 (e4594), placing the market reference in rung 1.\nAbout $699 billion: apply NVIDIA’s approximately 70% FY2028 growth outlook to the $411 billion FY2027 analyst estimate ($411 billion × 1.70).\nThe market reference is the $678 billion FY2028 analyst projection, which sits in rung 1. The implied outcome is about $699 billion: NVIDIA’s approximately 70% FY2028 growth outlook applied to the $411 billion FY2027 analyst estimate. The supply-constrained outlook and infrastructure risks temper the upside, while demand indications, pricing and deployment plans support the guided growth. The estimate remains above the $609.4 billion rung-1 boundary; lower analyst estimates are not enough to pull the forecast below it.\nNo change to the rung or its key supporting figures: the approximately 70% FY2028 outlook applied to a roughly $411 billion FY2027 base still implies about $699 billion. The newer evidence largely corroborates that outlook and the $678 billion market estimate.\ncite e4594,e2682,e2691,e2722,e3586,e2846,e2664,e3162,e4256,e4260,e3983,e2306,e3858,e4474 dependencies  themes v6:first,v7:first,v9:first,v16:first,v22:first,v26:first,v27:none\npush first=e4594,e2682,e2691,e2722,e2846,e2664,e3162,e4256 last=e3586,e4260,e3983,e2306,e3858,e4474 none=\nv7 2 rung 1 changed False\nAnalyst estimates put FY2028 revenue at about $678 billion (e4594), placing the market reference in rung 1.\nApply NVIDIA's approximately 70% FY2028 growth outlook to the $411 billion FY2027 estimate: $411 billion × 1.70 implies about $699 billion in FY2028 revenue.\nThe reference is about $678 billion of FY2028 revenue, which is rung 1. NVIDIA's approximately 70% FY2028 growth outlook applied to the $411 billion FY2027 estimate implies about $699 billion, also rung 1 and roughly $90 billion above the $609.4 billion boundary. Supply constraints cap the outlook, but management says available supply supports the 70% forecast; the customer-demand indication, price increases and deployment plans are supportive rather than a quantified reason to revise that forecast downward. Lower analyst estimates temper confidence but do not outweigh the more recent analyst projection and company outlook or move the implied result below the rung boundary.\nThe rung and implied outcome remain unchanged: the 70% outlook on a $411 billion FY2027 base gives about $699 billion, while the reference remains around $678 billion. Recent evidence reinforces the strong-growth outlook and its supply constraint, but does not provide a revised base or growth rate that changes the key forecast.\ncite e2682,e2691,e2722,e2723,e2846,e3586,e4594,e4596,e3961,e3827,e2664,e3162,e4256,e4260 dependencies  themes v16:first,v22:first,v26:first,v27:none,v6:first,v7:first,v9:first\npush first=e2682,e2691,e2722,e2723,e2846,e4594,e4596,e2664,e3162,e4256 last=e3586,e3961,e3827,e4260 none=\nv7 3 rung 1 changed False\nThe latest cited analyst projection is $678 billion for FY2028 (e4594), placing the market reference at rung 1. NVIDIA’s approximately 70% FY2028 growth outlook is a separate company reference and also implies rung 1 when applied to the FY2027 estimate.\nApplying NVIDIA’s approximately 70% FY2028 growth outlook to the $411 billion FY2027 revenue estimate gives about $699 billion ($411 billion × 1.70). The outlook is explicitly supply-constrained; the latest analyst FY2028 estimate of $678 billion is a lower cross-check, but both figures are above the $609.4 billion rung-1 threshold.\nThe latest stated analyst projection is $678 billion for FY2028, which is rung 1; NVIDIA’s own approximately 70% FY2028 growth outlook, applied to the $411 billion FY2027 estimate, implies about $699 billion, also rung 1. The outlook is supply-constrained, and infrastructure limits, customer-chip alternatives, and lower analyst estimates temper the upside, but the evidence does not pull the implied figure below the $609.4 billion rung-1 boundary.\nThe rung and implied outcome remain materially the same as the previous judgement: approximately $699 billion from the $411 billion FY2027 base and the 70% FY2028 outlook. The newer $678 billion analyst estimate also remains within rung 1; recent supply and infrastructure constraints temper the forecast but do not change the supporting key figure or cross a rung boundary.\ncite e4594,e2682,e2846,e3586,e2722,e3162,e4248,e4220,e4260,e2306,e3596,e3961,e3827,e3858,e4256,e4713 dependencies  themes v16:first,v22:first,v26:first,v27:first,v6:first,v7:first,v9:first\npush first=e4594,e2682,e2846,e2722,e3162,e4248,e4256,e4713 last=e3586,e4220,e4260,e2306,e3596,e3961,e3827,e3858 none=\nSOURCES\ne2300 N1 Morningstar expects technology leaders to use in-house chips for at least some AI workloads, citing Google TPUs and Amazon Trainium and Inferentia as chips designed for AI workloads.\ne2306 N1O1O3 Eagle Capital Management said NVIDIA’s largest customers’ internal silicon programs, including Google TPUs and Amazon Trainium, are likely to reduce NVIDIA’s market share over time.\ne2664 N1N3O1O2 AWS plans to deploy 2 million additional NVIDIA Blackwell Ultra, Rubin and Rubin Ultra GPUs across its global infrastructure in 2027–2028.\ne2682 N1N2O1O2O3 NVIDIA expects total revenue to grow approximately 70% in FY2028, with supply constraining growth.\ne2691 N1N2O1O2 NVIDIA said customer forecasts indicate demand for its growth would double next year, while its supply-constrained revenue outlook is approximately 70% growth in FY2028.\ne2722 N2O1O2O3 NVIDIA reported Q2 FY2027 revenue of $96.2 billion, up 106% year over year and 18% sequentially.\ne2723 O2 NVIDIA reported Q2 FY2027 Data Center revenue of $89.0 billion, up 117% year over year and 18% sequentially, driven by the Blackwell Ultra ramp.\ne2846 N2O1O2O3 NVIDIA CEO Jensen Huang said demand supports revenue growth above 70%, while available supply supports the company's 70% FY2028 growth forecast; he said NVIDIA would work with its supply chain to increase supply.\ne3162 N1N3O1O2O3 NVIDIA raised prices 15% on chips scheduled to ship next year, citing higher memory-chip input costs.\ne3586 N1N2N3O1O2O3 NVIDIA management said its FY2028 revenue outlook is supply-constrained, customer forecasts indicate faster growth, and supply is expected to remain a bottleneck through FY2028 year-end.\ne3596 O3 The author estimates NVIDIA has roughly 75% of global AI accelerator revenue share, down from a peak near 87%, as AMD and hyperscaler custom chips gain share.\ne3827 O2O3 Zacks consensus estimates FY2027 and FY2028 NVIDIA revenue growth of 85.4% and 2.2%, respectively.\ne3858 O1O3 The author reported a Wall Street consensus estimate of $397 billion in NVIDIA FY2027 revenue and calculated that 44% FY2028 growth would imply about $574 billion in FY2028 revenue.\ne3961 O2O3 Yahoo Finance analyst estimates put average NVIDIA revenue growth at 63% year over year in FY2028.\ne3983 O1 NVIDIA CEO Jensen Huang said memory chip shortages are limiting revenue growth, which he said would exceed 100% without them.\ne4220 N1N2O3 NVIDIA warned that shortages of land, power, and other data-center infrastructure could delay customer hardware deployments.\ne4248 N1O3 NVIDIA said it has improved visibility into memory and manufacturing capacity and the land, power, and data-center infrastructure needed for product deployment.\ne4250 N3 CFO Colette Kress said customer forecasts indicate NVIDIA revenue may double next year (FY2028).\ne4256 N1N2N3O1O2O3 NVIDIA expects the top five hyperscalers' combined capital expenditures to approach $800 billion in 2026 and $1.3 trillion in 2027.\ne4260 N1N2O1O2O3 NVIDIA warned that customers may delay purchases because of data-center infrastructure constraints or limited capital availability.\ne4474 O1 Jon Quast said Wall Street expected NVIDIA revenue growth of 45% in the coming year, compared with NVIDIA's 70% guidance.\ne4565 N3 NVIDIA CEO Jensen Huang reiterated that he expects NVIDIA total revenue to grow 70% year over year in FY2028.\ne4574 N2 Amazon and NVIDIA announced on August 26 an expanded deal for an additional 2 million NVIDIA GPUs, on top of AWS’s earlier plan for more than 1 million GPUs starting in 2026; the additional GPUs, including Rubin, are expected to arrive in AWS data centers in 2027 and 2028.\ne4594 N1N2N3O1O2O3 Analysts project NVIDIA revenue of $411 billion in FY2027 and $678 billion in FY2028, a 65% increase year over year.\ne4596 N1O2 The author estimates that NVIDIA's guidance implies about $700 billion of revenue in FY2028.\ne4713 N1N2O3 TSMC reported August 2026 revenue of NT$514.81 billion, a record and up 53.3% year over year.\nv6 N1N2N3O1O2O3 - v6 (ai-capex, due 2027-02-28) How much will the four largest AI hyperscalers spend on data center capital expenditures in calendar 2026?: now rung 4 $700 billion to below $800 billion; judged 2026-09-11; updated since this thesis was last judged; reasons: The reference is around $650 billion for the four hyperscalers, which sits in rung 3. The newer four-company estimates of roughly $745–$760 billion are total-capex figures, so I treat them as an anchor and upper proxy rather than equating all of that spending with data-center capex. NVIDIA’s estimat\nv7 N1N2N3O1O2O3 - v7 (ai-capex, due 2028-02-29) How much will the four largest AI hyperscalers spend on data center capital expenditures in calendar 2027?: now rung 4 $1.1 trillion to below $1.2 trillion; judged 2026-09-11; updated since this thesis was last judged; reasons: The reference is Bank of America's $1.08 trillion calendar-2027 analyst consensus, which sits in rung 3. NVIDIA's $1.3 trillion forecast is for the top five hyperscalers; subtracting the carried estimate of roughly $100–$150 billion for the fifth leaves about $1.15–$1.20 trillion for the four in thi\nv9 N1N2N3O1O2O3 - v9 (chip-export-controls, due 2027-01-31) Will China authorize purchases of Nvidia H200 chips by AI companies by January 31, 2027?: now rung 2 Yes; judged 2026-09-01; reasons: The reference is rung 1: the status quo of no Chinese authorization by the deadline, consistent with NVIDIA's zero-China-revenue Q3 outlook, though that guidance is not itself evidence that China will not authorize purchases. The evidence still makes limited authorization more likely than none: e244\nv16 N1N2N3O1O2O3 - v16 (chip-export-controls, due 2026-12-31) Will the U.S. Senate pass the Remote Access Security Act by December 31, 2026?: now rung 2 No; judged 2026-08-19; reasons: No market consensus is supplied. The deadline reference is passage by December 31, 2026 (rung 1); the alternative is that the Senate has not passed RASA by then (rung 2). RASA had passed the House but was still awaiting Senate action on July 23, and the August 19 report says it had still not passed.\nv22 N1N2N3O1O2O3 - v22 (chip-export-controls, due 2027-01-31) Will Taiwan’s Ministry of Economic Affairs adopt performance-threshold export controls for advanced chips by January 31, 2027?: now rung 2 No; judged 2026-07-28; reasons: The reference is rung 2: no formal adoption by the deadline is indicated in the available plan or timeline. The consultations and reported consideration make adoption more plausible than before, but the Ministry set no timeline; that evidence does not make formal adoption by January 31, 2027 the mor\nv26 N1N2N3O1O2O3 - v26 (chip-export-controls, due 2027-01-31) Will Taiwan adopt a criminal ban on all AI chip exports to China by January 31, 2027?: now rung 1 No; judged 2026-07-29; reasons: The reference is rung 1: the supplied evidence says Taiwan is considering a ban, not that it has adopted one. That consideration, in the context of an investigation into suspected smuggling, raises the prospect of action, but the evidence gives no adopted measure, legislative stage, or timetable. Th\nv27 N1N2N3O1O2O3 - v27 (chip-export-controls, due 2026-12-31) Will the U.S. Department of Commerce conclude its investigation into Chinese firms’ access to advanced U.S. chips by December 31, 2026?: now rung 2 No; judged 2026-08-07; reasons: The reference is rung 2 (No): no market consensus or Commerce completion deadline is supplied, and the stated status is a formal investigation, not a scheduled conclusion. The evidence makes non-conclusion by December 31, 2026 more likely: Commerce is still examining access through computing capacit\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_19","type":"command_execution","command":"/opt/homebrew/bin/zsh -lc \"python3 -c 'import json\np=json.load(open(\\\"poc/data/citation-probe-2026-11-22/packet-C.json\\\"))\nimport sys,re\nfor n in map(int,sys.argv[1:]):\n x=p[n]\n print(\\\"\\\\n\\\"+x[\\\"key\\\"],x[\\\"question\\\"])\n used={}\n for arm in (\\\"v12\\\",\\\"v7\\\"):\n  for idx,a in enumerate(x[arm],1):\n   print(arm,idx,\\\"rung\\\",a[\\\"rung\\\"],\\\"changed\\\",a[\\\"changed\\\"])\n   for k in (\\\"reference\\\",\\\"implied_outcome\\\",\\\"reasons\\\",\\\"why_changed\\\"): print(a[k])\n   print(\\\"cite\\\",\\\",\\\".join(a[\\\"cites\\\"]),\\\"dependencies\\\",\\\",\\\".join(a[\\\"cites_views\\\"]),\\\"themes\\\",\\\",\\\".join(y[\\\"view_id\\\"]+\\\":\\\"+y[\\\"push\\\"].replace(\\\"toward_\\\",\\\"\\\") for y in a[\\\"theme_pushes\\\"]))\n   ps={k:[] for k in (\\\"toward_first\\\",\\\"toward_last\\\",\\\"none\\\")}\n   for y in a[\\\"pushes\\\"]:ps[y[\\\"push\\\"]].append(y[\\\"evidence_id\\\"])\n   print(\\\"push\\\",*(k.replace(\\\"toward_\\\",\\\"\\\")+\\\"=\\\"+\\\",\\\".join(v) for k,v in ps.items()))\n   for t in {*a[\\\"cites\\\"],*a[\\\"cites_views\\\"],*(y[\\\"evidence_id\\\"] for y in a[\\\"pushes\\\"]),*(y[\\\"view_id\\\"] for y in a[\\\"theme_pushes\\\"])}:used.setdefault(t,[]).append((\\\"N\\\" if arm==\\\"v12\\\" else \\\"O\\\")+str(idx))\n print(\\\"SOURCES\\\")\n for k in sorted(used,key=lambda k:(k[0],int(k[1:]) if k[1:].isdigit() else 99999)):\n  v=x[\\\"quotes\\\"].get(k,\\\"MISSING\\\")\n  print(k,\\\"\\\".join(used[k]),re.sub(r\\\"\"'^- '\"\\\\S+ \\\\[[\"'^'\"\\\\n]+?\\\\] \\\",\\\"\\\",v.split(\\\"\\\\n\\\",1)[0]))\n' 5\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
