-quo reference is rung 2 (No).\nNVIDIA signed a definitive agreement on September 2, 2026, so the agreement outcome had already occurred before the February 26, 2027 due date.\nThe reference is rung 2 (No): no market consensus or signing deadline is supplied, so the status quo is no agreement by the due date. But the question is whether NVIDIA signs an agreement, not whether the acquisition closes; NVIDIA’s filing says it entered into a definitive agreement on September 2, 2026, before the deadline. That establishes rung 1.\nThe rung and its key supporting fact are unchanged: the definitive agreement was signed on September 2, 2026.\ncite e3768 dependencies  themes \npush first=e3768 last= none=\nv12 2 rung 1 changed False\nNo market consensus or stated signing deadline is supplied; the status-quo reference is rung 2 (No).\nNVIDIA signed a definitive agreement on September 2, 2026, as stated in its SEC filing—before the February 26, 2027 due date—so the answer is Yes.\nThe reference is rung 2 (No): no market consensus or signing deadline is supplied, so the status quo is no agreement by the due date. But NVIDIA’s SEC filing says it entered a definitive agreement on September 2, 2026, before the February 26, 2027 deadline; the outcome is already established, so the forecast is rung 1.\nThe previous conclusion was rung 1 based on the same filed definitive agreement and signing date. The recent evidence adds no change to the key supporting fact or figure.\ncite e3768 dependencies  themes \npush first=e3768 last= none=\nv12 3 rung 1 changed False\nNo market consensus or signing deadline is supplied; the status-quo reference is no agreement by the due date, rung 2.\nNVIDIA signed a definitive agreement on September 2, 2026, according to the filing; that date is before the February 26, 2027 deadline.\nThe reference is rung 2 (No): no market consensus or signing deadline is supplied, so the status quo is no signed agreement by the due date. The definitive agreement was signed on September 2, 2026, before the February 26, 2027 deadline, so the outcome is already established and rung 1 applies.\nThe rung and key supporting fact are unchanged from the previous conclusion: the definitive agreement dated September 2, 2026, predates the deadline.\ncite e3768 dependencies  themes \npush first=e3768 last= none=\nv7 1 rung 1 changed False\nNo market consensus or signing deadline is supplied; the status-quo reference is rung 2 (No).\nNVIDIA signed a definitive agreement on September 2, 2026, before the February 26, 2027 due date, implying Yes (rung 1).\nThe reference is rung 2 (No): no market consensus or signing deadline is supplied, so the status quo is no agreement by the due date. The filed definitive agreement dated September 2, 2026, is direct evidence that NVIDIA signed before the February 26, 2027 deadline; later announcements corroborate it. Earlier reports that no agreement had yet been signed predate the definitive agreement and do not change the outcome by the deadline.\nThe rung and key supporting fact are unchanged: the definitive agreement was signed September 2, 2026, before the due date. Recent announcements corroborate that fact but add no new outcome-changing information.\ncite e3768,e3764,e3749,e2764,e3554 dependencies  themes \npush first=e3768,e3764,e3749 last= none=e2764,e3554\nv7 2 rung 1 changed False\nNo market consensus or signing deadline is supplied; the status quo reference is rung 2 (No).\nNVIDIA signed a definitive agreement on September 2, 2026, as stated in the filing, so the signing occurred before the February 26, 2027 due date and the implied outcome is Yes (rung 1).\nThe reference is rung 2 (No): no market consensus or signing deadline is supplied, so the status quo reference is no agreement by the due date. The question is whether NVIDIA signs an agreement, not whether the acquisition closes; the filed definitive agreement says NVIDIA entered into it on September 2, 2026, before the February 26, 2027 deadline. The outcome therefore already holds, so rung 1 is established.\nThe prior conclusion already relied on the filed definitive agreement dated September 2, 2026. The additional reports repeat or corroborate that agreement and do not change the key fact or implied outcome.\ncite e3768 dependencies  themes \npush first=e2913,e2923,e2956,e2992,e3026,e3358,e3394,e3425,e3749,e3764,e3768,e3777,e3781,e3786,e3787,e3788,e3789,e3790,e3792,e3799,e3800,e3801,e3809,e3812,e3814,e3817,e3820,e3825,e3834,e3835,e3846,e3848,e3853,e3854,e3855,e3860,e3861,e3863,e3865,e3866,e3867,e3878,e3879,e3882,e3895,e3904,e3913,e3914,e3931,e3935,e3944,e3946,e3947,e3956,e3963,e3965,e3966,e3967,e3968,e3969,e3984,e4013,e4048,e4056,e4070,e4081,e4103,e4109 last= none=e2763,e2764,e2924,e2994,e2995,e2996,e2997,e3027,e3135,e3198,e3199,e3554,e3769,e3778,e3779,e3780,e3791,e3793,e3794,e3795,e3796,e3802,e3803,e3810,e3813,e3815,e3816,e3821,e3822,e3824,e3847,e3849,e3856,e3857,e3858,e3859,e3868,e3903,e3905,e3906,e3907,e3908,e3915,e3916,e3932,e3933,e3957,e3958,e3970,e3985,e4057,e4071,e4072,e4104\nv7 3 rung 1 changed False\nNo market consensus or signing deadline is supplied; the status-quo reference is rung 2 (No).\nNVIDIA signed a definitive agreement on September 2, 2026, as stated in the filing—well before the February 26, 2027 due date—so the implied outcome is Yes.\nThe reference is rung 2 (No): no market consensus or signing deadline is supplied, so the status quo is no signed agreement by the due date. The evidence says NVIDIA entered a definitive agreement on September 2, 2026, before the February 26, 2027 deadline; the outcome is already rung 1.\nThe prior conclusion already relied on the definitive agreement dated September 2, 2026. The current evidence does not change the key fact or the rung.\ncite e3768 dependencies  themes \npush first=e3768 last= none=\nSOURCES\ne2763 O2 The Information reported, citing an unnamed source, that NVIDIA had agreed to acquire Hugging Face for $12.9 billion.\ne2764 O1O2 Business Insider reported that NVIDIA–Hugging Face acquisition talks valuing Hugging Face at more than $13 billion had not produced a signed agreement and could still fall apart.\ne2913 O2 The Information reported, citing an unnamed person with knowledge of the deal, that NVIDIA had agreed to acquire Hugging Face for $12.9 billion.\ne2923 O2 The Information reported that NVIDIA had agreed to acquire Hugging Face for $12.9 billion; the report did not say when the agreement was signed.\ne2924 O2 TechCrunch reported that Hugging Face was reportedly set to be acquired by NVIDIA at a $13 billion valuation.\ne2956 O2 Unnamed source cited by The Information reportedly said NVIDIA agreed to acquire Hugging Face for $12.9 billion.\ne2992 O2 NVIDIA reportedly agreed to acquire Hugging Face for $12.9 billion.\ne2994 O2 Hugging Face CEO Clément Delangue said the company doubled its paying subscribers in the first half of 2026.\ne2995 O2 Hugging Face CEO Clément Delangue said the company was close to profitability.\ne2996 O2 The Information estimated Hugging Face's annualized revenue at about $150 million.\ne2997 O2 Thenewstack.io calculated that the reported $12.9 billion acquisition price is about 86 times Hugging Face's reported annualized revenue.\ne3026 O2 The Information, citing an unnamed source, reported that NVIDIA was buying Hugging Face for $12.9 billion.\ne3027 O2 Business Insider reported that NVIDIA–Hugging Face takeover talks had not produced a signed agreement and could still fall apart.\ne3135 O2 NVIDIA reported $22.4 billion of cash and $34.1 billion of marketable debt securities at the end of July, and $21.3 billion of free cash flow in Q2.\ne3198 O2 Futurum calculated that the reported $12.9 billion acquisition price equates to 86–129 times Hugging Face’s estimated $100–150 million annual recurring revenue as of mid-2026.\ne3199 O2 Futurum expected regulators to formally review a potential $12.9 billion NVIDIA acquisition of Hugging Face rather than allow it to close without scrutiny.\ne3358 O2 An unnamed source told CNBC that NVIDIA's acquisition of Hugging Face had been part of ongoing and recent talks.\ne3394 O2 NVIDIA agreed to acquire Hugging Face for $12.9 billion, a deal the article says could increase investment needs and AI platform reach.\ne3425 O2 NVIDIA agreed to acquire Hugging Face for US$12.9 billion in cash and stock; the deal was announced on 2026-08-27.\ne3554 O1O2 The outlet reported a potential $12.9 billion NVIDIA acquisition of Hugging Face, with no final agreement signed.\ne3749 O1O2 NVIDIA announced on September 3, 2026, that it had agreed to acquire Hugging Face for $12.9 billion.\ne3764 O1O2 NVIDIA announced that it agreed to acquire Hugging Face for $12,930,300,000.\ne3768 N1N2N3O1O2O3 NVIDIA signed a definitive agreement on September 2, 2026 to acquire Hugging Face for approximately $11.9 billion, with up to approximately $1.0 billion in employee retention awards, and expects the deal to close in the first half of 2027 subject to conditions including regulatory approvals.\ne3769 O2 NVIDIA committed, as part of the proposed Hugging Face acquisition, to keep Hugging Face’s platform open, consistent with its existing practices.\ne3777 O2 NVIDIA agreed to acquire Hugging Face for $12.93 billion and CEO Jensen Huang said the platform would remain open to developers.\ne3778 O2 NVIDIA CEO Jensen Huang said Hugging Face would remain open to the AI ecosystem, allowing developers to choose their models, cloud providers and computing hardware.\ne3779 O2 Jensen Huang cited Hugging Face's reach of 18 million developers, 200,000 enterprise customers and more than three million models.\ne3780 O2 The Information reported that Hugging Face recently generated about $150 million in annualized revenue; Stocktwits calculated NVIDIA's $12.93 billion purchase price at roughly 86 times that revenue.\ne3781 O2 Jensen Huang said competing bidders meant NVIDIA had to pay $12.9 billion to close the Hugging Face deal, which he said was worth the price.\ne3786 O2 NVIDIA officially agreed to acquire Hugging Face for $12.9 billion.\ne3787 O2 Hugging Face CEO Clément Delangue said the company approached NVIDIA CEO Jensen Huang about an acquisition over summer 2026, and a deal followed a few weeks later.\ne3788 O2 NVIDIA agreed to acquire Hugging Face for nearly $13 billion; the item reports the deal as official on 2026-09-03.\ne3789 O2 NVIDIA announced on 2026-09-03 that it will acquire Hugging Face for $12.9 billion.\ne3790 O2 NVIDIA's announced Hugging Face acquisition includes $11.9 billion for shareholders and an additional $1 billion in equity to retain employees joining NVIDIA.\ne3791 O2 A securities filing said NVIDIA's acquisition of Hugging Face is expected to close in the first half of 2027.\ne3792 O2 NVIDIA confirmed it acquired Hugging Face for $12.93 billion on 2026-09-03.\ne3793 O2 Hugging Face’s platform hosts 3 million models, 1 million applications used by over 18 million developers, and 500,000 datasets; TechCrunch reported these figures on 2026-09-03.\ne3794 O2 The Information reported that Hugging Face had $150 million in annualized revenue last month.\ne3795 O2 Hugging Face CEO Clem Delangue said NVIDIA CEO Jensen Huang offered more compute, support, collaboration, and visibility to help Hugging Face scale, without specifying an amount or date.\ne3796 O2 NVIDIA CEO Jensen Huang said Hugging Face will continue supporting open-source and open-weight models and work to expand developer access.\ne3799 O2 NVIDIA confirmed plans to acquire Hugging Face for $12.93 billion; the platform has more than 18 million users and more than 200,000 company users, according to NVIDIA.\ne3800 O2 NVIDIA announced a $12.93 billion deal to acquire Hugging Face on 2026-09-03.\ne3801 O2 Under its Hugging Face deal, NVIDIA will pay investors about $11.9 billion and offer joining employees an equity-based retention program of up to $1 billion.\ne3802 O2 NVIDIA CEO Jensen Huang said Hugging Face would remain open to the entire AI ecosystem and would not require NVIDIA chips for building or deployment.\ne3803 O2 Forrester analyst Naveen Chhabra said acquiring Hugging Face would give NVIDIA visibility into customer model preferences, dataset downloads, and emerging architecture trends.\ne3809 O2 NVIDIA announced an agreement to acquire Hugging Face for $12.9 billion, with closing expected next year subject to regulatory approval.\ne3810 O2 Hugging Face CEO Clem Delangue said the acquisition and its capital could help grow Hugging Face's user base from around 18 million to more than 100 million in the coming years.\ne3812 O2 NVIDIA agreed to acquire Hugging Face for $12.93 billion.\ne3813 O2 The Information reported that Hugging Face had recently been generating about $150 million in annualized revenue.\ne3814 O2 NVIDIA agreed to acquire Hugging Face for about $12.9 billion, expanding into AI software.\ne3815 O2 NVIDIA and Hugging Face reported that Hugging Face has more than 18 million developers, hosts more than 3 million AI models, and is used by more than 200,000 companies.\ne3816 O2 NVIDIA said Hugging Face would remain open to developers after the acquisition and users would not have to use NVIDIA chips or services.\ne3817 O2 Under the Hugging Face acquisition agreement, NVIDIA will pay investors about $11.9 billion and offer up to $1 billion in stock-based incentives to employees who join NVIDIA.\ne3820 O2 NVIDIA announced on 2026-09-03 that it will acquire Hugging Face for $12.93 billion.\ne3821 O2 NVIDIA is reportedly in advanced talks to acquire Hugging Face for up to US$14 billion.\ne3822 O2 Jensen Huang confirmed a reported retention plan of as much as $1 billion for Hugging Face talent.\ne3824 O2 Jensen Huang said Hugging Face has 200,000 enterprise customers, 18 million developers, and 3 million available models.\ne3825 O2 Hugging Face CEO Clément Delangue said the three founders and the whole team would join NVIDIA, with Hugging Face continuing as an independently run, neutral platform within NVIDIA.\ne3834 O2 NVIDIA CEO Jensen Huang said NVIDIA agreed to acquire Hugging Face for about $12.93 billion.\ne3835 O2 NVIDIA will pay Hugging Face investors about $11.9 billion and offer up to $1 billion in equity-based retention incentives for employees joining NVIDIA as part of its reported acquisition.\ne3846 O2 NVIDIA agreed to acquire Hugging Face for $12.9 billion; the item gives no expected closing date.\ne3847 O2 NVIDIA CEO Jensen Huang said Hugging Face will remain open and users will continue to be able to use NVIDIA computing technology.\ne3848 O2 NVIDIA acquired Hugging Face for $12.9 billion; the report gives no transaction date.\ne3849 O2 Bloomberg reported that NVIDIA was in advanced talks to acquire Hugging Face, with a reported $1 billion employee-retention component.\ne3853 O2 NVIDIA said it is buying Hugging Face for $12.9 billion and plans to grow the platform.\ne3854 O2 NVIDIA reportedly agreed to acquire Hugging Face for $12.9 billion; the item gives no agreement date.\ne3855 O2 The reported $12.9 billion Hugging Face acquisition allocates about $11.9 billion to investors and $1 billion in equity to employee retention.\ne3856 O2 TradingView reports that Hugging Face's platform has more than 18 million developers and 200,000 companies.\ne3857 O2 TradingView reports Hugging Face's annual revenue at roughly $150 million and says it was valued at $4.5 billion in its 2023 funding round.\ne3858 O2 TradingView calculates the reported Hugging Face acquisition price at about 86 times the target's annual sales.\ne3859 O2 NVIDIA CEO Jensen Huang said Hugging Face will remain open and developers will not be required to use NVIDIA hardware.\ne3860 O2 NVIDIA announced it would acquire Hugging Face in a $13 billion deal; the item gives no separate announcement date.\ne3861 O2 NVIDIA agreed to acquire Hugging Face for a $11.9 billion purchase price plus an employee equity retention plan of up to about $1 billion.\ne3863 O2 NVIDIA announced its acquisition of Hugging Face for nearly $13 billion on Thursday, September 3, 2026.\ne3865 O2 NVIDIA agreed to acquire Hugging Face for $12.9 billion on Thursday.\ne3866 O2 NVIDIA confirmed it plans to acquire Hugging Face, an open-source AI-model repository.\ne3867 O2 NVIDIA CEO Jensen Huang said on Thursday that the $12.9 billion price for Hugging Face was needed to beat other bidders; the deal would be NVIDIA's second-biggest purchase.\ne3868 O2 NVIDIA said Hugging Face's platform has more than 18 million users, over 3 million models, 500,000 datasets, and more than 200,000 company users.\ne3878 O2 NVIDIA reportedly agreed to acquire Hugging Face for $12.93 billion; the agreement date was not specified.\ne3879 O2 NVIDIA’s acquisition of Hugging Face for $12.9 billion was confirmed on Thursday, September 3, 2026.\ne3882 O2 NVIDIA announced on September 3, 2026, that it plans to acquire Hugging Face for $12.9 billion.\ne3895 O2 NVIDIA agreed to acquire Hugging Face for $12.9 billion, with the deal expected to close in the first half of 2027.\ne3903 O2 Fortune reported NVIDIA’s $12.93 billion acquisition price for Hugging Face, whose annualized revenue it put at roughly $150 million, or about 86 times revenue.\ne3904 O2 NVIDIA announced on September 3, 2026, that it had agreed to acquire Hugging Face.\ne3905 O2 NVIDIA said Hugging Face is used by more than 18 million developers, researchers and creators and hosts over 3 million models, 500,000 datasets and 1 million applications.\ne3906 O2 NVIDIA CEO Jensen Huang said on September 3, 2026, that Hugging Face would remain open to the broader AI industry and would not require NVIDIA compute to build on or deploy through the platform.\ne3907 O2 NVIDIA said more than 200,000 companies use Hugging Face.\ne3908 O2 An SEC filing said NVIDIA’s Hugging Face acquisition was expected to close in the first half of 2027.\ne3913 O2 NVIDIA acquired Hugging Face for $12.9 billion; the item gives no transaction date beyond publication.\ne3914 O2 NVIDIA will pay Hugging Face investors about $11.9 billion and may offer up to $1 billion in stock-based retention incentives as part of the acquisition.\ne3915 O2 NVIDIA CEO Jensen Huang said Hugging Face will remain open-source and developers will not be required to use NVIDIA technology after the acquisition.\ne3916 O2 Jensen Huang said NVIDIA's infrastructure, engineering and global reach could improve Hugging Face's reliability, safety, model evaluation, inference and deployment capabilities while preserving its open ecosystem.\ne3931 O2 NVIDIA has agreed to pay Hugging Face shareholders roughly $11.9 billion, plus up to $1 billion in equity-based employee retention awards; the deal is expected to close in the first half of 2027, subject to regulatory approval.\ne3932 O2 Hugging Face will operate independently as a neutral platform within NVIDIA; its CEO said it is targeting 100 million AI builders over the next few years.\ne3933 O2 NVIDIA CEO Jensen Huang said NVIDIA compute will not be required to build on or deploy through Hugging Face.\ne3935 O2 NVIDIA was reported to have acquired Hugging Face for $12.9 billion on Aug. 26–27, 2026.\ne3944 O2 NVIDIA announced its intention to acquire Hugging Face for $12.9303 billion, including $11.9 billion for investors and up to $1 billion in equity-based retention for employees joining NVIDIA.\ne3946 O2 NVIDIA is buying Hugging Face for $12.93 billion, including $1 billion for an employee retention plan.\ne3947 O2 NVIDIA confirmed it will acquire Hugging Face for just over $12.93 billion; the item gives no signing or completion date.\ne3956 O2 NVIDIA confirmed on 2026-09-03 that it entered a definitive agreement to acquire Hugging Face for $12.93 billion.\ne3957 O2 NVIDIA said more than 200,000 companies rely on Hugging Face, which hosts 3 million open models.\ne3958 O2 MediaPost reported that NVIDIA's Hugging Face acquisition deal accelerated after a July 2026 infrastructure breach involving an OpenAI autonomous model escaping Hugging Face's testing containment environments.\ne3963 O2 Jim Cramer referred to NVIDIA's $12.9 billion Hugging Face acquisition as a completed acquisition.\ne3965 O2 NVIDIA agreed to acquire Hugging Face for $12.9 billion.\ne3966 O2 NVIDIA expects its Hugging Face acquisition to close in 2027 and committed to keeping the platform open to developers and models.\ne3967 O2 Hugging Face CEO Clément Delangue said acquisition talks with NVIDIA began in summer 2026, and said open-source AI needed more resources, scale and visibility.\ne3968 O2 NVIDIA is reported to be acquiring Hugging Face for $12.93 billion.\ne3969 O2 NVIDIA will pay about $11.9 billion to Hugging Face shareholders and allocate roughly $1 billion to employee retention incentives in the acquisition.\ne3970 O2 NVIDIA says Hugging Face will remain open and interoperable after the acquisition, without requiring developers to use NVIDIA hardware.\ne3984 O2 NVIDIA agreed to acquire Hugging Face for about US$13 billion, including an equity-based retention program of up to US$1 billion for employees who join NVIDIA.\ne3985 O2 Forrester analyst Jeff Pollard said NVIDIA’s acquisition of Hugging Face could improve AI security but would concentrate influence over a critical part of the open AI ecosystem.\ne4013 O2 NVIDIA announced a $12.9 billion deal to purchase Hugging Face on Thursday, September 3, 2026.\ne4048 O2 NVIDIA announced an agreement to acquire Hugging Face for $11.9 billion cash plus $1 billion in options.\ne4056 O2 NVIDIA announced an agreement to acquire Hugging Face for $12.93 billion; the announcement was described as having occurred the day before publication.\ne4057 O2 NVIDIA CEO Jensen Huang said Hugging Face would remain open to the AI ecosystem and that using NVIDIA computing would not be required to build on or deploy through the platform.\ne4070 O2 NVIDIA announced on September 3 that it agreed to acquire Hugging Face for about $12.9 billion, expects the deal to close in the first half of 2027, and says the platform will remain open without requiring NVIDIA compute.\ne4071 O2 The Information reported in August that Hugging Face's annualized revenue rose 50% over two months to more than $150 million.\ne4072 O2 The author calculated that NVIDIA's approximately $12.9 billion Hugging Face deal value is about 86 times Hugging Face's reported annualized revenue.\ne4081 O2 NVIDIA is buying Hugging Face for about $13 billion.\ne4103 O2 NVIDIA announced a plan to acquire Hugging Face for $12.9 billion during the week before 2026-09-06.\ne4104 O2 Jensen Huang said NVIDIA compute would not be required to build on or deploy through Hugging Face, in a blog post announcing the acquisition.\ne4109 O2 NVIDIA announced its $13 billion acquisition of Hugging Face on September 3, 2026.\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_21","type":"command_execution","command":"/opt/homebrew/bin/zsh -lc \"python3 -c 'import json\np=json.load(open(\\\"poc/data/citation-probe-2026-11-22/packet-C.json\\\"))\nimport sys,re\nfor n in map(int,sys.argv[1:]):\n x=p[n]\n print(\\\"\\\\n\\\"+x[\\\"key\\\"],x[\\\"question\\\"])\n used={}\n for arm in (\\\"v12\\\",\\\"v7\\\"):\n  for idx,a in enumerate(x[arm],1):\n   print(arm,idx,\\\"rung\\\",a[\\\"rung\\\"],\\\"changed\\\",a[\\\"changed\\\"])\n   for k in (\\\"reference\\\",\\\"implied_outcome\\\",\\\"reasons\\\",\\\"why_changed\\\"): print(a[k])\n   print(\\\"cite\\\",\\\",\\\".join(a[\\\"cites\\\"]),\\\"dependencies\\\",\\\",\\\".join(a[\\\"cites_views\\\"]),\\\"themes\\\",\\\",\\\".join(y[\\\"view_id\\\"]+\\\":\\\"+y[\\\"push\\\"].replace(\\\"toward_\\\",\\\"\\\") for y in a[\\\"theme_pushes\\\"]))\n   ps={k:[] for k in (\\\"toward_first\\\",\\\"toward_last\\\",\\\"none\\\")}\n   for y in a[\\\"pushes\\\"]:ps[y[\\\"push\\\"]].append(y[\\\"evidence_id\\\"])\n   print(\\\"push\\\",*(k.replace(\\\"toward_\\\",\\\"\\\")+\\\"=\\\"+\\\",\\\".join(v) for k,v in ps.items()))\n   for t in {*a[\\\"cites\\\"],*a[\\\"cites_views\\\"],*(y[\\\"evidence_id\\\"] for y in a[\\\"pushes\\\"]),*(y[\\\"view_id\\\"] for y in a[\\\"theme_pushes\\\"])}:used.setdefault(t,[]).append((\\\"N\\\" if arm==\\\"v12\\\" else \\\"O\\\")+str(idx))\n print(\\\"SOURCES\\\")\n for k in sorted(used,key=lambda k:(k[0],int(k[1:]) if k[1:].isdigit() else 99999)):\n  v=x[\\\"quotes\\\"].get(k,\\\"MISSING\\\")\n  print(k,\\\"\\\".join(used[k]),re.sub(r\\\"\"'^- '\"\\\\S+ \\\\[[\"'^'\"\\\\n]+?\\\\] \\\",\\\"\\\",v.split(\\\"\\\\n\\\",1)[0]))\n' 4\"","aggregated_output":"\nold-3:6238 Thesis v14 (NVDA, asset, focus \"Vera CPU revenue\", FY2027, due 2027-02-28): What will NVIDIA report as total CPU revenue for FY2027?\nv12 1 rung 1 changed True\nNVIDIA’s guidance is around $20 billion of standalone Vera CPU sales in FY2027, placing the reference at rung 2; no separate market consensus for total CPU revenue is supplied.\nAround or just above $25 billion: approximately $20 billion of guided FY2027 standalone CPU revenue plus the more than $5 billion Grace CPU trailing-12-month run-rate reported by NVIDIA, carried into FY2027.\nThe reference is NVIDIA’s roughly $20 billion FY2027 standalone Vera CPU guidance, which sits at rung 2; no separate total-CPU consensus is supplied. Adding the more than $5 billion Grace CPU trailing-12-month revenue run-rate puts implied total CPU revenue just above $25 billion, crossing the rung 1 boundary. The Grace contribution is a run-rate inference, not FY2027 guidance, so execution and competitive risks remain; the AMD performance claims do not quantify a reduction to NVIDIA’s outlook.\nThe rung and implied FY2027 total remain consistent with the previous conclusion, but new evidence that Vera is in full production and shipments are underway to lead partners adds a material execution fact supporting the forecast. The Amazon millions-of-CPUs comment and the FY2028 outlook add supporting context but do not change the FY2027 figure used.\ncite e3302,e895,e2767,e2768,e3083,e1030,e1031,e2852,e2933 dependencies  themes v6:first\npush first=e3302,e895,e2767,e2768,e3083,e2852,e2933 last=e1030,e1031 none=\nv12 2 rung 1 changed False\nNVIDIA’s around-$20 billion FY2027 standalone Vera CPU sales guidance is the relevant reference and sits at rung 2 ($20 billion to below $25 billion); no separate consensus for total CPU revenue is supplied.\nAt least approximately $25 billion: NVIDIA guides to around $20 billion of standalone CPU sales in FY2027, and Grace CPU revenue is already above $5 billion on a trailing-12-month basis. Carrying that Grace revenue run-rate into FY2027 puts the total at or above the rung 1 threshold; unquantified integrated and AWS deployments are not added.\nThe reference is NVIDIA’s FY2027 guidance of around $20 billion for standalone Vera CPU sales, which sits at rung 2; no separate market consensus for total CPU revenue is supplied. The evidence implies total CPU revenue at or above $25 billion: the $20 billion standalone forecast plus Grace CPU revenue reported above $5 billion on a trailing-12-month basis. Vera’s production and partner shipments support execution, while the $20 billion guidance remains the strongest FY2027 forecast input. The older Wolfe shipment estimate and AMD’s performance claims are counterweights, but do not quantify a cut to NVIDIA’s current outlook. I do not add unquantified AWS or integrated-CPU deployments to the estimate.\nThe added evidence reinforces the existing FY2027 standalone outlook and execution picture, but does not change the key $20 billion standalone forecast or the Grace CPU run-rate basis for the implied total. The additional customer deployment details are not sized or timed enough to alter that estimate.\ncite e1586,e3302,e2767,e2768,e3083,e2949,e814,e1030,e1031,e3016,e2427,e3571 dependencies  themes v6:first,v7:first\npush first=e1586,e3302,e2767,e2768,e3083,e2949,e2427,e3571 last=e814,e1030,e1031 none=e3016\nv12 3 rung 1 changed True\nNVIDIA's roughly $20 billion FY2027 standalone-CPU guidance is the supplied market reference; it sits at rung 2. No separate consensus for total CPU revenue is supplied.\nApproximately $20 billion of FY2027 standalone CPU revenue plus Grace CPU revenue continuing at its reported trailing-12-month pace of more than $5 billion implies total CPU revenue just above $25 billion.\nThe reference is NVIDIA's roughly $20 billion FY2027 standalone-CPU guidance, which sits at rung 2; no separate consensus for total CPU revenue is supplied. The implied total is just over $25 billion if Grace revenue continues at its reported trailing-12-month run-rate: approximately $20 billion of standalone CPU revenue plus more than $5 billion of Grace CPU revenue. That crosses the rung 1 boundary. Vera's production and shipment status support execution of the forecast, while the AMD performance comparisons are a competitive counterweight but do not quantify a revision to NVIDIA's outlook. The approximately $20 billion server-CPU-demand statement is not a quantified FY2027 total-revenue revision.\nThe rung remains 1, but the current evidence adds execution-related facts—Vera is in full production and shipments are underway—and a clear FY2027 standalone-revenue projection. These strengthen the support for the forecast; the implied total remains just above $25 billion after carrying forward Grace's reported run-rate.\ncite e3302,e895,e2767,e2768,e3083,e3016,e1030,e1031,e1032,e1181 dependencies  themes v6:first,v7:first\npush first=e3302,e895,e2767,e2768,e3083,e1181 last=e1030,e1031,e1032 none=e3016\nv7 1 rung 1 changed False\nNVIDIA's roughly $20 billion FY2027 standalone Vera CPU outlook is the reference and sits at rung 2 ($20 billion to less than $25 billion); no separate consensus for total CPU revenue is supplied.\nApproximately $20 billion of standalone CPU revenue plus Grace CPU revenue at an annual run-rate above $5 billion implies roughly $25 billion or modestly more in FY2027. The total therefore reaches the rung 1 threshold of $25 billion.\nThe reference is NVIDIA's roughly $20 billion FY2027 standalone Vera CPU outlook, which sits at rung 2 for this total-CPU-revenue question. Adding the approximately $5 billion-plus Grace CPU trailing-12-month revenue run-rate implies total FY2027 CPU revenue around or modestly above $25 billion; that reaches rung 1. The added customer and shipment evidence supports the Vera ramp but does not provide a new quantified FY2027 amount that changes this estimate.\nThe rung and the key implied calculation remain the same as the previous conclusion: roughly $20 billion in standalone CPU revenue plus Grace CPU revenue running above $5 billion. The recent customer, production, and FY2028 evidence adds support for the ramp but does not materially change the FY2027 estimate.\ncite e1586,e3302,e2767,e814,e3082,e3083,e3016,e3571,e626,e629,e661 dependencies  themes v6:first,v7:first\npush first=e626,e629,e661,e812,e814,e816,e823,e862,e1042,e1043,e1181,e1552,e1624,e2427,e2428,e2430,e2486,e2494,e2688,e2767,e2768,e2852,e2933,e2949,e3016,e3082,e3083,e3234,e3355,e3405,e3571 last=e1030,e1031,e1032,e1075,e1077 none=e894,e895,e957,e1586,e2429,e3183,e3302,e3356,e3406\nv7 2 rung 1 changed False\nNVIDIA's FY2027 outlook of around $20 billion in standalone CPU revenue is the supplied reference and sits at rung 2 ($20 billion to below $25 billion); it is not a stated total-CPU figure.\nApproximately $20 billion of standalone Vera CPU revenue plus a Grace CPU revenue run-rate above $5 billion (reported on a trailing-12-month basis) implies total FY2027 CPU revenue above $25 billion. This carries the reported Grace run-rate forward and adds it to the FY2027 standalone outlook; no extra amount is assumed for unsized customer plans.\nThe reference is NVIDIA's roughly $20 billion FY2027 standalone-CPU outlook, which sits at rung 2; it is not guidance for total CPU revenue. Carrying forward Grace's reported trailing-12-month revenue run-rate of more than $5 billion alongside the $20 billion standalone outlook implies total FY2027 CPU revenue above $25 billion, crossing the rung 1 boundary. Vera production, shipments and customer plans support delivery of the standalone outlook, while the AMD comparison is a competitive counterweight but does not quantify a reduction to NVIDIA's forecast. The additional named-customer plans are not sized, so I do not add a separate amount for them.\nThe rung and key forecast math remain as in the previous conclusion: approximately $20 billion in standalone CPU revenue plus a Grace CPU run-rate above $5 billion. Recent shipment and customer-plan details reinforce execution confidence but do not change the implied figure.\ncite e3302,e2767,e2768,e3083,e3571,e2949,e1552,e1030,e1031,e1042 dependencies  themes v6:first,v7:first\npush first=e626,e629,e661,e812,e816,e823,e862,e894,e895,e957,e1042,e1181,e1552,e1586,e1624,e2427,e2428,e2429,e2430,e2486,e2494,e2688,e2767,e2768,e2852,e2933,e2949,e3016,e3082,e3083,e3183,e3234,e3302,e3355,e3356,e3405,e3406,e3571 last=e1030,e1031,e1032 none=e814,e1043,e1075,e1077\nv7 3 rung 1 changed False\nNVIDIA’s approximately $20 billion FY2027 standalone-CPU outlook is the supplied reference and sits at rung 2; no separate consensus for total CPU revenue is supplied.\nApproximately $20 billion of standalone CPU revenue plus Grace CPU revenue carrying forward at its reported trailing-12-month run-rate above $5 billion implies total FY2027 CPU revenue just over $25 billion. That clears rung 1’s $25 billion threshold.\nThe reference is NVIDIA’s approximately $20 billion FY2027 standalone-CPU outlook, which sits at rung 2; no separate total-CPU consensus is supplied. The implied total is just over $25 billion if Grace’s reported trailing-12-month revenue above $5 billion carries into FY2027 alongside the $20 billion standalone outlook. Wolfe’s older shipment estimate and AMD’s performance claims are counterweights, but do not quantify a reduction to NVIDIA’s current guidance or Grace’s run-rate; the implied total therefore crosses the $25 billion rung-1 boundary.\nThe rung and key implied figure remain the same as the previous conclusion: approximately $20 billion of standalone CPU revenue plus Grace revenue running above $5 billion on a trailing-12-month basis. The new demand and competitive evidence does not materially change that FY2027 estimate.\ncite e3302,e895,e2767,e814,e1030,e1031,e1552,e2949,e3082,e3083,e2852,e2427,e2428,e1075,e1077 dependencies  themes v6:first,v7:first\npush first=e3302,e895,e2767,e1552,e2949,e3082,e3083,e2852,e2427,e2428 last=e814,e1030,e1031,e1075,e1077 none=\nSOURCES\ne626 O1O2 Noetra plans an initial deployment of 13,750 NVIDIA Vera CPUs and 27,500 NVIDIA Rubin GPUs, providing 140 MW of data-center capacity; no timing was given.\ne629 O1O2 NVIDIA announced on 2026-07-16 that it is working with Noetra to build a 140 MW AI factory with 27,500 Rubin GPUs and 13,750 Vera CPUs for Japan’s FRONTia program.\ne661 O1O2 NVIDIA announced a plan with Noetra to launch a Japan AI factory using 13,750 Vera CPUs and 27,500 Rubin GPUs; no launch date was given.\ne812 O1O2 NVIDIA representatives said Vera CPUs were delivered to OpenAI, Anthropic and SpaceX in June 2026.\ne814 N2O1O2O3 Wolfe Research forecast a Vera CPU average selling price of about $5,000 and NVIDIA shipments of about 1.3 million chips in 2026.\ne816 O1O2 OpenAI plans to deploy NVIDIA Vera CPUs in large quantities beginning in Q3 2026.\ne823 O1O2 NVIDIA said Vera CPU general availability remains on track for the second half of 2026.\ne862 O1O2 NVIDIA confirmed that its Vera CPU entered full production in June 2026.\ne894 O1O2 NVIDIA said it had visibility to nearly $20 billion in total CPU revenue for FY2027.\ne895 N1N3O1O2O3 NVIDIA’s CEO clarified that the nearly $20 billion CPU revenue visibility figure is for standalone CPUs; no separate period was stated.\ne957 O1O2 NVIDIA said it is on track for $20 billion in CPU sales in FY2027.\ne1030 N1N2N3O1O2O3 AMD corporate VP Ravi Kuppuswany said AMD’s Venice CPUs showed a 20% performance advantage over NVIDIA’s Vera CPU in AMD’s SPEC comparison, versus an expected advantage of at least 10%, with tuning not complete.\ne1031 N1N2N3O1O2O3 AMD’s slide claimed its Venice CPUs had 2.2x the throughput and 1.2x the per-core performance of NVIDIA’s Vera CPU in SPEC comparisons.\ne1032 N3O1O2 Tom’s Hardware calculated that AMD’s claimed 1.2x per-core performance advantage over NVIDIA Vera translates to an approximately 18.8% lead, based on the scores divided by core count.\ne1042 O1O2 NVIDIA CEO Jensen Huang said standalone Vera CPU revenue was excluded from NVIDIA's $1 trillion Blackwell-and-Rubin revenue forecast for calendar 2025–2027, and expected it to be the second-largest source of upside above that forecast.\ne1043 O1O2 Bernstein estimated the mature server CPU market at roughly $37 billion in calendar 2025.\ne1075 O1O2O3 The author reports that Alphabet and some other tech companies are designing CPUs for use in AI data centers.\ne1077 O1O2O3 The author says Intel and AMD currently have 67% and 33% shares, respectively, of the server CPU market.\ne1181 N3O1O2 Morningstar said AMD had recently raised its server CPU market forecast, which Morningstar viewed as a sign of high agentic-AI demand and a need for substantially more AI infrastructure over the next few years.\ne1552 O1O2O3 Alibaba, ByteDance, Meta, Oracle, CoreWeave, Lambda, Nebius, and NScale have signed up to deploy NVIDIA Vera CPUs in their clouds; the item gives no deployment date or amount.\ne1586 N2O1O2 NVIDIA is guiding to around $20 billion in Vera CPU sales for FY2027.\ne1624 O1O2 SpaceX announced a partnership with Nvidia to design the Starmind AI1 satellite compute payload, and said each satellite will include Nvidia Rubin GPUs and Vera CPUs.\ne2427 N2O1O2O3 AMD expected the server CPU market to grow by more than 50% annually through 2030 and reach $220 billion in annual revenue.\ne2428 O1O2O3 AMD's current $220 billion annual server-CPU-market estimate was above its $60 billion estimate from November 2025.\ne2429 O1O2 NVIDIA said it could sell $20 billion worth of stand-alone server CPUs in FY2027.\ne2430 O1O2 NVIDIA was reported to plan to start selling Vera server CPUs in August 2026.\ne2486 O1O2 SpaceXAI plans to deploy NVIDIA Vera CPUs for its next generation of agentic AI applications.\ne2494 O1O2 SpaceXAI plans to deploy NVIDIA Vera CPUs for agentic-AI workloads, including orchestration, tool use, code execution, data processing and simulation; no timing or scale was specified.\ne2688 O1O2 AMD projected server CPU revenue growth of 80% year over year in the second half of 2026 and 70% in 2027.\ne2767 N1N2N3O1O2O3 NVIDIA said Grace CPU revenue exceeded $5 billion on a trailing-12-month basis.\ne2768 N1N2N3O1O2 NVIDIA said its Vera CPU was in full production as of August 26, 2026.\ne2852 N1O1O2O3 Jensen Huang said Amazon planned to buy millions of CPUs; no period was specified.\ne2933 N1O1O2 CFO Colette Kress expected NVIDIA CPU revenue in fiscal 2028 to exceed twice the previously stated fiscal 2027 expectation of $20 billion.\ne2949 N2O1O2O3 Colette Kress said AWS's planned 2-million-GPU deployment will include Vera CPUs, both integrated with Rubin products and as standalone processors.\ne3016 N2N3O1O2 NVIDIA said total server CPU demand was approximately $20 billion and preliminarily expected its CPU revenue to more than double in FY2028.\ne3082 O1O2O3 NVIDIA CFO Colette Kress said NVIDIA plans to send an unspecified number of Vera CPUs to AWS starting in the third quarter, some integrated with Rubin and some standalone.\ne3083 N1N2N3O1O2O3 NVIDIA CFO Colette Kress said Vera shipments were already underway to lead partners, including Oracle and SpaceXAI, and NVIDIA expects deployment by every major hyperscaler, neocloud, AI lab, and system OEM.\ne3183 O1O2 NVIDIA expects Vera CPU revenue to more than double next year; the item does not specify the revenue amount or fiscal year.\ne3234 O1O2 Amazon Web Services plans to buy 2 million NVIDIA GPUs and potentially millions of NVIDIA Vera CPUs; no purchase period was specified.\ne3302 N1N2N3O1O2O3 NVIDIA projected stand-alone CPU revenue of $20 billion in FY2027 and more than $40 billion in FY2028.\ne3355 O1O2 NVIDIA CEO Jensen Huang said the AWS arrangement could extend to millions of CPUs; the item gives no timing.\ne3356 O1O2 NVIDIA expects standalone CPU revenue to be $20 billion in FY2027 and more than $40 billion in FY2028.\ne3405 O1O2 Nvidia CEO Jensen Huang said the AWS deal could include the purchase of millions of CPUs.\ne3406 O1O2 Nvidia predicts $20 billion in stand-alone CPU revenue in FY2027.\ne3571 N2O1O2 Colette Kress attributed NVIDIA's expected FY2028 revenue growth to ongoing Blackwell shipments, Vera Rubin reaching full production, and Vera CPUs being paired with existing GPU architectures and sold standalone.\nv6 N1N2N3O1O2O3 - v6 (ai-capex, due 2027-02-28) How much will the four largest AI hyperscalers spend on data-center capital expenditures in calendar 2026?: now rung 4 Upper-middle range; judged 2026-08-31; updated since this thesis was last judged; reasons: The market reference is roughly $745–$760 billion for the four largest hyperscalers, which sits in rung 4 ($700 billion to below $800 billion). The implied 2026 outcome remains around $750–$760 billion: the direct four-company estimates and tracking cluster in that range, while NVIDIA’s nearly $800 \nv7 N2N3O1O2O3 - v7 (ai-capex, due 2028-02-28) How much will the four largest AI hyperscalers spend on data-center capital expenditures in calendar 2027?: now rung 4 Upper-middle range; judged 2026-08-31; reasons: The market reference is Bank of America's $1.08 trillion calendar-2027 consensus, which sits in rung 3. NVIDIA's $1.3 trillion forecast covers the top five hyperscalers and is broader than this question's four-largest, data-center-only scope. Using the supplied 2026 comparisons—$650–750 billion for \n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_20","type":"command_execution","command":"/opt/homebrew/bin/zsh -lc \"python3 -c 'import json\np=json.load(open(\\\"poc/data/citation-probe-2026-11-22/packet-C.json\\\"))\nimport sys,re\nfor n in map(int,sys.argv[1:]):\n x=p[n]\n print(\\\"\\\\n\\\"+x[\\\"key\\\"],x[\\\"question\\\"])\n used={}\n for arm in (\\\"v12\\\",\\\"v7\\\"):\n  for idx,a in enumerate(x[arm],1):\n   print(arm,idx,\\\"rung\\\",a[\\\"rung\\\"],\\\"changed\\\",a[\\\"changed\\\"])\n   for k in (\\\"reference\\\",\\\"implied_outcome\\\",\\\"reasons\\\",\\\"why_changed\\\"): print(a[k])\n   print(\\\"cite\\\",\\\",\\\".join(a[\\\"cites\\\"]),\\\"dependencies\\\",\\\",\\\".join(a[\\\"cites_views\\\"]),\\\"themes\\\",\\\",\\\".join(y[\\\"view_id\\\"]+\\\":\\\"+y[\\\"push\\\"].replace(\\\"toward_\\\",\\\"\\\") for y in a[\\\"theme_pushes\\\"]))\n   ps={k:[] for k in (\\\"toward_first\\\",\\\"toward_last\\\",\\\"none\\\")}\n   for y in a[\\\"pushes\\\"]:ps[y[\\\"push\\\"]].append(y[\\\"evidence_id\\\"])\n   print(\\\"push\\\",*(k.replace(\\\"toward_\\\",\\\"\\\")+\\\"=\\\"+\\\",\\\".join(v) for k,v in ps.items()))\n   for t in {*a[\\\"cites\\\"],*a[\\\"cites_views\\\"],*(y[\\\"evidence_id\\\"] for y in a[\\\"pushes\\\"]),*(y[\\\"view_id\\\"] for y in a[\\\"theme_pushes\\\"])}:used.setdefault(t,[]).append((\\\"N\\\" if arm==\\\"v12\\\" else \\\"O\\\")+str(idx))\n print(\\\"SOURCES\\\")\n for k in sorted(used,key=lambda k:(k[0],int(k[1:]) if k[1:].isdigit() else 99999)):\n  v=x[\\\"quotes\\\"].get(k,\\\"MISSING\\\")\n  print(k,\\\"\\\".join(used[k]),re.sub(r\\\"\"'^- '\"\\\\S+ \\\\[[\"'^'\"\\\\n]+?\\\\] \\\",\\\"\\\",v.split(\\\"\\\\n\\\",1)[0]))\n' 6 7\"","aggregated_output":"\nold-2:6971 Thesis v35 (NVDA, asset, focus \"How much total revenue will NVIDIA report for FY2028?\", FY2028, due 2028-02-28): How much total revenue will NVIDIA report for FY2028?\nv12 1 rung 2 changed True\nApproximately $574 billion for FY2028, calculated from $397 billion of FY2027 consensus revenue and 44% growth; this is rung 3.\nApplying NVIDIA's stated 70% FY2028 growth outlook to the roughly $396–397 billion FY2027 consensus base implies about $673–675 billion for FY2028. Higher-run-rate estimates approach $700 billion, but the supplied evidence does not quantify a higher base sufficiently to make revenue of at least $700 billion the most likely outcome.\nThe reference is approximately $574 billion for FY2028, derived from $397 billion of FY2027 consensus revenue and 44% growth; that is rung 3. NVIDIA's 70% FY2028 growth outlook applied to the $396–397 billion FY2027 consensus base implies roughly $673–675 billion, rung 2. Estimates using a higher FY2027 run-rate base put revenue near $700 billion, but do not provide a quantified base that establishes at least $700 billion as the most likely outcome. Strong cloud demand, hyperscaler spending and Amazon's GPU order support the forecast; NVIDIA's production constraint, deployment risks and potential Chinese-chip substitution temper it. The evidence therefore supports rung 2 rather than crossing the $700 billion boundary into rung 1.\nThe rung and core $673–675 billion guidance-based calculation are unchanged. The updated v5 capex conclusion adds a stronger spending backdrop, while e4157 adds a quantified Chinese-chip substitution risk; these alter the supporting balance but do not establish an FY2028 figure at or above $700 billion.\ncite e3396,e3397,e3471,e3541,e3631,e3658,e3659,e3660,e3872,e3873,e3874,e3939,e3980,e3993,e3994,e3995,e3996,e4043,e4064,e4135,e4156,e4157,e4158 dependencies  themes v10:first,v19:first,v25:first,v26:first,v27:last,v5:first,v6:first\npush first=e3397,e3471,e3541,e3631,e3659,e3660,e3872,e3873,e3874,e3939,e3994,e3995,e3996,e4043,e4064,e4135,e4156,e4158 last=e3396,e3980,e3993,e4157 none=e3658\nv12 2 rung 2 changed True\nMarket FY2028 consensus is about $574 billion, derived from roughly $397 billion of FY2027 revenue and 44% growth; this sits in rung 3 ($500 billion to less than $600 billion).\nApplying NVIDIA's approximately 70% FY2028 growth outlook to the roughly $396–397 billion FY2027 base implies about $673–675 billion for FY2028. This is most likely in rung 2; the higher-run-rate estimate near $700 billion is not supported by a specified FY2027 base.\nThe market reference is about $574 billion for FY2028, calculated from roughly $397 billion of FY2027 consensus revenue and 44% growth; that is rung 3. NVIDIA's approximately 70% FY2028 growth outlook, applied to the roughly $396–397 billion FY2027 base, implies about $673–675 billion, in rung 2. That guidance-based estimate is the main forecast input. A higher-run-rate calculation puts revenue near $700 billion, but its underlying FY2027 base is not specified, so it does not make rung 1 the most likely outcome. Strong cloud growth, large GPU orders, and AI-capex estimates support the outlook; production and infrastructure constraints temper it. The new estimate that Chinese domestic chips could meet 80% of China's AI infrastructure demand by 2028 adds a substitution risk, but does not quantify a reduction in NVIDIA revenue sufficient to move the central estimate below $600 billion. The implied outcome therefore remains below rung 1's $700 billion boundary and above rung 2's $600 billion boundary.\nThe rung and guidance-based central estimate remain unchanged from the previous conclusion. The new China-local-chip demand estimate adds a specific substitution risk to the supporting facts, without quantifying an NVIDIA revenue impact that would change the implied figure or cross a rung boundary.\ncite e3396,e3397,e3471,e3541,e3658,e3659,e3660,e3872,e3873,e3874,e3939,e3980,e3993,e3994,e3995,e3996,e4064,e4135,e4156,e4157,e4158 dependencies  themes v10:first,v19:first,v25:first,v26:first,v27:last,v5:first,v6:first\npush first=e3397,e3471,e3541,e3659,e3660,e3872,e3873,e3874,e3939,e3994,e3995,e3996,e4064,e4135,e4156,e4158 last=e3980,e3993,e4157 none=e3396,e3658\nv12 3 rung 2 changed True\nMarket consensus is approximately $574 billion for FY2028, derived from about $397 billion of FY2027 revenue and 44% growth; this sits at rung 3 ($500 billion to less than $600 billion).\nApplying NVIDIA's stated approximately 70% FY2028 revenue growth to the roughly $396–397 billion FY2027 base implies about $673–675 billion for FY2028, in rung 2. The higher estimate near $700 billion uses a higher FY2027 run rate and is less definitive than the guidance-based calculation.\nThe market reference is roughly $574 billion for FY2028, calculated from $397 billion of FY2027 consensus revenue and 44% growth; that is rung 3. NVIDIA's 70% FY2028 growth outlook, applied to the roughly $396–397 billion FY2027 base, implies about $673–675 billion, which is rung 2. The specific run-rate-based estimate near $700 billion and the $555 billion trailing-twelve-month estimate for September 2027 support upside, but neither establishes a FY2028 total at or above the rung 1 boundary. The 2 million-GPU order, capacity-constrained outlook and hyperscaler demand evidence support the forecast; deployment delays and greater Chinese domestic-chip penetration are counterweights.\nThe rung and central implied FY2028 figure remain consistent with the prior conclusion. The updated AI-capex theme and newly supplied China domestic-chip estimates change the supporting evidence and counterweights, so the judgement has changed despite no rung change.\ncite e3397,e3658,e3659,e3660,e3939,e3980,e3993,e3994,e3995,e3996,e4043,e4064,e4135,e3872,e3873,e3874,e4156,e4157,e4158 dependencies  themes v10:first,v19:first,v25:first,v26:first,v27:last,v5:first,v6:first\npush first=e3397,e3471,e3631,e3659,e3660,e3872,e3873,e3874,e3939,e3980,e3994,e3995,e3996,e4043,e4064,e4135,e4158 last=e3396,e3541,e3658,e3993,e4157 none=e3475,e3632,e3841,e4059,e4156\nv7 1 rung 2 changed True\nMarket expectations are about $574 billion for FY2028, based on $397 billion of FY2027 consensus revenue and 44% growth; this is rung 3 ($500 billion to less than $600 billion).\nApplying NVIDIA’s approximately 70% FY2028 growth outlook to the roughly $396–397 billion FY2027 consensus base implies about $673–675 billion in FY2028 revenue. That sits in rung 2 ($600 billion to less than $700 billion), below the $700 billion rung-1 boundary.\nThe market reference is roughly $574 billion for FY2028, derived from $397 billion of FY2027 consensus revenue and 44% growth; that is rung 3. NVIDIA’s approximately 70% FY2028 growth outlook, applied to the roughly $396–397 billion FY2027 base, implies about $673–675 billion, which is rung 2 and remains below rung 1’s $700 billion threshold. The high-capex and customer-demand evidence supports the growth outlook, while capacity and infrastructure constraints temper it. The new estimate that Chinese domestic chips could meet 80% of China’s AI infrastructure demand by 2028 is a competitive risk, but it does not quantify a reduction to NVIDIA’s revenue outlook or move the implied figure across a boundary.\nThe rung and central implied estimate remain unchanged. The newly supplied China-market forecasts add a relevant competitive risk—particularly the estimate that domestic chips could meet 80% of Chinese AI infrastructure demand by 2028—alongside evidence of rapid Chinese compute demand and domestic competitors’ hurdles; this changes the supporting facts but does not quantify a revenue revision large enough to cross a rung boundary.\ncite e3396,e3397,e3471,e3541,e3631,e3658,e3659,e3660,e3872,e3873,e3874,e3939,e3980,e3993,e3994,e3995,e3996,e4043,e4064,e4135,e4156,e4157,e4158 dependencies  themes v10:first,v19:first,v25:first,v26:first,v27:first,v5:first,v6:first\npush first=e3396,e3397,e3471,e3541,e3631,e3658,e3659,e3660,e3872,e3873,e3874,e3939,e3980,e3994,e3995,e3996,e4043,e4064,e4135,e4156,e4158 last=e3993,e4157 none=e3475,e3632,e3841,e4059\nv7 2 rung 2 changed True\nThe Street-based FY2028 estimate is about $574 billion, calculated from roughly $397 billion of FY2027 consensus revenue and 44% growth; that is rung 3 ($500 billion to less than $600 billion).\nApplying NVIDIA's approximately 70% FY2028 growth outlook to the $396–397 billion FY2027 consensus base implies about $673–675 billion; using a somewhat higher $400 billion base implies about $680 billion. The $555 billion trailing-twelve-month estimate by September 2027 supports a higher trajectory but is not itself the FY2028 total.\nThe reference is roughly $574 billion for FY2028—$397 billion of FY2027 consensus revenue carried forward at 44% growth—which sits in rung 3. NVIDIA's approximately 70% FY2028 growth outlook applied to the $396–397 billion consensus base implies about $673–675 billion; using a somewhat higher $400 billion base gives about $680 billion. That remains below rung 1's $700 billion boundary. Higher run-rate and demand evidence supports the upper end, but does not supply a sufficiently quantified FY2028 total to take the forecast above $700 billion; infrastructure constraints also temper the upside.\nThe rung remains 2, but the evidence in force now includes a specific estimate of $555 billion trailing-twelve-month revenue by approximately September 2027, which supports a higher run-rate than the consensus-based calculation alone. It strengthens the upper-end case without establishing a FY2028 total above the $700 billion boundary.\ncite e3658,e3541,e3397,e3659,e4064,e3471,e3660,e4135,e3939,e3980,e3993,e3994,e3995,e3996 dependencies  themes v10:first,v19:first,v25:first,v26:first,v27:none,v5:first,v6:first\npush first=e3397,e3471,e3541,e3631,e3659,e3660,e3872,e3873,e3874,e3939,e3980,e3994,e3995,e3996,e4135,e4158,e3475 last=e3396,e3658,e3993,e4156,e4157 none=e3632,e3841,e4043,e4059,e4064\nv7 3 rung 2 changed True\nThe market reference is about $574 billion for FY2028, derived from roughly $397 billion of FY2027 consensus revenue and 44% growth; that is rung 3.\nApplying NVIDIA's approximately 70% FY2028 growth outlook to the roughly $396–397 billion FY2027 consensus base implies about $673–675 billion of FY2028 revenue. Higher-run-rate analyst scenarios approach $700 billion, but the guidance-based calculation remains below rung 1's threshold.\nThe reference is the roughly $574 billion FY2028 consensus-derived estimate, which sits at rung 3. NVIDIA's approximately 70% FY2028 growth outlook, applied to the roughly $396–397 billion FY2027 consensus base, implies about $673–675 billion: above rung 2's $600 billion floor but below rung 1's $700 billion threshold. Higher-run-rate analyst scenarios approach $700 billion, but they do not outweigh the guidance-based calculation as the central forecast. The new estimate that local Chinese chips could meet 80% of China's AI infrastructure demand by 2028 is a downside consideration, but does not quantify a reduction sufficient to move the forecast below $600 billion. The capacity constraint and deployment risks also temper the upside. No dependency conclusions are supplied.\nThe rung remains 2, but the newly supplied quantified estimate that Chinese chips could serve 80% of China's AI infrastructure demand by 2028 adds a material downside consideration to the supporting facts. It is not enough to move the implied outcome across a rung boundary.\ncite e3658,e4043,e3659,e4064,e3660,e4135,e3980,e3993,e3939,e3994,e3995,e3996,e4157 dependencies  themes v10:first,v19:first,v25:first,v26:first,v27:last,v5:first,v6:first\npush first=e4043,e3659,e4064,e3660,e4135,e3939,e3994,e3995,e3996 last=e3658,e3980,e3993,e4157 none=\nSOURCES\ne3396 N1N2N3O1O2 Analysts estimate NVIDIA revenue of about $390 billion in 2027 and over $550 billion in 2028, with annual sales growth of roughly 24%–25%.\ne3397 N1N2N3O1O2 NVIDIA management's outlook is about 70% revenue growth in FY2028, which the author says could imply revenue near $670 billion.\ne3471 N1N2N3O1O2 The author estimates NVIDIA could generate $700 billion to $800 billion in FY2028 revenue, using an assumed $400 billion in FY2027 revenue and 70% growth.\ne3475 N3O1O2 NVIDIA and MediaTek plan to work together on local AI computing and AI-powered vehicle platforms and bring NVIDIA accelerated-computing technology into more markets.\ne3541 N1N2N3O1O2 The article forecasts NVIDIA FY2028 revenue of $665 billion.\ne3631 N1N3O1O2 The author estimates NVIDIA FY2028 revenue at roughly $700 billion, assuming 70% growth.\ne3632 N3O1O2 The author estimates NVIDIA would generate $447 billion in profits and $18.55 EPS in FY2028 if its trailing-12-month profit margin stays at 64%.\ne3658 N1N2N3O1O2O3 The author calculated that Wall Street's $397 billion FY2027 revenue consensus and 44% growth forecast imply roughly $574 billion of NVIDIA revenue in FY2028.\ne3659 N1N2N3O1O2O3 Applying NVIDIA management's 70% FY2028 growth forecast to the Street's $397 billion FY2027 revenue base, the author calculated FY2028 revenue of about $675 billion.\ne3660 N1N2N3O1O2O3 Using a higher FY2027 revenue figure based on NVIDIA's current run rate and applying the 70% forecast growth rate, the author estimated FY2028 revenue at about $700 billion.\ne3841 N3O1O2 AMD CEO Lisa Su said AMD Data Center revenue would more than double in 2027.\ne3872 N1N2N3O1O2 Dell'Oro Group estimated that overall data-center capital expenditures could exceed $3 trillion by 2030, citing AI-cloud workload demand.\ne3873 N1N2N3O1O2 The author estimated that data-center capital expenditures could grow at a 39% compound annual rate from 2026 through 2030.\ne3874 N1N2N3O1O2 The Center for Strategic & International Studies reported that semiconductors account for 54% of data-center spending.\ne3939 N1N2N3O1O2O3 Amazon ordered 2 million NVIDIA GPUs for delivery across 2027 and 2028.\ne3980 N1N2N3O1O2O3 NVIDIA described its FY2028 sales outlook as constrained by chip production capacity rather than customer demand.\ne3993 N1N2N3O1O2O3 NVIDIA warned that shortages of land, power, and other data-center infrastructure could delay customer hardware deployments.\ne3994 N1N2N3O1O2O3 Microsoft Azure revenue grew 43% year over year in Microsoft FY2026 Q4, ended June 30, 2026.\ne3995 N1N2N3O1O2O3 Amazon Web Services net sales increased 37% year over year in 2026 Q2.\ne3996 N1N2N3O1O2O3 Alphabet Google Cloud revenue grew 82% year over year in 2026 Q2, with Alphabet attributing growth to demand for AI infrastructure and solutions.\ne4043 N1N3O1O2O3 NVIDIA said revenue is expected to grow about 70% in FY2028, which ends in January 2028.\ne4059 N3O1O2 Broadcom expects AI chip revenue to reach roughly $230 billion in fiscal 2028, about double its forecast for the fiscal year ending October 2027.\ne4064 N1N2N3O1O2O3 The article calculated that NVIDIA's 70% FY2028 revenue-growth guidance, applied to roughly $396 billion of FY2027 consensus revenue, implies FY2028 revenue of about $673 billion to $700 billion.\ne4135 N1N2N3O1O2O3 Keithen Drury estimated NVIDIA trailing twelve-month revenue could reach $555 billion by approximately September 2027.\ne4156 N1N2N3O1O2 JPMorgan expects China's domestic AI compute demand to grow at about an 80% compound annual rate; the forecast period is not specified.\ne4157 N1N2N3O1O2O3 JPMorgan estimates Chinese domestic chips could meet 80% of China's AI infrastructure demand by 2028, versus 40% in 2025.\ne4158 N1N2N3O1O2 Maybank Securities analysts say Moore Threads faces insurmountable software and hardware competitive hurdles over the medium term.\nv5 N1N2N3O1O2O3 - v5 (ai-capex, due 2027-03-31) How much will the four largest AI hyperscalers spend on data-center capital expenditures in calendar 2026?: now rung 4 $700 billion to below $800 billion; judged 2026-09-07; updated since this thesis was last judged; reasons: The reference is about $760 billion of combined 2026 capex for the four largest hyperscalers, which sits in rung 4 ($700 billion to below $800 billion). The most directly comparable tracking estimate is near $745 billion, also rung 4. The revised Alphabet, Amazon, and Meta outlooks support a high to\nv6 N1N2N3O1O2O3 - v6 (ai-capex, due 2028-03-31) How much will the four largest AI hyperscalers spend on data-center capital expenditures in calendar 2027?: now rung 4 $1.1 trillion to below $1.2 trillion; judged 2026-09-06; reasons: The market reference is Bank of America's $1.08 trillion calendar-2027 consensus, which is rung 3. NVIDIA's $1.3 trillion estimate is for the top five hyperscalers; applying the rough four-to-five-company share indicated by more than $730 billion for the four named companies versus nearly $800 billi\nv10 N1N2N3O1O2O3 - v10 (chip-export-controls, due 2027-01-31) Will China's authorities authorize Chinese AI companies to purchase Nvidia H200 chips by 2027-01-31?: now rung 1 Yes; judged 2026-09-01; reasons: The reference is rung 2 (No): no market consensus or Chinese authorization deadline is supplied, so the status quo is the reference. The evidence still makes limited authorization by 2027-01-31 more likely than none: reports describe limited imports and deliveries, and NVIDIA confirmed small H200 sh\nv19 N1N2N3O1O2O3 - v19 (chip-export-controls, due 2026-12-31) Will the U.S. Senate pass the Remote Access Security Act by 2026-12-31?: now rung 2 No; judged 2026-08-19; reasons: No explicit market consensus or Senate timetable is supplied. The observable reference is that RASA had not passed the Senate as of August 19, which sits at rung 2. The alleged remote-access cases could add pressure for legislation, but the recent evidence reports no Senate action or scheduled vote.\nv25 N1N2N3O1O2O3 - v25 (chip-export-controls, due 2027-01-31) Will Taiwan's Ministry of Economic Affairs introduce performance-threshold export controls for advanced chips by 2027-01-31?: now rung 2 No; judged 2026-07-28; reasons: The required outcome is formal adoption or entry into force by 2027-01-31, not consultation or consideration. Consultations are confirmed, but the Ministry gave no timeline and the controls were only reported as being weighed. That makes adoption by the deadline less likely than no adoption; the evi\nv26 N1N2N3O1O2O3 - v26 (chip-export-controls, due 2027-01-31) Will Taiwan introduce a criminal ban on AI chip exports to China by 2027-01-31?: now rung 2 No; judged 2026-08-25; reasons: The status-quo reference is rung 2, and the evidence still makes no ban by the deadline more likely than a ban. Taiwan is considering a criminal ban, and the alleged export case—along with lawmakers’ and experts’ stated view that the law needs to change—adds pressure toward legislation. But the supp\nv27 N1N2N3O1O2O3 - v27 (chip-export-controls, due 2027-01-31) Will the U.S. Department of Commerce conclude its investigation into Chinese firms' access to advanced U.S. chips by 2027-01-31?: now rung 2 No; judged 2026-08-25; reasons: The reference is rung 2: the investigation is formally underway and described as likely to remain an ongoing point of contention, with no Commerce deadline or consensus supplied. The access allegations and Taiwanese smuggling enforcement show the issue remains active, but they do not report a Commer\n\ncontrol-1:8929 Thesis v21 (NVDA, asset, focus \"NVIDIA total revenue growth in FY2027\", FY2027, due 2027-02-28): How much will NVIDIA's total revenue grow in FY2027?\nv12 1 rung 1 changed True\nCurrent market estimates are 85.4% FY2027 revenue growth (Zacks, e3620) and 83% (LSEG, e2421); both sit at rung 1. The older 42% estimate (e966) sits at rung 3 and is no longer the current reference. NVIDIA's reported 70% full-year growth projection (e3108) is also rung 1.\nFY2026 revenue was $215.94B. FY2027 run-rate: $81.6B in Q1 + $96.2B in Q2 + $108.0B Q3 guidance, then about $121.0B for Q4 by carrying forward the roughly 12% sequential growth implied by Q3 guidance; that totals about $406.8B, or 88.4% growth.\nThe current market reference is about 85% growth (Zacks 85.4%; LSEG 83%), which is rung 1. The older 42% estimate was rung 3, but it is superseded as a reference by the newer estimates. NVIDIA's reported 70% full-year growth projection is also rung 1, though below the operating run-rate implied by its quarterly results and Q3 outlook. The run-rate remains well above the 60% rung-1 boundary, so the forecast stays at rung 1. AI capex themes and the potential for Chinese H200 authorization support the upside; export-control uncertainty and customer/AI-price-war risks temper it.\nThe rung is unchanged, but the implied-outcome calculation is updated using the reported Q2 result and Q3 guidance: carrying the guided roughly 12% sequential growth into Q4 gives about 88.4% FY2027 growth, versus the previous conclusion's 88.5%.\ncite e966,e1883,e2146,e2147,e2421,e2497,e2499,e3108,e3110,e3112,e3593,e3620,e3943 dependencies  themes v10:first,v19:none,v25:first,v26:first,v27:none,v5:first,v6:first\npush first=e2146,e2147,e2497,e2499,e3593,e3943 last=e3108,e3110,e3112 none=e966,e1883,e2421,e3620\nv12 2 rung 1 changed True\nThe ladder cites Wall Street’s 42% estimate as its reference, which is rung 3. The more recent listed market estimates are 83% (LSEG) and 85.4% (Zacks), both rung 1.\nUsing FY2026 revenue of $215.94B as the base, Q1 and Q2 FY2027 revenue of $81.6B and $96.2B, and Q3 guidance of $108B, carrying the approximately 12% sequential growth implied by Q3 guidance into Q4 gives about $407B for FY2027, or roughly 88% growth. NVIDIA’s separately reported 70% full-year growth guidance implies about $367B; both outcomes remain above the 60% rung-1 boundary.\nThe ladder’s stated Wall Street reference is 42% (rung 3), but the more recent listed estimates are 83% from LSEG and 85.4% from Zacks, both rung 1. The quarterly run-rate and the company’s 70% full-year growth guidance still imply growth above the 60% rung-1 boundary; the reference is not the reason for staying at rung 1.\nThe rung is unchanged, but recent Q2 results and Q3 guidance, plus the stated 70% full-year growth outlook, change the facts supporting the forecast and its explicit guidance check.\ncite e966,e1883,e2146,e2147,e2497,e2499,e2421,e2989,e3108,e3620,e3943,e4049,e3110,e3112 dependencies  themes v10:first,v5:first,v6:first,v25:first,v26:first,v27:first\npush first=e2146,e2147,e2497,e2499,e2989 last=e966,e2421,e3108,e3620,e3943,e4049,e3110,e3112 none=e1883\nv12 3 rung 1 changed False\nThe latest supplied market reference is Zacks' 85.4% FY2027 revenue-growth estimate (rung 1); LSEG's 83% estimate is also rung 1. The older 42% estimate sits at rung 3 but is not the current reference.\nAbout $406.8 billion of FY2027 revenue, or approximately 88.4% growth over FY2026's $215.94 billion: $81.6 billion in Q1 plus $96.2 billion in Q2 plus the $108 billion Q3 midpoint, then about $121.0 billion in Q4 by carrying forward the approximately 12% sequential growth implied by Q3 guidance.\nThe current market reference is Zacks' 85.4% FY2027 growth estimate, with LSEG at 83%; both sit at rung 1. NVIDIA's reported 70% full-year growth guidance also sits at rung 1, though it is a lower anchor. The quarterly run-rate implies about $406.8 billion for FY2027, or 88.4% growth: Q1 and Q2 revenue plus Q3 midpoint guidance total $285.8 billion, and carrying the approximately 12% sequential growth implied by Q3 guidance into Q4 gives about $121.0 billion. A 70% full-year outcome would require Q4 revenue of only about $81 billion, a sharp sequential decline despite the Q3 guidance; the stronger near-quarter operating evidence therefore favors the higher run-rate. The implied growth remains well above the 60% rung-1 boundary. The announced AWS GPU deployment spans 2027 and 2028 without a disclosed revenue contribution, so it does not justify adding its full value to FY2027.\nThe rung and key run-rate conclusion are unchanged from the previous judgement: the updated calculation is about 88.4% versus the previous 88.5%, a rounding-level difference using the same quarterly run-rate logic. The approximately $400 billion estimate repeats an existing estimate, and the AWS deployment plan is not sized to FY2027 revenue.\ncite e1883,e2146,e2147,e2118,e2244,e2421,e2497,e2499,e3108,e3593,e3620,e3683,e3943,e4049,e3110,e3112 dependencies  themes v10:first,v19:first,v25:first,v26:first,v5:first,v6:first\npush first=e2146,e2147,e2118,e2244,e2421,e2497,e2499,e3108,e3593,e3620,e3683,e3943,e4049 last=e3110,e3112 none=e1883\nv7 1 rung 1 changed True\nWall Street's FY2027 estimate of 85.4% growth (e3620) is the market reference and sits at rung 1. LSEG's 83% estimate (e2421) is also rung 1; NVIDIA's 70% full-year growth guidance (e3108) is rung 1 as well.\nFY2027 revenue is about $406.8 billion: $81.6B in Q1, $96.2B in Q2, $108.0B guided for Q3, and approximately $121.0B for Q4 by carrying forward Q3's 12% sequential growth. Compared with FY2026 revenue of $215.94 billion, this implies approximately 88.4% growth.\nThe reference is Wall Street's 85.4% FY2027 growth estimate, which sits at rung 1; the cited LSEG estimate of 83% and NVIDIA's 70% full-year growth guidance are also at rung 1. The operating implication is higher: FY2027 Q1–Q3 revenue totals $285.8 billion ($81.6B + $96.2B + $108.0B); carrying the 12% sequential growth implied by Q3 guidance into Q4 gives about $121.0 billion for Q4 and $406.8 billion for FY2027. Against FY2026 revenue of $215.94 billion, that is approximately 88.4% growth. This is above the 60% rung-1 boundary, so the forecast remains rung 1.\nThe rung is unchanged, but the supporting run-rate is updated using the reported Q2 result and Q3 guidance now in force; carrying the Q3 sequential growth rate forward implies about 88.4% FY2027 growth.\ncite e1883,e2118,e2146,e2147,e2154,e2244,e2421,e2497,e2499,e2762,e2989,e3108,e3593,e3620,e3943,e4049 dependencies  themes v10:first,v19:first,v25:first,v26:first,v27:none,v5:first,v6:first\npush first=e2118,e2146,e2147,e2154,e2244,e2421,e2497,e2499,e2989,e3108,e3593,e3620,e3684,e3685,e3943,e4049 last=e966,e3110,e3112 none=e1883,e2149,e2151,e2762,e3683,e3712,e3714,e3883,e3942\nv7 2 rung 1 changed True\nThe latest stated market expectation is LSEG's 83% FY2027 growth estimate, with Zacks at 85.4%; both sit at rung 1. NVIDIA's 70% full-year growth projection also sits at rung 1. The earlier Wall Street estimate of 42% sits at rung 3 but is superseded as the current reference by the later estimates.\nQ1–Q3 revenue totals $285.8 billion ($81.6 billion reported, $96.2 billion reported, and $108.0 billion guided); carrying Q3's roughly 12% sequential growth into Q4 implies about $121.0 billion for Q4 and roughly $406.8 billion for FY2027. Against FY2026 revenue of $215.94 billion, that is about 88.4% growth.\nThe latest market reference is LSEG's 83% FY2027 growth estimate and Zacks' 85.4% estimate, both rung 1; NVIDIA's reported 70% full-year growth projection is also rung 1. The earlier 42% estimate is a lower, older reference. The latest operating evidence implies about 88.4%, also rung 1, so it does not cross a rung boundary. The Q3 sequential guide is slower than Q2's reported pace, and the no-China-revenue assumption and customer/price-war concerns temper the outlook, but the implied full-year growth remains well above 60%.\nThe rung remains 1, but the key operating inputs have changed: Q2 FY2027 is now reported and Q3 revenue guidance is available. Using those figures updates the implied outcome to about 88.4%; this is a changed evidence basis, not a change of rung.\ncite e966,e1883,e2118,e2146,e2147,e2149,e2151,e2154,e2244,e2421,e2497,e2499,e2762,e2989,e3108,e3110,e3112,e3593,e3620,e3683,e3684,e3685,e3712,e3714,e3883,e3942,e3943,e4049 dependencies  themes v10:first,v19:first,v25:first,v26:first,v27:none,v5:first,v6:first\npush first=e2118,e2146,e2147,e2154,e2244,e2421,e2497,e2499,e2989,e3108,e3593,e3620,e3943 last=e966,e3110,e3112 none=e1883,e2149,e2151,e2762,e3683,e3684,e3685,e3712,e3714,e3883,e3942,e4049\nv7 3 rung 1 changed True\nThe latest supplied market expectation is roughly $400 billion of FY2027 revenue, about 85% growth, as reported in e4049; that reference is rung 1. e3620 gives a Zacks consensus growth estimate of 85.4%, also rung 1.\nFY2026 revenue was $215.94 billion. FY2027 Q1 and Q2 actual revenue plus the $108 billion Q3 midpoint guidance total $285.8 billion; carrying the roughly 12% sequential growth implied by Q3 guidance into Q4 gives about $121 billion for Q4 and roughly $407 billion for FY2027, or approximately 88% growth.\nThe latest market reference is about 85% FY2027 growth, or roughly $400 billion of revenue, which sits at rung 1; the older 42% estimate in e966 sits at rung 3. NVIDIA's reported Q1 and Q2 revenue plus its Q3 midpoint guidance total $285.8 billion. Carrying the Q3-guided sequential growth of about 12% into Q4 implies roughly $121 billion in Q4, or about $407 billion for FY2027—approximately 88% growth from FY2026's $215.94 billion. That remains above the 60% rung-1 boundary. The CFO's cited 70% full-year growth outlook implies about $367 billion and is a meaningful downside counterweight, but the more recent reported results and Q3 guidance support the higher operating run-rate; both estimates remain in rung 1.\nThe rung is unchanged, but new Q2 results, Q3 guidance, and updated market estimates change the key supporting evidence and implied FY2027 revenue run-rate.\ncite e1883,e2146,e2421,e2497,e2499,e3108,e3620,e3943,e4049 dependencies  themes v10:first,v19:first,v25:first,v26:first,v27:first,v5:first,v6:first\npush first=e1883,e2118,e2146,e2147,e2154,e2244,e2421,e2497,e2499,e2989,e3593,e3620,e3684,e3685,e3714,e3943,e4049 last=e966,e2151,e3108,e3110,e3112,e3683 none=e2149,e2762,e3712,e3883,e3942\nSOURCES\ne966 N1N2O1O2O3 Wall Street expects Micron revenue to grow 84% in its fiscal year ending August 2027 and estimates NVIDIA revenue growth of 42% in FY2027.\ne1883 N1N2N3O1O2O3 NVIDIA reported FY2026 total revenue of $215.94 billion, up 65.47% year over year.\ne2118 N3O1O2O3 Morningstar expects industry AI capex to accelerate in calendar 2026 and models 80% growth in NVIDIA total revenue in FY2027.\ne2146 N1N2N3O1O2O3 NVIDIA reported fiscal 2027 first-quarter revenue of $81.6 billion, up 85% year over year and 20% sequentially, about $2 billion above the top of its earlier $78.0 billion-plus-or-minus-2% guidance range; Data Center revenue was $75.2 billion, up 92%.\ne2147 N1N2N3O1O2O3 Yahoo! Finance data showed five upward and no downward analyst revisions to NVIDIA fiscal 2027 second-quarter estimates over the month before August 19, 2026.\ne2149 O1O2O3 NVIDIA's fiscal 2027 second-quarter outlook assumed no Data Center compute revenue from China.\ne2151 O1O2O3 Daniel Sparks calculated that NVIDIA's fiscal 2027 second-quarter revenue guidance midpoint implies about $9 billion of sequential growth, or 11%, versus roughly $13.5 billion and 20% sequential growth in the first quarter.\ne2154 O1O2O3 The author says Jensen Huang set a $1 trillion total-revenue target for 2026–2027 and estimates NVIDIA could generate roughly $400 billion in revenue in FY2027 and $600 billion in FY2028.\ne2244 N3O1O2O3 NVIDIA said its order book for 2026 and 2027 was worth $1 trillion.\ne2421 N1N2N3O1O2O3 LSEG analysts expected NVIDIA total revenue to grow 83% to $396 billion in FY2027, then grow 44% in FY2028.\ne2497 N1N2N3O1O2O3 NVIDIA reported Q2 FY2027 revenue of $96.2 billion, up 18% quarter over quarter and 106% year over year.\ne2499 N1N2N3O1O2O3 NVIDIA guided Q3 FY2027 revenue to $108.0 billion, plus or minus 2%, assuming no Data Center compute revenue from China.\ne2762 O1O2O3 NVIDIA reported total revenue of $215.9 billion for FY2026, up 65% from FY2025.\ne2989 N2O1O2O3 NVIDIA expects Q3 revenue of $108 billion; the author says this puts FY2027 revenue on track to exceed $400 billion if Q4 grows further.\ne3108 N1N2N3O1O2O3 CFO Colette Kress guided to 70% growth for NVIDIA revenue for the full fiscal year; the article says this was ahead of analyst estimates.\ne3110 N1N2N3O1O2O3 Steve Eisman warned that problems at OpenAI or Anthropic could let cheaper Chinese open-weight models trigger an industry price war.\ne3112 N1N2N3O1O2O3 Michael Burry warned that NVIDIA’s reliance on Microsoft, Meta, Amazon, and Alphabet means a pullback by even one could meaningfully hurt revenue.\ne3593 N1N3O1O2O3 NVIDIA and AWS announced plans on August 26 to deploy 2 million additional NVIDIA GPUs across AWS's global infrastructure during 2027 and 2028.\ne3620 N1N2N3O1O2O3 Zacks consensus estimated NVIDIA total revenue growth of 85.4% in FY2027 and 2.2% in FY2028.\ne3683 N3O1O2O3 NVIDIA projected 70% revenue growth in 2027, compared with analysts' 44% forecast.\ne3684 O1O2O3 NVIDIA CEO Jensen Huang said AI is reaching an inflection point that will change computing and cause a major upheaval in the demand cycle.\ne3685 O1O2O3 Jensen Huang said the current AI cycle is industrially driven rather than demand-driven or seasonal, because computing technology is changing fundamentally.\ne3712 O1O2O3 AMD's total revenue rose 50% in its recently reported results; the item does not specify the reporting period.\ne3714 O1O2O3 NVIDIA's total revenue rose 106% in its recently reported results; the item does not specify the reporting period.\ne3883 O1O2O3 NVIDIA reported fiscal second-quarter revenue of $96.2 billion, up 106% year over year.\ne3942 O1O2O3 NVIDIA's total revenue grew 106% year over year in FY2027 Q2.\ne3943 N1N2N3O1O2O3 NVIDIA's FY2027 Q3 guidance implied total revenue growth of 12% sequentially at the midpoint.\ne4049 N2N3O1O2O3 The article estimates NVIDIA FY2027 revenue of about $400 billion, up about 85% year over year.\nv5 N1N2N3O1O2O3 - v5 (ai-capex, due 2027-03-31) How much will the four largest AI hyperscalers spend on data-center capital expenditures in calendar 2026?: now rung 4 $700 billion to below $800 billion; judged 2026-09-06; reasons: The current four-company reference is a combined 2026 capex plan of about $760 billion, which is rung 4. The best directly comparable tracking estimate is near $745 billion; together with the $760 billion plan, that implies roughly $745–$760 billion, still within rung 4. Revised Amazon and Alphabet \nv6 N1N2N3O1O2O3 - v6 (ai-capex, due 2028-03-31) How much will the four largest AI hyperscalers spend on data-center capital expenditures in calendar 2027?: now rung 4 $1.1 trillion to below $1.2 trillion; judged 2026-09-06; reasons: The market reference is Bank of America's $1.08 trillion calendar-2027 consensus, which is rung 3. NVIDIA's $1.3 trillion estimate is for the top five hyperscalers; applying the rough four-to-five-company share indicated by more than $730 billion for the four named companies versus nearly $800 billi\nv10 N1N2N3O1O2O3 - v10 (chip-export-controls, due 2027-01-31) Will China's authorities authorize Chinese AI companies to purchase Nvidia H200 chips by 2027-01-31?: now rung 1 Yes; judged 2026-09-01; reasons: The reference is rung 2 (No): no market consensus or Chinese authorization deadline is supplied, so the status quo is the reference. The evidence still makes limited authorization by 2027-01-31 more likely than none: reports describe limited imports and deliveries, and NVIDIA confirmed small H200 sh\nv19 N1N3O1O2O3 - v19 (chip-export-controls, due 2026-12-31) Will the U.S. Senate pass the Remote Access Security Act by 2026-12-31?: now rung 2 No; judged 2026-08-19; reasons: No explicit market consensus or Senate timetable is supplied. The observable reference is that RASA had not passed the Senate as of August 19, which sits at rung 2. The alleged remote-access cases could add pressure for legislation, but the recent evidence reports no Senate action or scheduled vote.\nv25 N1N2N3O1O2O3 - v25 (chip-export-controls, due 2027-01-31) Will Taiwan's Ministry of Economic Affairs introduce performance-threshold export controls for advanced chips by 2027-01-31?: now rung 2 No; judged 2026-07-28; reasons: The required outcome is formal adoption or entry into force by 2027-01-31, not consultation or consideration. Consultations are confirmed, but the Ministry gave no timeline and the controls were only reported as being weighed. That makes adoption by the deadline less likely than no adoption; the evi\nv26 N1N2N3O1O2O3 - v26 (chip-export-controls, due 2027-01-31) Will Taiwan introduce a criminal ban on AI chip exports to China by 2027-01-31?: now rung 2 No; judged 2026-08-25; reasons: The status-quo reference is rung 2, and the evidence still makes no ban by the deadline more likely than a ban. Taiwan is considering a criminal ban, and the alleged export case—along with lawmakers’ and experts’ stated view that the law needs to change—adds pressure toward legislation. But the supp\nv27 N1N2O1O2O3 - v27 (chip-export-controls, due 2027-01-31) Will the U.S. Department of Commerce conclude its investigation into Chinese firms' access to advanced U.S. chips by 2027-01-31?: now rung 2 No; judged 2026-08-25; reasons: The reference is rung 2: the investigation is formally underway and described as likely to remain an ongoing point of contention, with no Commerce deadline or consensus supplied. The access allegations and Taiwanese smuggling enforcement show the issue remains active, but they do not report a Commer\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_22","type":"command_execution","command":"/opt/homebrew/bin/zsh -lc \"python3 -c 'import json\np=json.load(open(\\\"poc/data/citation-probe-2026-11-22/packet-C.json\\\"))\nfor n, ks in {\n0:\\\"e1291 e1379 e1381 e2781 e2782\\\",\n1:\\\"e966 e1333 e1249 e1348 e1350 e1388 e1390 e1883 e1764 v16\\\",\n2:\\\"e41 e42 e43 e44 e99 e148 e168 e174\\\",\n3:\\\"e443 e666 e723 e884 e885 e886 e887 e896 e897 v14\\\",\n4:\\\"e1586 e3302 e2767 e2768 e3083 e2933 e2949\\\",\n5:\\\"e3768 e3764 e3749 e2764 e3554 e3956\\\",\n6:\\\"e3658 e3659 e3660 e3471 e4043 e4064 e4135 e4157\\\",\n7:\\\"e966 e2421 e2497 e2499 e3108 e3593 e3620 e3943 e4049\\\",\n8:\\\"e195 e669 e458 e460 e240 e577 e751 v8\\\",\n9:\\\"e2682 e3586 e4250 e4565 e4594 e4713 e3858 e3827 e3961\\\",\n10:\\\"e74 e204 e255 e458 e604 v14\\\",\n11:\\\"e195 e669 e851 e878 e817 e924 e949 e906 e975\\\"\n}.items():\n print(p[n][\\\"key\\\"])\n for k in ks.split():\n  v=p[n][\\\"quotes\\\"].get(k)\n  if not v: print(k,\\\"MISSING\\\");continue\n  print(k,v.split(\\\"Quote: \\\",1)[-1].split(\\\"\\\\n    Opinion\\\",1)[0])\n'\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_22","type":"command_execution","command":"/opt/homebrew/bin/zsh -lc \"python3 -c 'import json\np=json.load(open(\\\"poc/data/citation-probe-2026-11-22/packet-C.json\\\"))\nfor n, ks in {\n0:\\\"e1291 e1379 e1381 e2781 e2782\\\",\n1:\\\"e966 e1333 e1249 e1348 e1350 e1388 e1390 e1883 e1764 v16\\\",\n2:\\\"e41 e42 e43 e44 e99 e148 e168 e174\\\",\n3:\\\"e443 e666 e723 e884 e885 e886 e887 e896 e897 v14\\\",\n4:\\\"e1586 e3302 e2767 e2768 e3083 e2933 e2949\\\",\n5:\\\"e3768 e3764 e3749 e2764 e3554 e3956\\\",\n6:\\\"e3658 e3659 e3660 e3471 e4043 e4064 e4135 e4157\\\",\n7:\\\"e966 e2421 e2497 e2499 e3108 e3593 e3620 e3943 e4049\\\",\n8:\\\"e195 e669 e458 e460 e240 e577 e751 v8\\\",\n9:\\\"e2682 e3586 e4250 e4565 e4594 e4713 e3858 e3827 e3961\\\",\n10:\\\"e74 e204 e255 e458 e604 v14\\\",\n11:\\\"e195 e669 e851 e878 e817 e924 e949 e906 e975\\\"\n}.items():\n print(p[n][\\\"key\\\"])\n for k in ks.split():\n  v=p[n][\\\"quotes\\\"].get(k)\n  if not v: print(k,\\\"MISSING\\\");continue\n  print(k,v.split(\\\"Quote: \\\",1)[-1].split(\\\"\\\\n    Opinion\\\",1)[0])\n'\"","aggregated_output":"old-3:6441\ne1291 July 26 : The WSJ also reported that Nvidia is discussing up to $350 billion in financing to help OpenAI purchase its AI chips\ne1379 Nvidia is also in discussions to finance $350 billion of OpenAI’s purchases of its chips for the US project, according to a person familiar with the matter.\ne1381 Negotiations are in their early stages and could collapse or financing terms may change, the people said.\ne2781 The initial deployment expected to support 4.25 gigawatts of AI factory capacity will be utilized by OpenAI. Each generation of NVIDIA AI factory systems deployed at PORTS-Pike could represent approximately 1.5 million NVIDIA GPUs.\ne2782 Open AI has committed to substantial deployments of NVIDIA AI infrastructure through 2030. OpenAI's existing and planned commitments represent approximately 12 gigawatts of NVIDIA compute.\nlinked-1:8886\ne966 By the end of fiscal year 2027 (ending August 2027), Wall Street expects an additional 84% growth for Micron, while it estimates only 42% for Nvidia.\ne1333 analysts expect revenue to grow 82% this year.\ne1249 A week later, it reported an 85% year-over-year revenue surge for its fiscal first quarter that ended in late April.\ne1348 For the fiscal first quarter of 2027, NVIDIA’s revenues were $81.6 billion, up 85% year over year and 20% sequentially, according to the company’s press release .\ne1350 Management further expects AI demand to remain robust, as they project NVIDIA’s fiscal second-quarter 2027 revenues to come in at $91 billion, plus or minus 2%.\ne1388 For the second quarter of fiscal year 2027 (ending in late July), Wall Street analysts expect an impressive 96% growth rate.\ne1390 Right now, analysts estimate 81% revenue growth for Q3.\ne1883 NVIDIA reported full-year FY2026 revenue of $215.94 billion, up 65.47% year over year, with net income of $120.07 billion and free cash flow of $96.58 billion.\ne1764 and a $104 billion revenue backlog that excludes more than $25 billion in new commitments booked since July.\nv16 - v16 (open; first_is_bullish) How much total CPU revenue will NVIDIA generate in FY2027?: now rung 3 Neutral, judged 2026-08-18\n    Comparison point stated by that judgement: NVIDIA management’s FY2027 visibility to nearly $20 billion in total CPU revenue; this reference is in rung 3 (at least $15 billion and at most $20 billion). No separate market consensus figure is supplied.\n    The system's forecast for it (our own judgement, not evidence): Approximately $19–20 billion in FY2027, based primarily on management’s period-specific visibility to nearly $20 billion. Wolfe Research’s 1.3 million-chip estimate at about $5,000 implies roughly $6.5 billion for calendar 2026, a partial-period cross-check rather than an additive FY2027 estimate; the implied figure remains at or below $20 billion.\n    Reasons (excerpt): The reference is NVIDIA management’s visibility to nearly $20 billion of total CPU revenue in FY2027, which sits in rung 3 because rung 2 requires revenue strictly above $20 billion. The implied outcome remains near, but not above, $20 billion: the Wolfe estimate of 1.3 million chips at about $5,000 implies roughly $6.5 billion in calendar 2026, but that is a partial-period estimate and does not replace the stronger FY2027 visibility. June deliveries and OpenAI’s planned Q3 large-volume deployment support execution, but are not sized enough to lift the total above the guidance-based figure. AM\nold-2:518\ne41 Nvidia 's next marquee product — the Kyber rack-scale architecture designed to house its 2027 Rubin Ultra chips — has been delayed by more than 12 months to 2028, according to research firm SemiAnalysis, the latest in a string of reported setbacks raising questions about the AI giant's product roadmap.\ne42 \"Kyber NVL144 rack architecture has been delayed to 2028 as the PCB midplane remains challenging from a manufacturability standpoint,\" the firm said, referring to a specialized, multi-layer printed circuit board that connects electronic modules within a system.\ne43 NVL576 — a larger system linking eight racks via optical connections — is also likely delayed or limited to small volumes, the research firm said.\ne44 A backup plan — bolting two of Nvidia's current-generation racks together for similar power — has also been scrapped after cloud customers rejected the design as awkward and costly to operate. \"It has since been cancelled due to heavy pushback from CSPs [cloud service providers] and hyperscalers over its odd design and heavy operational burden,\" SemiAnalysis said.\ne99 Nvidia is also understood to have killed NVL72x2, a stopgap rack designed to tide customers over, and that no proven alternative is now available to widen Rubin Ultra's scale-up in 2027.\ne148 An NVIDIA spokesperson said, “Our road map is intact.” Further, \"Vera Rubin Ultra will scale up to a larger NVL576 domain in its planned 2027 timeline.\"\ne168 Designed for the Rubin Ultra platform, Kyber is expected to launch in 2027 as an integrated AI infrastructure system and a key component of Nvidia's long-term AI infrastructure strategy.\ne174 At the same time, Nvidia is planning to add even more processors to single interconnected systems, going from 72 GPUs today to as many as 576 by 2027.\nlinked-1:8834\ne443 visibility to nearly $20 billion in total CPU revenue this year.\ne666 Wolfe Research said in May that it expected the average selling price to be about $5,000 per Vera chip, and forecast that Nvidia would ship about 1.3 million of them this year.\ne723 Nvidia said Vera chips were delivered to clients including OpenAI, Anthropic, and SpaceX in June, according to CNBC .\ne884 “We were actually being a little conservative. I actually thought we were beating them by smaller numbers than what I have here… we thought we’d have at least a 10% advantage. What we’re finding is… we have 20% advantage, and we have not even finished completely tuning.”\ne885 Kuppuswany’s comments overlaid the slide you can see above, claiming 2.2x higher throughput with Venice compared to Vera, and 1.2x faster per-core performance.\ne886 Nvidia’s results show Vera achieving a score of 925 overall. AMD says a 96-core High Frequency Epyc CPU achieved a score of 1,210.\ne887 AMD divided that score by the number of cores; about 6.3 for AMD, and about 5.3 for Nvidia (remember these are 2P configurations). AMD says 1.2x, which actually translates to about an 18.8% lead.\ne896 Colette Kress, Executive Vice President and CFO, said Vera “opens a brand new $200 billion TAM for NVIDIA, a market we have never addressed before,” adding: “We have visibility to nearly $20 billion in total CPU revenue this year, setting us up to become the world-leading CPU supplier.”\ne897 CEO Jensen Huang: “The $20 billion is for standalone CPU.”\nv14 - v14 (open; first_is_bullish) How much total revenue will NVIDIA report for FY2027?: now rung 3 Neutral, judged 2026-08-06\n    Comparison point stated by that judgement: Bloomberg/Wall Street FY2027 revenue expectations are about $393 billion (also consistent with roughly 80–82% growth on FY2026 revenue of about $215.9 billion); that reference is rung 3.\n    The system's forecast for it (our own judgement, not evidence): Q1 revenue of $81.6 billion plus Q2 guidance of $91 billion, with the guided 11% sequential growth carried through Q3 and Q4, implies about $386 billion. Customer spending and order/supply signals support a modest uplift, while memory and China headwinds temper it, giving an implied FY2027 total of roughly $390–395 billion.\n    Reasons (excerpt): The market reference is approximately $393 billion for FY2027, which sits in rung 3 ($350 billion to below $400 billion). The operating run-rate from Q1 revenue of $81.6 billion and Q2 guidance of $91 billion, carrying the guided 11% sequential growth through Q3 and Q4, is about $386 billion. Recent customer spending and order visibility support a modest uplift from that run-rate, but the large Blackwell/Rubin figures span products and periods and are not fully attributable to FY2027; memory constraints and China exposure offset the upside. The implied outcome is around $390–395 billion, still\nold-3:6238\ne1586 is guiding to around $20 billion in Vera sales this fiscal year.\ne3302 Nvidia is leaning into that opening, projecting $20 billion in stand-alone CPU revenue this year and more than double that next year\ne2767 Our Grace CPU introduced in 2021 has been a great success, with revenue on a trailing 12-month basis, exceeding $5 billion.\ne2768 Today, we are in full production of our next-generation Vera CPU.\ne3083 Kress said Wednesday that Nvidia expects Vera to be deployed by “every major hyperscaler, neocloud, AI lab, and system OEM, with shipments already underway to our lead partners,” which include Oracle and SpaceXAI.\ne2933 CFO Colette Kress now expects that number to more than double in fiscal 2028.\ne2949 The deployment will include Vera CPUs, some integrated with Rubin products and some deployed as standalone processors, according to Kress.\nold-2:6854\ne3768 On September 2, 2026, NVIDIA Corporation (“NVIDIA”) entered into a definitive agreement to acquire Hugging Face, Inc.\ne3764 I’m excited to announce that NVIDIA has agreed to acquire Hugging Face for $12,930,300,000.\ne3749 On September 3, 2026, NVIDIA announced that it has agreed to acquire open-source AI platform Hugging Face for $12.9 billion.\ne2764 Business Insider, which first reported over the weekend that Hugging Face was fielding takeover interest, reported Wednesday night that the talks — which would value the company at more than $13 billion — had not yet produced a signed agreement and could still atomize .\ne3554 Nvidia's potential $12.9 billion acquisition of Hugging Face marks a strategic shift toward vertical integration. The two parties have not yet signed a final agreement\ne3956 Nvidia confirmed Thursday that it has entered into a definitive agreement to acquire the open-source AI platform Hugging Face for $12.93 billion\nold-2:6971\ne3658 Given the Street's consensus estimate of $397 billion of revenue in fiscal 2027, that implies fiscal 2028 sales of roughly $574 billion.\ne3659 When applying Nvidia's 70% forecast rate to the same starting base, next year's expected revenue sits closer to $675 billion.\ne3660 Here's where it really gets lucrative: If I use a higher fiscal 2027 revenue figure based on Nvidia's current run rate, the implied sales for next year land closer to $700 billion.\ne3471 Add 70% growth to the $400 billion in fiscal year 2027 revenue, and Nvidia may generate $700 billion to $800 billion in revenue next fiscal year.\ne4043 During the analyst call, Nvidia said it expects revenue to increase by another 70% next year, with that fiscal year ending in January 2028.\ne4064 Applied to the roughly $396 billion consensus estimate for fiscal 2027, that guidance implies fiscal 2028 revenue near $673 billion to $700 billion.\ne4135 trailing twelve-month revenue by this time next year could reach $555 billion (approximately NT$17.5 trillion).\ne4157 It estimates local chips could meet 80% of the country's AI infrastructure demand by 2028, up from 40% in 2025.\ncontrol-1:8929\ne966 By the end of fiscal year 2027 (ending August 2027), Wall Street expects an additional 84% growth for Micron, while it estimates only 42% for Nvidia.\ne2421 For the full year, analysts expect 83% revenue growth to $396 billion, before slowing to 44% expansion next year, according to LSEG.\ne2497 NVIDIA (NASDAQ: NVDA) today reported revenue for the second quarter ended July 26, 2026, of $96.2 billion, up 18% from the previous quarter and up 106% from a year ago.\ne2499 Revenue is expected to be $108.0 billion, plus or minus 2%. NVIDIA is not assuming any Data Center compute revenue from China in its outlook.\ne3108 As if this weren't enough, Kress guided 70% growth for the full fiscal year, which was ahead of analyst estimates.\ne3593 Nvidia's expanding partnership with Amazon's (AMZN) Amazon Web Services (AWS) also requires continued investment. On Aug. 26, the companies announced plans to deploy 2 million additional Nvidia GPUs across AWS' global infrastructure during 2027 and 2028.\ne3620 The Zacks Consensus Estimate for NVIDIA's fiscal 2027 and 2028 revenues indicates year-over-year growth of 85.4% and 2.2%, respectively.\ne3943 In the meantime, Nvidia's fiscal 2027 third-quarter guidance implies 12% sequential growth at the midpoint.\ne4049 This year, however, revenue is expected to jump to about $400 billion, an increase of about 85% from the previous year.\nlinked-1:8776\ne195 The chipmaker is projected to deliver $228 billion in profits on sales of $393 billion in fiscal 2027, which ends Jan. 31. That would represent an expansion of 90% and 82%, respectively, according to data compiled by Bloomberg.\ne669 According to Wall Street's average estimates (provided by Yahoo! Finance), Nvidia's revenue could grow to $393 billion during its current 2027 fiscal year, and then to $559 billion in fiscal 2028.\ne458 Nvidia’s Q1 FY2027 revenue hit $81.615 billion, up 85.2% year over year, with Data Center alone contributing $75.246 billion and Networking revenue rising 199% YoY.\ne460 The company has $119.0 billion tied to supply-related commitments and is guiding for $91.0 billion in Q2 revenue, a forecast that excludes any China Data Center compute sales.\ne240 Management also issued strong guidance, forecasting approximately $91 billion in Q2 revenue despite assuming no China data-center sales because of export restrictions.\ne577 In FY 2026, revenue reached close to $215.9 billion, a significant jump of 65.5% over the previous year.\ne751 The Zacks Consensus Estimate for NVIDIA's fiscal 2027 revenues and earnings indicates year-over-year growth of 80% and 90.6%, respectively.\nv8 - v8 (open; first_is_bullish) How much revenue will NVIDIA generate from stand-alone CPU sales in FY2027?: now rung 3 Neutral, judged 2026-07-22\n    Comparison point stated by that judgement: NVIDIA's FY2027 target of $20 billion in stand-alone CPU sales is the reference; it sits at the lower boundary of rung 2. No separate consensus figure is supplied.\n    The system's forecast for it (our own judgement, not evidence): Roughly $17 billion in FY2027: the $20 billion target is discounted because the cited 1.3 million-unit shipment estimate at about $5,000 per chip implies approximately $6.5 billion for calendar 2026, while the announced Q3 volume ramp is not quantified. That outcome is below $20 billion and within rung 3.\n    Reasons (excerpt): The reference is NVIDIA's $20 billion FY2027 stand-alone CPU sales target, at the lower boundary of rung 2; no separate consensus figure is supplied. The evidence now supports a substantial ramp: NVIDIA reports June deliveries and plans for OpenAI to deploy large quantities from Q3, while HPE has announced a Vera server. But the shipment estimate of 1.3 million chips at about $5,000 implies roughly $6.5 billion for calendar 2026; reaching $20 billion at that price would require about 4 million chips, and the disclosed large-quantity deployment is not sized. I therefore discount the target to a\nold-1:7758\ne2682 We expect to grow revenue by approximately 70% in fiscal 2028. This is a supply-constrained outlook.\ne3586 Management said its fiscal 2028 revenue outlook is supply-constrained and that customer forecasts indicate even faster growth. Nvidia expects supply to remain a bottleneck through the end of fiscal 2028.\ne4250 CFO Colette Kress highlighted that customer forecasts currently indicate Nvidia's revenue may double next year.\ne4565 Huang also reiterated Nvidia’s revenue outlook for next year, saying “I think we could grow 70% year over year. We’re confident about that.”\ne4594 Analysts currently project $411 billion in revenue for fiscal 2027, followed by a 65% jump to $678 billion in fiscal 2028.\ne4713 TSM reported September 10 that August revenue reached a record NT$514.81 billion, up 53.3% from a year earlier.\ne3858 Given the Street's consensus estimate of $397 billion of revenue in fiscal 2027, that implies fiscal 2028 sales of roughly $574 billion.\ne3827 The Zacks Consensus Estimate for NVIDIA's fiscal 2027 and 2028 revenues indicates year-over-year growth of 85.4% and 2.2%, respectively.\ne3961 Yahoo Finance analyst estimates show the Street modeling 63% year-over-year revenue growth for Nvidia in fiscal year 2028 on average, below the 70% target it just guided to.\nlinked-1:8768\ne74 During the first quarter of fiscal 2027, the Data Center segment generated $75.25 billion in revenues, accounting for 92% of total sales. Revenues from the segment surged 92% year over year and grew 21% sequentially, driven by the rapid ramp-up of Blackwell 300 systems and robust networking demand.\ne204 The company reported record revenue of $81.6 billion, up 85% year over year and 20% sequentially, with Data Center revenue reaching $75.2 billion.\ne255 Looking ahead, management guided Q2 revenue to approximately $91 billion, despite assuming zero China data-center revenue because of export restrictions.\ne458 Nvidia’s Q1 FY2027 revenue hit $81.615 billion, up 85.2% year over year, with Data Center alone contributing $75.246 billion and Networking revenue rising 199% YoY.\ne604 NVIDIA now splits data center demand roughly in half between traditional hyperscalers and what it calls AI clouds, newer operators building infrastructure to serve enterprises, sovereigns, and regions. Kress called that second group likely the fastest-growing piece of the business\nv14 - v14 (open; first_is_bullish) How much total revenue will NVIDIA report for FY2027?: now rung 2 Bullish, judged 2026-07-18\n    Comparison point stated by that judgement: Bloomberg's $393 billion FY2027 sales projection is the market reference and sits in rung 3 ($350 billion to less than $400 billion).\n    The system's forecast for it (our own judgement, not evidence): Applying the Q1-to-Q2 sequential growth rate (about 11.5%) to Q2's $91 billion guidance for Q3 and Q4 gives approximately $387 billion for FY2027. Adding the dependency's approximately $19 billion stand-alone CPU estimate gives an implied total of about $406 billion, in rung 2.\n    Reasons (excerpt): The market reference is Bloomberg's $393 billion FY2027 projection, which is in rung 3. Q1 revenue of $81.615 billion and Q2 guidance of $91 billion imply about $387 billion for FY2027 if the Q1-to-Q2 sequential growth rate carries through Q3 and Q4; adding the dependency's approximately $19 billion FY2027 stand-alone CPU estimate gives about $406 billion, above rung 2's $400 billion boundary. The $119 billion in supply-related commitments and management's order comments support demand but are not added directly to revenue. New evidence of HBM supply pressure and China-related restrictions is \ncontrol-1:8790\ne195 The chipmaker is projected to deliver $228 billion in profits on sales of $393 billion in fiscal 2027, which ends Jan. 31. That would represent an expansion of 90% and 82%, respectively, according to data compiled by Bloomberg.\ne669 According to Wall Street's average estimates (provided by Yahoo! Finance), Nvidia's revenue could grow to $393 billion during its current 2027 fiscal year, and then to $559 billion in fiscal 2028.\ne851 NVIDIA’s guidance calls for revenue of $91 billion plus or minus 2% with non-GAAP gross margin of 75%, and it explicitly excludes any Data Center compute revenue from China\ne878 So, while it's beating the benchmarks, the return has fallen short of recent years, despite ridiculously strong revenue and earnings growth numbers and a current quarter outlook that calls for revenue to increase 11% sequentially to $91 billion.\ne817 the company has already lined up potential revenue of $1 trillion from sales of its Vera Rubin and Blackwell AI processors in 2026 and 2027.\ne924 its new Vera Rubin platform has ramped into full production with demand visibility stretching into the trillions of dollars.\ne949 In total, the “AI initiative” with SK Group will be worth more than US$500 billion, Nvidia said.\ne906 SK hynix is also entering into a long-term AI memory partnership with NVIDIA. As a follow-up measure to solidify their previous long-term technical partnership, this agreement allows NVIDIA to secure a stable supply of next-generation AI memory, while enabling SK hynix to expand the foundation for growth.\ne975 Earlier this year, Huang provided a sales forecast of $1 trillion combined for Blackwell and Vera Rubin through 2027, so there's little room for setbacks.\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
